1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Ryves
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Ryves (c.1570–1648) was a member of a distinguished Dorsetshire family. He enjoyed a successful legal career in Ireland, holding office as Attorney General for Ireland and justice of the Court of King's Bench (Ireland)
2 CONC . For a time he acted as Deputy to the Lord Chancellor of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Ryves, born 1570, ranks along with his brothers, George and Thomas, and his cousin, Dr. Brune Ryves, as among the most illustrious of the Ryves family. He was admitted to the Middle Temple in 1592 and was made Master o
2 CONC f the Bench in 1619. He was knighted and sent to Ireland in 1629 as Attorney General. He became the third Justice of the King's Bench in 1660. It appears he owned some land called Great Horestowne, Shiphouse Close, Abington, Hawkw
2 CONC ell and other property in the parish of St. Gi'es within the suburb of Oxford and Wolvercott Holliwell and Marston.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1634 he appears as a member of Parliament in the Irish House of Commons. In 1636 he was appointed one of the Judges of the King's Bench. In 1641 he was appointed as speaker of the Irish House of Lords. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife's name is unknown but she appears to be the daughter of a man named Latham of Lancashire. He had several children including John, Wiliam, Thomas, Charles, George, John, a daughter who marrid Sir John Stanley, a daughter w
2 CONC ho married Sir Arthur Lee, a daughter, Elizabeth who married Sir Arthur Leigh.
2 CONT
2 CONT He then married Dorothy Waldram, daughter of John Waldram but had no additional children.
2 CONT
2 CONT For additional decendents see pages 22-24 of Ryves chronical.
