1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Ryves
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas Ryves (c.1583–1652) was an English civilian. He was a member of a prominent Dorsetshire gentry family. He became a specialist in ecclesiastical law and Admiralty law.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The eleventh and last child of John Ryves of Damory Court. Elected to Winchester College at the age of 11 and was admitted in 1590. He received his BCL (1604), Master in Chancery (1618), Judge of the Faculties and Prerogative Cour
2 CONC t in Ireland. Advocate General to Charles I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was knighted in 1644 and is buried in St. Clement Danes.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is noted that he studied at the best universities in France and is apparently the first in the family to reside in Ireland (be followed by his brothers Sir William and James).
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the recommendation of Sir Arthur Chichester, Viceroy of Ireland, King James I granted him the title of "Professor of the Civil Laws" (office of the probate of wills). 
2 CONT
2 CONT His appointment provoked something of a storm but powerful kinsmen such as Sir John Davys, Attorney General of Ireland came to his defense.
2 CONT
2 CONT His widow wrote that "her husband was Advocate General to King James I and attended Charles I even to his murder with such fidelity that on the treaty in the Isle of Wight, the king requested permission from him with a few others 
2 CONC to come to him. Being violently enforced to take his most dismal and sorrowful last long leave of His Majesty he was so utterly broken hearted that he languished and died leaving her in a most distressed condition."
