1 NAME James /Baskerville/
2 GIVN James
2 SURN Baskerville
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1530
2 PLAC Hereford, Herefordshire, England
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1573
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Peter Bartrum, http://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/6171/BASKERVILLE_56.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (March 4, 2017; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''BASKERVILLE, James (by 1506-72), of Eardisley, Herefs.
2 CONT *'''b. by 1506, 1st s. of Sir James Baskerville of Eardisley by Elizabeth, da. of John Breynton of Sugwas and of London, bro. of John and Sir Thomas. m. by Nov. 1537, Elizabeth, da. of Walter Devereux, 3rd Ld. Ferrers of Chartley 
2 CONC (later 1st Visct. Hereford), s.p.; at least 1s. illegit. by Elizabeth Harris. suc. fa. 13 Nov. 1546, uncle 21 July 1551. Kntd. 24 Nov. 1547.2
2 CONT *Following the death of John Rudhale in 1530 Cromwell suggested that his place in the Commons as one of the knights for Herefordshire should be taken by either John Scudamore or '''James Baskerville'''. Cromwell seems to have pref
2 CONC erred Scudamore and it was he who replaced Rudhale, but when in 1533 the other knight of the shire, Sir Richard Cornwall, also died it may well have been Baskerville who was by-elected. If so, he probably also sat in the Parliamen
2 CONC t of 1536 in accordance with the King’s request for the re-election of the previous Members.4
2 CONT *'''Of the two James Baskervilles, father and son, who could have been nominated, and perhaps elected, it is the older man on whom the choice is likely to have fallen. He was by then an established figure in his county and a not u
2 CONC nknown one at court, where in 1516 he had been made an esquire of the body extraordinary. Seated at Eardisley, he had over a period of 20 years become a prominent local administrator: a justice of the peace since 1511, he had serv
2 CONC ed on several subsidy commissions and one of gaol delivery, and in 1523 had been pricked sheriff. Eight years later his standing was such that he was included in a list of ‘lords marchers’ which began with the Duke of Richmond, an
2 CONC d in 1532 he would have been a natural alternative to Scudamore, a man of comparable rank and experience, as a nominee for the vacant knighthood of the shire. That the preference for Scudamore was no reflection on Baskerville’s st
2 CONC anding is shown by the knighthood conferred on Baskerville at Anne Boleyn’s coronation a few months later and by the increasing use made of him in many capacities, including a second term as sheriff, until his death in 1546.5
2 CONT *'''If by 1532 the elder Baskerville was in the midstream of his career, the younger one was only on the brink of his. Although his father’s knighthood serves in general to distinguish the two in the years after 1533 there remains
2 CONC  some risk of confusion between them as there was at the time. What chiefly differentiates them is their separate areas of activity, for whereas the older man continued to operate chiefly in Herefordshire the younger became identi
2 CONC fied with Pembrokeshire: thus it is at St. David’s that he is first met with in an official capacity, as a commissioner for tenths of spiritualities, and at Pembroke that he became steward or deputy steward. There can be little do
2 CONC ubt that he owed his position in Pembrokeshire to his marriage with his kinswoman Elizabeth Devereux, for a house and lands in that shire formed one of the bases on which her father, Lord Ferrers, rested his power in South Wales. 
2 CONC It was to his father-in-law, ‘now in these parts’, that Baskerville wrote from Pembroke on 1 Nov. 1537 to report the seditious words attributed to a Portuguese shipmaster there. His connexion with Ferrers was also emphasized three
2 CONC  months later in a letter of complaint from one Roger Barlow to officials of the augmentations about interference by the authorities of Pembroke in a fair which the writer claimed the right to hold as lessee of a suppressed chapel
2 CONC ry: the burgesses of Pembroke, wrote Barlow, had now chosen Baskerville (whom he mistakenly calls Sir James) as mayor so that he might hold the fair, and as Baskerville was Ferrers’s son-in-law ‘he may do what he list, more by for
2 CONC ce than by justice’. In 1541 the Council ordered Baskerville and John ap Philip to store some bells seized at Milford Haven in Pembroke castle.6
2 CONT *'''Baskerville’s career entered a new phase with his father’s death in 1546. The two had recently worked together in the military sphere, the son leading a contingent of 50 footmen to France in 1544 after his father had been excu
2 CONC sed service, and both of them being concerned with the musters of the next two years. It was as the new head of the family that Baskerville was returned for Herefordshire to the Parliament of 1547, and in the course of its first s
2 CONC ession he was knighted. Nothing is known of his part in the proceedings of the Commons or in politics outside the House, but he may be presumed to have welcomed the downfall of the Protector Somerset, as did his father-in-law. Con
2 CONC currently with his attendance in Parliament went his advancement in local government: he followed his father on the midland commission of oyer and terminer and in the shrievalty, which he obtained in 1550 after being passed over i
2 CONC n the previous year, but appears not to have joined the commission of the peace until the following reign. The support which his father-in-law, made Viscount Hereford, gave to Somerset’s victorious rival Northumberland lends colou
2 CONC r to the statement (made by Browne Willis) that Baskerville sat again for Herefordshire in the Parliament which met in March 1553 under Northumberland’s aegis, but this is unconfirmed by evidence.7
2 CONT *'''The remainder of Baskerville’s life was to be devoted to his own and his county’s affairs. In 1564 the bishop of Hereford rated him a ‘neuter in religion’ and he was one of the witnesses to this tepid description of himself to
2 CONC  the Council: five years later he signed the submission to the Act of Uniformity. He made his will on 18 Aug. 1572. After asking to be buried in the church at Eardisley, he provided for his younger brothers Humphrey and Walter, a 
2 CONC ward Anne Riddall (?Rudhale), an illegitimate son Charles, and servants. He left to his brother John his armoury and signet ring and to his ‘very dear friend’ Charles Foxe a gelding, with six mourning rings for distribution among 
2 CONC his relatives. As residuary legatee and executor he appointed his cousin Walter Baskerville, and as supervisor Charles Foxe. He died on 28 Sept. 1572 and the will was proved early in the following year. He was succeeded in the fam
2 CONC ily property by his brother John.8
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1509-1558/member/baskerville-james-1506-72 _________________________
2 CONT *'''A genealogical and heraldic history of the commoners of Great Britain and ... By John Burke
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=uo9AAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA90&lpg=PA90&dq=John+Baskerville+1408&source=bl&ots=3tCQwhIQHs&sig=sp2CQXVUFaG1P0MuqGFhNvDIEYA&hl=en&sa=X&ei=NxtoT5afOMPRiALMuLWVBw&ved=0CDYQ6AEwBDge#v=onepage&q=John%20Baskervill
2 CONC e%201408&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 90
2 CONT *   SIR JAMES BASKERVILLE, knt. of Erdisley, sheriff of Hereford, 28th HENRY VI. 4th EDWARD IV. and 14th HENRY VII. which office had been frequently filled by his ancestors. This gentleman espoused Sibill, daughter of Walter Dever
2 CONC eux, Lord Ferrers, of Chartley,* and had issue,  .....
2 CONT *Pg. 91
2 CONT *Sir James Baskerville was s. by his son,
2 CONT *   SIR WALTER BASKERVILLE, knt. of Erdisley, sheriff of Hereford, 9th of EDWARD IV. who was created a knight of the bath, on the marriage of Prince ARTHUR, in 1501. He m. first, Anne, daughter of Morgan ap Jenkin ap Philip, of Pe
2 CONC ncoyd, and had issue,
2 CONT **	I. SIR JAMES BASKERVILLE, knt. of Erdisley, who m. Elizabeth, daughter and co-heir of John Breynton, of the county of Herford, and had, with several daughters, five sons, viz.  
2 CONT ***'''		1. JAMES (Sir), of Erdisley, sheriff of Hereford 34th of HENRY VIII. m. Catherine, daughter of Walter, Viscount Hereford, but d. s. p. in 1573.
2 CONT ***		2. JOHN, of Erdisley, whose male line terminated in 1617, upon the demise, issueless, of his great grandson, SIR HUMPHREY BASKERVILLE, knt. of Eardesley, sheriff of Hereford in 1610.
2 CONT ***		3. THOMAS (sir), of Brinsop, county of Herford, called in Nash's Worcestershire, of Wolves Hill, in the county of Worcester. This gentleman m. Eleanor, dau. and co-heir of Richard Abington, esq. of Brockhampton, in the county
2 CONC  of Hereford, and relict of John Dansey, esq. of Brinsop,  by whom he had an only daughter and heiress, ....
2 CONT ***		4. Walter, of Erdisley, who m. Jane, relict of Thomas Thame, of Stovil or Stowell, but d. s. p.
2 CONT ***		5. HUMPHREY, of Aberedow and Lambedr, in the county of Radnor, which estates he acquired in marriage with Eleanor, dau. and heiress of John ap Gwillem. His
2 CONT ***Pg. 92
2 CONT ***great-great-great grandson and representative,  ..... _________________________
2 CONT *'''The visitation of Herefordshire made by Robert Cooke, Clarencieux, in 1569 (1886)
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/details/visitationofhere00cookrich
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/visitationofhere00cookrich#page/7/mode/1up
2 CONT **	Baskervile (of Eardisley, R. 106 (ped.)). Harl. 6:5, ff. 23b, 24.
2 CONT *Sir John Baskervile (4 H. IV.) = doughter to Symon abruges.; ch: Sir John (m. _ Audeley), Rulf (m. Anne Blakett) Baskervile.
2 CONT *Sir John Baskervile (b. 1408, d. 1455.) =  . . .  doughter to Lord Audeley.; ch: Sir James (m. Sybil Devereulx), John (m. Elynor Holcott), Humfreye Baskervile.
2 CONT *Sir James Baskervile (H. S. 38 H. VI.) = Sybil, doughter to Sir Walter Devereulx.; ch: Sir Walter (m. dau. of Morgan John Ap Philipes), John, dau. (m. _ Roden), Philipe Baskervile
2 CONT *Sir Walter Baskervile (d.1505). = . . .  doughter to Morgan John Ap Philipes of Penecoyte, (Morgan of Pencoed, see Philllips' Glam., Ped. 37. Also see "Notices of Pencoyd Castle" by Octavius Morgan, 1864.); ch: Sir James (m. Eliz
2 CONC abethe Abreyton (Breynton)), John (2 sonne), Sir Philipe (3 sonne), Thomas (4 sonne), Elizabethe (m. Senacre Delabere) Baskervile
2 CONT *Sir James Baskervile of Heredisley in the conty of Hereforde, Knight, mar. (d. 1546) = Elizabethe, doughter to John Abreyton (Breynton); ch: (Pg.8 '''Sir James (m. Elizabethe. d. of Viscounte Hereforde)''', Sir Thomas (m. Elinor 
2 CONC Abington), Walter (m. Jane Grevill), Humfrey (m. Elynor Aguilliam), Sybill (m. Sir Robert Whitney), Elizabethe (m. Charles Vaughan) Baskervile)
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/visitationofhere00cookrich#page/8/mode/1up
2 CONT *'''Sir James Baskervile of Hardisey in the conty of Hereforde, Knight, married Elizabethe, doughter to Walter Viscounte Hereforde (he died s.p. 1573). ______________
2 CONT *Walter Devereux, 9th Baron Ferrers of Chartley, created 1st Viscount Hereford (1488 – 17 September 1558) was an English Peer.
2 CONT *He was born in either Chartley Castle or the Chartley Manor which replaced it as the residence of the Barons Ferrers of Chartley following the Wars of the Roses. He was a son of John Devereux, 8th Baron Ferrers of Chartley and Ce
2 CONC cily Bourchier.
2 CONT *His paternal grandparents were Anne Ferrers, 7th Baroness Ferrers of Chartley and her husband Walter Devereux, Lord Ferrers of Chartley. His maternal grandparents were William Bourchier, Viscount Bourchier and Anne Woodville.
2 CONT *William Bourchier was a son of Henry Bourchier, 1st Earl of Essex and Isabel of Cambridge. Anne Woodville was a daughter of Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers and Jacquetta of Luxembourg.
2 CONT *On 5 March 1501, his father died and Walter succeeded him as the 9th Baron Ferrers of Chartley. He served with distinction in the War of the League of Cambrai (1508–1516) and the Italian War of 1521-1526.
2 CONT *In 1523, the Baron was created a Knight of the Garter by Henry VIII of England alongside diplomat Thomas Boleyn, later Earl of Wiltshire. In the late 1520s he came into increasing conflict with Welsh magnate Rhys ap Gruffydd, aft
2 CONC er King Henry granted Devereux major powers in Wales. This reached a head when Rhys with a gang of armed supporters threatened Ferrers with a knife in 1529. The two men were allowed to air their grievances, but Rhys' family contin
2 CONC ued to stir up trouble. Eventually Rhys was charged with treason, convicted and executed. The consolidation of Devereux's position in Wales helped prepare the way for the reformation.[1]
2 CONT *He served as Custos Rotulorum of Cardiganshire from 1543 to his death.
2 CONT *He was created Viscount Hereford in 1550.
2 CONT *He was first married to Mary Grey (1491- 22 February 1538) on 15 December 1503. She was a daughter of Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset and his second wife Cecily Bonville, Baroness Harington and Bonville. Cecily Bonville was t
2 CONC he daughter and heiress of William Bonville, 6th Baron Harington by his wife Katherine Neville. Her maternal grandparents were Alice Montagu, 5th Countess of Salisbury and her consort Richard Neville, 5th Earl of Salisbury (jure u
2 CONC xoris). By Mary Grey he had three children:
2 CONT ** 1.Sir Richard Devereux (d. 13 October 1547). Married Dorothea Hastings. She was a daughter of George Hastings, 1st Earl of Huntingdon and Anne Stafford. They were parents to Walter Devereux, 1st Earl of Essex.
2 CONT ** 2.Sir William Devereux of Mirevale Abbey. Married Jane Scudamore, daughter of John Scudamore. The marriage produced two daughters, Barbara Devereux and Margaret Devereux.
2 CONT ** 3.Henry Devereux, died unmarried.
2 CONT *In 1539, he married secondly Margaret Garneys, daughter of John Garneys of Kenton. They had two children:
2 CONT ** 1.Sir Edward Devereux, 1st Baronet of Castle Bromwich. Married Catherine Arden. They were parents of Walter Devereux, 5th Viscount Hereford, fourth-generation ancestors of Price Devereux, 9th Viscount Hereford and fifth-generat
2 CONC ion ancestors of Edward Devereux, 11th Viscount Hereford.
2 CONT **''' 2.Katherine Devereux. Married Sir James Baskerville.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Devereux,_1st_Viscount_Hereford ____________________________ See Peter Bartrum, http://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/6171/BASKERVILLE_56.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (Decem
2 CONC ber 20, 2016; Anne Brannen, curator)
