1 NAME Thomas /Hall/ Esq.
2 GIVN Thomas
2 SURN Hall
2 NSFX Esq.
2 _MARNM Hall
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1510
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bradford-on-Avon
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE BEF 1575
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bradford-on-Avon
3 STAE England
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Thomas Hall==
2 CONT * b. c1510 
2 CONT * d. Bfr 1575 
2 CONT * Parents: William Hall, Elizabeth Tropenell
2 CONT * Wife: Elizabeth Mervyn
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT * 1.  John Hall I, son and heir b. c1540 d. 9/1/1620, of Forde, m. Dorothy Rogers d. and heiress of Anthony of Bradford  
2 CONT * 2.  Robert Hall, Wiltshire, England  
2 CONT * 3.  Joan Hall, Wiltshire, England  
2 CONT * 4.  Dorothy Hall, Wiltshire, England  
2 CONT * 5.  Ann Hall, Wiltshire, England  
2 CONT * 6.  Thomas Hall, Wiltshire, England  
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://home.comcast.net/~wdegidio/Hall/hallfamily4.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT A 1575 lawsuit states, “Thomas Hall Esq. deceased is, and his ancestors have for a long time, been seized of water mills and other lands' in the manor of Bradford, held by the abbey of Shaftesbury.”  
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Hall married Elizabeth Marvyn, daughter of John Mervyn of Fonthill. Of the various families of this name mentioned in the "Visitations of Wilts," Somerset, and others, among the most prominent was that of Sir John Marvin, o
2 CONC f Fonthill Abbey, Wiltshire, grandson of Richard Marvyn, who died in the 17th year of the reign of Henry VI. The Marvin family held the fine estate of Fonthill Abbey, in the parish of Hindon, for more than two centuries. The ances
2 CONC tors of Reinold Marvin were all "Yeomen," owning the lands they occupied or inherited. Many derived incomes from the tenants who held under them, but none of them appear to have used armorial device but intermarried with "armorial
2 CONC  families." 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Mervyn was of Pertwood and Fonthill Abbey, he married Joan, daughter of Lord Hungerford.  William Fletcher, owner of Pertwood Manor, in right of his wife Joan, daughter of John Brother of Pertwood, died early in the 16th cent
2 CONC ury, leaving as his heirs Agnes, wife of John Maton, and Margaret, wife of John Ingram. Margaret married, as a second husband, William Mervyn and her share of the manor passed to her son John Mervyn. The moiety belonging to Agnes 
2 CONC Maton subsequently passed to George Ludlow, who sold it in 1553 to John Mervyn, thus possessing the both portions of the property.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas acquired his father's estates and he is said to be the builder of a small Chantry Chapel twelve and a half feet in width and sixteen in length at the southeast angle of the Nave. It was previously thought William Hall, who 
2 CONC married Elizabeth Tropenell, of Chalfield, was the builder of the Chantry Chapel, also called, “The Kingston Aisle" or sometimes referred to as “The Dukes House.”  It was renamed Kingston Aisle after eventually passing from the Ha
2 CONC ll family to the Dukes of Kingston.    
2 CONT
2 CONT The Dukes of Kingston acquired the property after the estates devolved onto Rachel Baynton in 1708.  She was from the neighboring Little Chalfield Manor.  Rachel married soon after her inheritance the 1st Duke of Kingston, William
2 CONC  Pierrepont.  Unfortunately, the marriage was short-lived when William died of smallpox leaving Rachel a widow with two small children.  Her descendant, Evelyn Pierrepont eventually succeeded as the 2nd Duke of Kingston and his da
2 CONC ughter, Frances, married into the Medows family who were eventually ennobled as Earl Manvers.  After Evelyn died leaving no male heirs, the Hall and Pierrepont fortunes fell to Frances's son and the future Earl Manvers.  In conseq
2 CONC uence the name changed to the "Kingston Aisle,” but has since reverted to being called, "The Hall." 
1 FAMS @F6000000019376229469@
1 RFN geni:6000000001783414445
1 SUBM @S6000000001782804924@
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/14/16/fb/46/5344483cd947d5f7/duv43vej_original.jpg?hash=3e89b36993a0abfa1c049756de83c0a5b50bf2782690cd0959b8f050d76bcfd3.1794643199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Hall. Sable. 3 poleaxes argent. (This coat with the crest of Hall)
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE Over the chimney piece of a panelled room upstairs, (being the third of the plates above referred to) are still to be seen two shields carved in oak, each bearing the following quarterings: 
3 CONT
3 CONT
3 CONT 1. Hall. Sable. 3 poleaxes argent. (This coat with the erest of Hall,
3 CONT "an arm unbowed in armour, proper, garnished or, holding a poleaxe argent," is upon a shield in stone over Hall's aimhouse in the town of Bradford).
3 CONT 2. Attobd. Three cylindrical open-barred spindles or reels, apparently
3 CONT for winding yarn. (Or are they eel-traps, called in heraldy, weels ?) The device is very rare and uncertain: but it is evidently some kind of mill apparatus. At-ford was the name of an heiress who married one of the early Halls of
3 CONC  Bradford: and in an old Herald's note book in the Harleian collection of MSS. (4199. p. 91) the word Atford is, just perceptibly, written against this quartering in a rough sketch of the arms of Gore of Alderton.
3 CONT Giles Gore, Esq., of that place (the purchaser, from the Crown, of the Glastonbury Abbey estate at Grittleton in 1561) married Edith, daughter and heiress of a Julian Hall of Bradford (a younger branch of this family). Edith was b
3 CONC uried in Alderton Church, where a gravestone, in the south aisle, still preserves her initials "E. G. 1560" without further inscription. Thomas Gore, the writer on heraldry, used the quarterings 1 and 2 (Hall and Atford) in his bo
3 CONC okplates: and the same arms were also to be seen in Aubrey's time on stained glass in the windows of old Alderton house now destroyed.
3 CONT 8. ?A bend between S leopards or lions heads erased. [The
3 CONT Wilts Visitation of 1565, gives in the drawing of Hall's coat, 3 etoiles on the bend.]
3 CONT *.———? An eagle sable, preying on a fish azure. [This was also found on a seal attached to one of the old deeds lately discovered in Kingston House].
3 CONT 5. Besill. Argent, 3 torteauz, two and one.
3 CONT 6. Hall. As No. 1.
1 OBJE
