1 NOTE  J. Gardner Bartlett's "John Hill of Dorchester, Mass., 1633, & 5 Generations of His Descendants" (c.1904):
2 CONT WILLIAM HILL, born about 1515, established himself at Poundsford in Pitminster, where he died in 1594, having been granted in 1570 the coat of arms mentioned above. Three children of his large family were born before 1548, as per 
2 CONC his mother's will. His own will, dated June 6, 1592, mentions daughter Eleanor Hill, and daughter Lucy Lawrence; sons Alexander and Gabriel; sons John, Thomas, and Benjamin; eldest son and heir Roger Hill. None of the children are
2 CONC  mentioned as being minors. He married first about 1540, Anne, daughter of John Trowbridge of Colyton, Devonshire; she died about 1560; he married second, about 1563, Lucy, daughter of John Ryves of Dorset
2 CONT
2 CONT Initial source: "Ancestors of Samuel Eugene Hill" by Mildred Hill and
2 CONT Will R. Bolen, 1963. It says John Hill was youngest child of William and
2 CONT Margery but doesn't give her surname. This must be an error as the
2 CONT accompanying pedigree gives the names of the two wives of this William
2 CONT as Ann Trowbridge and Lucy Ryves, and shows that his mother was
2 CONT Margery. It also says William was named a son in the will of Margery,
2 CONT wife of Roger Hill, and states that William had 14 children, but doesn't
2 CONT name them. It doesn't say whether any of the children were of his
2 CONT second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT From "The Ancestors of Samuel E. Hill": "William Hill of Pondsford,
2 CONT grandson of William of Taunton and second son of Roger and Margery of
2 CONT Taunton, removed away a short distance from the town of his ancestors.
2 CONT He established himself in the hamlet of Pondsford, in Pitsminister, a
2 CONT parish four miles south of Taunton, where he died in 1594 at the age of
2 CONT seventy nine years. It was this William Hill of Pondsford who was
2 CONT granted the Hill 'arms with sheaves' in 1570. He had fourteen children.
2 CONT "The armorial bearings of William Hill of Pondsford are described: 'Gules
2 CONT engrailed a cheveron ermine between three garbs or, within a bordure
2 CONT arg.' The crest is described: 'A dove rising azure, legged gules, holding
2 CONT in his beak an oak branch vert, fructed or'."
2 CONT The translation is given on the previous page along with a picture. The
2 CONT arms: "a red shield with scalloped ermine cheveron between gold
2 CONT sheaves all bordered in silver." The crest: "a rising blue dove with red
2 CONT legs, holding in its beak a green oak branch with gold acorns."
2 CONT "Since all the emblazures (the agrarian wheat, the peaceful dove, the
2 CONT steadfast English oak with its golden fruit) bespeak nothing of feats of
2 CONT arms, we assume that the citation was for something other than
2 CONT military service, probably monetary, as we believe the Hills of
2 CONT Pitsminister to have been yeomen merchants for several generations in
2 CONT several branches of the family.
2 CONT "It is the grandson of this William of Pondsford from whom the
2 CONT American family of John Hill of Dorchester claims descent."
