1 OCCU Bishop of Durham and Author
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} On the death of Edward VI in 1553 the line of succession fell to Queen Mary I, a Roman Catholic, intent on restoring England to the Church of Rome. During her reign, Pilkington escaped the Marian persecutions in En
2 CONC gland by fleeing to the continent, ensuring his survival.  While there, many protestants were persecuted and executed in England. James Pilkington went to Zürich, Geneva, Basel, and Frankfurt  where he educated local protestant ch
2 CONC ildren and associated with the leaders of the protestant cause in Europe, raising support for Princess Elizabeth. He returned to England in 1559 after the death of Queen Mary knowing the line of succession went to Queen Elizabeth 
2 CONC I. James Pilkington was very involved in stamping out Catholic recusant familes of the North during the Rising of the North.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Rising of the North'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The bishop and his family had fled to London dressed as beggars in 1569 during the Rising of the North. Charles Neville, 6th Earl of Westmorland and Thomas Percy, 7th Earl of Northumberland led the rising. They plotted to overthro
2 CONC w Elizabeth I and reinstate Roman Catholicism. The city of Durham, the seat of the bishop, was not strongly fortified and the rebel earls entered the city on 14 November, with three hundred horsemen, "where they rent and trampled 
2 CONC underfoot the English bibles and Books of Common Prayer". They celebrated Mass in Durham Cathedral and issued a proclamation claiming that their intention was to restore the Catholic religion, but not to unseat Queen Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT The rising had popular support in the region and an army of 1000 on horse and 6000 on foot was raised. The rising, which was plotted at Brancepeth and Raby Castles failed. Brancepeth and Raby were confiscated but the bishop was no
2 CONC t allowed to profit by these forfeitures, which he claimed by right of his Palatinate.There were 700 rebels listed for execution. Those who were not executed were mostly pardoned, a few escaped from the country or were banished. A
2 CONC n Act of attainder passed in 1571 outlawed 56 of the principal rebels. People who could not afford individual pardons were included in large numbers of "group pardons" issued on 25 April 1570 and listed in the Calendar of Patent R
2 CONC olls. At the time of Northern Rising he was in London and the queen did not allow him to profit by the forfeitures which followed, his claim being set aside 'for that time.'
2 CONT
2 CONT James Pilkington died at Bishop Auckland on 23 January 1576 at the age of fifty-five. A contemporary remarked that "this wicked country ... caused Mr James Pilkington to spend his life in continual pains and mournings and at lengt
2 CONC h ended him". His body was buried at Bishop Auckland and reburied on 24 May 1576 at the head of Bishop Beaumont's tomb in front of the high altar of Durham Cathedral.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT _________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pilkington family has its origins in the ancient township of Pilkington in the historic county of Lancashire, England. After about 1405 the family seat was Stand Old Hall which was built to replace Old Hall in Pilkington. The 
2 CONC new hall was built on high land overlooking Pilkington's medieval deer park. Stand Old Hall was replaced by Stand Hall to the south in 1515 after the Pilkingtons were dispossessed. Stand Old Hall became a barn. It is possible that
2 CONC  Sir Thomas Pilkington had permission to “embattle” his manor house in 1470 building a stone tower. It was a ruin by the 1950s and demolished in the early 1960s.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pilkington name is taken from the manor of Pilkington in Prestwich, Lancashire.[3] The Pilkington arms consist of an argent cross patonce voided gules. The Pilkington crest has a mower with his scythe and has a legend that an 
2 CONC ancestor of the family, being sought at the time of the Norman Conquest, disguised himself as a mower and escaped. Ye Olde Man & Scythe Inn in Bolton derives its name from the reaper using a scythe on the family crest.[4] The cres
2 CONC t was first recorded on a seal from 1424.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Throughout the county there were a number of branches of the family, including those from Rivington Hall, Rivington near Chorley and from Windle Hall near St Helens, founders of the Pilkington glass manufacturers. ________________
2 CONC _______________
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''''Profile Picture'''''=
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''''Reproduction of Seal depicting James Pilkington'''''=
2 CONT
2 CONT James Pilkington (1520–1576), was born in Rivington, Lancashire, England. He became the first Protestant Bishop of Durham from 1561 until his death in 1576. He founded Rivington Grammar School and was an Elizabethan author and ora
2 CONC tor.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Pilkington_(bishop)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Dictionary of the National Biography:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Pilkington,_James_(DNB00)
2 CONT
2 CONT PILKINGTON, JAMES (1520?–1576), first protestant bishop of Durham, the third son of Richard Pilkington of Rivington Hall, in the parish of Bolton-le-Moors, Lancashire, was born there about 1520. His mother was Alice, daughter of L
2 CONC aurence Asshawe or Hassall, and sister to Roger Hassall of Charnock Heath, Lancashire (Foster, Durham Pedigrees, p. 255). Leonard Pilkington [q. v.] was a younger brother. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When he was 16 he entered at Pembroke Hall, Cambridge, whence he migrated to St. John's College. He graduated B.A. in 1538–9, and was elected fellow of St. John's on 26 March 1539. In 1542 he proceeded M.A., and in 1551 B.D. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 April 1548 he became one of the preachers of St. John's College, and on 3 July following was admitted a senior fellow of the college, of which he was appointed president in 1550. Strongly inclined by education and conviction 
2 CONC in favour of the Reformation, he forwarded the change of religion by taking part in a ‘disputation’ on transubstantiation held at Cambridge on 24 June 1549, and by lecturing in the public schools of the university on the Acts of t
2 CONC he Apostles. Edward VI, in December 1550, appointed him vicar of Kendal in Westmoreland, but in the next year he resigned the benefice and returned to Cambridge. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Marian persecutions began in 1554, he fled, with other protestants, to the continent, living in succession at Zürich, Basle, Geneva, and Frankfort. While at Basle he lectured on Ecclesiastes, St. Peter's Epistles, and Gal
2 CONC atians. He was at Frankfort when Queen Mary died, in 1558, and was the first to sign, if he did not also write, the ‘Peaceable Letter’ sent to the English church at Geneva.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning to England, he was appointed one of the commissioners to revise the Book of Common Prayer, which was begun in December 1558 and completed in April 1559. During the latter year he acted on the commission for visiting Camb
2 CONC ridge University in order to receive the oath of allegiance from the resident members of the university. On 20 July 1559 he was admitted master of St. John's College and regius professor of divinity, and was afterwards associated 
2 CONC with Sir John Cheke [q. v.] in settling the pronunciation of Greek. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 8 March 1559–60 he preached at St. Paul's Cross in favour of assisting scholars at the universities and increasing the incomes of the clergy. At this period he was termed bishop-elect of Winchester. He delivered the funeral ora
2 CONC tion on the exhumation of the remains of Martin Bucer and Paulus Fagius at a solemn commemoration held at Great St. Mary's, Cambridge, on 20 July 1560. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the course of 1560 he published his ‘Exposition upon Aggeus,’ and was married to Alice, daughter of Sir John Kingsmill. The marriage was apparently private, and he is said to have concealed the fact at first, probably because o
2 CONC f the prejudice of the queen against married clergy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the close of 1560 he was appointed bishop of Durham, and was thus the first protestant occupant of the see. The royal assent was given on 20 Feb. 1560–1, his consecration took place on 2 March, and his enthronement on 10 A
2 CONC pril. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two days prior to the last date he preached at St. Mary Spital, London, before the lord mayor. Shortly afterwards (October 1561) he resigned his mastership of St. John's, Cambridge, wherein he was succeeded by his brother Leonard.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT The bishop had three brothers in the church, and took care to provide for them all. Leonard was presented to the rectory of Whitburn in 1563, John was made archdeacon of Durham, and Lawrence was collated to the vicarage of Norham 
2 CONC in 1565. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 8 June 1561 he preached a memorable sermon at St. Paul's Cross on the causes of the destruction of St. Paul's Cathedral by fire. This discourse, in which he denounced certain abuses of the church, occasioned an angry reply from
2 CONC  John Morwen, chaplain to Bishop Bonner. Pilkington then issued a ‘confutation’ in which he vigorously followed up his original exposure of the Roman catholic church. In June 1562 he preached a sermon before the queen, in which he
2 CONC  exposed the pretensions of Ellys, the self-styled prophet. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a hand in settling the Thirty-nine articles promulgated in 1562. A letter written by him to Archbishop Parker in 1561 or 1564 sets forth in graphic terms the general negligence and relaxed morals of the clergy in the north 
2 CONC of England. In another letter, addressed to Dudley, earl of Leicester, in 1564, he showed himself favourable to discontinuing the use of vestments. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a great stickler for the rights and emoluments of his see, and on 10 May 1564 obtained from the queen confirmation of the various charters relating to his bishopric. In June 1566 he procured restitution of certain temporali
2 CONC ties, but only in consideration of a heavy annual fine to the crown. At a later date (1570) he was unsuccessful in a suit for the forfeited estate of the Earl of Westmorland, but in 1573 he successfully resisted the claim of the c
2 CONC rown to the fisheries at Norham. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the northern rebellion of 1569 in favour of the Roman catholic revival, when the insurgents broke into Durham Cathedral, Pilkington and his family thought it expedient to flee for their lives. After his return to his dioces
2 CONC e he wrote to Sir William Cecil, secretary of state, an account of the miserable condition of the country, and he subsequently brought under the notice of Cecil the teachings and machinations of the English catholics at Louvain, d
2 CONC irected against the Anglican establishment. He was one of the commissioners for the visitation of King's College, Cambridge, in February 1569–70.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1561 and 1567 he held visitations of his cathedral, and on the second occasion the injunctions for the removal of superstitious books and ornaments and defacing idolatrous figures from the church plate were carried out with gre
2 CONC at rigour. The palaces and other edifices in his see were left by him in a wofully ruinous state, and many buildings—some, at least, of which probably were already in bad repair—were demolished by him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Strype characterises him as ‘a grave and truly reverend man, of great piety and learning, and such frugal simplicity of life as well became a modest christian prelate;’ and this character is borne out by contemporary writers, by o
2 CONC ne of whom he is said to have been ‘much more angry in his speeches than in his doings.’ 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 30 Jan. 1565–6 he granted a charter of incorporation to the citizens of Durham to be governed by an alderman and twelve burgesses. He also incorporated several of the trade companies of the city. Stimulated, it is said, by the 
2 CONC example of his friend Bernard Gilpin, he founded and endowed a free grammar school at Rivington, which was opened in 1566, and he encouraged the foundation of a free school at Darlington. The church at Rivington was founded by his
2 CONC  father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pilkington died at Bishop Auckland on 23 Jan. 1575–6, aged 55, leaving a wife and two daughters, Deborah and Ruth. He was buried at Auckland, but his remains were removed to Durham Cathedral and interred before the high altar on 2
2 CONC 4 May 1576. His tomb, now destroyed, contained a very long Latin inscription. In his will, dated 4 Feb. 1571–2, he desired to be buried with ‘as few popish ceremonies as may be, or vain cost,’ and he left his library at Auckland t
2 CONC o ‘the school at Rivington and to poor collegers and others.’ None of his books remain at Rivington.
2 CONT
2 CONT The church at Rivington contains a curious painting representing the bishop's parents and their twelve children. The only known portrait of the bishop is given in this picture, which was damaged by fire in 1834, but has been resto
2 CONC red from a copy taken in 1821.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among his recorded writings are several which were perhaps never printed. Those that survive are: 
2 CONT *1. ‘Disputation on the Sacrament with W. Glynn, D.D.’ (in Foxe's ‘Actes and Monuments’). 
2 CONT *2. ‘Sermon before the University of Cambridge on the Restitution of Bucer and Fagius’ (in Foxe's ‘Actes and Monuments,’ and in Latin in Bucer's ‘Scripta Anglicana’). 
2 CONT *3. ‘Aggeus the Prophete declared by a large Commentary,’ London, 1560, 8vo. 
2 CONT *4. ‘Aggeus and Abdias, Prophetes; the one corrected, the other newly added,’ &c., London, 1562, 8vo. 
2 CONT *5. ‘A Confutacion of an Addicion, with an Apologye written and cast in the Stretes of West Chester, against the causes of burning Paules Church,’ &c., 1563, 8vo. 
2 CONT *6. ‘A Godlie Exposition upon certaine Chapters of Nehemiah,’ Cambridge, 1585, 4to; edited by John Foxe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The above, with extracts from the statutes of Rivington School, and a ‘Tractatus de Predestinatione,’ from the manuscript in Corpus Christi College, Cambridge, were collected as Pilkington's works for the Parker Society (ed. Schol
2 CONC efield) in 1842. He wrote the homilies against gluttony, drunkenness, and excess of apparel.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bishop's younger brother, John (1529?–1603), matriculated as a sizar of St. John's College, Cambridge, in May 1544, obtained a scholarship there, and is commemorated for his learning in Ascham's account of the college (Strype,
2 CONC  Cheke, p. 49). He graduated B.A. in 1546, M.A. 1549, B.D. 1561, and was elected a fellow of Pembroke Hall in 1547. He was prebendary (of Mapesbury) in St. Paul's Cathedral from 20 Nov. 1559 to 1562, was ordained priest by Bishop 
2 CONC Grindal in January 1560, was collated next year by his brother James, whose chaplain he was, to a Durham prebend, and from 1562 until his death in the autumn of 1603 was archdeacon of Durham and rector of Easington. He was buried 
2 CONC at St. Oswald's, Durham, on 31 Oct. 1603. He appears to have married Ann Forde of London in November 1564.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT *[Strype's Works (see references in general index, 1828); 
2 CONT *Scholefield's Memoirs in Pilkington's Works (Parker Soc.), 1842; 
2 CONT *Cooper's Athenæ Cantabr. i. 344; 
2 CONT *Cooper's Annals of Cambridge, ii. 31, 151, 154, 161; 
2 CONT *Baker's St. John's, Cambridge (ed. Mayor); 
2 CONT *Harland and Axon's Genealogy of the Pilkingtons, 1875; 
2 CONT *Pilkington's Lancashire Family of Pilkington, 1894 (with portrait, also in Trans. Historic Soc. of Lancashire and Cheshire, 1893); 
2 CONT *Durham Wills (Surtees Soc.), ii. 8; 
2 CONT *Machyn's Diary (Camden Soc.), 1847; 
2 CONT *Foxe's Actes and Monuments; Surtees's Durham; 
2 CONT *Gent. Mag. November 1860, p. 484; 
2 CONT *Fuller's Worthies and Church History; 
2 CONT *Milman's St. Paul's Cathedral; 1868, p. 277; 
2 CONT *Longman's St. Paul's Cathedral, 1873, p. 57; 
2 CONT *Gilpin's Bernard Gilpin, 1830, p. 147; 
2 CONT *Mullinger's Univ. of Cambridge, ii. 1884.]
