1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Biography: Thomas Meggs, Gent. (c. 1480 – 1559)
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Meggs (also recorded as Megges or Maggs) was a Tudor-era gentleman of Downham in the Isle of Ely, Cambridgeshire. His marriage to the Coplestone heiress was the defining event of his lineage, merging the East Anglian Meggs 
2 CONC family with the ancient West Country houses of Coplestone, Hawley, and Tresilian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lineage and Early Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was the eldest son and heir of William Meggs.[^1] While his early years were centered in the Fenlands of Cambridgeshire, his marriage to a Devonshire heiress necessitated a relocation to the South West. By the mid-16th cent
2 CONC ury, the family had established themselves as prominent landowners in Dorset.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Chronological Correction
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas married Agnes (alias Ann) Coplestone circa 1505–1510. Agnes was the daughter and sole heiress of John Coplestone of Exeter (the second son of the Coplestone-Hawley union).[^2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Note on Dates: Although some digital databases (such as Ancestry and WikiTree) suggest a birth date of 1507, this is biologically impossible as his eldest son, Nicholas, was active by the 1530s. A birth date of c. 1480 aligns with
2 CONC  the generational timeline of his father-in-law (b. c. 1422) and his own death in 1559 at approximately 79 years of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Estate
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas died on July 22, 1559, at the Ancient Manor of Holway, Dorset.[^3] His death occurred during the first year of the reign of Elizabeth I, marking the final transition of the family seat to the West Country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Thomas and Agnes (Coplestone) Meggs:
2 CONT
2 CONT # i. Nicholas Meggs, Esq. (c. 1512 – 1579).
2 CONT # ii. Robert Meggs.
2 CONT # iii. Henry Meggs.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
