2 CONT * See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173392923963 (October 2, 2018; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT * ''''See Peter Bartrum, http://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/5005/Godwin%208.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (February 1, 2018; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Herbert,_1st_Earl_of_Pembroke_(died_1469)_ William Herbert, 1st Earl of Pembroke, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT William Herbert, 1st Earl of Pembroke, had three illegitimate sons, but the identities of their mothers are unconfirmed, including:
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Mawd Turberville
2 CONT *Father Adam Turberville b. c 1400 
2 CONT *'''Mawd Turberville married Sir William Herbert, 1st Earl Pembroke, 1st Lord Herbert, Sheriff of Glamorgan & Morgannock, Chief Justice of North & South Wales, son of Sir William ap Thomas, Sheriff of Cardiganshire, Carmarthenshir
2 CONC e, & Glamorganshire, Steward of Abergavenny and Gwladys Gam, NOT MARRIED; By various mistresses, he had 6 sons (Richard, Esq; Philip; William; Thomas; John; & William).1
2 CONT *'''Family Sir William Herbert, 1st Earl Pembroke, 1st Lord Herbert, Sheriff of Glamorgan & Morgannock, Chief Justice of North & South Wales b. c 1423, d. 27 Jul 1469 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT **◦Elizabeth (Eleanor) Herbert+2
2 CONT **◦Richard Herbert, Gentleman Usher to Henry VII+ b. c 1442, d. 12 Sep 1510
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *1.[S16] Douglas Richardson, Magna Carta Ancestry, 2nd Edition, Vol. II, p. 388-389.
2 CONT *2.[S74] Brent Ruesch's Research Notes.
2 CONT *http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p1744.htm#i52403
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/HERBERT1.htm#William HERBERT (1º E. Pembroke)1 William HERBERT (1º E. Pembroke), by The Tudor Place]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''William HERBERT (1º E. Pembroke)
2 CONT *Born: 1423, Pembroke, Pembrokeshire, Wales
2 CONT *Acceded: 1468, created by Edward IV
2 CONT *Died: 27 Jul 1469, Banbury, Oxfordshire / Northamptonshire, England
2 CONT *Buried: Tintern Abbey
2 CONT *Notes: Knight of the Garter. Leading Yorkist in War of Roses. Captured by *Lancastrians and beheaded 1469 at Banbury.
2 CONT *Father: William Ap THOMAS HERBERT (Sir Knight)
2 CONT *Mother: Gladys GAM
2 CONT *Married: Anne DEVEREUX (C. Pembroke) ABT 1440, Hereford, Herefordshire, England
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Maud HERBERT (C. Northumberland)
2 CONT **2. William HERBERT (1º E. Huntingdon)
2 CONT **3. Walter HERBERT (Sir Knight)
2 CONT **4. George HERBERT
2 CONT **5. Phillip HERBERT
2 CONT **6. John HERBERT
2 CONT **7. Margaret HERBERT
2 CONT **8. Thomas HERBERT
2 CONT **9. Cecily HERBERT
2 CONT **10. Isabel HERBERT
2 CONT **11. Anne HERBERT
2 CONT **12. Catherine HERBERT (C. Kent)
2 CONT
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **13. Richard HERBERT (Sir)
2 CONT **14. John HERBERT
2 CONT *Associated with: Frond Verch HOESGYN
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **15. Anne HERBERT
2 CONT **16. George HERBERT (Sir Knight)
2 CONT **17. William HERBERT of Troye (Sir)
2 CONT *_____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Richard Herbert (died 1510) of Ewyas, Herefordshire was the illegitimate son of ''''''William Herbert, 1st Earl of Pembroke and Maud, daughter of Adam ap Howell Graunt (Gwynn)''''''.[1][2][3] Richard had a full brother named G
2 CONC eorge.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Richard Herbert of Ewyas is distinguished from his uncle, Sir Richard Herbert of Coldbrook, brother of his father William Herbert.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Herbert legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT While the Earl of Pembroke title went to his younger, legitimate half-brother William,[3] Richard realized success through his own merit and his descendants: "Sir Richard Herbert, of Ewyas, who, though illegitimate, is ancestor of
2 CONC  the men who have really, in modern times, rendered the name of Herbert illustrious."[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1465, Richard Herbert was granted Westminster, manors of Grove, Radnore, Mookas, Brutescourt, Throuckeston, Westhide, Egelton, Redehire, Howton and Wormeton Tirell, co. Hereford.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was successful as a Gentleman Usher to Henry VII and appointed Constable and Porter of Abergavenny Castle on 22 July 1509.[3][4] While Richard is most frequently titled "Sir Richard Herbert," there is debate as to whether Richa
2 CONC rd was a knight. Richard Herbert in "The Knights of England" was knighted in 1513, three years after this subject's death.[7] According to Sil, Richard was never knighted, but was an Esquire.[3] Richard is titled Esquire in 1465 w
2 CONC hen he was granted manors and lands.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT In many sources, however,[1][2][4][5] Richard is referred to as "Sir Richard Herbert." An article titled "The Family of Herbert" from The Gentleman's Magazine states that he was knighted by King Henry VIII,[8] in which case Richar
2 CONC d would have been knighted in the last year of Richard's life (died 1510) and the first year of Henry VIII's reign, which began in 1509.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard married Margaret, daughter of Sir Matthew Cradock of Swansea and Alice (Jane) Mancell, widow of John Malefant.[1] Sir Matthew Cradow was receiver of Glamorgan,[3] through whom Castleston Castle passed to his daughter Marga
2 CONC ret and Richard.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard and Margaret had three sons, one of whom was William Herbert, 1st Earl of Pembroke (1501–1570), who became the 1st Earl of Pembroke (2nd Creation)[5] on 11 October 1551.[3] Existing Earls of Pembroke[2] and Montgomery, and
2 CONC  of Carnarvon, of the Duke of Powis, of Pool Castle (extinct 1747), descend from Sir Richard Herbert. Through the female line, of the Marquis of Bute, derives his Glamorganshire estates.[5] William married Anne Parr, sister of Que
2 CONC en Catherine Parr, the sixth and last wife of Henry VIII. William served Henry VIII in many capacities, including Chief Gentleman on the Privy Chamber and the Privy Council and Receiver of the King's revenues. He was knighted in 1
2 CONC 544 and later elected to the Knights of the Garter.[4] The other son was Sir George Herbert of Swansea[10] was ancestor to the Herberts of Swansea, Cogan, Cookham and the White Friars, extinct in 1739. Candleston Castle passed fro
2 CONC m Richard and Margaret to George.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard also was father to the illegitimate Herberts of Dinas Powis and Hengastell[11] (Hen Gastell).
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard died between 2 and 12 September 1510 and was buried in Abergavenny Church; his tomb has a lavishly decorated wall arch and an alabaster effigy.[1][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
