2 CONT Sir Richard Herbert (died 1510) of Ewyas, Herefordshire was the illegitimate son of William Herbert, 1st Earl of Pembroke and Maud, daughter of Adam ap Howell Graunt (Gwynn). Richard had a full brother named George.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Richard Herbert of Ewyas is distinguished from his uncle, Sir Richard Herbert of Coldbrook, brother of his father William Herbert.
2 CONT
2 CONT Herbert legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT While the Earl of Pembroke title went to his younger, legitimate half-brother William, Richard realized success through his own merit and his descendants: "Sir Richard Herbert, of Ewyas, who, though illegitimate, is ancestor of th
2 CONC e men who have really, in modern times, rendered the name of Herbert illustrious."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1465, Richard Herbert was granted Westminster, manors of Grove, Radnore, Mookas, Brutescourt, Throuckeston, Westhide, Egelton, Redehire, Howton and Wormeton Tirell, co. Hereford.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was successful as a Gentleman Usher to Henry VII and appointed Constable and Porter of Abergavenny Castle on 22 July 1509. While Richard is most frequently titled "Sir Richard Herbert," there is debate as to whether Richard was
2 CONC  a knight. Richard Herbert in "The Knights of England" was knighted in 1513, three years after this subject's death.  According to Sil, Richard was never knighted, but was an Esquire. Richard is titled Esquire in 1465 when he was 
2 CONC granted manors and lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT In many sources, however, Richard is referred to as "Sir Richard Herbert." An article titled "The Family of Herbert" from The Gentleman's Magazine states that he was knighted by King Henry VIII, in which case Richard would have be
2 CONC en knighted in the last year of Richard's life (died 1510) and the first year of Henry VIII's reign, which began in 1509.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard married Margaret, daughter of Sir Matthew Cradock of Swansea and Alice (Jane) Mancell, widow of John Malefant. Sir Matthew Cradow was receiver of Glamorgan, through whom Castleston Castle passed to his daughter Margaret an
2 CONC d Richard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard and Margaret had three sons, one of whom was William Herbert, 1st Earl of Pembroke, who became the 1st Earl of Pembroke (2nd Creation) on 11 October 1551. Existing Earls of Pembroke and Montgomery, and of Carnarvon, of the
2 CONC  Duke of Powis, of Pool Castle (extinct 1747), descend from Sir Richard Herbert. Through the female line, of the Marquis of Bute, derives his Glamorganshire estates. William married Anne Parr, sister of Queen Catherine Parr, the s
2 CONC ixth and last wife of Henry VIII. William served Henry VIII in many capacities, including Chief Gentleman on the Privy Chamber and the Privy Council and Receiver of the King's revenues. He was knighted in 1544 and later elected to
2 CONC  the Knights of the Garter. The other son was Sir George Herbert of Swansea was ancestor to the Herberts of Swansea, Cogan, Cookham and the White Friars, extinct in 1739. Candleston Castle passed from Richard and Margaret to Georg
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard also was father to the illegitimate Herberts of Dinas Powis and Hengastell (Hen Gastell).
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard died between 2 and 12 September 1510 and was buried in Abergavenny Church; his tomb has a lavishly decorated wall arch and an alabaster effigy.
