2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwladys was named "the star of Abergavenny" 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Father:  Dafydd 'gam' Ap Llewellyn       
2 CONT *Mother:  Gwenllian Ferch Gwilym      
2 CONT
2 CONT *Birth:   1395 Peutun, Llan-Ddew, Breconshire, Wales  
2 CONT *Death:   1454 
2 CONT *Burial:  Priory Church, Abergavenny, Monmouthshire, Wales  
2 CONT
2 CONT *Spouse 1:  Roger Vaughan     Disc #75     Pin #644252  
2 CONT *Marriage:  1409  Bredwardine, Herefordshire, England  
2 CONT
2 CONT *Spouse 2:  William Ap Thomas Herbert     Disc #75     Pin #650359  
2 CONT *Marriage:  1421  Bredwardine, Herefordshire, England  
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dafydd_Gam
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of Dafydd's descendants, who adopted the surname 'Games' to mark their connection to him, remained one of the most powerful families in the Breconshire area till Stuart times.[5] They were noted for their support for Welsh ba
2 CONC rds. His beautiful daughter Gwladys ferch Dafydd Gam, the 'Star of Abergavenny', made two good marriages, the first to Sir Roger Vaughan, who also died at Agincourt. Her second was to Sir William ap Thomas of Raglan Castle who sur
2 CONC vived the battle. Her son became the extremely powerful William Herbert, 1st Earl of Pembroke (1423-1469) and took the surname Herbert, later to become one of the most well known names in the nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT From Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwladus (the star of Abergavenny, as she was hailed by the poet Lewys Glyn Cothi), died in 1454 and her tomb and effigy can also be seen in Abergavenny in the Priory Church of St Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT From thePeerage.com:
2 CONT
2 CONT      Gladys Dafydd is the daughter of Thomas ap Gwilym and Maud Morley.1 She married Sir William ap Thomas, son of Gwilym ap Jenkin and Gwenillian Hywel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Gladys Dafydd and Sir William ap Thomas
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Richard Herbert+ d. Jul 1469
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried 	Herbert Chapel, Priory Church, Y Fenni, Monmouthshire, Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT COGNOMEN: She was calls "Ddû" on account of her dark hair. (Bradney, History of Monmouthshire, vol. 2 p. 3)
2 CONT
2 CONT DISTINCTION: Lady Vaughan. (Bradney, History of Monmouthshire, vol. 2 p. 3)
2 CONT
2 CONT RESIDENCE: Living at Coldbrook with her son Richard, as a widow, 1454. (Bradney, History of Monmouthshire, vol. 1 p. 338)
2 CONT
2 CONT MONUMENTAL INSCRIPTION: Y Fenni> under the arch between the Herbert Chapel and the choir. (Bradney, History of Monmouthshire, vol. 1 p. 164, 165) MARWNAD {FUNERAL ELEGY}: An elegy written in a marwnad by Hywel ap Dafydd ab Ieuan a
2 CONC p Rhys, poet and historian of Glamorganshire, for her can be found on page 6 and 7. (Bradney, History of Monmouthshire, vol. 2 p. 6, 7) MARWNAD {FUNERAL ELEGY}: A marwnad was written by Lewys "Glyn Cothi" in which he calls her "Y 
2 CONC Seren o'r Fenni {'''the Star of Abergavenny'''}. (Bradney, History of Monmouthshire, vol. 1 p. 186)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Welsh
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://histfam.familysearch.org/getperson.php?personID=I5983&tree=Welsh
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Spouses/Children: 1. Sir Roger VAUGHAN of Bredwardine
2 CONT
2 CONT Watkin (Walter) VAUGHAN of Bredwardine Thomas VAUGHAN of Hergest, Esq. Sir Roger VAUGHAN of Tretower Court Elizabeth VAUGHAN+ Blanch VAUGHAN 2. Sir William ap THOMAS of Raglan Margaret HERBERT+ William HERBERT K.G., 1st Earl of Pe
2 CONC mbroke+ Born: Abt 1385, Peutun, Llan Ddew, Breconshire, Wales Married (1): 1403 Married (2): After 1415 Died: 1454
2 CONT
2 CONT   http://www.celtic-casimir.com/webtree/6/36384.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT    Research Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Dafydd's daughter, Gwladus, married into two of the most prominent families in fifteenth-century South Wales. Her first husband was Roger Fychan (or Vaughan) of Bredwardine (Herefs.), who supposedly died with his father-in-law at
2 CONC  Agincourt having served as an archer in his retinue.... The three sons of Gwladus and Roger Fychan — Walter (or Watcyn), Thomas and Roger (d. 1471) — became staunch supporters of the House of York and great patrons of Welsh poets
2 CONC  later in the century. Gwladus' second husband was Sir William ap Thomas of Raglan, progenitor of the Herbert family and another man who is supposed to have served at Agincourt. Though it should be remembered that the surviving mu
2 CONC sters for the 1415 campaign are far from complete, there is no mention of either Roger Fychan or William ap Thomas in any of the extant musters or financial documentation. Moreover, there are several factors which should give us p
2 CONC ause in taking at face value the story that Roger Fychan died at Agincourt. First, we find, drawing on muster rolls for Henry V's invasion of Normandy in 1417, a man named Roger Fychan, a man-at-arms in the retinue of the earl of 
2 CONC Warwick. While his origins are not specified, Roger was a sufficiently unusual name in a Welsh context for this at least to be noteworthy. If this is a reference to the same man it is unlikely that Roger lived much longer, but no 
2 CONC alternative date of death has been suggested. Secondly, there is the survival of a memorial effigy said to be Roger's in the church at Bredwardine. Memorials present several difficulties as sources, not the least of which is that 
2 CONC such effigies could have been supplied either some time before, or some time after, the death of the recipient. It is also the case that very little is known of the fates of the bodies of those on the English side killed at Aginco
2 CONC urt, including that of Dafydd Gam. What is evident is that the style of armor on this effigy is rather later than that contemporary with Agincourt and is likely to date from around 1450. It has much in common, for example, with th
2 CONC at of Sir William ap Thomas (d. 1445), which survives in Abergavenny Priory. Both effigies bear the Lancastrian SS collar, customarily worn by those in royal service. In Roger's case, such a tomb would hardly be in keeping with th
2 CONC e status of an archer, but such a fine alabaster tomb would well reflect the status of his widow and, particularly, their children, who were so prominent in the "Herbert decades" of the 1450s and 1460s. Finally, the first written 
2 CONC account suggesting that Roger Fychan died at Agincourt appears in Powell's Historie, demonstrating that it was current in the second half of the sixteenth century but was unknown before this time. It was later recited in Sir Samue
2 CONC l Rush Meyrick's editions of Dwnn's genealogies, published in the nineteenth century, and it is from there that Nicholas and Wylie took this detail, which has subsequently be taken at face value. .... What is remarkable about fift
2 CONC eenth-century poetic references to the descendants of Dafydd Gam and Roger Fychan is that their service in France (and their definite or apparent deaths) is never recorded. Two generations after 1415 one of Dafydd's grandsons, Phi
2 CONC lip Fychan, was killed by a cannon ball at the siege of Harlech in 1468; the poet Huw Cae Llwyd in an elegy mentions both Philip's illustrious grandfather and Philip's own service in France. What Huw failed to do, however, was to 
2 CONC commemorate Dafydd's death in more noble circumstances at Agincourt. Perhaps this is not surprising given the proximity of Agincourt to the end of a failed national rebellion. Equally, since the majority of their descendants took 
2 CONC the Yorkist side in the domestic wars of the fifteenth century, the memory of Dafydd's consistent support of the Lancastrian regime during the rebellion might have been uncomfortable. Even so, Agincourt remains perhaps the only ba
2 CONC ttle of the Hundred Years War not recorded in Welsh poetry. In light of the evidence discussed above, however, it seems that, for Watcyn Llwyd and Roger Fychan, no grandiose commemoration of their deaths at Agincourt was appropria
2 CONC te: they did not die there."
2 CONT
2 CONT    Marriage Information:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwladus married Sir Roger VAUGHAN of Bredwardine, son of Roger VAUGHAN of Bredwardine and Anne DEVEREUX, in 1403. (Sir Roger VAUGHAN of Bredwardine was born about 1377 in Bredwardine, Herefordshire, England and died on 25 Oct 1415
2 CONC  at the Battle of Agincourt, France.)
2 CONT
2 CONT     Marriage Information:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwladus also married Sir William ap THOMAS of Raglan, son of Sir Thomas ap GWILYM of Perth-Hir and Mawd MORLEY, Heiress of Llansantffraed, after 1415. (William ap THOMAS was born about 1377 in Plas yn-y-berth-hir (Perth-hir), Monm
2 CONC outhshire, Wales, died in 1446 in Raglan Castle, Usk, Monmouthshire, Wales and was buried in Priory Church, Abergavenny, Monmouthshire, Wales.)
2 CONT
2 CONT    Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Burke's Peerage; Journal of Medieval Military History: Volume IX: Soldiers, Weapons and Armies in the Fifteenth Century, Anne Curry, Adrian R. Bell (eds.), pp. 60-62
2 CONT --------------------
