1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/TOUCHET.htm#John TOUCHET (3° B. Audley of Heleigh)
2 CONT
2 CONT John TOUCHET (3º B. Audley of Heleigh)
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 1420, Heleigh, Staffordshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 26 Sep 1490, Heleigh, Staffordshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes: had livery of his lands in 1459-60, joined Edward IV, was summoned to parliament from 1461 to 1483, and was sworn of the privy council in 1471. He was employed in Britany in 1475, and was present at the coronation of Richar
2 CONC d III, who appointed him lord treasurer in 1484.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: James TOUCHET (2º B. Audley of Heleigh)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Margaret De ROS (B. Audley of Heleigh)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: Anne ECHINGHAM (B. Audley of Heleigh) (b. ABT 1420 - d. 7 May 1498) (dau. of Sir Thomas de Echyngham and Margaret Knyvett) (w. of Sir John Rogers) BEF 1456, Heleigh, Staffordshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Eleanor TOUCHET
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Anne TOUCHET
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Elizabeth TOUCHET
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. James TOUCHET (4º B. Audley of Heleigh)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. John TOUCHET
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Thomas TOUCHET
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Touchet,_5th_Baron_Audley
2 CONT
2 CONT James Touchet, 5th Baron "Audley was first married on February 24, 1414/1415 to Margaret, daughter to William de Ros, 7th Baron de Ros and Margaret Fitzalan. Margaret was daughter to John Fitzalan and Eleanor Maltravers. They were
2 CONC  parents to three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Touchet, 6th Baron Audley (born circa 1420 - September 26, 1490).
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Touchet (born circa 1422 - died before 1480). Married Richard Grey, 3rd Earl of Tankerville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Touchet (born circa 1427 - 1503). She married Sir Thomas Dutton. Sir Thomas Dutton died in the battle of Blore Heath along with his father-in-law James Touchet, Lord Audley, 5th Baron of Audley of Heighley Castle."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p4729.htm#i47290
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tuchet, 6th Lord Audley (of Heleigh) was the son of James Tuchet, 5th Lord Audley (of Heleigh) and Margaret de Ros.1 He married Anne Echingham, daughter of Sir Thomas Echingham and Margaret Knyvett, before 1456.1 He died on 2
2 CONC 6 September 1490.1
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tuchet, 6th Lord Audley (of Heleigh) succeeded to the title of 6th Lord Audley, of Heleigh [E., 1313] on 23 September 1459, by writ.1 In 1461 at Calais, France, he was taken prisoner.1 He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P
2 CONC .C.) in 1471.1 He was joint commander of the Army in 1471.1 He held the office of Master of the King's Dogs on 5 July 1471.1 He held the office of Lord Treasurer [England] in 1484.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of John Tuchet, 6th Lord Audley (of Heleigh) and Anne Echingham
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Sir James Tuchet, 7th Lord Audley (of Heleigh)+2 b. c 1463, d. 28 Jun 1497
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 341. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume I, page 342.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.servinghistory.com/topics/John_Tuchet,_6th_Baron_Audley
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tuchet, 6th Baron Audley 1423-1490. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tuchet was the son of James Tuchet, 5th Baron Audley circa 1398-1459. He married Ann Echingham, daughter of Sir Thomas Echingham with whom he had seven children. He acquired his title by writ in 1459 on the death of his fathe
2 CONC r. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1461 at Calais, then belonging to the English crown, he was taken prisoner by the Earl of Warwick whilst on a military expedition during the Wars of the Roses. He there met the future Edward IV and was persuaded to defect to th
2 CONC e Yorkist cause and fought for Edward at the Battles of Mortimer's Cross, Barnet and Tewkesbury. He was subsequently invested by Edward in 1471 as a Privy Counsellor PC and later 1484 served as Lord Treasurer and Commissioner of A
2 CONC rray. He was joint commander of the Army and held the office of Master of the King's Dogs in 1471. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 26 September 1490 and was buried at Shere, Surrey. He was succeeded by his eldest son, Sir James Tuchet, 7th Baron Audley 1463-1497. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.british-history.ac.uk/report.aspx?compid=15114
2 CONT
2 CONT PURITON AND DOWNEND
2 CONT
2 CONT Three hides of land at Pirition, assumed to be Puriton, formed part of Gastonbury abbey's estate by 854. (fn. 7) In 1066 six hides at Puriton were held by Queen Edith. By 1086 possession had passed to the church of St. Peter in Ro
2 CONC me. (fn. 8) By 1186–7 Maud de Chandos, widow of Philip de Columbers (I) (d. c. 1185) and a descendant of Alfred d'Epaignes, the Domesday owner of Nether Stowey, held the land, which by 1212 was part of the barony of Stowey. (fn. 9
2 CONC ) Maud was followed, as at Nether Stowey, by her son Philip de Columbers (II) (d. c. 1216), her grandson Philip (III) (d. 1257), and her great-grandson Philip (IV) (d. 1262). The last was followed successively by his sons Philip (
2 CONC V) (d. 1277) and John (d. 1306). John's heir was his son Philip (VI) who died childless in 1342. Philip's widow Eleanor, who had held the estate jointly with him, died in 1343 when her heir was her nephew James Audley, Baron Audle
2 CONC y. James died in 1386 and was followed by his son Nicholas, who died childless in 1391. (fn. 10) On Nicholas' death a claim to the estate was made by Thomas Stawell as descendant of a sister of the last Philip de Columbers, (fn. 1
2 CONC 1) but ownership was retained by the Audleys in the person of Elizabeth, widow of Nicholas. On her death in 1400 the heirs were declared to be John Tuchet, grandson of Joan, one of Nicholas's sisters, and Margaret, wife of Roger H
2 CONC illary, the other sister. (fn. 12) John Tuchet died in 1408 and Margaret childless in 1411, both leaving John's young son James as their heir. (fn. 13) The two halves of the manor remained separate during James's minority (fn. 14)
2 CONC  but had been reunited by 1428. (fn. 15) James Tuchet, Baron Audley, died in 1459 and was succeeded by his son John (d. 1490) and by John's son James. (fn. 16) James was attainted and executed in 1497 for rebellion against the kin
2 CONC g and in 1510 the manor was granted for his life to Sir William Sandys (cr. Baron Sandys 1523, d. 1540). (fn. 17) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Puriton and Downend manors were restored to John Tuchet, James's heir, in 1512, (fn. 18) but in 1538 Sandys sold his life interest to Edward Elrington, who already had covenanted to assure the reversion to Tuchet. (fn. 19) 
2 CONT
2 CONT From: 'Puriton', A History of the County of Somerset: Volume 8: The Poldens and the Levels (2004), pp. 146-160. URL: http://www.british-history.ac.uk/report.aspx?compid=15114  Date accessed: 26 September 2010.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes: had livery of his lands in 1459-60, joined Edward IV, was summoned to parliament from 1461 to 1483, and was sworn of the privy council in 1471. He was employed in Britany in 1475, and was present at the coronation of Richar
2 CONC d III, who appointed him lord treasurer in 1484.
