2 CONC e Holland by Elizabeth Burghersh widow of Edward le Despenser.  This is not only corrected by a statement in "Notes and Queries", 4s, v.3, p.608, but it is observed that Elizabeth was 27 or more in 1369, so she would have been too
2 CONC  old to have been the mother of Eleanor, who could hardly have been born much earlier than the year 1400 and still be young enough to be mother to the childrem accredited to her.  The claim that Eleanor was the daughter of Thomas 
2 CONC de Holand by the listed Constance does not appear likely, since Constance was the wife of Thomas le Despenser from as early as 1379 until he was beheaded 13 Jan 1399/1400 one week after Thomas de Holand was beheaded.  The claim th
2 CONC at Thomas de Holand was associated with Constance as the widow of Thomas le Despenser is therefore impossible.  It is believed that Constance was in liason with Edmund de Holand, brother of Thomas, during the period from 17 Feb 14
2 CONC 04/1405 until possibly as late as 18 Jun 1406.  Inasmuch as Edmund was not married to his wife Lucy until 24 Jan 1406/1407, there is every indication that he could be the father of Eleanor, which is, of course, justified by Eleano
