2 CONT REDE, William I (c.1500-58), of Mitton, Worcs. and Tewkesbury, Glos.
2 CONT Family and Education
2 CONT b. .1500, 2nd s. of William Rede (d.10 Aug. 1508) of Boddington, Glos. by Margaret, da. of Richard Beauchamp, 2nd Baron Beauchamp of Powick. m. by 1530, Catherine, '''da. of Richard Rowdon of Gloucester, Glos. by Cecily Arnold''',
2 CONC  2s. 3da.1
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1509-1558/member/rede-william-i-1500-58
2 CONT _______
2 CONT '''Thomas Berkeley, de jure 5th Baron Berkeley''', (1472 – 22 January 1532) was an English soldier and aristocrat.
2 CONT He was born to Sir Maurice Berkeley, de jure 3rd Baron Berkeley, and Isabel Meade, in England. He was the younger brother to Maurice Berkeley, de jure 4th Baron Berkeley, and had a younger brother, James, and a younger sister, Ann
2 CONC e.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT '''His second wife was Cecily Arnold''', daughter and co-heiress to Sir Arnold of Gloucestershire, '''widow of Richard Rowdon, Sheriff of Gloucestershire.'''
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Berkeley,_5th_Baron_Berkeley
2 CONT __________
2 CONT Maurice Berkeley, de jure 3rd Baron Berkeley (1435 – September 1506), of Thornbury[1] in Gloucestershire, Maurice the Lawyer,[2] was an English nobleman.
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT He was born at Berkeley Castle in Gloucestershire, the younger son of James Berkeley, 1st Baron Berkeley (c. 1394–1463), James the Just,[3][4] by his third wife Lady Isabel, daughter of Thomas Mowbray, 1st Duke of Norfolk. He was 
2 CONC the younger brother and heir of William Berkeley, 1st Marquess of Berkeley, 2nd Baron Berkeley (1426–1492), William the Waste-All.[5][6]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT In 1465 Maurice married Isabel Mede (1444 - 29 May 1514), the daughter of Philip Mede (c.1415-1475) of Wraxall Place in the parish of Wraxall in Somerset, MP, an Alderman of Bristol and thrice Mayor of Bristol, in 1458-9, 1461-2 a
2 CONC nd 1468-9. Philip Mede was a merchant, and although a very wealthy man, was therefore considered to be below the social rank of gentry, which caused Maurice's brother to disinherit him for supposedly bringing the noble family of B
2 CONC erkeley into disrepute.[12] In fact Philip Mede had provided valuable financing and had provided soldiers (and his nephews William Mede and Thomas Meade went up there, around 1470, perhaps to provide assistance) to fight on the si
2 CONC de of William Berkeley, 1st Marquess of Berkeley, 2nd Baron Berkeley in the Battle of Nibley Green, a private battle over the inheritance of the Berkeley estates with his cousin Thomas Talbot, 2nd Viscount Lisle. By his wife he ha
2 CONC d four children:
2 CONT *Sir Maurice Berkeley, de jure 4th Baron Berkeley (1467 - 12 September 1523), eldest son and heir, who was made a Knight of the Bath at the coronation of King Henry VIII in 1509. In 1484 he married Catherine Berkeley, a daughter o
2 CONC f Sir William de Berkeley of Stoke Gifford in Gloucestershire.
2 CONT *'''Thomas Berkeley, de jure 5th Baron Berkeley''' (1472 - 22 January 1532), 2nd son, who was knighted on 9 September 1513 by Thomas Howard, Earl of Surrey at the Battle of Flodden. He married firstly in 1505 to Alienor Constable 
2 CONC (c. 1485 - d. 1540), widow of John Ingleby of Yorkshire and daughter of Marmaduke Constable by his wife Joyce Stafford. '''He married secondly to Cecily Arnold''', a daughter and co-heiress to Sir .... Arnold of Gloucestershire, a
2 CONC nd '''widow of Richard Rowdon, Sheriff of Gloucestershire.'''
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maurice_Berkeley,_3rd_Baron_Berkeley
2 CONT ________
