1 NAME Roger /ap Richard/ MP
2 GIVN Roger
2 SURN ap Richard
2 NSFX MP
2 _MARNM Mynors
1 NAME Sir Roger Mynors, MP
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Duffield
3 STAE Derbyshire
3 CTRY UK
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1485
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1537
1 OCCU
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} d.s.p.:
2 CONT Please see Peter Bartrum: Mynors; https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173426885924 (Steven Ferry, June 13, 2024.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and Education b. by 1478, 1st s. of Richard Mynors of Treago, and bro. of Reginald. m. 1500/1, Alice, da. of Sir William Mill of Harescombe, Glos., wid. of Nicholas Kniveton of Mercaston, Derbys., s.p. Kntd. 20 June 1527; s
2 CONC uc. fa. by 1528.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT J.p. Derbys. 1502-d., Herefs. 1528-32; gent. usher by 1509; serjeant of the cellar 1509-23 or later, of the buttery 1509-20; commr. subsidy, Derbys. 1512, 1514, 1523, 1524; other commissions, Derbys. and Herefs. 1517-35; sheriff, 
2 CONC Notts. and Derbys. 1513-14.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography There is no trace of a Mynors family in Derbyshire before the 16th century nor any mention of Roger Mynors in the pedigree of the Lichfield family of that name as it is recorded in the Staffordshire visitations. That the
2 CONC  Derbyshire knight of the shire in the Parliament of 1529 was of Herefordshire stock, a son of Richard Mynors of Treago, is borne out by Mynors’s references in his will to his property in that county and by his mention of his brot
2 CONC her Thomas, clearly Richard Mynors’s third son.3
2 CONT
2 CONT It was his marriage to Alice Mill which established Mynors in Derbyshire. She must have been much older than her second husband, for her son John Kniveton was already in his late ’teens. The couple were married without a licence—p
2 CONC resumably needed because part of the Kniveton property was held by knight service and the heir was under age—and for this dereliction they sued out a pardon on 29 Nov. 1501. In 1514 Mynors was settled at Windley Hill, three miles 
2 CONC south of Belper, on part of the Kniveton inheritance.4
2 CONT
2 CONT Although he was active in county administration, Mynors’s career lay elsewhere, in the royal household. It was one into which he was drawn both by inheritance and by marriage. His father had been an usher of the chamber to both Ed
2 CONC ward IV and Richard III, and was succeeded as usher to Henry VII by Nicholas Kniveton, whose widow’s marriage with Roger Mynors has the air of a professional alliance. The younger Mynors had become a gentleman usher by 1509 and it
2 CONC  was in this capacity that he attended Henry VII’s funeral, but the new reign saw his promotion first to be serjeant of the cellar and then to the same status in the buttery. He accompanied Henry VIII on his French campaign in 151
2 CONC 3, and in 1520 he was at the Field of Cloth of Gold and at the meeting with Charles V at Gravelines. His post in the buttery he kept until November 1520 and that in the cellar until at least 1523, although as he was assessed for s
2 CONC ubsidy (at £100) as a member of the Household in both 1524 and 1527 he may have retained the second one somewhat longer until succeeded by his younger brother Thomas, who was in the buttery in 1532 and ‘of the buttery and cellar’ 
2 CONC at the time of Anne Boleyn’s coronation. Roger Mynors received the customary reward for his services. Already in November 1509 he had received a 21-year lease, renewable throughout his life, of Wirksworth manor in Derbyshire and a
2 CONC bout 1525 this and neighbouring property was granted to him. In 1523 he was made steward of a castle and lordship in Herefordshire and given the custody of Postern park in Duffield, Derbyshire, during the minority of Henry Bradsha
2 CONC w. His knighting in 1527 may have accompanied his succession to his Herefordshire patrimony, and in the following year he was added to the commission of the peace in his native county.5
2 CONT
2 CONT It was thus as a retired officer of the Household and landed gentleman that Mynors entered the House of Commons in 1529. The list of Members of that Parliament is torn at the point where the names of the Derbyshire knights occur o
2 CONC n it, but enough letters are left to identify them as Mynors and William Coffin. If Mynors’s position in the county made him eligible for election, it was the King’s decision to have the writs for Derbyshire and Nottinghamshire de
2 CONC livered by the 3rd Duke of Norfolk which must explain why he was chosen: the writs were doubtless accompanied by nominations when they reached the sheriff, Nicholas Strelley, himself a long-standing member of the Household. Like C
2 CONC offin, whose career shows a striking resemblance to his own, Mynors thus ranks as a royal nominee. As a surviving Member of this Parliament in 1536 he is also likely to have sat in its successor of that year, when the King asked f
2 CONC or the re-election of the previous Members. Of his role in the proceedings of the Commons nothing is known.6
2 CONT
2 CONT Mynors was within a few months of his death when in October 1536 he was one of the neighbouring gentlemen who advised the 4th Earl of Shrewsbury in his campaign against the Lincolnshire rebels. His name appears on the Derbyshire c
2 CONC ommission of the peace for the last time on the following 4 Feb., and his will (which he had drawn up on 16 Dec. 1534 and added to on 12 Mar. 1536) was proved on 9 May. He named among his trustees Sir Robert Norwich, chief justice
2 CONC  of the common pleas, and Walter Devereux, 3rd Lord Ferrers. In accordance with his direction that he should be buried in one of the parish churches ‘where my households be, either in Derbyshire or Herefordshire’, he was buried at
2 CONC  Duffield, four miles north of Derby, where a monument to him and his wife still survives. Mynors left no issue and his brother Thomas succeeded to his estate.7
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1509-1558 Author: C. J. Black Notes 1. Date of birth estimated from age at fa.’s i.p.m., C142/80/166. Vis. Herefs. ed. Weaver, 89; Vis. Glos. (Harl. Soc. xxi), 216; PCC 5 Dyngeley. 2. CPR, 1494-1509, p. 635; LP Hen. V
2 CONC III, i-v, viii; Statutes, iii. 79, 114; Rot. Parl. pp. xxxii, lxvi; Derbys. Arch. Soc. rec. ser. iii. 43-44, 47-48. 3. Wm. Salt Arch. Soc. v(2), 213; PCC 5 Dyngeley. 4. CIPM Hen. VII, ii. 542; Somerville, Duchy, i. 530; Harl. 5809
2 CONC , f.6v; CPR, 1494-1509, pp.254, 303; LP Hen. VIII, ii. 5. CPR, 1467-77, p.451; 1476-85, p.516; LP Hen. VIII, i, iii, iv, vi; Rutland Pprs. (Cam. Soc. xxi), 57; Derbys. Chs. ed. Jeayes, 238; C1/1034/77; HMC Bath, iv. 6. 6. SP1/56, 
2 CONC p.2; LP Hen. VIII, iv, xi. 7. LP Hen. VIII, xii; PCC 5 Dyngeley; J. C. Cox, Derbys. Churches, iii. 136-7, C1/1034/78-79; 142/64/156.
