1 NOTE {geni:about_me} JOHN GODARD de Upham, then spelled Uppam, died Mar. 10, 1545; son 
2 CONT
2 CONT of Walter Godard , married Elizabeth, dau. of William Berenger, of Manning- 
2 CONT
2 CONT ford Bruce. He "Became a large landed proprietor towards the end of the 
2 CONT
2 CONT fifteenth century; and was in fact the founder of the family. He possessed 
2 CONT
2 CONT property at Standen-Hussey, Cliffe Pypard, Swindon, Berwick Bassett, Clatford, 
2 CONT
2 CONT etc., and established his sons in those places; from whom branched out the 
2 CONT
2 CONT Goddards of Engleshaw, South Marston, Castle Eaton, Rudloe, Hartham, Purton. 
2 CONT
2 CONT etc. . . . His second son, Thomas, was the ancestor of the Goddards of Swindon." 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Goddard had a grant of Land from Henry VIII., in 1541, in Wanborough 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Wanburg) and in Upham. After the dissolution of monasteries John Goddard 
2 CONT
2 CONT of Upham purchased some land at Westlecote, Swindon, which had belonged to 
2 CONT
2 CONT the nuns of Locock Abbey; having been given to them by Catherine Lovel. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This was the nucleus of the present large estate held by Goddard of Swindon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1539 the master of Wiltz in the chapter-house of Westminster, contains 
2 CONT
2 CONT entries of Wlllyam Godeard (under-tithing of Cherell), Aldeborne; and John 
2 CONT
2 CONT Goddard, gentylman, three harness (?). He appears to have been succeeded 
2 CONT
2 CONT in the Swindon and Upham estates, or part of them, by Thomas Goddard. who 
2 CONT
2 CONT was commemorated by the monument in Aldbourne Church.
