1 OCCU Member of Parliament; Fought for Henry VII at  battle of Stoke 1487. MP for the county of Bedford. Speaker of the House of Commons. Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster, 1504.
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Mordaunt_(speaker)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Mordaunt (died c.1505) was an English politician of the Tudor period and Speaker of the House of Commons. Offices held included Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of William Mordaunt of Turvey, Bedfordshire and entered the Middle Temple to train as a lawyer. He was also summoned by Richard III to serve against the Scots in 1484, and fought for Henry VII at Stoke in 1487.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1485 and 1487 he served as an MP for unidentified constituencies, (probably Bedfordshire), on the latter occasion being chosen to serve as speaker of the house. He was elected MP for Grantham in 1491  and knight of the shire fo
2 CONC r Bedfordshire in 1495. In the 1490s he became more active as a government administrator and lawyer and was knighted for his services in 1503. He was appointed High Steward of Cambridge University in 1504 and later the same year n
2 CONC ominated to follow Sir Reginald Bray as Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately he died soon afterwards and was buried in Turvey church. He had married Edith, coheir of Sir Nicholas Latymer, with whom he had at least two sons and a daughter. His eldest son John would become first Lord Mordaunt.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Possibly wounded at battle of Barnet?  Fought for Henry VII at  battle of Stoke, 1487. Knighted by Henry VII.  MP  for the county of Bedford; Speaker of the Commons. Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster, 1504.  High Steward of Cam
2 CONC bridge University.  Male line became Earls of Peterborough and Monmouth.
2 CONT
2 CONT His Will mentions Lady Agnes Pecke, late wife of John Pecke of Cople, daughter of John Vynter of Crick (Creke), Northamptonshire. The testators were his brother William Mordaunt, John Vynter and William Gascoigne. John Mordaunt, w
2 CONC ounded at the battle of Barnet, was a commander at the battle of Stoke (1487); Speaker of the House of Commons; Sergeant-at-law (10 Sep 1495); King's Sergeant (25 Nov 1495); attorney to Prince Arthur; Chief Justice of Chester (c. 
2 CONC 1499); Knighted (1502-3); High Steward of Cambridge University (1504); Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster (24 Jun 1504); Lawyer at Middle Temple.  He married Edith. His son and heir John, aged 21 at his father's death, was creat
2 CONC ed Lord Mordaunt of Turvey and married Elizabeth Vere, sister of Etheldreda Vere mentioned in the Will.  [Source: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/MORDAUNT.htm]
2 CONT
2 CONT See also: 'Oxford Dictionary of National Biography' and  http://www.turveybeds.com/mordaunts.html
