3 CONT
3 CONT
3 CONT Knight of the Shire representing Somerset in the Parliament of 1624. John Symes registered his pedigree in the 1623 Visitation of the County of Somerset. Matriculated February 23, 1587/8 at Exeter College, Oxford, received his B.A
3 CONC . in 1591, and was a student at Lincoln's Inn 1589. He served as Justice of the Peace in Somerset 1608-1645, High Sheriff of Somerset 1626-1627, member of the Court of Oyer and Terminer for the Western Circuit 1629-1643, Deputy Li
3 CONC eutenant of Somerset 1637-1642, commissioner of Assessment (1641-1642) and Array (1642), and several other public offices. His chief residence was Poundisford Lodge at Pitminster, Somerset, and he was also lord of the manors of Ba
3 CONC rwick and West Coker along with their advowsons. John's opponents from a more genteel background described his father, a successful merchant, as "a peddler, and a base fellow." Indeed it was John's marriage which allied him with a
3 CONC  lineage based family. The contract of betrothal was made on December 29, 1595, between his father William Symes, who was to settle £2000 on the couple, and the bride's father Thomas Horner of Cloford and grandfather Sir John Poph
3 CONC am of Wellington. Along with his brother-in-law Sir John Horner, Symes oversaw part of the polls during elections in Somerset. In 1624 he was elected to Parliament and served on a few committees. Described as "a rich and decrepit 
3 CONC man", he supported the King during the Civil War, but was charged by Sir Richard Grenville "with some inclination to the rebels" and fined £1000. He never paid the fine, but compounded with Parliament for his delinquency with a fi
3 CONC ne of £945, and then was charged with concealing mortgages to the value of £5000. He sought "a repose for his old age" in Gloucestershire where he died in his 89th year. He was the only member of his immediate family to sit in Par
3 CONC liament. By his wife Amy Horner, he was the father of four sons, John, Henry, William, and Thomas, and ten daughters, including Jane (wife of Sir John Seymour of Bitton, Gloucestershire), Susanna (wife of Rev. George Newton of Tau
3 CONC nton, Somerset), Elizabeth (wife of Sir Nicholas Martyn of Exeter, Devon, married at Pitminster, July 4, 1622), Katharine (wife of William Drewe of The Grange, Honiton, married at Pitminster, August 9, 1627), Amy, Eleanor, Dorothy
3 CONC , Edith Symes (wife of Col. John Gorges of Somerset House, Colraine, Northern Ireland), and Dulcibella (wife of Thomas Hodges of Shipton Moyne, married at Pitminster, December 26, 1625).
