1 NAME Thomas /Thynne/ MP
2 GIVN Thomas
2 SURN Thynne
2 NSFX MP
2 _MARNM Thynne
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1578
2 PLAC Longleat, Wiltshire, England
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 AUG 1639
2 ADDR
3 CITY Richmond
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Family and Education b. c.1578, 1st s. of John Thynne by Joan, da. of Sir Rowland Hayward of London. educ. Brasenose, Oxf. m. (1) c.1601, Maria, da. of George, Lord Audley; (2) c.1612, Catherine, da. of Charles How
2 CONC ard, 6s. 1da. suc. fa. 1604. Kntd. 1604.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheriff, Wilts. 1607-8, Glos. 1621-2, Som. 1629-30, Salop 1633-4.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Thynne, the heir to Longleat, allied himself in marriage with a nobleman’s daughter who was also grand-daughter to (Sir) James Marvyn, perhaps the leading man in Wiltshire after the Earl of Pembroke. The borough of Hindo
2 CONC n lay within Marvyn’s territory and it is thus highly probable that the Thomas Thynne esquire who was returned for it in 1601 was Marvyn’s young kinsman, rather than Thomas Thynne I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thynne died 1 Aug. 1639.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1558-1603 Author: S. T. Bindoff Notes 1. Wilts. Vis. Peds. (cv, cvi), 193; Ch. Inq. P. M. Misc. 535, 18 Chas. I, pt. 30, no. 47; Wilts. Arch. Mag. xxiii. 283
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Thomas Thynne''' (ca. 1578–1639), of Longleat, Wiltshire, was an English landowner and member of parliament.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thynne was the son and heir of Sir John Thynne of Longleat, a knight of the shire, by his marriage to Joan Hayward, daughter of Sir Rowland Hayward, a Lord Mayor of London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thynne first made his mark in May 1594, at the age of sixteen, when he clandestinely married Maria (or Mary) Touchet, also sixteen, a gentlewoman at the court of Queen Elizabeth and a daughter of Lord Audley. The two were married 
2 CONC on the day they first met and for some time kept their marriage secret because their fathers were bitterly opposed to each other. When their story became known, Thynne's father, John Thynne the Younger, tried unsuccessfully to hav
2 CONC e the marriage annulled. The story is said to have contributed to the inspiration for Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet. With Maria, Thynne had three sons, before his wife died in childbirth. Two of these sons survived childhood, Jam
2 CONC es Thynne (died 1670) and Sir Thomas Thynne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria's surviving correspondence between 1595 and 1611 was published by the Wiltshire Record Society in 1983 under the title Two Elizabethan Women: correspondence of Joan and Maria Thynne.[6] After the death of Maria in 1611, Thyn
2 CONC ne married secondly Catherine Howard, a daughter of Hon. Charles Howard, son of the first Viscount Howard and niece of Lord Howard of Bindon. With her he had further sons, including Sir Henry Frederick Thynne, 1st Baronet (1615–16
2 CONC 80), ancestor of the Marquesses of Bath.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thynne succeeded his father to the family estates in 1604. He was already one of the members of parliament for Hindon, and on his father's death he had himself returned at a by-election for the county of Wiltshire. However, this e
2 CONC lection was disallowed by the Commons on the grounds that a sitting member was not eligible to be returned for a second constituency. Thus, Sir Walter Vaughan gained the vacant seat. Although Thynne sat in five more Parliaments of
2 CONC  England, he never became one of the knights of the shire. He was elected for Heytesbury in 1621 and 1624.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thynne was High Sheriff of Wiltshire for 1607–08.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the estates Thynne inherited was one at Kempsford in Gloucestershire, where he built a new country house, demolishing an important fortified manor house which since the 13th century had defended a crossing of the River Thame
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thynne died on 1 August 1639. Among his many descendants are Diana, Princess of Wales, and her sons William and Harry.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Thynne_%28died_1639%29
