1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonard_Pilkington Wikipedia: Leonard Pilkington]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonard Pilkington (1527–1599) was an English academic and clergyman. A Marian exile, he became Regius Professor of Divinity at Cambridge and Master of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_John%27s_College,_Cambridge St John's Colleg
2 CONC e, St John's Street, Cambridge CB2 1TP 52.20806, 0.11667] at the start of the reign of Elizabeth I. In his subsequent church career, he followed the way opened when his brother James Pilkington became Bishop of Durham.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Life''
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born at Rivington, Lancashire, and graduated B.A. at St John's College in 1544. He became a Fellow there in 1546, and graduated M.A. in 1547. When Mary I of England came to the throne, he was deprived of his college positio
2 CONC ns, and left the country. While in exile he married.
2 CONT
2 CONT He returned to England and was restored to his fellowship at St John's, his wife having died. He became Master there in 1561, replacing his brother James and initiating a period of divisive governance and an effort to reform the c
2 CONC ollege by replacing Catholic associations with the Protestantism of Geneva. He brought in William Fulke as Fellow, and ensured the succession of Richard Longworth, and then resigned as Master. He became a canon of [https://en.wiki
2 CONC pedia.org/wiki/Durham_Cathedral Durham Cathedral Durham, County Durham DH1 3EH 54.77361, -1.57611] in 1567.
