2 CONT Elizabeth Constance HEWSTER GORING was born about 1500 in Burton Sussex, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the floor of the south chapel is a 16th century brass plate to Sir John Kingsmill (ob. Aug 11, 1559), who married Constance Goring, with a Latin inscription giving an account of the large family born to a Kingsmill of Sydmonton
2 CONC , and on a shield are the Kingsmill arms impaling quarterly (1) a chevron between three rings, (2) on a chief indented three molets, (3) on a chief three roundels, (4) on a bend cotised four lions passant, (5) barry of six with a 
2 CONC leopards head on a quarter. There are four other brasses in this chapel, one to John Bossewell, gentleman and 'notarye pulique', who died in 1580. Above the inscription is a shield bearing the arms of Bossewell, which are Argent a
2 CONC  fesse indented gules with three molets sable in the chief. The next is a small brass figure of a priest in Mass vestments and having a shaven head, and the insciption shows him to be 'Willm Estwood late Vycar of this churche and 
2 CONC Psonne of Newnom' who died in 1519.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cousin of Thomas Bembridge who was burned for heresy in Hampshire on 29 July 1558.
2 CONT
2 CONT (This quote from Protestantism and the national church in sixteenth century England By Peter Lake, Maria Dowling (google books) Pages 154/155)
2 CONT
2 CONT 'However, the Kingsmill-Gifford group clearly continued to be the focus of a vigorous and committed protestant community among the Hampshire gentry. Even before Elizabeth's reign, they attempted to resist and subvert the burning o
2 CONC f a relative, Thomas Bembridge, as a heretic.
2 CONT
2 CONT This episode took place on 29 July 1558 and was the only Marian burning to take place in Hampshire and it could have been called a success for the Queen and her followers. Bembridge was a bachelor and so without any direct heirs t
2 CONC o his property.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to his execution, therefore, he executed an indenture on 26 April 1558 which left his land to Richard and Anne Gifford, Anne being his 'Kinswoman'.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the default of their heirs, his property was to go to 'CONSTANCE KINGSMILL' of Sidmontan, Bembridge's 'cousin' and the widow of John Kingsmill the protestant J.P.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus the only heretic burned in Hampshire came from the respectable gentry and was just a radical artisan sectary.'
2 CONT
2 CONT The events leading up to Bembridge's arrest by John White, the Bishop of Winchester, are not known. During his imprisonment and trial he made no attempt to conceal or deny his heretical opinions.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his examination before Bishop White, he denied the papal supremacy, admitted only the three major sacraments and denied the existence of both purgatory and transubstantiation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bembridge also managed to bring out that he thought 'Martin Luther died a good Christian man, whose doctrine and life he did approve and allow', even though his examiners had not even Elizabethan religious settlement.)'
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father was supposed to be John Goring/Gering, but as her mother Constance married HEWSTER first it not believed that her father was HEWSTER
2 CONT
2 CONT She was co heir? Her Maritagium was half of Litchfield Manor, nr. Basingstoke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her nephew Nicolas Dering (son of her sister Eleanor) sold his half of the Litchfield Manor to Contance's husband Sir John KINGSMILL for ?6 12s 8d in 1577??
2 CONT
2 CONT Level Item Finding No. 19M61/2933 Title Ink drawing by Alfred Scott Gatty, York Herald of the arms of Sir John Kingsmill, married Constance Goring
2 CONT
2 CONT The now missing Sidmanton register, stated her at death
2 CONT
2 CONT 'The right worshippfull the Lady Constance Kingsmill buried 16th May, 1581', but she was buried in Kingsclere, Hampshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Constance Kyngesmill or Kingesmill, Widow 09 June 1581 PROB 11/63
2 CONT
2 CONT St Mary's church Kingsclere On the floor of the south chapel is a 16th century brass plate to Sir John Kingsmill (ob. Aug 11, 1559), who married Constance Goring, with a Latin inscription giving an account of the large family born
2 CONC  to a Kingsmill ofSydmonton, and on a shield are the Kingsmill arms impaling quarterly (1) achevron between three rings, (2) on a chief indented three molets, (3) on achief three roundels, (4) on a bend cotised four lions passant,
2 CONC  (5) barry ofsix with a leopards head on a quarter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Constance Kyngesmill or Kingesmill, Widow 09 June 1581 PROB 11/63 THE FIRST OF MARCH IN THE YEARE of oure Lorde God a thousande fyve hundrede three score and ninetine. In the name of God amen. First I bequeathe my sowle to
2 CONC  allmightie God my redeemerdesiringe his mercie and forgivenes for all my sinnefull and wretched lief trustinge assuredlie of his mercie through Jhesus Christe by whose passion and bloud shedinge I truste to be saved and by no oth
2 CONC er meanes and this is my verye belief and therefore I saie amen. And for the disposinge of my gooddes and chattels I Constance Kyngesmill widowe beinge in perfecte mynde and memorie doe ordayne and make my last will and testament 
2 CONC in manner and forme followinge that is to saie. I give to the Chappell of Sidmanton fortie shillinges to repaire it. Item I geve and bequeathe to Coxes widowe to poore Johan that dwellethe at Coxes to Martyns widdowe at the gate t
2 CONC o Elsabethe Grege and William Grege tenne shillinges and to Joan Kinge to Bryans wieffe to Estese wiefe and to Regelle widow sometyme my seranntes to either of theme tenne shillinges. Also I geve tenne poundes to buye canvess of t
2 CONC enne pence the elle to bestowe uppon the poore as mine executors thinke the beste. Also I geve to Richarde Wallys which was my servannte twentie shillinges which he oweth me. Also I geve to my servanntes Richarde Martyne, to Peter
2 CONC , to John Martyne, to Estese, to Mason, to either pf theme three poundes. Item I give to James my servannte twentie shillinges. Item I geve to Josye my brewar and to Thomas my servannt to everie of them fortie shillinges. Item I g
2 CONC eve to Marye Pyckman, to Joan Parsons my servanntes to either of theme three poundes. Item my will is that myne execut ors shall release and dischardge Sir William Kingesmyll knight myne eldest sonne of all suche monneye as he and
2 CONC  his wieffe at tymes hathe borowed of me the whiche as appearethe by notes made withe myne owne hande amountethe to three score and and eleven poundes eightene shillinges eighte pence or right theraboutes. Also I geve and bequeath
2 CONC e to the saide Sir William Kingismill all the furniture that is in the best chamber on the chamber wheare Roger Lingesmill my sonne usethe to lye saving the quisshian which my daughter Thomas Kingesmill his wieffe muste have. Also
2 CONC  I bequeathe to the saide Sir William Kyngesmill my sonne the hanginges in the greate parlor, the furnace in the brewinge howse withe all they implmentes belonging to the same withe thee chayne of the well, the furnace in the wass
2 CONC hing howse, the bordes in the hall, the bordes in the butterye, the boardes in the kytchin, the boardes in the larder, the boardes in the bakehowse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I bequeathe to Constance Kyngesmill my sonne Richarde Kingesmill his daughter five of the beste sylver saltes guilte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I geve and bequeathe to Roger Kyngesmill my sonne fortie poundes to be paide twentie markes by the yeare untill thee saide forty poundes be paide.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I geve and bequeathe to Thomas Kingesmill my sonne three score poundes to be paide twentie poundes by the yeare untill the saide three score poundes be paide.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I geve and bequeathe to Roger Kingesmill all that he hathe in his chamber and the bedde in the nexte chamber wheare Peter lyethe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I geve and bequeathe to John Kyngesmyll my sonne Thomas Kingesmyll his sonne and Samuell Kyngesmill my sonne Thomas Kyngesmyll his sonne to either of theme twentie poundes to be paide unto theme when theie shall come to the a
2 CONC ge of one and twentie yeares.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I geve to Andrewe Kingsmill my sonne Thomas Kyngesmill his sonne tenne poundes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I geve to Marye Kyngesmill my sonne Thomas Kyngesmill his daughter tenne poundes to be payd to everie of theme when theie shall come to the age of one and twentie yeares.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also I geve to my daughter Cupper, my daughter Pilkenton, my daughter Norton, my daughter Thornbroghe, my daughter Goddard to either of theme and englishe bible.
2 CONT
2 CONT The reste of my gooddes I bequeathe and geve to John Kynsmill my sonne and George Kyngesmyll my sonne (my legacies dischardged my debtes paide).
2 CONT
2 CONT John Kyngesmyll my sonne and George Kyngesmyll my sonne I doe ordayne and make my executors of this my laste will and testamente.
2 CONT
2 CONT And I desyre William Kingesmyll and Richarde Kyngesmill my sonnes to be myne overseers the daie and yeare above written. Constance Kyngesmill. Witnesses
2 CONT
2 CONT hereof theise whose names are hereafter written, decimo die alplio? Anno domini 1580. Peter Pheerhard, Robert Mason, Anthony Daulbye, Nathaniel Mawle.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/290786379/constance-kingsmill
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Dame Constance Goring Kingsmill was the wife of Sir John Kingsmill (c.1497–1556) of Sydmonton Court, Hampshire, and a member of the Goring family of Burton, Sussex. She lived during the Tudor period and was the mother of a large a
2 CONC nd prominent family.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband, Sir John Kingsmill, was a lawyer and royal official: admitted to Lincoln's Inn in 1516, called to the bar in 1521, knighted by King Henry VIII in 1530, appointed Justice of the Peace, and served as Sheriff of Hampshir
2 CONC e. He acquired Sydmonton manor in 1540 following the Dissolution of the Monasteries and resided there until his death in 1556. He was buried in Kingsclere Church, Hampshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance survived her husband and made her will as a widow. Her last will and testament was proved in the Prerogative Court of Canterbury (PROB 11/63/325) in 1581/1582. In her will, she entrusted her burial to the discretion of h
2 CONC er executors and made bequests to her children and grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the mother of seventeen children, many of whom are commemorated with their parents in a brass memorial formerly located in the Kingsmill Chapel inside St Mary's Church, Kingsclere. Though the chapel was later repurposed, t
2 CONC he brass is a significant contemporary record of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children named in her will and family records include:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Kingsmill
2 CONT Roger Kingsmill (and wife Anne)
2 CONT Thomas Kingsmill
2 CONT John Kingsmill
2 CONT George Kingsmill
2 CONT Richard Kingsmill (father of granddaughter Constance Kingsmill)
2 CONT Mary Kingsmill, wife of Edward Goddard (named in the will as "my daughter Goddard")
2 CONT Daughters known by married names: Cooper, Pilkington, Norton, and Thornborough
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Dame Constance Kingsmill did not name a burial place in her will, directing instead that her body be buried as her executors deemed fit. Given that:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Kingsmill family seat was Sydmonton Court
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband was buried at Kingsclere Church
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The family brass commemorating Sir John, Constance, and their seventeen children is located in St Mary's Church, Kingsclere
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT It is highly probable that Dame Constance Kingsmill was buried in Kingsclere, Hampshire, likely at St Mary's Church or its churchyard.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT And I cite an extract from JOHN KINGSMILL (1626-1694), ESQ, OF ANDOVER, HANTS: HIS ANCESTRY AND MEDIEVAL ROYAL DESCENT
2 CONT
2 CONT by Michael P Bodman :
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Kingsmill (junior) (c.1497-1556), of Sydmonton, Hampshire, son and heir; admitted to Lincoln's Inn in October 1516; called to the bar on 9 May 1521; knighted by Henry VIII in 1530; appointed JP in 1337; served as sheriff 
2 CONC of Hampshire in 1538-9 and 1543-4; and died 11 August 1556.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Constance (d.1580/1), daughter of John Goring of Burton,Sussex.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Rupert Willoughby says "Sir John Kingsmill, being one of the commissioners for the dissolution of such monasteries, acquired much of the spoil locally, including, in 1540, the manor of Sydmonton, a former possession of the Abbey o
2 CONC f Romsey. Sir John resided at
2 CONT
2 CONT Sydmonton and was buried in 1556 in nearby Kingsclere Church. He is commemorated, with
2 CONT
2 CONT his seventeen children, in a brass in the Kingsmill Chapel, which was regrettably converted in 1978 into a kitchen and meeting room. Coincidentally, the rectory of Kingsclere had (from 1261. been one of the many livings held by hi
2 CONC s ancestor Walter de Merton".
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Constance Kingsmill, widow, Prerogative Court of Canterbury, PROB 11/63/325 (1581/2)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael P. Bodman, John Kingsmill (1626–1694), Esq., of Andover, Hants: His Ancestry and Medieval Royal Descent
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Kingsclere parish and Kingsmill family memorial traditions
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Nota bene: The exact burial place of Dame Constance Kingsmill is not recorded. Her will directs that her body be buried at the discretion of her executors. Given the Kingsmill family's association with Sydmonton Court and the pres
2 CONC ence of the family brass at St Mary's Church, Kingsclere, she was most likely buried in the Kingsclere parish churchyard. The cemetery listing reflects probable parish burial, not a documented grave location. Current search to con
2 CONC firm exact location of burial is ongoing.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Inscription
2 CONT Inscription on the brass plate of St Mary's Church in Kingsclere :
2 CONT
2 CONT On the floor beside the tomb was a large brass plate bearing a rhyming Latin inscription to the memory of Sir John Kingsmill, his wife Constance, and their 17 children.
