1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Ryves-16
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Tale of 3 Robert Ryves Reeves The Name The name “Reeves” is the Anglicization of the old word “Rives,” pronounced the same way our name is today, and spelled Ryves in older English documents. Many descendants of the English “Reeve
2 CONC s” name took their surname from the profession of Reeve or Shire Reeve, the origin of the word “Sheriff,” which was a law-enforcing judiciary in Pre-Elizabethan times. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Many have asserted that such is the case with the Rives family of Dorset. However, evidence strongly suggests that the Rives of Dorset were of Norman descent, and that the name is actually of French derivation, the family’s most l
2 CONC ikely point of origin. Given the derivation from Shire Reeve, it seems more than likely that our Reeves line, that of Dorset, is the oldest one that survives to this day. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rives of Dorset were, throughout history, recorded as Rives, Ryves, and even Reve, Rive, or Ryve. Ancient Brittania freely interchanged Y and I as common vowel sounds, and ir can be assumed that “Reve” is the original pronunci
2 CONC ation of the name given the earliest recorded interchanges. This supports the idea that Ryves or Rives is of French origin. The letters I and Y in the French language are pronounced “EE” as in the current spelling of our Reeves li
2 CONC ne. Also, the final S was probably originally silent, also a French inflection. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At some point, the name was Anglicized in order to retain the S in pronunciation and prevent its loss. Rivis is Latin for a stream, channel, or dyke. The modern day French term “rives” (pronounced “reeve”) literally means river ba
2 CONC nk. There are two villages in the south of France that bear the name Rives, one in the department of Lot-et-Garonne, and the other in Herault. One of these villages was a chief manufacturer of swords used in the crusades, and is n
2 CONC ow best known for a high grade of paper that bears the Rives name.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are many records of families by the name of Rives in France prior to and at the same time of our Ryves in Dorset. These records include “Rives et Reaumont: Au Moyen Age” by Phillipe Chanaron, in which a time line (1020-1308)
2 CONC  is given for a feudalistic house that controlled a region in the south of France. The lords of this region were known as “de Rives.” (“Of Rives.”) A Rives family was recorded as part of an inquisition held by the Catholic Church 
2 CONC between 1318 and 1325 in the small village of Montaillou, located long the Pyrenees. In addition, one of the Rives families in Quebec has been traced to a family of shipmasters from Normandy in the 16th century. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Norman Conquest resulted in the replacement of the old Anglo-Saxon and English land owning class with a French aristocracy. The Domesday Book reveals that by 1086, 95% of English landowners had been ousted by the Norman Lords.
2 CONC  William the Conqueror and his successors ensured that all of the nobility of England were of Norman descent. He divided the lands amongst the nobles, the knights that fought at Hastings, and their French kinsman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1066 until the English Civil War in 1642, no one occupied a large amount of land unless his family was of Norman Nobility. At the time of his death, Robert Rives was a member of the noble class and was a peer of King Henry VI
2 CONC II’s court. Robert was an armiger – possessing a coat of arms granted by the throne – and held fabulous estates in both Dorset and Somerset, both of which are in the southernmost part of England, just across the channel from Franc
2 CONC e. His nobility, his name, and this history indicate clearly that the Robert Ryves of Henry VIII’s Court is of Norman descent. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is therefore most likely that the line of Robert Ryves continues back to include a French Rives line spanning approximately 1020-1490. It is not known when the family migrated from the southern river regions of France to the no
2 CONC rth shores and across the Channel.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Coined Greyhound 
2 CONT
2 CONT A contributing argument to Rivas, France being the source of our Rives line is the inclusion of “bezantee” on our coat-of-arms crest. The greyhound, our crest, is listed as being “bezantee,” or “scattered with gold coins.” This he
2 CONC raldic feature of scattered with gold coins was introduced in ancient times to denote service in the Crusades, since many of the knights gathered Bezants, or gold coins native to Byzantium. Given the importance of the town of Rive
2 CONC s, France during the crusades, it would make sense that a noble family from that region would have served in the Crusades and therefore added “bezantee” to their crest. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reeves Heraldry 
2 CONT
2 CONT Our family coat of arms description reads, in armiger’s nomenclature: “Argent, on a bend cotised sable three lozenges ermine, crest: a greyhound segant sable, bezantee, collared or.” It has been recorded that “argent on a chief gu
2 CONC les a lion rampant regardant” was included sometime after 1500, indicating a red banner with a silver lion was to be placed atop the shield, indicating the noble family as close to the throne, either as a courtier, a personal frie
2 CONC nd, or in Robert Ryves’ case, both.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ryves coat of arms can be seen in numerous locales throughout Dorset County even today. George Ryves [1627-1689] had them placed above the doorway on the Almshouse at Blandford, County Dorset, where they still stand. Robert Ri
2 CONC ves [1623-1658] is honored with a memorial at the Church of Fifehead Neville in Dorset, and the bronze plaque bears a detailed sculpture of the Ryves achievement.
2 CONT
2 CONT The shield has been recorded amongst those of the noble families of England since the 15th century. The shield is marshaled, meaning that the right side (left if you are viewing it; remember shields are borne from the back) bears 
2 CONC the Ryves arms and the left (right from the viewer’s standpoint) bears the Buckler arms, those of his mother’s side. Family motto “Bene Vivas, et Disce et Evolve.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Ryves I (abt 1440 – 1510) is the progenitor of the modern-day Reeves family, our most ancient ancestor by name and direct lineage. There is some mention of his father, Robert of France, but it is not conclusive. His family 
2 CONC came to Dorset from the Languedoc region of France, but the exact date of his birth, and the date the family first settled in England is the subject of great contention and debate amongst even the most well-studied of Ryves schola
2 CONC rs.
2 CONT
2 CONT One school of research suggests that the Ryves were Huguenot Knights from a Noble Norman family who had fought with the English during the 100 years war. In 1450 the French mounted a vigorous campaign that resulted in the conquest
2 CONC  of Normandy and, in 1451, most of Guyenne. By 1453, the English had lost all of their holdings in France with the exception of Calais.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, Henry made peace with France in 1453 and the Hundred Years’ War came to an official end. This event, coupled with the persecution of the Huguenots in France, caused our Robert Rives of France (Ryves in England) with his S
2 CONC on Robert Ryves I, to relocate permanently to England. Because of their service to the English Crown, and intermarriage with the English nobility, the Ryves were granted some lands, and purchased others.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Grandson of Robert of France and son of Robert Ryves I (abt 1440 – 1510), is Robert Ryves II (1490-1551) and probably represents the second generation in England. This Robert Ryves (Reves) was part of Henry VIII's court and wa
2 CONC s a noble with a coat of arms and possibly participated in the crusades.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1545, Robert Ryves II (1490-1551) purchased the 1600 acre estate of Randleston (Ranston). In 1548, he obtained the beautiful Damory Court in Blandsford Forum from the Duke of Somerset. In 1546, his friend King Henry VIII grante
2 CONC d, in letters patent, the manor of Milton and the "messuage" of Luscombe (Lyssecomb) in Dorset to Robert. He also held diverse lands in the East Orchard district of Dorset County. In addition to his Dorsetshire estates, Robert hel
2 CONC d lands in North Cheriton and Pointington, County Somerset."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: "History of the Hundred Years War"; All Ancestry Sources attached to these 3 early Robert Ryves in the Faulk Folks Tree; and UK National Archives on the web. Compiled by "Nanamidge" Marjorie Sorrels
