1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Henry Hastings (c. 1561[1] – 15 October 1650), was an English landowner and an eccentric country sportsman. He supported the Royalist cause in the English Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Hastings was the typical country squire of the time, "well-natured, but soon angry." He always dressed in green, and keeping all sorts of hounds and hawks, devoted himself to hunting. His hall was hung with sporting trophies, wh
2 CONC ile favourite dogs and cats occupied every corner. His table was provided from his farms and fishponds, and hospitable. The pulpit of a disused neighbouring chapel formed his larder."
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. Henry Hastings was baptised on 20 June 1552 at Lubbesthorpe Manor, Leicestershire, England.3 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of George Hastings, 4th Earl of Huntingdon and Dorothy Port.1,4
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Dorothy Willoughby, daughter of Sir Francis Willoughby and Elizabeth Lyttelton, in 1587 at Wollaton, Nottinghamshire, England.3 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Anna Langton on 3 September 1639 at Pimperne, Dorset, England.3 He died in 1650.2
2 CONT
2 CONT He sold Christchurch in 1601.3 He lived at Woodland, Dorset, England.4
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Hon. Henry Hastings and Dorothy Willoughby
