1 OCCU Burkes General Armory lists the arms granted to Robert Harbin, Esquire of Newton, Somershire, in 1618, Mercer, builder of Newton Surmaville
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Our story begins on the west coast of England during the reign of Henry the  eighth, in the County of Somerset. The  name Harbin can be found in English records long before this but their descendants have not been 
2 CONC linked with the Harbin family lines addressed in this study.  The earliest recorded Harbins in a direct ancestral line are Robert Harbin I (01.001), his brother William Harbin (01.005) of Blandford, and two sisters, Anne  Harbin W
2 CONC heeler (01.006) and Emma Harbin Andrews (01.007).  Emma and her  husband had a son, 02.017 Richard Andrews, but there is no record  of the children of 01.008 William Wheeler and Anne.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was born ca. 1526, only 34 years after Columbus discovered  America, and lived in Blandford Foreum until 1576.  About this  time, his first wife had died and he had married a second time and moved to Wyke Hall in Gillingham
2 CONC  in Dorset on the Somerset border.   He appears to have been rather prosperous as he began to buy up a  number of small properties in Dorset and Somerset.  In 1608 he  purchased his last property, Newton. He and his oldest son, Jo
2 CONC hn,  commissioned the building of a new house at Newton which was  completed around 1612.  Leaving Wyke to his oldest grandson,  Robert II, he and John moved into the completed Newton Surmaville  House.  After the death of Robert 
2 CONC I, John sold Wyke and his son  Robert II moved to Meedford, about three miles east of Yeovil.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I was granted the Coat of Arms in 1612 by William Camden.  His portrait was painted in 1619 and today hangs in the Manor House which is still in the Harbin family. He died December 24,  1621 and was buried in the Harbin vau
2 CONC lt beneath the family pew in Harbin Chapel, later renamed Holy Spirit Chapel, in the Church of St. John the Baptist, Yeovil, Somerset. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in 1526, parentage not established.  He was first married to 01.002_Margaret Mounsell and secondly to 01.003 Margaret Mervin. His first wife was the daughter of 00.001 Peter Mounsell of Blandford, Dorset and his second wife w
2 CONC as the daughter of 00.002 Sir John Mervin of Fonthill Wiltshire and the widow of 01.004 William Drewry, who had been steward to the Earl of Hertford at Savernoke in Wiltshire.  Robert's will was dated August 2, 1615  and showed th
2 CONC at he owned extensive holdings in Dorset and  Somerset.  The children of Robert and his first wife were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Our story begins on the west coast of England during the reign of Henry the eighth, in the County of Somerset. The name Harbin can be found in English records long before this but their descendants have not been linked with the Ha
2 CONC rbin family lines addressed in this study. The earliest recorded Harbins in a direct ancestral line are Robert Harbin I, his brother William Harbin of Blandford, and two sisters, Anne Harbin Wheeler and Emma Harbin Andrews. Emma a
2 CONC nd her husband had a son, Richard Andrews, but there is no record of the children of William Wheeler and Anne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was born ca. 1526, only 34 years after Columbus discovered America, and lived in Blandford Forum until 1576. About this time, his first wife had died and he had married a second time and moved to Wyke Hall in Gillingham in 
2 CONC Dorset on the Somerset border. He appears to have been rather prosperous as he began to buy up a number of small properties in Dorset and Somerset. In 1608 he purchased his last property, Newton. He and his oldest son, John, commi
2 CONC ssioned the building of a new house at Newton which was completed around 1612. Leaving Wyke to his oldest grandson, Robert II, he and John moved into the completed Newton Surmaville House. After the death of Robert I, John sold Wy
2 CONC ke and his son Robert II moved to Medford, about three miles east of Yeovil.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert I was granted the Coat of Arms in 1612 by William Camden. His portrait was painted in 1619 and today hangs in the Manor House which is still in the Harbin family. He died December 24, 1621, and was buried in the Harbin vaul
2 CONC t beneath the family pew in Harbin Chapel, later renamed Holy Spirit Chapel, in the Church of St. John the Baptist, Yeovil, Somerset.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in 1526, parentage was not established. He was first married to Margaret Mounsell and secondly to Margaret Mervin. His first wife was the daughter of Peter Mounsell of Blandford, Dorset and his second wife was the daughter of
2 CONC  Sir John Mervin of Fonthill Wiltshire and the widow of William Drewry, who had been steward to the Earl of Hertford at Savernoke in Wiltshire. Robert's will was dated August 2, 1615, and showed that he owned extensive holdings in
2 CONC  Dorset and Somerset. 
