1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT === Biography: Joan Coplestone (c. 1425 – after 1458)
2 CONT Joan Coplestone (frequently recorded as Joan Coppleton) was a 15th-century English gentlewoman whose identity is defined by her marriage into the powerful Coplestone-Hawley alliance. While her specific paternal origins remain a su
2 CONC bject of genealogical debate, her role as a matriarch in a junior branch of the house is well-documented.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parentage and Identity
2 CONT
2 CONT The parentage of Joan is currently unknown in primary heraldic records. Her surname is often recorded as Coppleton or a variant of Coplestone, which has led some researchers to suggest she may have been a cousin from a collateral 
2 CONC branch of the same family—a common practice in the 15th century to consolidate dower lands and family interests. Unlike the major heiresses of the period, Joan does not appear in the Inquisitions Post Mortem as a holder of a signi
2 CONC ficant manor in her own right.[^1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married John Coplestone III, the second son of the influential lawyer John Coplestone II and the Dartmouth heiress Elizabeth Hawley.[^2] While the eldest branch of the family focused on the primary seat at Colebrooke, Joan an
2 CONC d John III established a household that supported the family's broader regional influence in Devon.
2 CONT
2 CONT The union produced at least two daughters. While the names "Anne" and "Agnes" were sometimes synonymous in later periods, 15th-century Devonshire records generally treat them as two distinct individuals:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Anne Coplestone: Recorded as a daughter of this branch.[^3]
2 CONT * Agnes Coplestone: Recorded as the daughter who married into the Maggs family.[^4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Historical Position
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan lived during the transition from the late medieval period to the early Tudor era. Her marriage provided the social and domestic foundation for a branch of the Coplestone family that successfully translated legal and merchant 
2 CONC wealth into localized landed status across the South Hams.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===NGS Style Register Report
2 CONT
2 CONT First Generation
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan [Coppleton] was born circa 1425. Her parentage is currently unproven. She married John Coplestone III, son of John Coplestone and Elizabeth Hawley, circa 1445. He was born circa 1422 and died after 1458.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John and Joan (Coppleton) Coplestone:
2 CONT
2 CONT # i. Anne Copplestone.
2 CONT # ii. Agnes Coplestone. She married [—] Maggs.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Footnotes
2 CONT
2 CONT * [^1]: John Lambrick Vivian, The Visitations of the County of Devon: Comprising the Herald's Visitations of 1531, 1564, & 1620 (Exeter: H.S. Eland, 1895), 224. This source identifies her as the wife of John (2 son) but does not p
2 CONC rovide a paternal house for her. https://books.google.com/books?id=GmqlIibS95IC&pg=PA224.
2 CONT * [^2]: "Joan Coplestone," Geni.com, accessed March 9, 2026. "Wife of John Coplestone, III; Mother of Anne Copplestone and Agnes (Coplestone) Maggs." https://www.geni.com/people/Joan-Coplestone/6000000006110683005.
2 CONT * [^3]: "Anne Copplestone," Geni.com, accessed March 9, 2026. "Daughter of John Coplestone, III and Joan Coppleton." https://www.geni.com/people/Anne-Copplestone/6000000006110931664.
2 CONT * [^4]: "Agnes Maggs (Coplestone)," Geni.com, accessed March 9, 2026. "Daughter of John Coplestone, III and Joan Coppleton; Wife of [—] Maggs." https://www.geni.com/people/Agnes-Maggs/6000000006110963604.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Bibliography
