1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/William-Meggs-citizen-draper-of-London/6000000003177999606
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Biography: William Meggs of Canterbury (c. 1450 – c. 1510)
2 CONT
2 CONT William Meggs was a 15th-century gentleman whose career and marriage established the foundation for the Meggs family’s transition from local Kentish elites to significant landowners in the West Country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins and Status
2 CONT
2 CONT As a "Gentleman of Canterbury," William was part of the urban and peri-urban elite surrounding the ecclesiastical and administrative hub of Kent. His birth is estimated at c. 1450, placing his early adulthood during the reign of E
2 CONC dward IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: The West Inheritance
2 CONT
2 CONT William married Elizabeth West, sister and heir to Thomas West. This alliance was the critical "engine" of the family's social ascent. As an heiress, Elizabeth likely brought Kentish property into the Meggs family jure uxoris (in 
2 CONC right of his wife), providing the capital that allowed their son, Thomas, to later secure the prestigious Coplestone marriage in Devon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial and Death Records
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: No specific death date is recorded in the Visitations. Based on the birth of his grandchildren and the migration of his son to the Isle of Ely, he likely died circa 1505–1515.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: While no monument survives, as a prominent Canterbury gentleman, he was likely interred within one of the city's parish churches, such as St. Dunstan’s or St. Mildred’s, or potentially within the precincts of Canterbury Ca
2 CONC thedral if he held an administrative office there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inquisition Post Mortem (IPM): No IPM has been located for William. In the late 15th century, IPMs were only mandated for "Tenants-in-Chief" (those holding land directly from the King). As a Canterbury gentleman, William likely he
2 CONC ld his lands from the Archbishop of Canterbury or a local manorial lord, meaning his death would be recorded in local manorial court rolls rather than national Chancery records.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===NGS Style Register Report
2 CONT
2 CONT First Generation
2 CONT
2 CONT William Meggs of Canterbury, co. Kent, Gent., was born circa 1450. He died circa 1510. He married Elizabeth West, sister and heir to Thomas West, circa 1475.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of William and Elizabeth (West) Meggs:
2 CONT
2 CONT # i. Thomas Megges, Gent. (c. 1480 – 1559). He married Agnes [Ann] Coplestone.
2 CONT # ii. Alice Meggs. She married Ralf Touke.
2 CONT # iii. Katherine Meggs. She married Peter Bruyne.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Researcher’s Note: Disambiguation of William Meggs
2 CONT
2 CONT The following distinction is critical for researchers to resolve frequent "smash-ups" in digital genealogical databases (e.g., Geni, Ancestry, FamilySearch) where two contemporary men of the same name are often erroneously merged 
2 CONC into a single identity:
2 CONT
2 CONT William Meggs of Canterbury (c. 1450 – c. 1510):
2 CONT * Status: A member of the "Landed Gentry" branch of Kent.
2 CONT * Marital Alliance: Married Elizabeth West, the sister and sole heir of Thomas West of Kent.
2 CONT * Proven Issue: Thomas (who married the Coplestone heiress), Alice (who married Ralf Touke), and Katherine (who married Peter Bruyne).
2 CONT * Historical Legacy: Established the lineage that produced the Meggs of Bradford Peverell, Dorset.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Meggs of London (c. 1480 – 1559):
2 CONT * Status: A member of the "Mercantile Elite"; a Citizen and Draper of London.
2 CONT * Marital Alliance: Married Katherine Haugh, daughter of Sir John Haugh, Justice of the Common Pleas.
2 CONT * Proven Issue: William Meggs, Jr. (also a Draper) and a separate circle of London-based descendants.
2 CONT * Historical Legacy: This William died on July 22, 1559, during the "New Ague" epidemic. His death date is frequently and incorrectly assigned to William of Canterbury or Thomas Meggs due to their shared surname and overlapping ch
2 CONC ronological windows.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Footnotes
2 CONT * [^1]: William Harvey, The Visitation of Dorsetshire, A.D. 1565, ed. Walter C. Metcalfe (Exeter: William Pollard & Co., 1887), 30. This is the primary authority establishing William’s residence in Canterbury and his marriage to t
2 CONC he West heiress. It explicitly lists his children as Thomas, Alice, and Katherine. https://archive.org/stream/visitationofdors00harvuoft#page/30/mode/1up
2 CONT * [^2]: Walter C. Metcalfe, ed., The Visitations of Hertfordshire: Made by Robert Cooke, 1572, and Sir Richard St. George, 1634, Publications of the Harleian Society, Vol. 22 (London: Mitchell and Hughes, 1886), 166. This record c
2 CONC orroborates the Canterbury branch by documenting the marriage of William's daughter, Alice Meggs, to Ralf Touke (Tooke). https://archive.org/details/visitationsofher2222cook/page/166/mode/1up
2 CONT * [^3]: "Pedigree of Meggs," in The Genealogist, Vol. 3, ed. George W. Marshall (London: George Bell and Sons, 1879), 41. This genealogical summary confirms the "West" inheritance, identifying Elizabeth as the sister and sole heir
2 CONC  to Thomas West of Kent. https://archive.org/details/genealogist03lond1886
2 CONT * [^4]: Edward Hasted, The History and Topographical Survey of the County of Kent, 2nd ed., Vol. 11 (Canterbury: W. Bristow, 1800), 147–150. While not a pedigree, Hasted’s survey of Canterbury gentry and the West family of Kent pr
2 CONC ovides the historical and geographical context for William's social standing. https://archive.org/details/historytopograph11hast
2 CONT * [^5]: John Lambrick Vivian, The Visitations of the County of Devon: Comprising the Herald's Visitations of 1531, 1564, & 1620 (Exeter: H.S. Eland, 1895), 224. Provides the Coplestone side of the alliance, verifying the status of
2 CONC  William’s son, Thomas, as the husband of the Coplestone heiress. https://archive.org/details/visitationofcoun00vivian
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
