1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 24 44. Sir William Tracy, sheriff of Gloucestershire in 1512. He being one of the first of the nobility who embraced the reformed religion in England. His second son Robert, was one of the English Judges, 1700-1726
2 CONC . 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Knighted by Henry VIII in 1513
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference:
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Tracy-69
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sir William "of Toddington" Tracy
2 CONT * Born 1460 in Toddington, Gloucestershire, Englandmap
2 CONT * Son of Henry Tracy and Alice (Baldington) Tracy
2 CONT * Brother of Elizabeth Tracy, Richard Tracy, Ralph Tracy, Anne Tracy and Margaret Tracy
2 CONT * Husband of Margaret (Throckmorton) Tracy — married 1498 in Toddington, Gloucestershire, , Englandmap
2 CONT * Father of 
2 CONT *Richard Tracy MP,
2 CONT * William (Tracy) de Tracy, 
2 CONT * Margaret Tracy, 
2 CONT * Robert Tracy 
2 CONT * Elizabeth Tracy
2 CONT * Died 1531 in Toddington, Gloucestershire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Tracy_(JP)
2 CONT
2 CONT William Tracy (died 1530) was an English justice of the peace and prominent early Lutheran convert. After his death both his will and his remains became caught up in the struggle around the Protestant Reformation in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life[edit]
2 CONT He was a justice in the reigns of Henry VII and Henry VIII, and was made sheriff in 1513. He adopted Martin Luther's religious views, and shortly before his death in October 1530 he made a will in which he expressed his belief in 
2 CONC justification by faith, and refused to make any bequests to the clergy. Objection was taken to the will as an heretical document when it came to be proved in the ecclesiastical courts, and eventually it was brought before convocat
2 CONC ion. After prolonged discussions, the will was pronounced heretical on 27 February 1532 by Archbishop William Warham. Tracy was declared unworthy of Christian burial, and Warham directed Dr. Thomas Parker, vicar-general of the bis
2 CONC hop of Worcester, to exhume Tracy's body. Parker exceeded his instructions, and had Tracy's remains burnt at the stake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aftermath
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Tracy, the younger son who, with his mother, was executor to the will, induced Thomas Cromwell to take the matter up, and Parker had eventually to pay a fine. Tracy's will became a sort of sacred text to the reformers; pos
2 CONC sessing copies of it was frequently made a charge against them. In 1535 was published The Testament of Master Wylliam Tracie, esquier, expounded both by William Tindall (i.e. William Tyndale, who knew Tracy well) and Jhō Frith (i.
2 CONC e. John Frith); other editions appeared in 1546, 1548 and 1550.[1] Hugh Latimer, John Bale, and James Pilkington all used the incident in polemic fashion against the Catholic clergy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT By his wife Margaret, daughter of Sir Thomas Throckmorton, William Tracy had issue two sons, William and Richard. William, the elder, inherited the Toddington estates, and was great-grandfather of Sir John Tracy, in 1642–43 create
2 CONC d Baron and Viscount Tracy of Rathcoole in the peerage of Ireland. Robert Tracy, the judge, was younger son of the second viscount.[2] The peerage became extinct on the death of Henry Leigh Tracy, 8th Viscount Tracy on 29 April 17
2 CONC 97.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his sister Elizabeth he was uncle to James Bainham, who was executed for heresy in 1532.[2]
2 CONT .
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://family.ray-jones.org.uk/rootspersona-tree/william-tracy-sir-3/
2 CONT
2 CONT From Burke's Extinct and Dormant Baronetcies 1841:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William Tracy, of Toddington, was sheriff of Gloucestershire, in the 5th Henry VIII, anno 1512. He was a gentleman of excellent parts and sound learning, and is memorable for being one of the first who embraced the reformed re
2 CONC ligion in England, as appears by his last will dated 22nd Henry VIII which was condemned inthe Bishop of London's Court, after his decease, and an order sent to Parker, Chancellor of Worcester, to raise his body; but he too offici
2 CONC ously burning the corpse, the recorder only warranting him to raise the body according to the law of the church, he was afterwards fined £400, and turned out of the chancellorship. The will being rather of a curious nature and cha
2 CONC racteristic of the times, we insert an abstract from it at foot.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===The Will of Sir William:
2 CONT
2 CONT "In the name of God Amen." "I, William Tracey, of Todington, in the County of Gloucester, make my Testament and last Will, as hereafter followeth:
2 CONT
2 CONT First and before all things I commit my self to God, and to his Mercy, believing, without any doubt or Mistrust, that by his Grace, and the Merits of Jesus Christ, and by virtue of his Passion and Resurrection, I have, and shall h
2 CONC ave, Remission of all my Sins, and Resurrection of Body and Soul, according as it is written: "I believe that my Redeemer liveth, and that at the last day I shall rise out of the Earth, and in my Flesh shall see my Saviour." This 
2 CONC my hope is laid up in my bosom.
2 CONT
2 CONT And touching the Wealth of my Soul, the Faith that I have taken and rehears'd, is sufficient (as I suppose) without any other Man's Works or Merits. My Ground and Belief that there is but one God, and one Mediator between God and 
2 CONC Man, which is Jesus Christ; so that I accept none in Heaven or in Earth, to be a Mediator between me and God, but only Jesus Christ; all others to be but as Petitioners in receiving of Grace, but none able to give Influence of Gra
2 CONC ce; and therefore will I bestow no part of my goods for that Intent, that any man shall say or do to help my soul, for therein I trust only to the promises of Christ, "He that believeth, and is baptiz'd, shall be sav'd; and he tha
2 CONC t believeth not, shall be damned."
2 CONT
2 CONT As touching the burying of my body, it availeth me not whatsoever be done thereto; for St. Augustine saith, "De Cura agenda pro Mortuis," that the Funeral Pomps are rather the Solace of them that live, than the Wealth and Comfort 
2 CONC of them that are dead, and therefore I remit it only to the discretion of my Executors.
2 CONT
2 CONT And touching the distribution of my temporal Goods, my purpose is, by the Grace of God, to bestow them to be accepted as the Fruits of Faith; so that I do not suppose that my Merit shall be by the Good bestowing of them, but my Me
2 CONC rit is the Faith of Jesus Christ only, by whom such Works are good; according to the words of our Lord: "I was hungry, and thou gavest me Meat etc." And it followeth, "That ye have done to the least of my Brethren; ye have done it
2 CONC  to me:" and ever we should consider the true saying, "That a good Work maketh not a good man, but a good man maketh a good work; for Faith maketh a man both good and righteous, for a righteous man liveth by Faith, and whatsoever 
2 CONC springeth not of Faith, is Sin."
2 CONT
2 CONT For my Temporal Goods..............
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT * The Will is reprinted in the Works of Tyndale (Parker Society), iii.268–83. The will is also printed in Hall's Chronicle, pp. 796–7; Foxe, Actes and Mon.; Atkyns, Gloucestershire, pp. 410–11; and Rudder, Gloucestershire, pp. 771
2 CONC –2.
2 CONT * "Tracy, Robert" . Dictionary of National Biography. London: Smith, Elder & Co. 1885–1900.
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Tracy_(JP)]
2 CONT ** This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: "Tracy, Richard". Dictionary of National Biography. London: Smith, Elder & Co. 1885–1900
