1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT https://thepyeplate.blogspot.com/2019/06/sir-walter-pye-1571-1636-of-mynde-much.html
2 CONT
2 CONT >Roger Pye of The Mynde (rhymes with tinned) was one of a large clan of gentry in the western marches of Herefordshire. He gained some distinction by marrying an Esquire’s daughter, Bridget Kyrle (pronounced Curl). Their first son
2 CONC , Walter (1571-1636) was educated at Oxford then went on to legal training at the Chancery Inns, then New Inn, then Middle Temple. He qualified as a barrister in 1597. He married in 1602, '''Joanne Rudhall, daughter of William and
2 CONC  Margaret (Croft) Rudhall.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * Pye in Barnes, Robert. MORE ON EDWARD PYE, in The Archivist's Bulldog Vol. 11 No. 17, Newsletter of the Maryland State Archives, September 22, 1997. [https://msa.maryland.gov/msa/refserv/bulldog/bull97/html/bul11-17.html link]  
2 CONC The Pyes were an armigerous family of Herefordshire, England. Their estate was called The Mynde. In heraldic terms, their arms were described as: Ermine (white with black spots), a bend lozengy (a diagonal strip of diamonds) Gules
2 CONC  (red); Crest: on a wreath, a cross-crosslet fitchee gules between two wings erect argent white or silver). Edward Pye's father, John Pye, son of '''Sir Walter and Joane (Rudhall) Pye''', was baptized on 17 Dec 1620. He married Bl
2 CONC anche, sister of Sir Henry Lingen of Stoke Edith. John and Blanche were the parents of "twenty-three children, and Blanche was 30 before she was married." One of these twenty-three was Edward.
2 CONT * https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/1493870/person/-1238201532/facts
2 CONT * The Herald and Genealogist, Volume 5.  Page 133.  [https://books.google.com/books?id=hjE9AQAAIAAJ&lpg=PA133&dq=Blanche%20(Lingen)%20Pye&pg=PA133#v=onepage&q=Blanche%20(Lingen)%20Pye&f=false GoogleBooks]
