1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === John Pye
2 CONT * Birth:	Dec 16 1620 - Herefordshire, England, UK.
2 CONT * Death:	Feb 5 1701 - Herefordshire, England, UK.
2 CONT * Parents:	Walter T. Pye, Joane Pye (born Rudhall)
2 CONT * Siblings:	Margaret Walwyn (born Pye), Bridget Pye, Joanna Beale (born Pye Of The Mynde (Pye)), Anne Williams (born Pye), Walter Pye, Mary Tomkyns (born Pye), William Pye, Alice Lingen (born Pye), Robert Pye, Frances Pye, Elizabe
2 CONC th Pye
2 CONT * Wife:	Blanche Pye (born Lingen)
2 CONT * Son:	Edward (Col.) Pye
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Anne_Pye_%281%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Pye in Barnes, Robert. MORE ON EDWARD PYE, in The Archivist's Bulldog Vol. 11 No. 17, Newsletter of the Maryland State Archives, September 22, 1997. [https://msa.maryland.gov/msa/refserv/bulldog/bull97/html/bul11-17.html link] 
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Unusual Inventory" described by Pat Meville in the last issue of the Bulldog, belonged to Edward Pye, who arrived in Maryland by 1682, held a number of offices, and died in 1696. In order to place him in his proper familial a
2 CONC nd social setting, the following information was garnered from my forthcoming book on British Roots of Maryland Families. Basic information on the Pye family was taken from "Memorials of the Family of Pye," The Herald and Genealog
2 CONC ist, 5:130-133. This pedigree is "from the Visitation of Herefordshire, 1569, and is continued from wills, monumental inscriptions, parish registers, and other sources."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pyes were an armigerous family of Herefordshire, England. Their estate was called The Mynde. In heraldic terms, their arms were described as: Ermine (white with black spots), a bend lozengy (a diagonal strip of diamonds) Gules
2 CONC  (red); Crest: on a wreath, a cross-crosslet fitchee gules between two wings erect argent white or silver).
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Pye's father, John Pye, son of Sir Walter and Joane (Rudhall) Pye, was baptized on 17 Dec 1620. He married Blanche, sister of Sir Henry Lingen of Stoke Edith. John and Blanche were the parents of "twenty-three children, and
2 CONC  Blanche was 30 before she was married." One of these twenty-three was Edward.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Pye, son of John, came to Maryland in 1682 and died in 1696. He married Anne, widow of Benjamin Rozer, daughter of Henry and Jane (Lowe) Sewall, sister of Nicholas Sewell. Pye was the step-son-in-law of Charles Calvert, 3rd
2 CONC  Lord Baltimore, and Anne was the sister-in-law of Philip Calvert. Edward Pye was a member of the Upper House and Governor's Council, a member of the Board of Deputy Governors, and a colonel in the army. At his death, his personal
2 CONC  property was valued at £1,200.0.0.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
