1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Stirnet, behind paywall: https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/pp/pembridge1.php -- they are using Visitations from Gloucestershire, Herefordshire, and Shropshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT STOP HERE. Verification of lineage needed past this point.
2 CONT
2 CONT The line of Sir Richard de Pembridge KG (d.1375) is given in a pedigree in Compton Reade and Earl of Liverpool's 'The House of Cornewall' (1908) p.193 [apparently following Robinson's Mansions and Manors of Herefordshire, which se
2 CONC ems to now be on Google Books] as going:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph of Pembridge and Newland c.1200, dcd 1219 Henry 1219 of Newland and Clehonger Henry of Newland and Clehonger 13EI, 1284-5 Henry sp; & Richard, founded Chantry in Clehonger, dcd 1346=Petronilla fl 1336, had Sir Richard KG, et
2 CONC  al.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Ralph and Henry's dates match fine with the pair briefly deprived of lands in 1216. However, as we've seen there to be an unbroken succession of Henrys afterwards, one alive 1284-5 can only be the younger son of Henry wh
2 CONC o d.1279, meaning that the pedigree above has amalgamated four or five Henrys into just two.
2 CONT
2 CONT A reminder of the place of the younger Henry:
2 CONT > 1. Henry de P 1254 fined to have marriages of Lucia and Euphemia de
2 CONT > Gamages for his two sons. [Lucia may have died, as it was Elizabeth de
2 CONT > Gamages who was married to the elder son. They were daughters and
2 CONT > coheiresses of Godfrey de Gamages who died bef 2 Oct 1253, leaving them
2 CONT > as minors]. Henry de P was sheriff of Hereford 1255 [1]. He was also
2 CONT > apparently known as Henry de 'Clehongre' from his manor at Clehonger
2 CONT > [2]. He had:
2 CONT > 2a. Henry, see below
2 CONT > 2b. William, who married Euphemia de Gamages and thereby had the
2 CONT > manor of Little Dymock or Gamage Hall in Dymock, Glos, as well as
2 CONT > interests in Mansell Gamage and Westbury. William d.1317; for his line
2 CONT > see the draft VCH account of Little Dymock at
2 CONT > www.gloucestershirepast.net/ under 'Online text: Dymock', then
2 CONT > 'manors'.
2 CONT >
2 CONT > 2a. Henry de P married Elizabeth De Gamages and thereby acquired
2 CONT > interests in Burghill, Herefs. and other places, including a claim to
2 CONT > Boughrood castle in Radnor (Powys). He also had the manor of Castle
2 CONT > Leigh at Leigh in Worcs. [2]. He was a supporter of de Montfort and
2 CONT > forfeited much as a result- Castle Leigh to Matthew de Gamages [2]
2 CONT > (apparently his wife's uncle), Winstone (and Weston?) to John Giffard
2 CONT > [3], and presumably the relevant Herefordshire estates to the lordship
2 CONT > of Radnor, Henry and his family being imprisoned at Wigmore[1]. This
2 CONT > Henry is said to have died circa January 1272, leaving Elizabeth as his
2 CONT > widow [4], perhaps holding Winstone in dower [3]. They had:
2 CONT > 3a. Henry, see below
2 CONT > 3b. Godfrey, occurs 1267 [4]
2 CONT >
2 CONT > 3a. Henry de P, the son and heir, failed to regain Pembridge in 1267
2 CONT > [1] but was admitted to king's peace 1268 [3] and recovered lands by
2 CONT > Dictum of Kenilworth 1272. He was presumably born 1255 or soon after so
2 CONT > would have been a minor during all this (unless there is more Henry
2 CONT > confusion here). His wife was Orabel (and variants thereof), daughter
2 CONT > of William de Harcourt [5], who brought him the manors and advowsons of
2 CONT > Tong, Salop and Aylestone, Leics. They were married by 26 Dec 1271 when
2 CONT > they had a grant from Henry III for a market and fair at Tong [5].
2 CONT > Orabel apparently died somewhat young leaving a son,
2 CONT > 4a. Fulk, see below.
2 CONT > as Henry, who died Jan 16 1279 [4] holding manors including Tong and
2 CONT > Gillow [6], seems to have left a widow Alice, living 1282 and 1300 [4],
2 CONT > apparently mother of a younger son,
2 CONT > 4b. Henry [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT This Henry evidently succeeded to Winstone, Glos by 1298, sold some land there to Hugh Despenser in 1303 and the whole manor in 1310 to Geoffrey de Pulham who then sold it to Despenser [3] (records relating to this are in the Nati
2 CONC onal Archives online catalogue: E/943, E40/948, E42/35). Despenser was a neighbour and this seems to have been part of their general acquisition of lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT If this Henry is the ancestor of the Clehonger branch, he may be the 'Henry of Penbrugge' who is named as a knight of William de Braose in the latter's petition of 1317-20 in the National Archives, SC8/204/10172, as several of the
2 CONC  Herefordshire manors were held of the Braoses. It may have been that as Fulk was to inherit Tong and Aylestone from his mother, the younger son had several of the paternal possessions.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of this Henry given in the truncated pedigree, another Henry who dsp, may be a further confusion, or alternatively perhaps he was the 'Henry de Penebrugge' who is appointed in a charter of Jan 6, 1359 along with Thomas de 
2 CONC la Barre (who would then be his brother-in-law) (NA, E156/28/44). However, there was a contemporary Henry (d.1362) in the collateral line at Dymock (see link in text above to the draft Dymock history). Whether or not the younger H
2 CONC enry existed (and if he was the 1359 one, he was presumably a younger son, as Richard had Clehonger), a Richard seems to have succeeded to Clehonger by 1321/2 as he is named in a list of 'contrariants', with Clehonger and Monningt
2 CONC on (also in Herefs.) his late possessions (National Archives, SC6/1145/6, also SC6/114516). '''Richard was MP for Heref. in Sept.1337 and Feb 1337/8; he endowed the chantry chapel at Clehonger April 1342 (National Archive, C143/25
2 CONC 8/20) and died 1345/6, MI at Clehonger [7].''' He married Petronilla who is said to have d. 1348 and to have a separate MI in the same place [8]; however, there is a fine of 'Pernel de Penbrigge' of 1367 [9] and a reference to 'Pe
2 CONC tronella de Penebrugge' having an MI at Monnington [10]; or perhaps this was an unmarried daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/GEN-MEDIEVAL/2006-11/1162898903
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
