1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Richard-de-Pembrugge/6000000002939285046
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Richard came from a Herefordshire family.  He was one of Edward III's most distinguished captains during the French wars and was made Knight of the Garter in 1368. In later life, Sir Richard became a prominent courtier, politi
2 CONC cian and civil servant.  He amassed a fortune and left detailed instructions for his elaborate tomb.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Richard Pembrugge was one of the earliest Knights of the Garter. Sir Richard, c.1320-1375, who was at Crecy 1346 and Poitiers 1356, KG 1368. He acquired further possessions including Wadley, Berks, Ayot St Lawrence, Herts, and
2 CONC  Burgate and Fordingbridge, Hants. He was briefly married (after 1360, when her previous husband died) to Elizabeth (d.16 Sept 1362), widow of Edmund Lord St John and of Gerard Lord Lisle. They had one son, Henry, who died 5 Oct 1
2 CONC 375 still a minor. Sir Richard died earlier that year, 26 July 1375 [7, 11].
2 CONT
2 CONT Pembridge Castle was the castle of Ralph de Pembridge.  During the reign of Henry III, Richard of Pembridge, Lord Warden of the Cinque Ports, lived there.  He fought at Poitiers and Crecy and was buried in the nave of Hereford Cat
2 CONC hedral in 1375.
2 CONT
2 CONT Even though the castle is now privately owned, it is open to the public certain times of the year.  Photos and further narratives can be found on the castle website. http://www.castlewales.com/pembrdg.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Pembridge helm (helmet) belonged to Sir Richard Pembridge, who fought in the Hundred Years' War between England and France.  It is the type that would have been worn at the Battles of Crecy in 1346 and Poitiers in 1356 and is only
2 CONC  one of four 14th century knights' helmets to survive.  It was originally placed above Sir Richard's tomb in Hereford Cathedral, as a symbol of his authority and a reminder of his military distinction.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Helm is on display at the National Museums of Scotland. https://www.nms.ac.uk/explore-our-collections/stories/art-and-design/pembridge-helm/
2 CONT
2 CONT Tomb photo by: http://www.flickr.com/photos/14265068@N00  David Merrett, Daventry, England
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Disputed Origins
2 CONT Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 44 .  Pembridge, Richard de by Charles Lethbridge Kingsford 
2 CONT < [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1885-1900/Pembridge,_Richard_de link] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT >PEMBRIDGE or PEMBRUGGE, Sir RICHARD de (d. 1375), soldier, was a native of Herefordshire. His family had been settled at Pembridge in that county as early as the reign of Stephen, but it seems impossible to fix his parentage with
2 CONC  certainty. Several members of the family were fairly prominent in the early part of the fourteenth century (cf. Roberts, Calendarium Genealogicum, i. 278, ii. 518–9; Palgrave, Parliamentary Writs, iv. 1271–2). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gross, A.  Pembridge, Sir Richard (c. 1320–1375), soldier and administrator. Oxford Dictionary of National Biography. Retrieved 3 Jan. 2022, from https://www.oxforddnb.com/view/10.1093/ref:odnb/9780198614128.001.0001/odnb-97801986
2 CONC 14128-e-21826.  (subscription needed to view).
2 CONT
2 CONT >Born about 1320 Richard Pembridge was son of Sir Richard Pembridge of Clehonger in Herefordshire and his wife, Petronilla. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His sisters Hawise and Amice were named in the Inquiry Post Mortem of his son Henry, which demonstrates Sir Richard’s family of origin.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Pembridge
2 CONT
2 CONT He married '''Elizabeth le Strange''' (d.1362), a daughter of John le Strange, 2nd Baron Strange (1305–1349), of Blackmere, by his wife Ankaret le Boteler, daughter of William Boteler, 1st Baron Boteler (died 1334), of Wem. Elizab
2 CONC eth was the widow successively of Edmund St John, 3rd Baron St John (d. 1347), of Basing, who died at the Siege of Calais in 1347, and of Gerard de Lisle, 1st Baron Lisle (1304-1360)of Kingston Lisle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By Elizabeth he had one son:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry Pembridge''' (1360-1375), who died at the age of 15 and predeceased his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 26 July 1375, Henry his son was his heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried in the Black Friars Monastery in Hereford. Monument with effigy of Sir Richard Pembridge, Hereford Cathedral, originally in the Black Friars Monastery, removed here at the Dissolution of the Monasteries.
2 CONT
2 CONT His eventual heirs were his nephews Sir Richard Burley and Sir Thomas de Barre.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT A. E. Stamp, J. B. W. Chapman, Cyril Flower, M. C. B. Dawes and L. C. Hector, 'Inquisitions Post Mortem, Edward III, File 251', in Calendar of Inquisitions Post Mortem: Volume 14, Edward III (London, 1952), pp. 193-214. British Hi
2 CONC story Online http://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-post-mortem/vol14/pp193-214 [accessed 22 May 2020]. Inquisition Post Mortem of Richard de Pembrugge, Knight. Item '''191'''. London.  He died on Monday after St. James last, sei
2 CONC sed of:—  Parish of St. Andrew, Cornhull. A tenement in Lymstret ward, held of the king in free burgage, as is the whole city of London.  Henry, his son, aged 15 years as the jurors understand, is his next heir. …. (Also inquiries
2 CONC  into property at Hertford, Southampton, Berks, Cambridge, Wilts.);
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT A. E. Stamp, J. B. W. Chapman, Cyril Flower, M. C. B. Dawes and L. C. Hector, 'Inquisitions Post Mortem, Edward III, File 251', in Calendar of Inquisitions Post Mortem: Volume 14, Edward III (London, 1952), pp. 193-214. British Hi
2 CONC story Online http://www.british-history.ac.uk/inquis-post-mortem/vol14/pp193-214 [accessed 3 January 2022]. The Inquisition Post Mortem of Henry, son and heir of Richard de Pembrugg, knight., following a Writ of 5 October, 49 Edwa
2 CONC rd III [1375] he was said to have died on Monday after Michaelmas last; on Saturday the feast of St. Michael last; and on 24 September last. His heirs were Richard de Burele, knight, son of ''Amice de Burele, sister of Richard de 
2 CONC Pembrugg'', and Thomas Barre, knight, son of ''Hawise, second sister of the same Richard''. They were both of full age.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Inquisition Post Mortem of his wife Elizabeth ["late the wife of Edmund de Sancto Johanne"] following a Writ of 16 October, 36 Edward III [1362][8] noted that Richard her son, aged 6 years was her heir [Richard was a son by he
2 CONC r second marriage to Gerard de L'isle].
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
