1 NAME Margaret /de Merbury/
2 GIVN Margaret
2 _MARNM de Merbury
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1380
1 DEAT
2 DATE JUN 1421
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Margaret was the widow of Edward le Latimer, Lord Latimer. Through this marriage she received Braybrooke Castle upon his death.
2 CONT  Margaret  predeceased her second husband Merbury  by some five months. The bulk of her jointure reverted first to a group of feoffees and then, after the prescribed interval, to Richard Griffin’s son, de jure 7th Lord Latimer.  L
2 CONC ord Merbury had no children, and on his death his next heir was found to be his brother, Sir Laurence.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Merbury’s prospects improved even further in September 1411, when he obtained a licence from Henry IV to marry Margaret, the widow of Edward, Lord Latimer. Before his death, in the previous January, Latimer had settled upon her a
2 CONC  sizeable jointure comprising the manor and hundred of Chipping Warden, the castle and manor of Braybrooke, and land in Rothwell. All this Northamptonshire property, together with certain unspecified holdings in Leicestershire, wa
2 CONC s valued at £47 a year in 1412, although it may well have produced rather more. Lord Latimer’s next heir was then his nephew, Richard Griffin, whose reversionary interest in the estates around Braybrooke was confirmed by Merbury a
2 CONC nd his wife in 1417." Sources: History of Parliament Online
