1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography: Cecilia (Leighton) Jenyns ==
2 CONT
2 CONT The profile of Cecilia Leighton of Wattlesborough represents a 16th-century member of the Shropshire gentry whose vital dates have been subject to significant "identity smash-ups" in digital pedigrees, often conflating her with la
2 CONC ter colonial figures or misplacing her within the Leighton chronology.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Curator’s Note: Chronological Correction ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Warning: The frequently cited birth year of circa 1499 is chronologically impossible. Her mother, Joyce Sutton, was born circa 1492; a 1499 birth would require the mother to be seven years old. Furthermore, her parents, J
2 CONC ohn Leighton and Joyce Sutton, did not marry until 1508 (Feckenham, Worcestershire).
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on the age of her eldest brother, Sir Edward Leighton (born c. 1525, died 1593), and the 1532 Inquisition Post Mortem (IPM) of her father, Cecilia was likely born between 1520 and 1530.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Biographical Summary ===
2 CONT
2 CONT * Birth: Estimated circa 1524–1530, Wattlesborough Tower, Alderbury, Shropshire.
2 CONT * Parentage: Daughter of John Leighton, Esq. (d. 1532) and Joyce Sutton (d. 1586).
2 CONT * Marriage: Married William Jenyns (or Jennings). This marriage is recorded in the Visitation of Shropshire (1623).
2 CONT * Death/Burial: While some secondary sources suggest a death in 1612, there is no verified parish register entry to confirm this; however, she is not mentioned as a widow in later Leighton manorial disputes, suggesting she may hav
2 CONC e predeceased her mother (who died in 1586).
2 CONT
2 CONT === Technical Extracts & Primary Evidence ===
2 CONT
2 CONT ==== Inquisition Post Mortem (IPM) - John Leighton (1532) ====
2 CONT Upon the death of John Leighton on 28 February 1532, the Inquisition held at Shrewsbury confirmed the succession of the Wattlesborough estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heir: Edward Leighton, aged 7 years at the time of the Inquisition (confirming a birth of c. 1525).
2 CONT
2 CONT Significance: As Edward was the "eldest son and heir," and his younger brother Thomas was born c. 1530, the daughters (including Cecilia) must fit within this 1510–1532 window, effectively debunking the 1499 claim.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==== Register & Visitation Records ====
2 CONT
2 CONT Visitation of Shropshire (1623): "Cecilia filia Joh’is Leighton de Wattlesburgh in com. Salop ar. nupta Will’o Jenyns."
2 CONT
2 CONT Property Interests: Her husband, William Jenyns, appears in local Shropshire records associated with minor manorial holdings, distinct from the primary Leighton estates at Wattlesborough and Stretton in le Dale.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Bibliography ===
2 CONT #Bindoff, Stanley Thomas. ''The History of Parliament: The House of Commons, 1509-1558''. Vol. 2. London: Secker & Warburg, 1982.
2 CONT # Botfield, Beriah. ''Stemmata Botevilliana: Memorials of the Families of De Boteville, Thynne, and Leighton''. Westminster: J.B. Nichols and Sons, 1858. https://archive.org/details/stemmatabotevil00botfgoog
2 CONT #Tresswell, Robert, and Augustine Vincent. ''The Visitation of Shropshire, Taken in the Year 1623''. Edited by George Grazebrook and John Paul Rylands. London: Harleian Society, 1889. https://archive.org/details/visitationofshro29
2 CONC tres
2 CONT # http://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/ll/leighton2.php viii. Cecilia Leighton m1. William Jennyns or Jenings of Williberne or Wallibone
