2 CONT Published 1914 Corbett family, genealogy https://archive.org/stream/familyofcorbetit02corb#page/n157/mode/2up
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard "official" occupation is listed on his tomb as "armiger".
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and Education b. by 1512, 2nd s. of Sir Robert Corbet, and bro. of Reginald and Roger. m. by Oct. 1546, Margaret, da. of Sir John Savile of Thornhill, Yorks., wid. of Thomas Wortley (d.1545) of Wortley, 1s.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT Member, the Household by 1538; carver, household of Prince Edward by 1544-7; j.p. Salop, Yorks. (W. Riding) 1554-d.; member, council in the north Dec. 1558-June 1566, council in the marches of Wales 1560; sheriff, Salop 1560-1; co
2 CONC mmr. offences, northern counties 1564.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Corbet’s position was enhanced by the grant, in 1539, of the wardship of Andrew Corbet, his nephew and the head of his house. His brother Reginald pursued a legal career but Corbet himself is hardly to be identified with
2 CONC  an entrant to Lincoln’s Inn of that name in 1545. He began his career in the royal service and in 1544 he served as a standard bearer in Henry VIII’s last French war. Although he attended the King’s funeral as a member of Prince 
2 CONC Edward’s entourage, his carvership is not known to have been extended after the accession of his young master. He married into a Yorkshire family, and to judge from his will he settled at his wife’s house at Wortley: when in Shrop
2 CONC shire he lived mostly at Poynton, to which his mother had retired, and it was there that he was to die.3
2 CONT
2 CONT Corbet has been called a Protestant and identified with the Sir Richard Corbet who was imprisoned in the Tower at the time of the fall of Jane Grey; '''it is unknown if he is the Richard Corbet knighted in celebration of the coron
2 CONC ation of Queen Anne Boleyn (see source), or if he is the same Richard Corbet who was imprisoned in the Tower of London'''. Is is know he was a supporter of Lady Jane Grey. It is true that at the beginning of Elizabeth’s reign he b
2 CONC ecame a member of both the council in the Marches and the council in the north, but the Corbets’ assiduity as county officials would have been enough to earn for him the verdict ‘meet to continue in office’ which he gained in 1564
2 CONC , their standing in the Marches sufficient to secure membership of the council there, and the friendship of the 5th Earl of Shrewsbury a help towards a seat on the council in the north. Corbet died on 17 July 1566 and was buried a
2 CONC t Shawbury, Shropshire, a monument being erected to his memory at Moreton Corbet. As his only child had predeceased him the heir was his nephew Sir Andrew Corbet.4
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1509-1558 Author: Alan Harding Notes 1. Date of birth estimated from father’s death and his having a younger brother. Vis. Salop (Harl. Soc. xxviii), 138; Vis. Yorks. (Harl. Soc. xvi), 275; A. E. Corbet, Fam. Corbet, 
2 CONC ii. 264-6; Wards 7/1/130. 2. LP Hen. VIII, xiii; E179/69/31a; LC2/2, f. 53; R. R. Reid, King’s Council in the North, 186, HMC De Lisle and Dudley, i. 323; P. H. Williams, Council in the Marches of Wales, 258, 346-7; CPR, 1563-6, p
2 CONC p. 123-4. 3. LP Hen. VIII, xiii, xiv; Trans. Salop Arch. Soc. iii. 267; PCC 22 Crymes; HMC 15th Rep. X, 75. 4. Corbet, ii. 265; PCC 22 Crymes; Cam. Misc. ix(3), 44; Trans. Salop Arch. Soc. iii. 267
