1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Her birth date, as given, indicates that she could not have been the mother of  Richard White "II", Susan Clarenceux, et al. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She could, however, have been their grandmother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Which branch of the Tyrrell family she belonged to is uncertain. She was probably not the daughter of the William Tyrrell who was killed at Barnet.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.oxford-shakespeare.com/Probate/PROB_11-6-417.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT >The testator’s eldest son, Sir William Tyrrell (d.1471?), is also said to have had two daughters, Alice Tyrrell, who married John or Robert Rochester, and Maud Tyrrell, who married Robert White (see King, supra, p. 90). However i
2 CONC n the will below the testator names the two daughters of Sir William Tyrrell as Elizabeth Tyrrell and Alice Tyrrell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moreover, in the Rochester wills from the same source, it is indicated that *Elizabeth*, not Alice, married Robert Rochester. (Or at least that an Elizabeth was his wife of record at the time of his death.) http://www.oxford-shake
2 CONC speare.com/Probate/PROB_11-31-550.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT # Loades, David. "Tonge [nee White], Susan [known as Susan Clarencius] (b. before 1510, d. in or after 1564), courtier." Oxford Dictionary of National Biography. 28 Sep. 2006; Accessed 25 Dec. 2024. https://www.oxforddnb.com/view/
2 CONC 10.1093/ref:odnb/9780198614128.001.0001/odnb-9780198614128-e-94978. 
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susan_Clarencieux Susan White, known as Susan Clarencius (before 1510 – in or after 1564), was a favourite lady in waiting and longtime friend of Queen Mary I of England.[1]  Susan's family, the Whi
2 CONC tes of Hutton, were a cadet branch of the White family of South Warnborough, Hampshire.[2] According to David Loades, Susan was "probably the youngest" of the four children of Richard White of Hutton, Essex and Maud Tyrrell,[3] th
2 CONC e daughter of Sir William Tyrrell of Heron, Essex.[4] She had two sisters: Mary, who first married a husband surnamed Whitehead and secondly a husband surnamed Spenser, and Joan, who married a husband surnamed Wilcocks;[5] and a b
2 CONC rother, Richard White, who married Margaret Strelley, the daughter of Nicholas Strelley of Strelley, Nottinghamshire, by whom he had a son, George White.[2][6]
2 CONT ## David Loades, Intrigue and treason: the Tudor court, 1547-1558 (Longman, 2004), pp. 216–217.
2 CONT ## White, George (c.1530-84), of Hutton, Essex, History of Parliament. Retrieved 1 May 2013. . c.1530, 1st s. of Richard White of Hutton by Margaret, da. of (?Sir) Nicholas Strelley of Strelley, Notts.  The Whites of Hutton were a
2 CONC  cadet branch of the family of that name of South Warnborough, Hampshire. George White probably owed his seat in Lancashire to Sir Edward Waldegrave, who although not yet officially chancellor of the duchy of Lancaster had probabl
2 CONC y been in control of most of its administration since the death in November 1557 of his uncle Sir Robert Rochester. White’s aunt, Susan Tonge, was first lady of the bedchamber to Queen Mary and in touch with Waldegrave and other p
2 CONC rominent figures, including (Sir) William Petre, to whom the Whites were related by marriage. ...
2 CONT # http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1509-1558/member/rochester-sir-robert-1500-57 b. c.1500, yr. s. of John Rochester of Terling by Griselda, da. and event. coh. of Walter Writtle of Bobbingworth. unm. KB 29 Sept. 15
2 CONC 53; KG nom. 23 Apr. 1557.1 ... Rochester was nominated a knight of the Garter on 23 Apr. 1557 but he had not been installed before his death on the following 28 Nov. As he was unmarried, a third of his lands passed to his elder br
2 CONC other William, and by his will of 27 May 1557 he left the greater part of the remainder to the Carthusians at Sheen. 
