2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT We are from the line of David Gam who was killed at battle of Aggencourt fighting for Henry V. His units actions got him knighted although he died of his wounds. He was born approx. 1385 is from a line of Welch Royalty. 
2 CONT
2 CONT David died at Battle of Agincourt, France, Oct. 25, 1415. ("Gam" is a nickname which like other Welsh nicknames, is the equivalent of a surname. 'Gam' means squinting. David is said to have married, Gwenllian, daughter of Gwilyn, 
2 CONC son of Hywel Grach. David's daughter, Gwladus, by her second husband, Sir William ab Thomas of Hagan, was the mother of William, the first Herbert, earl of Pembroke.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT SIR DAFYDD2 GAM (LLYWELYN AP HYWEL1 FYCHAN, HYWELA, HOWELL AP EINONB SAIS, EINONC, RHYS OFD ABERLLYFNI, HOWELLE, TRÆHÆRNF, GWRGANG, BLEDDYN APH MÆNARCH, MÆNARCH API GRIFFN, DRYFFINJ, HUGANUSK, GWENDT APL ANGHARAD, RODERICK THEM GR
2 CONC EAT, MERFYN-FRYCHN, CAWRDAFFO, CRADOCP) (Source: Brøderbund Software, Inc., World Family Tree Vol. 2, Ed. 1, (Release date: November 29, 1995), "CD-ROM," Tree #0612, Date of Import: Jul 7, 1998.) was born ç 1375 in Brecon, Wales, 
2 CONC and died October 25, 1415 in the Battle of Agincourt, Pas de Calàis, France {now Azincourt}. He married GWENLLIAN HOWELL (Source: Brøderbund Software, Inc., World Family Tree Vol. 2, Ed. 1, (Release date: November 29, 1995), "CD-R
2 CONC OM," Tree #0612, Date of Import: Jul 7, 1998.) Unknown, daughter of GWILYM. She was born Unknown in Wales, and died Unknown in Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for SIR DAFYDD GAM:
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Shakespeare penned this work nearly two hundred years after the Battle of Agincourt (1415), it remains the finest dramatic interpretation of what leadership meant to the men in the Middle Ages.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to the Battle, Henry V had led his English footmen across Northwestern France, seizing Calais and other cities in an attempt to win back holds in France that had once been in English possession and to claim the French crown 
2 CONC through the obscure but powerful Salig Law.
2 CONT
2 CONT The French, aware of Henry's troops weakening condition because of their distance from England and the attacks of Dysentery that had plagued the dwindling band, moved between King Henry and Calais, the port he needed to reach in o
2 CONC rder to return to England. The troops followed Henry's band along the rivers, preventing their crossing and daring them to a battle they thought they could not win.
2 CONT
2 CONT The English knights fought on foot after the manner devised by Edward III. Archers were to be used in support, the English and Welsh longbows having established their credentials both at Crecy (1347) and at Poiters (1356). But her
2 CONC e the French seemed to have sufficient numbers to deal with even this threat, and they refused to allow Henry pass, angered by the English seizure of the cities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Morale in the English line as they looked upon the overwhelming force of heavily armoured, highly skilled French knights must have been extremely low. King Henry, rising to the occasion, spoke words of encouragement that rallied t
2 CONC he English troops and carried them to a victory. As a result of the victory the French Princess Catherine was betrothed to Henry V, and France and England were at peace for the remainder of Henry's short life. He perished of dysen
2 CONC tery in 1422, but was survived by his son (Henry VI) and was buried at Westminster Abbey, close to the shrine of Edward the Confessor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the speech below is a work of fiction, it is evocative of the spirit with which Henry--and all strong medieval kings--ruled through the strength of their convictions and by force of their personality.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT St. Crispen's Day Speech
2 CONT
2 CONT William Shakespeare, 1599
2 CONT
2 CONT Enter the KING 
2 CONT
2 CONT WESTMORELAND. O that we now had here 
2 CONT
2 CONT But one ten thousand of those men in England 
2 CONT
2 CONT That do no work to-day! 
2 CONT
2 CONT KING. What's he that wishes so? 
2 CONT
2 CONT My cousin Westmoreland? No, my fair cousin; 
2 CONT
2 CONT If we are mark'd to die, we are enow 
2 CONT
2 CONT To do our country loss; and if to live, 
2 CONT
2 CONT The fewer men, the greater share of honour. 
2 CONT
2 CONT God's will! I pray Thee, wish not one man more. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By Jove, I am not covetous for gold, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nor care I who doth feed upon my cost; 
2 CONT
2 CONT It yearns me not if men my garments wear; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Such outward things dwell not in my desires. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But if it be a sin to covet honour, 
2 CONT
2 CONT I am the most offending soul alive. 
2 CONT
2 CONT No, faith, my coz, wish not a man from England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT God's peace! I would not lose so great an honour 
2 CONT
2 CONT As one man more methinks would share from me 
2 CONT
2 CONT For the best hope I have. O, do not wish one more! 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rather proclaim it, Westmoreland, through my host, 
2 CONT
2 CONT That he which hath no stomach to this fight, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Let him depart; his passport shall be made, 
2 CONT
2 CONT And crowns for convoy put into his purse; 
2 CONT
2 CONT We would not die in that man's company 
2 CONT
2 CONT That fears his fellowship to die with us. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This day is call'd the feast of Crispin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He that outlives this day, and comes safe home, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Will stand a tip-toe when this day is nam'd, 
2 CONT
2 CONT And rouse him at the name of Crispin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He that shall live this day, and see old age, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Will yearly on the vigil feast his neighbours, 
2 CONT
2 CONT And say 'To-morrow is Saint Crispin.' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Then will he strip his sleeve and show his scars, 
2 CONT
2 CONT And say 'These wounds I had on Crispin's day.' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Old men forget; yet all shall be forgot, 
2 CONT
2 CONT But he'll remember, with advantages, 
2 CONT
2 CONT What feats he did that day. Then shall our names, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Familiar in his mouth as household words- 
2 CONT
2 CONT Harry the King, Bedford and Exeter, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Warwick and Talbot, Salisbury and Gloucester- 
2 CONT
2 CONT Be in their flowing cups freshly rememb'red. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This story shall the good man teach his son; 
2 CONT
2 CONT And Crispin Crispin shall ne'er go by, 
2 CONT
2 CONT From this day to the ending of the world, 
2 CONT
2 CONT But we in it shall be remembered- 
2 CONT
2 CONT We few, we happy few, we band of brothers; 
2 CONT
2 CONT For he to-day that sheds his blood with me 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shall be my brother; be he ne'er so vile, 
2 CONT
2 CONT This day shall gentle his condition; 
2 CONT
2 CONT And gentlemen in England now-a-bed 
2 CONT
2 CONT Shall think themselves accurs'd they were not here, 
2 CONT
2 CONT And hold their manhoods cheap whiles any speaks 
2 CONT
2 CONT That fought with us upon Saint Crispin's day.
2 CONT
2 CONT More About SIR DAFYDD GAM:
2 CONT
2 CONT Cause of Death: battle wounds
2 CONT
2 CONT Military service: 1415, Died in the battle of Agincourt.
2 CONT
2 CONT misc.: The name Gam was aquired due to a 'squint eye' Dafydd had.
