2 CONT
2 CONT ==Croft Castle==
2 CONT *  Croft Castle [1] is a manor house and associated buildings near the village of Yarpole in Herefordshire, England some 7 km (4 mi) to the north-west of Leominster (grid reference SO449655).
2 CONT **  11th century origin
2 CONT *A building has been on the site from the 11th century and it has from this time been the home of the Croft family and Croft Baronets.
2 CONT *The Croft family were closely linked to their neighbours the Mortimers of Wigmore and later Ludlow. The Battle of Mortimer's Cross took place on Croft lands nearby in 1461.
2 CONT *The present building originated as a castle in the 14th century and has been much altered since.
2 CONT *It was the home of a John Croft who married one of Owain Glyndwr's daughters. In the 15th century the Croft family adopted the Welsh Wyvern crest, a wounded black dragon, seen as a subtle allusion to their Glyndwr heritage.
2 CONT *Croft Castle was restored after slighting in the Civil War.
2 CONT **	Some members of this Croft family
2 CONT *'''Sir Richard Croft (1429/30-1509), royal official for Kings Edward IV, Edward V, Richard III, and Henry VII
2 CONT *Thomas Croft (c.1435-1488), shipowner and patron of Atlantic exploration
2 CONT *Sir James Croft (c.1518-1590), lord deputy of Ireland and leading conspirator in Wyatt's Rebellion
2 CONT *Herbert Croft (1603-1691), bishop of Hereford, chaplain to King Charles I and dean of the chapels Royal to Charles II
2 CONT *William Croft (c.1678-1727), organist and composer
2 CONT *Sir Herbert Croft (1751-1816), writer and lexicographer
2 CONT *Sir Richard Croft (1762-1818), physician and man-midwife
2 CONT *Sir Henry Page Croft (1881-1947), 1st Baron Croft, soldier and politician, Under-Secretary of State for War 1940-1945
2 CONT *Sir James Herbert Croft (1907-1941), died on active service with No 1 Commando
2 CONT *Andrew Croft (1906-1998), explorer and member of Special Operations Executive
2 CONT **	Manor house
2 CONT *It now consists of a stone quadrangular manor house with a small castellated round tower at each corner [2] and a small square tower flanking the north side [3]. The castle is under the care of the National Trust and members of t
2 CONC he Croft family still live within it.
2 CONT *The main building shares some similarities to Treago Castle, also in Herefordshire.
2 CONT **	The church
2 CONT *The castle and 13th century St. Michaels church adjacent [4], lie in 1500 acres (6 km²) of glorious Herefordshire countryside.
2 CONT *'''Inside the church is the fine altar tomb of Sir Richard Croft (1430-1509), high official to four monarchs and his wife born Eleanor Cornewall, before her remarriage the widow of Sir Hugh Mortimer of Kyre Wyard and Martley Worc
2 CONC estershire, killed in action at the Battle of Wakefield.
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Croft_Castle
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''CROFT, Richard, kt. (d. 1509)
2 CONT *'''It is doubtful if this was the Richard Croft who was governor to Edward and Edmund, the sons of Richard of York, during their boyhood. That was more likely his younger brother, also Richard. Sir Richard’s wife was certainly th
2 CONC e lady governess to the two boys but this was possibly before her marriage to him, all of which leaves us in a confused state. Sir Richard Croft was certainly a Lancastrian supporter at the start of the conflict. By the time of th
2 CONC e battle of Mortimer's Cross, possibly influenced by his wife, he was fighting on the Yorkist side. There is some evidence that he was at the battle of Mortimer’s Cross. He may have been at Towton and was certainly at the battle o
2 CONC f Tewkesbury. Sir Richard was sheriff of Herefordshire five times all told. Under the Tudors he was Steward of the Household to Prince Arthur, and fought at Stoke in 1487. Sir Richard and his wife were buried in Croft.
2 CONT *From: http://www.richardiii.net/ww%20cr%20to%20hu.htm
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *2B) '''ELEANOR CORNEWALL''', born 1430/5, married 1st, Sir Hugh Mortimer, of Kyre Wyard and Martley, Worcestershire, and Tedstone Wafer, Herefordshire, heir of the Tedstone Wafer Mortimers. He was killed at Wakefield in December 
2 CONC 1460, and was buried in the Church of St. Peter, Martley. By him she had a son and a daughter. '''She married 2nd, Sir Richard Croft (d. 29 July 1509), of Croft Castle, Herefordshire, sheriff of Herefordshire 1471-72, 1477, 1486, 
2 CONC MP Herefordshire 1477, and had three more sons and five more daughters. The Crofts had occupied Croft Castle since before the Conquest. Eleanor was the governess of Edward IV's sons at Ludlow Castle. She died 23 Dec. 1519, at an a
2 CONC dvanced age, and was buried with her second husband in the chapel of Croft Castle (tomb now in St. Michael church, Croft). Issue: 
2 CONT **2B1) Sir JOHN MORTIMER, of Kyre Wyard, etc., born 1450/5, sheriff of Herefordshire 1477-8, 1481-2, steward of Abberley, Worcestershire during Warwick's minority 15 Aug. 1480, Squire of the Body 1481-5, knighted 1485, Knight of t
2 CONC he Body 1485-1504, sheriff of Worcestershire 1485-6, banneret at Stoke 16 June 1487, sheriff of Herefordshire 1493-4, 1501-2, MP Worcestershire 1495. He married, after 1485, Margaret Nevill (c.1466-31 Jan. 1528), third daughter an
2 CONC d co-heiress of John Nevill, Marquess of Montagu (d. 1471) by his wife Isabel Ingaldesthorpe. They had no issue, and Margaret went on to marry Charles Brandon, have it annulled the same year (1507), and then marry in 1522, one Rob
2 CONC ert Downes, in addition to having had an illegitimate daughter. Sir John Mortimer died without issue in October 1504 (writ of d.c.e. 1 Nov. 1504). 
2 CONT **2B2) ELIZABETH MORTIMER, LADY DE LA WARR, born 1455/60, married about 1480, Sir Thomas West, 8th Lord De La Warr (c.1457- 11 Oct. 1525), and died 29 June 1502, having had issue at least one son and four daughters. 
2 CONT **2B3) Sir EDWARD CROFT, of Croft Castle, born about 1465, sheriff of Herefordshire 1505, married Joyce Scull, said to be daughter of Sir Walter Scull, of Holt Castle, Worcestershire (d. about 1472; buried at Holt Castle church), 
2 CONC by his first wife Margaret Beauchamp of Holt (d. 1456; buried at Holt Castle church), but this seems unlikely due to chronology, and she was probably his daughter by his second wife Frances Mulle (d. 1483). Sir Edward died 23 Marc
2 CONC h 1541, having had four sons and seven daughters, and was ancestor of the Crofts of Croft Castle, descendants of whom still occupy the castle today. 
2 CONT **2B4) JOHN CROFT, of Holt, Worcestershire, born 1465/70, married Elizabeth Seymour, daughter of John Seymour of Wolf Hall, Wiltshire (d. 1491), by his wife Elizabeth Darrell, and was ancestor of the Crofts of Holt. 
2 CONT **2B5) ROBERT CROFT, of Kyre Wyard, Worcestershire, married and had a sole daughter and heiress. 
2 CONT **2B6) ANN CROFT, married Sir Thomas Blount (1455- 4 June 1524), of Kinlet, Shropshire, and died 27 Sep. 1549, having had at least five sons and six daughters. 
2 CONT **2B7) ELIZABETH CROFT, married John Whittingham, of Pauntley, Gloucestershire. 
2 CONT **2B8) JOYCE CROFT, married Thomas Mill, of Avenbury, Herefordshire. 
2 CONT **2B9) JANE CROFT, married Sir Edward Darrell (1465/6- 9 Mar. 1530), of Littlecote, Witshire, and died by 1492, having had issue, two sons and two daughters. 
2 CONT **2B10) SYBIL CROFT, married Sir George Herbert (c.1463/5- died after 1504), of St Julians, Monmouthshire, third son of William Herbert, Earl of Pembroke. He was knighted in 1487 at the battle of Stoke. Dame Sybil had two sons and
2 CONC  one daughter. 
2 CONT *From: http://newsgroups.derkeiler.com/Archive/Soc/soc.genealogy.medieval/2005-08/msg00683.html
2 CONT ----
