1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''The Abbey of St. James, Northampton'''
2 CONT "In 1485 Sir John Catesby of Arthingworth, jusitce of the common pleas, willed his body to be buried here. In 1490 RICHARD WOODVILLE, EARL RIVERS, bequeathed his body to be buried in the Abbey Church of St. James, Northampton,''in
2 CONC  a place made ready for the same.'"
2 CONT '''Source:''' ''The Victoria History of the County of Northampton'', Vol. 2; R.M. Serjeantson & W R.D. Adkins, eds. (London: Constable & Co., 1906),128.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Wikipedia:''''' Richard Woodville, 3rd Earl Rivers
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Woodville,_3rd_Earl_Rivers]
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Calendar of Inquisitions Post Mortem and Other Analogous Documents'', Vol. 1, p. 14 [https://books.google.com/books?id=xPMLAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA14#v=onepage&q&f=false] See attached page in Sources
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Richard Woodville was the last of his family to hold the title of Earl Rivers. He died unmarried and the Earldom became extinct.[4] The estates attached to the Earldom reverted to the crown.[5] His personal lands were bequeathed t
2 CONC o his nephew Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Woodville, 3rd Earl Rivers died 6 March 1491) succeeded his brother, Anthony Woodville, as the third Earl Rivers. He was the son of Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers and Jacquetta of Luxembourg, and was the brother of Eli
2 CONC zabeth Woodville, wife and Queen of King Edward IV of England. Richard Woodville was the last of his family to hold the title of Earl Rivers. Some historians and geneaologists believe that Richard secretly married and had a son, a
2 CONC lso named Richard, who was raised as a commoner in order to protect him from the fate that had befallen many in his family. This child was passed off as the son of another Richard Rivers, steward in the household of the Dukes of B
2 CONC uckingham, and was raised as a servant by the dowager Duchess of Buckingham who had been sister to the late Queen Elizabeth Woodville and to Anthony and Richard Rivers. As a servant the child was not a claimant to the Woodville in
2 CONC heritance which was King Henry VII's goal (Henry was impatient to inherit the remainder of the Woodville money and estates he had not already confiscated). Sir John Rivers (b 1510 Penshurst, Kent died 1583 Hadlow, Kent) was the so
2 CONC n of Richard Rivers. He married Elizabeth Barne and succeeded her father as Lord Mayor of London (in 1573-4). He attempted to prove he was Earl of Rivers during the reign of Queen Eliazabeth I, but was unsuccessful in his effort.
