2 NOTE All things considered, his still murdered his two nephews to become King of England.  
2 NOTE took place on MAR 27, 2015 01:30 PM ET
2 NOTE King Richard III finally got his monumental burial this morning, 530 years after his death in battle.
3 CONT
3 CONT A 2.3-ton tombstone was lowered overnight into place, sealing the king beneath and marking Richard’s place of honor in Leicester cathedral.
3 CONT
3 CONT Coming from a quarry in North Yorkshire which Richard would have owned as the Duke of York, the Swaledale fossil stone sits on a slab of dark Kilkenny marble, inscribed with the king’s name, dates, motto and coat of arms. The latt
3 CONC er is made in a variety of marble and semi-precious stones.
3 CONT
3 CONT PHOTOS: King Richard III Buried (Again)
3 CONT
3 CONT The Swaledale tombstone, which polishes to a fine finish, contains fossil remains of invertebrate marine creatures. The stone was formed at the bottom of a shallow sea that covered much of today’s United Kingdom during the early C
3 CONC arboniferous period, some 350 million years ago. ”The fossils within it are long dead creatures immortalized,” the cathedral said.
3 CONT
3 CONT Basically the shape and size of a sarcophagus, the tombstone features a deeply incised cross.
3 CONT
3 CONT “The deep cut in the stone will allow light to flood through it, symbolizing that death is not the end, but that we all receive new life in Christ,” the cathedral said in a statement.
3 CONT
3 CONT PHOTOS: Bidding Farewell to Richard III
3 CONT
3 CONT The stone tomb’s creation marks one of the biggest events in the history of Leicester. The design is slightly higher at the head, “as if rising to meet the risen Jesus,” the cathedral said.
3 CONT
3 CONT The reinterment is considered to be a final act and there are no plans to reopen the tomb in the future.
3 CONT
3 CONT While crowds continue to line up in the streets for their first sight of the monumental burial, work is underway for the final chapter of this week of pageantry and drama.
3 CONT
3 CONT Later in the evening 8,000 fire pots will be lit in the city, while a firework display will mark the end of King Richard III’s historic journey from his forgotten grave under a car park to a solemn burial in Leicester cathedral.
