1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Gloucester '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John of Gloucester''' (aka '''John of Pontefract''') (died 1499?) was a natural son of King Richard III of England. John is so called because his father was Duke of Gloucester at the time of his birth. His father appointed him 
2 CONC Captain of Calais, a position he lost after his father's death. He seems to have been held in custody at some point during the reign of Henry VII and may have been executed around 1499.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Early life
2 CONT * 2 Career
2 CONT * 3 Under Henry VII
2 CONT * 4 Sources
2 CONT * 5 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT =Early life=
2 CONT
2 CONT The identity of John's mother is not known, nor is his date of birth. However, since Katherine, Richard III's other illegitimate child, was old enough to be wedded in 1484 and John was old enough to be knighted in September 1483 i
2 CONC n York Minster (when his half brother Edward, Richard's only legitimate heir, was invested Prince of Wales) and to be made Captain of Calais in March 1485, most historians agree these 2 children were fathered during Richard's teen
2 CONC  years.[1][2] There is no trace of infidelity on Richard's part after his marriage in 1472, when he was around 20.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr Ashdown-Hill suggests that John was conceived during Richard's first solo expedition to the eastern counties in the summer of 1467 at the invitation of John Howard and that the boy was born in 1468 and named after his friend an
2 CONC d supporter. Richard himself noted John was still a minor (not being yet 21) when he issues the royal patent appointing him Captain of Calais on March 11, 1485, possibly on his seventeenth birthday.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT An order referring to his appointment as Captain of Calais calls him "John de Pountfreit Bastard."[5] Because of this, it has been suggested that he was born at Pontefract.[6] Michael Hicks has suggested that John's mother was Ali
2 CONC ce Burgh, who was granted an annuity of 20 pounds when Richard was at Pontefract on 1 March 1474; the grant states that it was made for "certain special causes and considerations."[7] Another candidate is Katherine Haute, who Rose
2 CONC mary Horrox has suggested was a mistress of Richard and possibly the mother of Richard's illegitimate daughter Katharine, who married William Herbert, 2nd Earl of Pembroke. Horrox notes that Richard granted Katherine Haute an annu
2 CONC ity of five pounds in 1477.[8] It is unknown, however, whether Katherine and John had the same mother. They were both half-siblings of Edward of Middleham, Prince of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Career=
2 CONT
2 CONT John was one of two persons knighted on 8 September 1483 in York during the celebrations which invested his half-brother Edward of Middleham as Prince of Wales.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT John is known to have been in Calais by November 1484 and was officially appointed Captain of Calais by his father on 11 March 1485. His letter of appointment has Richard referring to him as "our dear bastard son". The patent appo
2 CONC inting John gave him all of the necessary powers of his position, except of appointing officers, which it reserved until he turned twenty-one; it does not indicate how close to turning twenty-one he was. A warrant dated 9 March 14
2 CONC 85 to deliver clothing to "the Lord Bastard" probably refers to John.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Under Henry VII=
2 CONT
2 CONT After Richard was killed in the Battle of Bosworth Field (22 August 1485), Henry VII removed John from the position of Captain of Calais but did not further persecute him and, on 1 March 1486, granted him an annual income of 20 po
2 CONC unds sterling.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT In his confession, Perkin Warbeck stated that when he began his impersonation of Richard, Duke of York, in 1491, "King Richard's bastard son was in the hands of the king of England."[12] In the seventeenth century, an early defend
2 CONC er of Richard III, George Buck, claimed that around the time of the executions of Warbeck and Edward, Earl of Warwick, in 1499, "there was a base son of King Richard III made away, and secretly, having been kept long before in pri
2 CONC son." Buck, who does not identify John by name, claims that he was executed to prevent him from falling into the hands of certain Irishmen who wished to make him their chief or prince.[13] There are no other sources for John's exe
2 CONC cution.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Dr Ashdown Hill, The last days of Richard III and the fate of his DNA
2 CONT # Baldwin David, Richard III, 2012, p. 42
2 CONT # Kendall, P.M., Richard III, 1955, p. 387
2 CONT # John Ashdown-Hill, The last days of Richard III, 2010
2 CONT # Rosemary Horrox and P. W. Hammond, eds., British Library Harleian Manuscript 433 (Richard III Society, 1979), vol. I, p. 271.
2 CONT # Michael Hicks, Anne Neville (Tempus Publishing, 2007), p. 157.
2 CONT # Hicks, p. 157.
2 CONT # Richard III: A Study in Service (Cambridge University Press, 1991), p. 81.
2 CONT # Richard III by Charles Ross (Univ. of California Press, 1981) pgs. 150-151
2 CONT # Peter Hammond, "The Illegitimate Children of Richard III" in Richard III: Crown and People (Richard III Society, 1985), pp. 18-19.
2 CONT # W. Campbell, ed., Materials for the History of the Reign of Henry VII (Rolls Series: 1873), vol. I, p. 328.
2 CONT # D. M. Kleyn, Richard of England (Kensal Press, 1990), p. 206.
2 CONT # Arthur Noel Kincaid, ed., The History of King Richard the Third by Sir George Buck, Master of the Revels (Alan Sutton, 1979), pp. 170, 212.
2 CONT
2 CONT =External links=
2 CONT
