1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Dorothy Hussey1,2
2 CONT *'''F, #62488, b. circa 1518
2 CONT *Father	Sir John Hussey, Baron Sleaford b. 1466, d. 27 Aug 1537
2 CONT *Mother	Anne Grey1,2 d. bt 1 Mar 1545 - 11 Feb 1546
2 CONT *'''     Dorothy Hussey was born circa 1518 at of Sleaford, Lincolnshire, England.1,2 She married Edward Dockwray, Esq. circa 1538 at England.2
2 CONT *'''Family     Edward Dockwray, Esq. b. c 1515
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S61] Unknown author, Family Group Sheets, Family History Archives, SLC.
2 CONT *[S11581] Burke's Dormant & Extinct Peerages, p. 294.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p2079.htm#i62488 _________________
2 CONT *'''A genealogical history of the dormant, abeyant, forfeited, and extinct peerages of the British empire (1978) 
2 CONT *http://archive.org/details/agenealogicalhi00burkgoog
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/agenealogicalhi00burkgoog#page/n312/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg. 294 
2 CONT *   SIR JOHN HUSSEY, Knt., in the 2nd HENRY VII., was in arms for the king at the battle of Stoke, against John, Earl of Lincoln, and his adhereants; and in the 13th HENRY VIII., was made chief butler of England. In the 21st of th
2 CONC e same reign, he was one of the knights of the king's body; and being summoned as BARON HUSSEY, of Sleford, co. Lincoln (where he had erected a noble mansion), to the parliament begun at Westminster, 3 November, in that year, was 
2 CONC admitted into the house 1 December following; but his lordship's name does not occur in the list of summonses for that year, nor before 5 January, 1534, yet it is clear that he was summoned; for the year after (22nd HENRY,) he had
2 CONC , under the title of Lord Hussey, a grant of the custody of the manor of Harewoole, co. York; and he was one of the lords, who at that time signed the declaration to the Pope, regarding the king's divorce. In 1533, being then a lo
2 CONC rd of the council, he had a grant of the wardship and marriage of Thomas, the son and heir of Christopher Wymbushe, deceased; but in a few years afterwards, engaging in the common insurrection, (anno 1537,) when the feuds and diff
2 CONC erences about religion broke out, he was attainted of high treason; his manor of Sleford, with lands adjacent, worth L5,000 a year, confiscated, and he himself beheaded at Lincoln, when the Barony of Hussey, of Sleford, Expired. H
2 CONC is lordship m 1st, Anne Grey, dau. of George, Earl of Kent, and had issue,
2 CONT **   Giles, d. s. p.
2 CONT **   Thomas, d. s. p.
2 CONT **   Bridget, m. 1st, to Sir Richard Morrison, Knt., by whom she had issue, ....
2 CONT **   Bridget, Lady Morrison, m.2ndly, Henry, Earl of Rutland; and 3rdly, Francis, Earl of Bedford, but had issue by neither.
2 CONT **   Elizabeth, m. to _ Hungerford, Esq.
2 CONT **   Anne, m. 1st, to Sir Humphrey Browne, Knt., one of the justices of the Court of Common Pleas; and 2ndly, to _ Dimock, Esq.
2 CONT **'''   Dorothy, m. to _ Dockwray, Esq.
2 CONT *Lord Hussey m. 2ndly, Margaret, dau. and heir of Sir Simon Blount of Mangotsbury, co Gloucester, and had issue,
2 CONT **   William (Sir), of whom presently.
2 CONT **   Giles (Sir), of Caythorpe, co. Lincoln.
2 CONT **   Gilbert (Sir).
2 CONT **   Reginald.
2 CONT **   Elizabeth.
2 CONT *The attainder of his lordship was reversed in parliament, the 5th ELIZABETH, and his children restored in blood, but neither the estate nor honour granted to the heir, which heir,
2 CONT *   SIR WILLIAM HUSSEY, was sheriff of the co. Lincoln, in the 22nd HENRY VIII. He m. Ursula, dau. and co-heir of Sir Robert Lovell, Knt., and left two dau., viz., .... ______________________
2 CONT *John Hussey, 1st Baron Hussey of Sleaford (sometimes "Huse"; 1465/1466 – 1536/1537) was Chief Butler of England[2] from 1521 until his death.[3] He was a member of the House of Lords, and a Chamberlain to King Henry VIII's daught
2 CONC er, Mary I of England.
2 CONT *Hussey was born in Sleaford, Lincolnshire, England, son of William Hussey, an English judge and Chief Justice of the King’s Bench. His mother was the former Elizabeth Berkeley.[4] Hussey's siblings included Sir Robert Hussey (d.1
2 CONC 546), the father of Elizabeth Hussey, the 'Mistress Crane' at whose home at East Molesey the first of the Marprelate tracts, Martin's Epistle, was printed in October 1588; Elizabeth Hussey, who married Richard Grey, 3rd Earl of Ke
2 CONC nt; and Mary Hussey, who married William Willoughby, 11th Baron Willoughby de Eresby.
2 CONT *In 1497, at the Battle of Blackheath, Hussey was knighted. Six years later, he was made "Knight of the Body", bodyguard to King Henry VII, followed by an appointment as "Master of Lyfield Forest", Rutland in 1505 and Comptroller 
2 CONC of the Household in 1509. On 16 August 1513, at the battle of the Spurs, he was promoted to Knight banneret.
2 CONT *In 1493 Hussey was appointed Sheriff of Lincolnshire and by 1513 he was custos rotulorum for the county. On 6 July 1523, he was elected Member of Parliament as a knight of the shire for Lincolnshire. Three years later, 5 February
2 CONC  1526, he was appointed a judge. On 3 November 1529 he was re-elected to Parliament as knight of the shire for Lincolnshire but received a Writs of Summons on 1 December 1529 to the House of Lords as 'Johannes Hussey de sleford, c
2 CONC hivaler'. In June 1530, Hussey was named Lincolnshire Castle's Commissioner for Gaol Delivery, and later that same year, Hussey sold some of his large holdings (the Somersetshire manors of Batheaston, Bathampton, Bathford, Twerton
2 CONC ; the Wiltshire manors of Compton Bassett, Comerwell, and North Wraxall).[5]
2 CONT *On 10 September 1533, Lord Hussey attended the christening of princess Elizabeth (later Queen Elizabeth 1), daughter of Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn, and carried the canopy over the 3-day old child with George Boleyn, Viscount Roch
2 CONC ford, Lord Thomas Howard, and William Howard, 1st Baron Howard of Effingham.
2 CONT *Hussey was Chamberlain to King Henry's daughter, Mary, while Hussey's second wife, Lady Anne, was one of Mary's attendants. Though King Henry forbade anyone from calling his daughter, Mary, by the title of Princess, Lady Anne did
2 CONC  do so, after which she lost her attendant position around June 1534 and was imprisoned in the Tower of London in August. Asking for the King's pardon, she was released before the end of the year.[3]
2 CONT *In addition to his responsibilities at Court and Parliament, Hussey was steward to John Longland, the conservative Bishop of Lincoln,[6] and King Henry's confessor.[7]
2 CONT *Hussey was implicated along with his cousin as complicit in the 1536 uprising known as the Pilgrimage of Grace. Though Hussey denied participation in the rebellion, he was accused of conspiring to change laws and depose the king,
2 CONC  and that he abetted those who made war on the king in October 1536.[8] The charges may have been levied in part because of Hussey's Catholic sympathies,[9] and because Hussey and his wife, having served 'Princess' Mary, were part
2 CONC isans on her behalf.[10] Hussey was indicted and tried for treason, and found guilty by the House of Lords. He was beheaded in Lincoln in 1536,[1] while his cousin, Thomas Darcy, was executed on Tower Hill.[3]
2 CONT *Hussey's statement ("confession") survives.[11]
2 CONT *Hussey first married Margaret Blount in 1490 at Mangotsfield, by whom he had three sons:[3]
2 CONT **Sir William Hussey, Knt. (c. 1492)
2 CONT **Thomas Hussey (c. 1495)
2 CONT **Gilbert Hussey (c. 1497)
2 CONT *About 1509, he then married Lady Anne Grey (c. 1490, Denbigh – from 1 March 1544/1545 to 11 February 1545/1546), daughter of George Grey, 2nd Earl of Kent by his second wife, Catherine Herbert.[12] They had eight children:[3]
2 CONT **Sir Giles Hussey (born 1505, who married Jane Pigot, and had issue (descendants include President Richard Nixon (twice), actor James Dean and entrepreneurs J. A. Folger and Peter Folger[13])
2 CONT **Joan Hussey, wife of Sir Roger Forster.[14]
2 CONT **Elizabeth Hussey, second wife of Sir Robert Throckmorton of Coughton, Warwickshire (d. 1586), and had four daughters and two sons
2 CONT *Bridget Hussey (c. 1526 - 13 January 1600/1601, bur. Watford, Hertfordshire, will dated 2 June 1600) probated 12 January 1600/1601), wife of Sir Richard Morrison of Cashiobury, Hertfordshire (d. Strasbourg, 17 March 1556), Henry 
2 CONC Manners, 2nd Earl of Rutland before 1563, without issue, and second wife of Francis Russell, 2nd Earl of Bedford on 25 June 1566, without issue; her daughter by first husband Jane Sibella Morrison (d. July 1615, last will dated 6 
2 CONC March 1614/1615 probated 14 July 1615), naturalized as an English subject in 1575/1576, married c. 1571 Edward Russell, Baron Russell (d. bef. June 1572 without issue and intestate and his estate was administered on 30 June 1572, 
2 CONC bur. Chenies, Buckinghamshire), son of Francis Russell, 2nd Earl of Bedford and Margaret St John, and after 1572 Sir Arthur Grey, 14th Baron Grey de Wilton, and had issue
2 CONT **Anne or Agnes Hussey, who married Sir Humphrey Browne, Justice of the Common Pleas, by whom she was the mother of Christian Browne, wife of Sir John Tufton, 1st Baronet.[15]
2 CONT **'''Dorothy Hussey
2 CONT **Mary Hussey
2 CONT **William Hussey
2 CONT *After his execution, Hussey's home in Sleaford,[1] as well as his other estates were confiscated by the crown.[4] His children were restored to Parliament in 1563 during the reign of Queen Elizabeth, but Hussey's title was forfei
2 CONC ted, and the estates were not returned.[3]
2 CONT *6 December 1533, John Fewterer, Confessor-General of Syon Abbey, dedicated his book, The myrrour or glasse of Christes passion, to "the Honorable 'Lord Husey', from Syon".[3]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Hussey,_1st_Baron_Hussey_of_Sleaford ______________
