2 CONT *'''Henry Percy''', 2nd '''Earl of Northumberland'''
2 CONT *'''M, #10447, b. 25 July 1421, d. 29 March 1461
2 CONT *Last Edited=29 Jan 2013
2 CONT *'''     Henry Percy''', 2nd '''Earl of Northumberland was born on 25 July 1421 at Leconfield, Yorkshire, England.1 He was the son''' of Henry de Percy, 1st Earl of Northumberland and Lady Eleanor de Neville. '''He married Eleanor
2 CONC  de Poynings, Baroness de Poynings''', daughter of Sir Richard de Poynings and Joan Seamer, '''before 25 June 1435.2 He died on 29 March 1461 at age 39 at Towtown, Yorkshire, England, killed in action.
2 CONT *'''     He gained the title of 2nd Earl of Northumberland in 1455. He fought in the Battle of Towton on 29 March 1461.3
2 CONT *'''Children of Henry Percy, 2nd Earl of Northumberland and Eleanor de Poynings, Baroness de Poynings
2 CONT ** 1.Anne Percy+4
2 CONT ** 2.Lady Margaret Percy+3
2 CONT ** 3.Lady Elizabeth Percy+5
2 CONT ** 4.Henry Percy, 3rd Earl of Northumberland+ b. c 1449, d. 28 Apr 1489
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S125] Richard Glanville-Brown, online <e-mail address>, Richard Glanville-Brown (RR 2, Milton, Ontario, Canada), downloaded 17 August 2005.
2 CONT * 2.[S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 1666. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT * 3.[S1545] Mitchell Adams, "re: West Ancestors," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 6 December 2005 - 19 June 2009. Hereinafter cited as "re: West Ancestors."
2 CONT * 4.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Exti
2 CONC nct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume II, page 243. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT * 5.[S5991] Burke John and John Bernard Burke, The Royal Families of England, Scotland, and Wales, with their descendants, sovereigns and subjects (London: E. Churton, 1851), volume II, pedigree LXXVII. Hereinafter cited as The Ro
2 CONC yal Families.
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p1045.htm#i10447 _________________________
2 CONT *'''Henry PERCY (3º E. Northumberland)
2 CONT *'''Born: 25 Jul 1421, Leconfield, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT *'''Acceded: 1455
2 CONT *'''Died: 29 Mar 1461, battle of Towton
2 CONT *'''Buried: St. Denis, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT *'''Notes: The Complete Peerage,V.ix,p716.
2 CONT *Father: Henry PERCY (2º E. Northumberland)
2 CONT *Mother: Eleanor NEVILLE (C. Northumberland)
2 CONT *'''Married: Eleanor POYNINGS (B. Poynings) 25 Jun 1435
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Henry PERCY (4º E. Northumberland)
2 CONT **2. Anne PERCY
2 CONT **3. Margaret PERCY
2 CONT **4. Ralph PERCY (b. ABT 1440)
2 CONT **5. Elizabeth PERCY (B. Scrope of Bolton)
2 CONT **6. Mary PERCY
2 CONT **7. Eleanor PERCY
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/PERCY.htm#Henry PERCY (3º E. Northumberland) _____________________
2 CONT *'''Sir Henry Percy
2 CONT *'''Birth: 	Jul. 25, 1421  Alnwick, Northumberland, England
2 CONT *'''Death: 	Mar. 29, 1461  Towton, North Yorkshire, England
2 CONT *'''Knight, 3rd Earl of Northumberland, 6th Lord Percy. King's Knight, Warden of the east March, Captain of Berwick, Constable of Scarborough Castle.
2 CONT *'''Son and heir''' to Sir Henry Percy, 5th Lord Percy and Eleanor de Neville. '''Grandson''' of Sir Henry Hotspur Percy and Elizabeth de Mortimer, Sir Ralph de Neville and Joan de Beaufort, daughter of John of Gaunt. '''First cou
2 CONC sin''' of Edward IV of England, Margaret of York, George, Duke of Clarence and Richard III of England.
2 CONT *'''Husband of Lady Eleanor de Poynings''', daughter and heiress of Sir Richard Poynings, descendant of King Edward I and his second wife, Countess of Arundel, Eleanor de Berkeley, daughter of Sir John of Beverstone, descendant of
2 CONC  King John. '''They married before 25 June 1435 and had one son and three daughters:
2 CONT ** Sir Henry Percy, 4th Earl of Northumberland, Knight of the Garter
2 CONT ** Eleanor, wife of Sir Thomas West, Lord West, Lord la Warre
2 CONT ** Margaret, wife of Sir William Gascoigne of Gawthorpe, Yorkshire
2 CONT ** Elizabeth, wife of Sir Henry Scrope, Lord Scrope of Bolton
2 CONT *'''As a result of this marriage, he was summoned to Parliament from Dec 1446 to May 1455 as Henrico de Percy, chivaler, domino de Ponynges. Henry was taken prisoner at the Battle of Sark in 1448 where his father was defeated, at 
2 CONC the Battle of Wakefield 30 Dec 1460 and was slain fighting for King Henry VI at the Battle of Towden 29 March 1461, buried at St Dionis (St Denys) at Yorkshire. Eleanor died 11 Feb 1484. 
2 CONT *Parents:
2 CONT *  Henry de Percy (1391 - 1455)
2 CONT *  Eleanor de Neville de Percy (1398 - 1472)
2 CONT * Spouse:
2 CONT *'''  Eleanor Poynings Percy (1422 - 1484)*
2 CONT * Children:
2 CONT **  Elizabeth Percy Scrope (____ - 1512)*
2 CONT **  Margaret Percy Gascoigne (1447 - 1487)*
2 CONT **  Henry de Percy (1449 - 1489)*
2 CONT * Siblings:
2 CONT *  Henry Percy (1421 - 1461)
2 CONT *  Thomas Percy (1422 - 1460)*
2 CONT *  George Percy (1424 - 1474)*
2 CONT *  Richard Percy (1426 - 1461)*
2 CONT *  Ann Percy Raynsford Vaughan (1428 - 1522)*
2 CONT *  Anne Percy Vaughan (1436 - 1522)*
2 CONT *'''Burial:  St. Denys Parish Church, York, York Unitary Authority, North Yorkshire, England
2 CONT *Find A Grave Memorial# 104698815
2 CONT *From: http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=104698815 ________________________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 44
2 CONT *Percy, Henry (1394-1455)	by William Hunt
2 CONT *PERCY, HENRY, second Earl of Northumberland (1394–1455), son and heir of Sir Henry Percy [q. v.], called Hotspur, was born on 3 Feb. 1394. His father fell at Shrewsbury on 21 July 1403, and Henry was presented to Henry IV by his 
2 CONC grandfather, Henry de Percy, first earl of Northumberland [q. v.], at York in the following August.  .... etc.
2 CONT *By his wife Eleanor, daughter of Ralph, first earl of Westmorland [q. v.], previously married, or contracted, to Richard le Despenser, son of Thomas, earl of Gloucester, who died in 1414 at the age of fourteen, he had twelve chil
2 CONC dren: '''Henry (see below), who succeeded him'''; Thomas, lord Egremont; George, a prebendary of Beverley, born 1424; Sir Ralph [q. v.]; Sir Richard, slain at Towton on 29 March 1461; William, who was born in 1428, graduated D.D. 
2 CONC from Cambridge, where he was chancellor 1451–5, was pro- vided to the see of Carlisle in 1452, called to the privy council (cf. Nicholas, Proceedings, vi. 185 et seq.), and died in 1462 (three other sons died in infancy). Northumb
2 CONC erland's three daughters were: Joan, a nun, buried at Whitby Abbey; Catherine, born in 1423, married Edmund Grey, lord Grey of Ruthin [q. v.], created earl of Kent; and Anne, married (1) Sir Thomas Hungerford, (2) Sir Laurence Rai
2 CONC nsford, (3) Sir Hugh Vaughan, and died in 1522 (Collins).
2 CONT *'''Percy, Henry, third Earl of Northumberland (1421–1461), son''' of Henry, second earl (see above), '''was born at Leconfield, Yorkshire, on 25 July 1421, and was knighted by Henry VI on 19 May 1426, being the day on which the l
2 CONC ittle king was himself knighted (Fœdera, x. 356). In July 1439 he was appointed warden of the east marches and Berwick. By his marriage with Eleanor, granddaughter and heiress''' of Robert, lord Poynings, '''he in 1446 acquired th
2 CONC e baronies of Poynings, Fitzpaine, and Bryan, with estates in Kent, Sussex, Norfolk, Suffolk, and Somerset, and was in December summoned to parliament as Baron de Poynings. In May 1448 he invaded Scotland in company with Sir Rober
2 CONC t Ogle, afterwards first Baron Ogle [q. v.], and burnt Dunbar. The Scots retaliated by setting fire to his father's castles, at Alnwick in June and at Warkworth in July, and doing other damage. Accordingly, in October the king, ha
2 CONC ving advanced into the north, sent him to invade Scotland. He was met by Hugh Douglas, earl of Ormond, forced to retreat, and defeated and taken prisoner near the river Sark (Auchinleck Chronicle, p. 18). He regained his freedom, 
2 CONC and was recompensed by the king with the grant of half the goods of Sir Robert Ogle, then outlawed. In April 1451 he was a joint commissioner to treat with the ambassadors of James II of Scotland, and was one of the conservators o
2 CONC f the truce made at Newcastle in August (Fœdera, xi. 299). On the death of his father on 23 May 1455 he succeeded him as Earl of Northumberland, the king allowing him relief of his lands without payment, the new earl having on 3 J
2 CONC uly foiled by his careful preparations an attack of Scots on Berwick, for which he received the king's thanks. This attack on Berwick was probably connected with the war between King James and James, ninth earl of Douglas [q. v.],
2 CONC  in alliance with whom Percy seems to have acted against Scotland about this time. The feud between the Percys and the Nevilles still disturbed the north, and in January 1458 a great council was held at London to pacify that and o
2 CONC ther quarrels. To this council the earl came up at the head of a large armed force, and the Londoners, who admitted the Yorkists within their city, refused to admit him and the other Lancastrian lords, ‘because they came against t
2 CONC he peace,’ so they lodged outside the walls. After much debate a general reconciliation, in which the earl was included, was effected on 25 March (Political Poems, ii. 254). Northumberland attended the parliament at Coventry in No
2 CONC vember 1459, when the Duke of York was accused of the death of the old earl, and the Yorkist leaders were attainted, and he took the oath to maintain the succession in the king's line. He was appointed chief justice of the forests
2 CONC  north of Trent, and constable of Scarborough Castle (Doyle), and the king is said to have committed the government of the north to him and Lord Clifford as ‘his trusty and most faithful friends’ (Hall, p. 242). In November 1460 h
2 CONC e held a meeting at York with Lords Clifford, Dacres, and others, and plundered the tenants of the Yorkist lords. York went north against them, and on 29 Dec. they defeated him at Wakefield, in which battle Northumberland was enga
2 CONC ged (Will. Worc. Annals; Gregory, p. 210; Lancaster and York, ii. 236). After helping to raise an army for the queen, he marched southwards with her and the forces of the north, their army plundering and destroying as it marched, 
2 CONC and on 17 Feb. 1461 defeated Warwick at St. Albans. The earl then marched to York with the king and queen, and was, in conjunction with Somerset and Clifford, in command of the royal army which marched to oppose the advance of the
2 CONC  new king, Edward IV. At the battle of Towton on 29 March the earl commanded the van of the Lancastrian army. Seeing that his archers, who were blinded by a snowstorm, were unable to stand against the arrows of the Yorkists, he ha
2 CONC stened to come to close quarters, and was slain. By his wife Eleanor, who survived him, he left among other sons''' Henry, afterwards fourth Earl of Northumberland [q. v.], and Sir Ralph Percy [q. v.], '''and three daughters''': E
2 CONC leanor, married Lord De la Warr; Margaret, married Sir William Gascoigne of Gawthorp, Yorkshire; and Elizabeth, married Henry, lord Scrope of Bolton. '''He was, it is believed, buried in the church of St. Dionys at York, the churc
2 CONC h of the parish in which stood Percy's Inn, the York town house of his family. In this church there was a painted window with effigies of the Percys; it was taken down in 1590 (figured in Drake, Eboracum, p. 306).
2 CONT *[Engl. Chron., ed. Davies, Gregory's Chron. (Collections of a Citizen, &c.) ed. Gairdner, Three Fifteenth-Cent. Chron. ed. Gairdner, Plumpton Corr., Introd. (all four Camden Soc.); Engl. Chron. ed. Giles; Hardyng's Chron., Fabyan
2 CONC 's Chron., Hall's Chron. (all ed. Ellis); Holinshed's Chron. ed. Hooker, fo.; Stow's Annals, ed. Howes; Paston Letters, ed. Gairdner; Rolls of Parl., Rymer's Fœdera, Proc. of Privy Council (all three Record publ.); Fordun's Scotic
2 CONC hronicon, ed. Hearne; Chron. of Auchinleck, in ‘Ane Addicioun, &c.,’ ed. Thomson; Ramsay's Lanc. and York; Tytler's Hist. of Scotland; De Fonblanque's Annals of House of Percy; Collins's Engl. Peerage, ed. 1810; Doyle's Official B
2 CONC aronage; Dugdale's Baronage.]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Percy,_Henry_(1394-1455)_(DNB00)
