2 CONT *'''Ralph Neville, 4th Earl of Westmorland KG, (21 February 1498 – 24 April 1549) was an English peer and soldier'''. He was the grandson of Ralph Neville, 3rd Earl of Westmorland, and the father of Henry Neville, 5th Earl of West
2 CONC morland.
2 CONT *Ralph Neville, born 21 February 1498, was the son of Ralph Neville (d.1498) and Edith Sandys (d. 22 August 1529), daughter of Sir William Sandys of the Vyne by Edith Cheyney, daughter of Sir John Cheyney. He was the grandson of R
2 CONC alph Neville, 3rd Earl of Westmorland, and Isabel Booth.[1]
2 CONT *Neville had a brother who died young, and a sister, Isabel, who married firstly, Sir Robert Plumpton, and secondly, Lawrence Kighley, Esq.[2]
2 CONT *After his father's death in 1498, Neville's mother, Edith, married Thomas Darcy, 1st Baron Darcy of Darcy, who was beheaded on Tower Hill 30 June 1537 for his part in the Pilgrimage of Grace. She died at Stepney on 22 August 1529
2 CONC , and was buried at the Friars Observant, Greenwich.[3]
2 CONT *Neville inherited the earldom of Westmorland as an infant at the death of his grandfather on 6 February 1499. On 9 July 1510, at about the age of twelve, his wardship was granted to Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham.[4]
2 CONT *As a young man, Westmorland was among those who attended King Henry VIII at the Field of the Cloth of Gold in June 1520 and at his meeting with Emperor Charles V at Gravelines in July. On 7 November 1520 he had livery of his land
2 CONC s.[5]  .... etc. 
2 CONT *Westmorland died on 24 April 1549, aged 51, and was buried at Staindrop, Durham. '''His widow, Katherine, died 14 May 1555 at Holywell in Shoreditch, the house of her son-in-law''', Henry Manners, 2nd Earl of Rutland, and was bur
2 CONC ied 17 May 1555 at St. Leonard's, Shoreditch.[9]
2 CONT *Westmorland was first betrothed to Elizabeth Stafford (c.1497 – 30 November 1558), the eldest daughter of his guardian, Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham, and Eleanor Percy, with whom he is said to have been in love, and wh
2 CONC om he was to have married before Christmas 1512. However about that time Thomas Howard made suit for her, and Elizabeth married Howard, as his second wife, before 8 January 1513.[10] '''Westmorland married instead, before June 152
2 CONC 0, Stafford's second daughter, Katherine (d. 14 May 1555). They had eighteen children, including:[11]
2 CONT **Henry Neville, 5th Earl of Westmorland, who married, at a triple marriage ceremony on 3 July 1536 at Holywell in Shoreditch, Anne Manners, the daughter of Thomas Manners, 1st Earl of Rutland. Their son, Charles Neville, 6th Earl
2 CONC  of Westmorland, married, about 1563/4, Jane Howard (buried 30 June 1593), the daughter of Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey. He took part with his uncles, Christopher Neville and Cuthbert Neville, in the Northern Rebellion of 1569, wa
2 CONC s attainted in 1571, whereby all his honours were forfeited, and fled to the continent, where he was involved for many years in plots on behalf of Mary, Queen of Scots, against Queen Elizabeth. He died in exile at Nieuport in Flan
2 CONC ders on 16 November 1601.[12]
2 CONT **Sir Thomas Neville.[13]
2 CONT **Edward Neville.[13]
2 CONT **Christopher Neville (fl. 1549–1575) of Kirkbymoorside, Yorkshire, fourth son, who married Anne Fulthorpe (d. after 1570), widow of Francis Wandesford, of Kirklington, Yorkshire, and daughter and heir of John Fulthorpe of Hipswel
2 CONC l, Yorkshire. There were no issue of the marriage. He was attainted for treason in May 1571 for his part in the Northern Rebellion of 1569, and fled to the continent, where he died in exile.[14]
2 CONT **George Neville.[13]
2 CONT **Ralph Neville (d.1565).[citation needed]
2 CONT **Cuthbert Neville[13] (fl. 1549–1569) of Brancepeth, Durham. He took part in the Northern Rebellion of 1569 with his brother, was attainted, and died in exile in the Spanish Netherlands.[15]
2 CONT **Dorothy Neville[13] (d.1546), who married, at a triple marriage ceremony on 3 July 1536 at Holywell in Shoreditch, as his first wife, John de Vere, 16th Earl of Oxford,[16] and by him had a daughter, Katherine, who married Edwar
2 CONC d Windsor, 3rd Baron Windsor.
2 CONT **Mary Neville, who married Sir Thomas Danby of Farnley Hall, Yorkshire, eldest son of Sir Christopher Danby. Sir Thomas Danby appears to have participated with his brothers-in-law, Christopher Neville and Cuthbert Neville, in the
2 CONC  Northern Rebellion of 1569.[17]
2 CONT **Margaret Neville (d. 13 Oct 1559), who married, at a triple marriage ceremony on 3 July 1536 at Holywell in Shoreditch, as his first wife, Henry Manners, 2nd Earl of Rutland, and had issue.[18]
2 CONT **Elizabeth Neville, who married, as his first wife, Thomas Dacre, 4th Baron Dacre (d. 1 July 1566) of Gilsland, but died without issue. After Elizabeth Neville's death, Thomas Dacre married Elizabeth Leyburne (buried 18 September
2 CONC  1567), who, after Dacre's death, married, as his third wife, Thomas Howard, 4th Duke of Norfolk.[19]
2 CONT **Eleanor Neville, who married, as his first wife, Sir Bryan Stapleton (d. 13 December 1606) of Carlton, Yorkshire, eldest son of Sir Richard Stapleton (c.1516 – 1585), 'one of the carpet knights made at the accession of Queen Mar
2 CONC y', and Thomasin Amadas, the daughter of Robert Amadas, goldsmith and master of King Henry VIII's jewel house. After Eleanor Neville's death, Sir Brian Stapleton married Elizabeth Darcy,[20] the daughter of George Darcy, 2nd Baron
2 CONC  Darcy de Darcy.[21][22][23]
2 CONT **Anne Neville (buried 17 July 1583 at Alcester, Warwickshire), who married, about 1553, Sir Fulke Greville (1536-1606), de jure 4th Baron Willoughby de Broke, and by him had issue a son, Fulke Greville, 1st Baron Brooke, friend o
2 CONC f Sir Philip Sidney, and a daughter, Margaret Greville (1561–1631/2), who married Sir Richard Verney.[24]
2 CONT **Ursula Neville.[citation needed]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ralph_Neville,_4th_Earl_of_Westmorland _________________
