1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Percy,_6th_Earl_of_Northumberland
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Percy, 6th Earl of Northumberland (1502–1537) was the son of Henry Algernon Percy, 5th Earl of Northumberland. He is primarily remembered as the betrothed of Anne Boleyn, whom he was forced to give up before she became invol
2 CONC ved with King Henry VIII. Anne's purported anger and resentment over this have become the stuff of many novels and films about her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, Lord Percy, became betrothed to Anne Boleyn, the future second queen of King Henry, probably in the spring of 1523, when he was page to Cardinal Wolsey. On hearing the news, the Cardinal scolded Lord Percy before his servan
2 CONC ts, since permission for the marriage had not been sought from his father nor from the King, who had an interest due to the importance of the Northumberland earldom. In fact, Henry's father refused permission, and he was quickly m
2 CONC arried to Lady Mary Talbot (daughter of George Talbot, 4th Earl of Shrewsbury). This proved to be an unhappy union without issue. Mary later sought a divorce, citing her husband's precontract with Anne.
2 CONT
2 CONT As one of the premier Earls, Henry was a member of the jury that convicted Queen Anne of adultery. The charges are generally believed to have been fabricated by the Queen's enemies. Henry himself fainted following his 'guilty' vot
2 CONC e and had to be carried out. He was absent for the subsequent trial of George Boleyn, the Queen's brother, who according to the charges had had an incestuous affair with his sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry died the following year, after spending his inheritance lavishly and transferring various estates to friends, since he had chosen to disinherit his Percy relations as much as possible. He bequeathed the remainder of his esta
2 CONC tes to the Crown. The Earldom of Northumberland passed to his nephew Thomas Percy
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Percy, 6th Earl of Northumberland (1502–1537), was the son of Henry Algernon Percy, 5th Earl of Northumberland. He is primarily remembered as the betrothed of Anne Boleyn, whom he was forced to give up before she became invo
2 CONC lved with King Henry VIII. Anne's purported anger and resentment over this have become the stuff of many novels and films about her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, Lord Percy, became betrothed to Anne Boleyn, the future second queen of King Henry, probably in the spring of 1523, when he was page to Cardinal Wolsey. On hearing the news, the Cardinal scolded Lord Percy before his servan
2 CONC ts, since permission for the marriage had not been sought from his father nor from the King, who had an interest due to the importance of the Northumberland earldom. In fact, Henry's father refused permission, and he was quickly m
2 CONC arried to Lady Mary Talbot (daughter of George Talbot, 4th Earl of Shrewsbury). This proved to be an unhappy union without issue. Mary later sought a divorce, citing her husband's precontract with Anne.
2 CONT
2 CONT As one of the premier Earls, Henry was a member of the jury that convicted Queen Anne of adultery. The charges are generally believed to have been fabricated by the Queen's enemies. Henry himself fainted following his 'guilty' vot
2 CONC e and had to be carried out. He was absent for the subsequent trial of George Boleyn, the Queen's brother, who according to the charges had had an incestuous affair with his sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry died the following year, after spending his inheritance lavishly and transferring various estates to friends, since he had chosen to disinherit his Percy relations as much as possible. He bequeathed the remainder of his esta
2 CONC tes to the Crown. The Earldom of Northumberland passed to his nephew Thomas Percy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peerage of England 
2 CONT
