2 ADDR
3 CITY Birmingham
3 STAE England
1 OCCU Knight of the Garter, 6th Earl of Northumberland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sir Thomas Percy''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:  Son of Henry Algernon Percy, 4th Earl of Northumberland and Catherine Spencer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Married:
2 CONT # Eleanor Harbottle (1504-1566) daughter of Guiscard Harbottle and Jane Willoughby.  She married, 2nd, to Sir Richard Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Sir Thomas Percy and Eleanor Harbottle:
2 CONT # Joan PERCY
2 CONT # Thomas PERCY (7º E. Northumberland), 1st Earl of Northumberland+ b. c 1528, d. 22 Aug 1572
2 CONT # Henry PERCY (8º E. Northumberland)
2 CONT # Guiscard PERCY
2 CONT # Richard PERCY
2 CONT # Mary PERCY
2 CONT # Catherine PERCY
2 CONT
2 CONT ==The Pilgrimage of Grace==
2 CONT
2 CONT ''The Pilgrimage of Grace'' was a popular rising in York, Yorkshire during 1536, in protest against England's break with Rome and the Dissolution of the Monasteries, as well as other specific political, social and economic grievan
2 CONC ces. Technically the term Pilgrimage of Grace refers specifically and inclusively to the uprising around York, though sometimes it is used in relation to the risings in general which took place around Northern England; first from 
2 CONC Lincolnshire, twelve days before the actual Pilgrimage of Grace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Aske was chosen to lead the insurgents. King Henry VIII arrested Aske and several of the other leaders, such as Lords Darcy, Constable, and Bigod, who were all convicted of treason and executed. Aske was hanged in chains fr
2 CONC om the walls of York Castle as a warning to other would-be 'rebels'. Sir John Bigod, Sir Thomas Percy, Sir Henry Percy, Sir John Bulmer, Sir Stephan Hamilton, Sir Nicholas Tempast, Sir William Lumley, Sir Edward Neville, Sir Rober
2 CONC t Constable, the abbots of Barlings, Sawley, Fountains and Jervaulx Abbeys, and the prior of Bridlington were executed in July 1537. In all, 216 were put to death; lords and knights, half a dozen abbots, 38 monks, and 16 parish pr
2 CONC iests. The loss of the leaders enabled the Duke of Norfolk to quell the rising and martial law was imposed upon the demonstrating regions, ending predication.
2 CONT
