1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Margaret Talbot'''1
2 CONT Last Edited	5 Mar 2014
2 CONT F, #24165, '''b. circa 1450, d. 1475'''
2 CONT '''Father'''	Sir John Talbot, Lord, Baron & 1st Viscount Lisle1,2 b. c 1426, d. 17 Jul 1453
2 CONT '''Mother'''	Joan Cheddar2,1 b. c 1425, d. 15 Jul 1464
2 CONT '''Margaret Talbot was born circa 1450.3,1 She married Sir George Vere''', son of Sir John de Vere, 12th Earl Oxford and Elizabeth Howard, '''circa 1464; No issue.1,3 Margaret Talbot died in 1475 at of Earls Colne, Essex, England.
2 CONC '''1
2 CONT '''Family	Sir George Vere''' b. c 1443
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1. [S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. V, p. 265.
2 CONT 2. [S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. V, p. 123-124.
2 CONT 3. [S4] Douglas Richardson, Royal Ancestry, Vol. V, p. 124.
2 CONT From: https://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p805.htm#i24165
2 CONT ______
2 CONT John Talbot, 1st Baron Lisle and 1st Viscount Lisle (c. 1426 – 17 July 1453), English nobleman and medieval soldier, was the son of John Talbot, 1st Earl of Shrewsbury, and his second wife Margaret Beauchamp.
2 CONT etc. 
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT He married Joan Cheddar (b. c. 1425), the daughter of Thomas Chedder, Esquire and the widow of Richard Stafford in 1443 and had three children:
2 CONT *Elizabeth Talbot (d. 1487), married Edward Grey, Baron Ferrers of Groby. Grey later inherited the title of Baron Lisle, through Elizabeth.
2 CONT *Thomas Talbot, 2nd Viscount Lisle (c. 1449–1470)
2 CONT *'''Margaret Talbot (d. 1475), 1st wife of Sir George Vere''' (d. 1503[2]), son of John de Vere, 12th Earl of Oxford
2 CONT Ancestry
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Talbot,_1st_Viscount_Lisle
2 CONT _________
2 CONT John de Vere, 14th Earl of Oxford (14 August 1499 – 14 July 1526) was an English peer and landowner.
2 CONT By inheritance, he was Lord Great Chamberlain of England, and in June 1520, at the age of twenty, he attended King Henry VIII at the Field of the Cloth of Gold.
2 CONT The young earl was considered a wastrel: in 1523 the king ordered him to moderate his hunting, to eat and drink less, to give up late nights, and to be less extravagant in his dress.
2 CONT He died at the age of twenty-six.
2 CONT Family
2 CONT John de Vere, born 14 August 1499, was the second but only surviving son of '''Sir George Vere''' and his second wife, Margaret, the daughter of Sir William Stafford of Bishop's Frome in Herefordshire by Elizabeth Wrottesley, daug
2 CONC hter of Hugh Wrottesley, esquire.[1] '''Sir George Vere''' had been intended for the priesthood, and in 1459, when he was only sixteen, his father, John de Vere, 12th Earl of Oxford, appointed him to a benefice in Lavenham, Suffol
2 CONC k. However both the 12th Earl and his eldest son and heir, Aubrey de Vere, were executed in February 1462, and '''George''' then became second in line to the earldom as the potential heir to his brother, John de Vere, 13th Earl of
2 CONC  Oxford. Instead of '''George''', his younger brother, Richard, became a priest, and '''in 1459 George married, as his first wife, Margaret Talbot (d. December 1472), the sister and coheir of''' Thomas Talbot, 2nd Viscount Lisle (
2 CONC d. March 1470). He fought at the Battle of Barnet on 14 April 1471, and was with his elder brother, John de Vere, 13th Earl of Oxford, when he seized St. Michael's Mount in Cornwall on 30 September 1473. '''Sir George Vere''' was 
2 CONC attainted in Parliament in early 1475, together with his brothers, John and Thomas. Ross states that '''Sir George Vere''' was never officially pardoned, although Richardson states that his attainder was reversed when King Henry V
2 CONC II came to the throne in 1485.[2]
2 CONT By his father's second marriage to Margaret Stafford, John de Vere had four sisters:[3]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From:  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_de_Vere,_14th_Earl_of_Oxford
2 CONT ____
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret was stated to have married Sir George Vere and died without issue[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT _________
2 CONT
2 CONT "Letters Patent of Edward IV. granting to Elizabeth and Margaret Talbot special livery of the manors, lands, etc., which had descended to them on the death of their brother Thomas Viscount Lisle. Banbury, 6 April Anno 10 [1470]"[1
2 CONC ].
2 CONT
2 CONT _________
