1 NAME John /Stafford/ Archbishop of Canterbury
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Stafford
2 NSFX Archbishop of Canterbury
2 _MARNM Stafford
1 NAME Rt. Rev. John Stafford, Archbishop of Canterbury
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 25 MAY 1452
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} JOHN STAFFORD, (d. 1452), Archbishop of Canterbury, was son of Sir Humphrey Stafford, called 'of the silver hand,' who was sheriff of Somerset and Dorset and a kinsman of his contemporary, Humphrey Stafford, first 
2 CONC Duke of Buckingham. John Stafford's mother was Elizabeth Dynham, the heiress of the Dynhams of Hook, near Beaminster, Dorset, who had married, as her first husband, Sir John Maltravers. John Stafford's elder brother, Sir Humphrey 
2 CONC Stafford, was grandfather of Humphrey Stafford, Earl of Devon. Gascoigne speaks of the archbishop as illegitimate,1 an allegation for which there appears to be no foundation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stafford was educated at Oxford, where he graduated doctor of civil law before 1413, when his name appears at the head of the doctors of that faculty, who subscribed the letter submitting to the proposed visitation of the universi
2 CONC ty by Philip Repington, Bishop of Lincoln.2 In 1419 he became dean of the Court of Arches in succession to John Kemp (1380?-1454). On 9 Sept. of that year he was made Archdeacon of Salisbury, and in 1421 was also appointed chancel
2 CONC lor of the diocese. In May 1421 Stafford was made Keeper of the Privy Seal, to which office he was reappointed on the death of Henry V. In December 1422 he was promoted to the office of Treasurer, and made dean of St. Martin's, Lo
2 CONC ndon. On 9 Sept. 1423 he was advanced to the deanery of Wells, and in 1424 received the prebend of Stow in Lindsey at Lincoln.3
2 CONT
2 CONT In politics Stafford attached himself to Henry Beaufort, the Bishop of Winchester, through whose influence he was elected Bishop of Bath and Wells on 12 May 1425. He was consecrated by Beaufort at Blackfriars, London, on 27 May. S
2 CONC tafford now became one of the Lords of the Council during the King's [Henry VI] minority, but resigned his office as Treasurer on 13 March 1426, at the same time that Beaufort had to surrender the chancellorship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stafford seems to have been reappointed Keeper of the Privy Seal before 11 July 1428, and in this capacity accompanied the young king of France in 1430.4 After his return to England he was made Chancellor on 4 March 1432, and reta
2 CONC ined that office for nearly eighteen years. He is the first holder of the office who is known to have been called 'Lord Chancellor'.5 As Chancellor Stafford continued his support of Beaufort's policy, but without taking any very m
2 CONC arked share in public affairs beyond the duties of his office. He received his reward when the see of Canterbury fell vacant in 1443. Archbishop Chicheley had before his death intended to resign, and recommended Stafford as his su
2 CONC ccessor to the pope. Before the resignation could take effect Chicheley died, and Stafford was appointed to the archbishopric on 13 May 1443. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stafford's experience had made him indispensable, and he retained his office as Chancellor after his accession to the primacy. He continued his old political relations and supported William de la Pole, fourth earl of Suffolk, in t
2 CONC he negotiation of the king's marriage, at which he officiated on 22 April 1445. He took part in the reception of the French embassy in July, and as Chancellor replied to the ambassadors in a Latin speech. He was not, however, so z
2 CONC ealous in his support of the peace as the king wished, and seems to have endeavoured to hold the balance between the parties of Suffolk and Gloucester.6 Still he continued in office till 31 Jan. 1450, when in the midst of the cris
2 CONC is which attended the fall of Suffolk he resigned the chancery. Stafford does not seem to have shared in Suffolk's unpopularity, and his resignation was perhaps due to the loss of favour with the court. According to Fabyan, Staffo
2 CONC rd accompanied Humphrey, Duke of Buckingham, on his mission to endeavour to conciliate Cade on 30 June;7 but in this, as in a subsequent statement that Stafford as Chancellor issued a general pardon a few days later, the chronicle
2 CONC r has perhaps confused him with his successor, John Kemp.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Stafford was certainly on the commission which was appointed on 1 Aug. to try offenders in Kent.8 In August 1451 Stafford received the king when be came on a pilgrimage to the shrine of Thomas Becket at Canterbury. He die
2 CONC d at Maidstone on 25 May 1452, and was buried in the martyrdom at Canterbury Cathedral, where his tomb is marked by a marble slab with a brass.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stafford was engaged in the work of public administration during almost the whole of his career. He was 'a cautious experienced official',9 whose knowledge made him almost indispensable to the government. Bishop Stubbs says of him
2 CONC  that 'if he had done little good he had done no harm.'10 Archbishop Chicheley, in recommending Stafford as his successor to the pope, did so on the ground of his 'high intellectual and moral qualification, the nobility of his bir
2 CONC th, and his own almost boundless hospitality.'11 Gascoigne, who was hostile to the archbishop, says that Stafford was father of bastard offspring by a nun.12 Ecclesiastically the most important incident of Stafford's primacy was t
2 CONC he beginning of the dispute as to the heresy of Bishop Reginald Pecock. Pecock's teaching gave much offence, but though he forwarded a statement of his doctrine to Stafford in a document styled 'Abbreviatio Reginaldi Pecock,' Staf
2 CONC ford took no decisive action against him.13
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Gascoigne, Loci e Libro Veritatum, ed. Rogers, 1881, p. 40. 2. Anthony à Wood, The History and Antiquities of the University of Oxford, ed. Gutch, 1792, i. 556. 3. Le Neve, Fasti Ecclesiae Anglicanae, ed. Hardy, 1854. i. 152, i
2 CONC i. 211. 4. Nicolas, Proceedings of the Privy Council, iii. 310, iv. 29. 5. cf. Rotuli Parliamentorum, v. 103. 6. Letters and Papers Illustrative of the Reign of Henry VI, Rolls Series, i. 92, 104-110, 140; Hook, Archbishops of Can
2 CONC terbury, v. 152-5. 7. Fabyan, Chronicle, 1811, p. 623. 8. Ramsay, Lancaster and York, ii. 132. 9. Ramsay. 10. Stubbs, Constitutional History, iii. 148. 11. Anglia Sacra, ed. Wharton, 1691, i. 572. 12. Gascoigne, p. 231. 13. See ar
2 CONC t. Pecock, Reginald; Pecock, Repressor of overmuch Blaming of the Clergy, ii. 1115.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
