2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ralph_Stafford,_1st_Earl_of_Stafford
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph de Stafford, 1st Earl of Stafford, 2nd Baron Stafford (24 September 1301 – 31 August 1372), KG, of Stafford Castle and Madeley Castle[3] in Staffordshire, was an English nobleman and a notable soldier during the Hundred Year
2 CONC s' War against France. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1326, Stafford married his first wife, Katherine de Hastang. Katherine was the daughter of Sir John de Hastang, Knight, of Chebsey, Staffordshire. Ralph and Katherine had two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT #     Margaret Stafford, married Sir John de Stafford, Knight, of "Broomshull" (Bramshall near Uttoxeter, Staffordshire[6][7][8][9]) and Amblecote in the parish of Old Swinford, Worcestershire,[10] ancestor of several prominent St
2 CONC afford lines, most notably Stafford of Hooke in Dorset, Stafford of Southwick in Wiltshire and Stafford of Grafton in Worcestershire.
2 CONT #     Joan Stafford, married Sir Nicholas de Beke, Knight.
2 CONT
2 CONT He later sensationally abducted Margaret de Audley, 2nd Baroness Audley, daughter of Hugh de Audley, 1st Earl of Gloucester and Margaret de Clare, who was worth at least £2,314 a year, more than ten times his own estates. Her pare
2 CONC nts filed a complaint with King Edward III of England, but the King supported Stafford's actions. In compensation, the King appeased Hugh and Margaret by creating Hugh the 1st Earl of Gloucester. Margaret de Audley and Stafford ma
2 CONC rried before 6 July 1336 and they subsequently had two sons and four daughters: 
2 CONT
