1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Constance of York, Countess of Gloucester''' (c. 1374 – 28 November 1416) was the only daughter of [[6000000016643963931]], fifth son of [[6000000000202115160]], and his wife [[6000000001544542384]], daughter of
2 CONC  King Peter of Castile and his favourite mistress, Maria de Padilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly before 7 November 1379  Constance married [[6000000006721891155]], [1]  who was about six years old at the time. [2] Thomas was the ward of his mother, Elizabeth de Burghersh, since his father Edward’s death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was a friend and supporter of King Richard II, Constance's cousin, and was involved in the arrest and prosecution of the Appellant lords, the Duke of Gloucester and the Earl of Arundel in 1397; in return for which he receiv
2 CONC ed a share of their lands. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the king's  campaign in Ireland, Dispenser was a deputy commander of the army and commanded the rearguard. Richard II was unpopular,and when Henry Bolingbroke landed in Yorkshire, most of the English nobility went over to H
2 CONC enry's side. Henry captured Richard and became Henry IV, king of England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of his close links with Richard II, Despenser initially supported the accession of Henry Bolingbroke as Henry IV, after usurping Richard’s throne. However, after he was attainted for his role in the death of Thomas of Woo
2 CONC dstock, Duke of Gloucester, and deprived of his earldom, he became disillusioned with the new regime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despenser denied any responsibility for the death of Gloucester in Calais, but was briefly imprisoned in the Tower of London in 1397. Fearful of losing his estates, and possibly his life, in January 1400, he joined in a conspiracy
2 CONC  with the earls of Kent, Salisbury and Huntingdon, known as the Epiphany Rising. Thomas was betrayed to the king by Edward of Norwich, Constance’s older brother, and the conspirators were arrested and executed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was executed on 16 January 1400 in Bristol, aged 26 years. [2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children with Thomas Le Despencer===
2 CONT
2 CONT # Edward le Despenser ''(b. bef. 1400, died young)''
2 CONT # Elizabeth le Despenser ''(died young, c 1398)''  
2 CONT # Richard le Despenser, 4th Baron Burghersh (1396–1414). He married Lady Eleanor Neville (c. 1397 – 1472), daughter of Ralph de Neville, 1st Earl of Westmorland (d. 1425) and Joan Beaufort (d. 1440), daughter of John of Gaunt by K
2 CONC atherine Swynford. He died young without issue.
2 CONT # Hugh le Despenser b. c 1400, d. 1401 ''(died young)''
2 CONT # Isabel le Despenser b. 26 Jul 1400, d. 27 Dec 1439, born after her father's execution. She married, firstly, Richard Beauchamp, Earl of Worcester (d. 1422). A daughter, Elizabeth, Lady Abergavenny (b. 1415) was the sole product 
2 CONC of this union. Following Worcester's death, she married Richard de Beauchamp, 13th Earl of Warwick; they were parents to Henry Beauchamp, 1st Duke of Warwick and Anne Beauchamp, 16th Countess of Warwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>''Details as recorded in [http://thepeerage.com/p10743.htm#i107427 The Peerage]''
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's death, Constance was granted a life interest in the greater part of his lands and custody of her son, [3] due to her close relationship with the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, Constance was either betrothed to or lived as the mistress of [[6000000001588414225]] (1383–1408), brother of Constance’s step-mother, [[6000000000077812500]]. [4] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children with Edmund Holland, 4th Earl of Kent,===
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance had a daughter, Eleanor Holland with Edmund ''(b. Oct 1406 d. Sept 23, 1459).''[5] Eleanor married James Tuchet, 5th Baron Audley (who was killed at the Battle of Blore Heath, 1459), styled [[6000000000273220198]].
2 CONT
2 CONT Whether or not Eleanor’s parents married became a bone of contention for Eleanor when she attempted to lay claim to her father’s lands and titles in 1430. Although she produced witnesses to prove the marriage of her parents, in ab
2 CONC out 1404, on the petition of Edmund’s sisters, Joan Duchess of York (Eleanor’s step-grandmother) and Margaret Holland, Duchess of Clarence, Eleanor was adjudged illegitimate and unable to inherit from her father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of York, Countess of Gloucester, was buried at Reading Abbey in Berkshire.
2 CONT
