2 CONT *''''''Edward Brooke, 6th Lord Cobham (of Kent)1 
2 CONT *''''''M, #11771, d. 6 June 1464
2 CONT *Last Edited=20 Feb 2011
2 CONT *''''''     Edward Brooke, 6th Lord Cobham (of Kent) was the son of Sir Thomas Brooke and Joan Braybroke, Baroness Cobham (of Kent).1 He married Elizabeth Tuchet, daughter of James Tuchet, 5th Lord Audley (of Heleigh) and Eleanor 
2 CONC de Holland.1 He died on 6 June 1464.
2 CONT *''''''     He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Somerset in 1442.2 He succeeded to the title of 6th Lord Cobham [E., 1313] in 1442.1 He fought in the Battle of St. Albans on 23 May 1455, for the Yorkists.1,2 He f
2 CONC ought in the Battle of Northampton on 10 July 1460.2
2 CONT *''''''Child of Edward Brooke, 6th Lord Cobham (of Kent) and Elizabeth Tuchet
2 CONT ** 1.John Brooke, 7th Lord Cobham (of Kent)+1 b. a 1446, d. 9 Mar 1511/12
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S21] L. G. Pine, The New Extinct Peerage 1884-1971: Containing Extinct, Abeyant, Dormant and Suspended Peerages With Genealogies and Arms (London, U.K.: Heraldry Today, 1972), page 78. Hereinafter cited as The New Extinct Pee
2 CONC rage.
2 CONT * 2.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Exti
2 CONC nct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume III, page 346. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *http://www.thepeerage.com/p1178.htm#i11771
2 CONT *______
2 CONT *''''''Edward Brooke, 6th Baron Cobham (of Kent)[1] (died 6 June 1464) was a late medieval aristocrat. 
2 CONT *''''''His parents were Sir Thomas Brooke and Joan Braybroke, 5th Baroness Cobham[2].
2 CONT *''''''He was a Member of Parliament for Somerset in 1442[3], the same year he succeeded to his mother's title[4]. An ardent supporter of Richard Duke of York, he fought on the Yorkist side at the First Battle of St Albans on 23 M
2 CONC ay 1455[5][6] and at the Battle of Northampton on 10 July 1460[7].
2 CONT *''''''He married Elizabeth Touchet, born c. 1433, daughter of James Touchet, 5th Baron Audley and second wife Eleanor de Holland, and had at least two children, John Brooke, 7th Baron Cobham and Elizabeth Brooke, married to Rober
2 CONC t Tanfield; their son, also named Robert Tanfield, born in 1461, married Catherine Nevill, born before 1473, daughter of Edward Nevill, 1st Baron Bergavenny and second wife Katherine Howard, and had issue. His widow remarried Chri
2 CONC stopher Worsley, before 8 November 1464.[8]
2 CONT *References
