1 NAME Elizabeth /Courtenay/
2 GIVN Elizabeth
2 SURN Courtenay
2 _MARNM Luttrell
2 NICK Malet
2 NICK Audley
2 NICK Courtenay
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1430
2 ADDR
3 CITY Powderham
3 CTRY England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 SEP 1493
2 ADDR
3 CITY Dunster
3 CTRY England
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Primary Sources
2 CONT 1463/4 12 Kal. April. (21 March.) Siena. (f. 153.)	<br>
2 CONT To '''Humphrey Audeley, nobleman, donsel, and Elisabeth, noblewoman, relict of James Lottrell''', of the diocese of Bath and Wells. Absolution from sentence of excommunication incurred by their having formerly, not being ignorant 
2 CONC that they were related in the second degree of affinity, contracted marriage per verba legitime de presenti, and consummated it, although they did not have it solemnized before the church; with dispensation to contract the said ma
2 CONC rriage anew (de novo), have it solemnized before the church and remain therein, decreeing the offspring born, if any, and that to be born of the marriage legitimate for all legal purposes. (fn. 2)Oblate siquidem nobis. (G. de Picc
2 CONC olominibus. | lxxx. A. de Urbino. N. [de] Benzis.) [In the margin: Februarii and Jo. Horn, O. Principis. 2/3 p.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: 'Vatican Regesta 513: 1464', in Calendar of Papal Registers Relating To Great Britain and Ireland: Volume 11, 1455-1464, ed. J A Twemlow (London, 1921), pp. 677-678. British History Online http://www.british-history.ac.uk/
2 CONC cal-papal-registers/brit-ie/vol11/pp677-678 [accessed 2
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1450, the Bishop at Exeter issued a license for a marriage to be celebrated in the private chapel of Powderham Castle between James Luttrell and Elizabeth daughter of Sir Phillip Courtenay, his late guardian. A large part of th
2 CONC e Luttrell estate was settled on her in jointure, some two years later. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the proceedings of 1475, Lady Luttrell is described as a widow. She had, in point of fact, had two husbands. Sir James Luttrell had, as we have seen, been mortally wounded at the second battle of St. Albans and attainted. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death, she had married his cousin, Sir Humphrey Touchet, brother of Lord Audley, but he in turn was taken prisoner at the battle of Tewkesbury and beheaded. Even in those distracted times of civil war, there could not ha
2 CONC ve been many widows who had, within eleven years, lost two husbands fighting on behalf of the unfortunate House of Lancaster. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She eventually married a third husband, Thomas Mallet of Enmore in Somerset, but, according to common medieval custom, '''she retained the surname of Luttrell until her death''' in the reign of Henry VII. It was under that name th
2 CONC at she, in 1476, stood godmother to Richard, the short-lived son of her patron, George, Duke of Clarence. Her feelings at the christening must have been mixed, for it was performed at Tewkesbury, the very place where her late husb
2 CONC and had lost his head.
2 CONT
2 CONT ———-
