1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Primary Sources
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000046681493659& Inquisitions Post Mortem] for Hugh Luterell, Chevalier, Writ. 25 March 1428. <br>
2 CONT mention: Katherine his wife, still living, and John Strecche, her former husband <br>
2 CONT He died on 24 March [1428]. John Luterell was his son and next heir, and aged 30 and more.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family and Education==
2 CONT b.c.1364, yr. s. and event. h. of Sir Andrew Luttrell of Chilton, Devon by Elizabeth (d. 7 Aug. 1395), da. of Hugh Courtenay, earl of Devon, and Margaret de Bohun, gdda. of Edw. I, wid. of Sir John de Vere. m. bef. 1384, Katherine
2 CONC  (d. 28 Aug. 1435), da. of Sir John Beaumont† of Saunton and Sherwell, Devon, wid. of John, s. of Sir John Stretch* of Wambrook, Som., 2s. 4da.1 Kntd. by 1390. d. 24 Mar. 1428.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biographies==
2 CONT * [http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1386-1421/member/luttrell-sir-hugh-1364-1428 History of Parliament Online]
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Hugh_Luttrell wikipedia.org]
2 CONT
2 CONT ————-
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Hugh Luttrell (c. 1364 – 1428) (son of Sir Andrew Luttrell by his wife Lady Elizabeth Courtenay).[21] He married Catherine Beaumont (died 1435),[22] daughter of Sir John Beaumont (died 1379/80)[23] of Shirwell and Saunton in N
2 CONC orth Devon, MP for Devon 1376-80,[24] a substantial landowner in Devon, by his second wife Joan Crawthorne, granddaughter and heiress of Sir Robert Stockey, MP in 1318, of Crawthorne and Cranstone.[25] Alabaster effigies survive i
2 CONC n Dunster Church of himself and his wife, badly mutilated.[26] The arms of Beaumont (Barry of six vair and gules) appear in Dunster Church and on the Luttrell Table Carpet, c.1520, now in the collection of the Burrell Collection i
2 CONC n Glasgow.www.findagrave.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Hugh Luttrell
2 CONT BIRTH	1364
2 CONT DEATH	24 Mar 1428 (aged 63–64)
2 CONT Shaftesbury, North Dorset District, Dorset, England
2 CONT BURIAL	
2 CONT Priory Churchyard of St. George
2 CONT Dunster, West Somerset District, Somerset, England 
2 CONT PLOT	Chancel
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID	101213125
2 CONT
2 CONT n a volume at the College of Arms is a transcript of a French deed whereby Hugh Courtenay, Earl of Devon, granted his heraldic badges to his cousin Sir Hugh Luttrell on July 13, 1421:
2 CONT A tous y ceux que cestes nos lettres verront on orront hugh Courtnay Count de Devon et Sr d'Ockhampton feiz et hair a Monsr l'honorable et tresnoble Sr Edward Courtney Count de Devon et Sr d'Okhampton que Dieu assoile saluz en Die
2 CONC u, Sachez nous avon don et bon ame coseyn Hugh Lutrel Chr et Sr Donstarre nos Bages cest a savoire un Sengler Blanc arme d'or portans come nous portons avecque un difference dun doble rose dor sue lespald en dit sengler a avoir et
2 CONC  tenoir le dites Bages de nostre don al dit Sr Hugh de Luttrell et ses hoires a tous jours En testmonance de quell chose a y cestes nos presentz lettres nous avons mis notre seale de nous Armes Donne a Plimmouth le 13 jour de Juel
2 CONC l a temps que nous avons priz nostre voyage par Grace de Deux envers nostre tresouveraigne Roy en Normandie l'an du Raigne le dit nostre Sr le Roy Sr le Henri quint puis le Conquest 9e.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Members
2 CONT Parents
2 CONT Elizabeth Courtenay Luttrell
2 CONT unknown–1395
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse
2 CONT Katherine Beaumont Luttrell
2 CONT 1354–1435
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT Robert Luttrell
2 CONT 1389–1437
2 CONT John Luttrell
2 CONT 1394–1430From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT Sir Hugh Luttrell (about 1364 – 24 March 1428[1]), of Dunster Castle in Somerset, feudal baron of Dunster, was an English nobleman and politician, who was an important military officer during the Hundred Years' War. He was a close
2 CONC  associate of his cousin, King Richard II of England, and was one of his most valuable advisors. He was also an esquire of John of Gaunt,[2] and an extremely close friend to Queen Anne of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was an MP for Somerset and Devon.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Sir Hugh Luttrell was born in Dunster, a son of Elizabeth Courtenay (d. 7 August 1395), great-granddaughter of Edward I of England, and her second husband Sir Andrew Lutrell of Chilton. Sir Andrew was a direct descendant of an Iri
2 CONC sh noble family: the Luttrells, who were the original Earls of Carhampton. The family had strong ties with the Plantagenets, to which they were related. Given her loyal servitude to the royal family, Elizabeth received 200 pounds 
2 CONC from her close cousin, Richard II of England. Courtenay used this loan to buy large properties of land, which increased the family's wealth by leaps and bounds. This act would make Sir Hugh famous throughout England.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1378, Hugh's father died abruptly, and in 1379, he began his own career. When he was about 16, he became an esquire to John of Gaunt. He served Gaunt for about 12 years, and switched his services to the English Queen, Anne of B
2 CONC ohemia. They became very close, and Anne granted him numerous plots of land for his servitude and good nature, which boosted his social standing among the English nobility. Hugh again switched his services, this time to his cousin
2 CONC , Richard II. Hugh soon became the bearer of numerous titles, like Constable of Leed's Castle (1393–1399), and Keeper of her Forest of Gillingham in 1399.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Hugh's loyalty to Richard II was unbreakable, right up to his imprisonment. After all, he was one of the King's closest retainers and advisors. After the monarch's murder, Hugh felt sorrow for his cousin. However, his switchin
2 CONC g sides to the new king, Henry IV of England (also known as Henry of Bolingbroke), doubled his fortunes drastically. He flourished during Henry's reign, mostly because of his family's prominence, and usefulness to the king. In 140
2 CONC 0, he sailed to Calais, in the company of his uncle, Sir Peter, who had become the commander of the English garrison there. Luttrell spent three years being the deputy Lieutenant of Calais (1400–1403), and devoted his time in that
2 CONC  job to politics. He discussed numerous peace talks with French officials, and numerous other governmental matters. In 1403, he was assigned with Sir Thomas Swineburn and John Urban to renew a peace treaty with Flanders. The talks
2 CONC  were harsh, as surviving documents say, and the three Englishmen left Flanders, with an impression that the Flemings were not to be trusted.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he arrived back in England in 1404, he was elected to the English Parliament for the first time, and faced a serious dilemma. On 6 October 1404, Lady Mohun, a noblewoman that had sold vast plots of land to Hugh's late mother,
2 CONC  Elizabeth, had died at Canterbury. This was a serious blow to Luttrell, who had inherited Mohun's estates from his mother. After Lady Mohun's death, her heirs and Luttrell began a fierce legal battle over who would own the estate
2 CONC s. The battle went in Hugh's favor for a period of time, when copies of Mohun's will were handed over to him. The crown gave him full seisin over the properties, and the legal battle quieted down for a while. However, the legal ba
2 CONC ttle had to be resolved, and in May 1405 Parliament intervened. A year passed with no clear victor, and the House of Commons of England stepped in, this time on Luttrell's side. The debating went on for two more years, and in 1408
2 CONC  Luttrell prevailed, paying 100 marks for his succession to the barony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through 1406–1414, little was known of Hugh's activities. It is known that he was present at Glastonbury Abbey in 1408, with scores of other influential noblemen. It was then and there that Archbishop Thomas Arundel made a visit t
2 CONC o them, and discussed important political and religious matters with the noblemen. In 1410, the Queen of England, Joan of Navarre, made Luttrell her steward, a task which he enjoyed.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Henry V of England ascended to the throne in 1415, Sir Hugh's popularity increased. He traveled to France with the English army, to re-incite the 100 Years' War. He proved to be a daring leader, and was one of the key command
2 CONC ers at the Siege of Harfleur, and at the Siege of Rouen. After the siege, he returned home to England, and in 1417, he entered the service of Henry V, becoming one of his most loyal retainers. Throughout the rest of his life, he w
2 CONC ould have a splendid relationship with the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT In February 1417, he was appointed Lieutenant of Harfleur and embarked to France, where the war was still being fought, in the retinue of Thomas Montacute, 4th Earl of Salisbury. Luttrell had his own private battalion, consisting 
2 CONC of 20 men-at-arms and 60 archers. After assuming command over the city of Harfleur in 1418, he was given orders by his superiors to manage the English garrison there, and hang any deserters. During that same year, Sir Hugh negotia
2 CONC ted the surrender of the French Captains of Montivilliers and Fécamp. In 1419, he also negotiated the surrender of the town of Montreuil, and many others. In July 1419, Luttrell was appointed the Royal Seneschal of Normandy by Hen
2 CONC ry V, a position he held for two years (1419–1421). Sir Hugh thus became the ruler of one of the most important places in medieval France. Luttrell was thrilled with governing his new domain, describing himself as the "gret Senesc
2 CONC hal of Normandie." Sir Hugh kept Normandy under the mighty fist of the English, which Henry V of England benefited from greatly. In 1420, Luttrell corresponded with the king many times, praising Henry's marriage to Catherine of Va
2 CONC lois, saying in a letter that it caused him: "the gretest gladnesse and consolation that ever came unto my herte." He also reported to King Henry about the state of the country, saying: "ther ys no steryng of none evyl doers (ther
2 CONC e is no stirring of none evil-doers)", and also informed the king of the poverty of the country. In 1421, he was relieved of his duties as Seneschal of Normandy and Lieutenant of Harfleur, and was ordered to return to England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon arriving in England, Hugh met with many members of English nobility, and royal officials. He was also occupied with many royal assignments, which continued up to his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 24 March 1428, Sir Hugh Luttrell fell gravely ill and died on a visit to one of his daughters, a nun at Shaftesbury. A large funeral procession followed his coffin on the way to Dunster. After he was buried, a monument was buil
2 CONC t in his memory at the Dunster church. At that moment, one of the most influential figures in medieval England, a man who had lived through three reigns, was put to rest.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and progeny
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms of Beaumont of Shirwell: Barry of six vair and gules
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms of Sir Hugh Luttrell (c.1364-1428) (Or, a bend between six martlets sable) impaling arms of Beaumont of Shirwell, Devon, (Barry of six vair and gules, here shown incorrectly as barry of eight), the family of his wife Catherin
2 CONC e Beaumont (d.1435). Detail from 19th century Luttrell heraldic stained-glass windows, south wall of Dunster Church
2 CONT In 1384 Sir Hugh Luttrell married Catherine Beaumont (d.1435),[4] a daughter of Sir John Beaumont (d.1379/80)[5] of Shirwell and Saunton in North Devon, MP for Devon 1376–80,[6] a substantial landowner in Devon, by his second wife
2 CONC  Joan Crawthorne, granddaughter and heiress of Sir Robert Stockey, MP in 1318, of Crawthorne and Cranstone.[7] Alabaster effigies survive in Dunster Church of himself and his wife, badly mutilated.[8] The arms of Beaumont (Barry o
2 CONC f six vair and gules) appear in Dunster Church and on the Luttrell Table Carpet, c.1520, now in the collection of the Burrell Collection in Glasgow, probably made in about 1520 to record the marriage of his descendant Sir Andrew L
2 CONC uttrell (1484–1538) of Dunster and his wife Margaret Wyndham (d.1580). By his wife he had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Luttrell (c.1394-1430), eldest son and heir, of Dunster, who in about 1422 married Margaret Tuchet (d.1438), daughter of John Tuchet, 4th Baron Audley (1371-1408).[9] He was buried probably at Bruton Priory.
2 CONT William Luttrell[citation needed]
2 CONT Margaret Luttrell[citation needed]
2 CONT Elizabeth Luttrell[citation needed]
2 CONT Anne Luttrell[citation needed]
2 CONT Joan Luttrell[citation needed]
2 CONT Famous descendants
2 CONT Sir Hugh Luttrell's famous descendants include:[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Carter I: A wealthy Virginia colonist and slave-owner, one of the richest men in Virginia at his time.
2 CONT Benjamin Harrison V: A famed American revolutionary and founding father of the United States of America.
2 CONT William Henry Harrison: 9th President of the United States, American General during the War of 1812.
2 CONT Benjamin Harrison: 23rd President of the United States.
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Richardson, Douglas; Everingham, Kimball G. (2004). Plantagenet Ancestry: A Study in Colonial And Medieval Families. Genealogical Publishing Com. p. 475. ISBN 9780806317502.
