2 CONT Extract from the Papal Letters cited in Yorkshire Antiquities, Yorkshire Archaeological Society, 1907,p.243) 1354 July 30 Avignon "Confirmation with exemplification at the request of Richard de Baskerville, knt, and Isabella his w
2 CONC ife, of the diocese of Hereford, of the letters issued by Clement VI, 2 Non. Jul., anno 3, ruling in the case of John Earl of Warenne and Joan de Barro, that dispensation of the marriage of persons related in the fourth degree of 
2 CONC kindred shall hold good if they are related in the fourth and third degrees"
2 CONT
2 CONT This may imply that Isabella was a widow when Richard married her, and that an awkwardly close relationship was found between her previous and current husband. (Yes, canon law '''was''' concerned with non-genetic relationships as 
2 CONC well as the obvious genetic ones.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The next question, of course, is: was she a Hampton by birth and a Caveley/Paveley by marriage, or vice versa?
2 CONT
2 CONT ==========================================================
2 CONT
2 CONT *''''''A genealogical and heraldic history of the commoners of Great Britain and ... By John Burke
