2 CONT Sir John Blaket
2 CONT Born about 1380 in Icomb, Worcestershire, England
2 CONT Son of [father unknown] and [mother unknown]
2 CONT [sibling(s) unknown]
2 CONT Husband of Margaret (Hastings) Blaket — married May 1401 in England
2 CONT Husband of Margaret (Beaupyne) Blaket — married 1409 in Englandap
2 CONT Husband of Elizabeth (Trillowe) Conyers — married 1422 [location unknown]
2 CONT DESCENDANTS 
2 CONT Father of Anne (Blackett) Baskerville
2 CONT Died 24 Jun 1430 at about age 50 in Icomb, Worcestershire, England
2 CONT Profile manager: Steve Hunt
2 CONT Profile last modified 7 Jul 2022 
2 CONT Created 6 Mar 2016
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Sir John Blaket of Icomb, Worcestershire (now in Gloucestershire), may have been a descendant of Roger Blaket and his wife Margaret who were holding half a knights fee in Icomb in 1352.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT His year of birth is unknown and is estimated as c 1380.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and Children
2 CONT He married three widows:
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Hastings (d. 1407; daughter of Sir Ralph Hastings; widow of Sir Roger Heron; her monument is at Nosely, Leicestershire)[1][2]
2 CONT Margaret Beaupyne (d. 1420; daughter of Thomas Beaupyne; widow of William Wroughton alias Worfton)[2]
2 CONT Elizabeth (d. 1445; daughter of Sir John Trillowe; widow of William Wilcotes)[1][2]
2 CONT Royce[1] suggested that Sir John Blaket's second wife was a Margaret d'Eynsford, daughter of Sir John d'Eynsford, but he appears to be mistaken and his second wife was Margaret Beaupyne.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Edmund Blaket (b. c 1411, d. 1444)
2 CONT Anne Blaket
2 CONT They are presumed to be by his second wife Margaret Beaupyne.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT Sir John Blackett built the manor-house at Icomb, 'Gloucestershire', about 1400.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Inquisition Post Mortem of Elizabeth, widow of John, Earl of Kent, in 1411 it was recorded that John Blaket held half a knights fee at Iccomb.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is presumed to be the John Blaket who was with Henry V in France, some say he fought at Agincourt, others that he may have been part of the garrison at Honfleur.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Knighted between May and October 1415.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT 24 Jan, 2 Henry VI (1423/1424) Richard Hastings, second son of Sir Ralph Hastings, granted the manor of Kirkby, Leicestershire, and two parts of Wiston and Brankeston, Leicestershire, and of Welford, North Hants, and Drakenage, Wa
2 CONC rwickshire, to hold to Sir John Blaket, for life.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Before his death he and his third wife Elizabeth were plaintiffs with others in a case concerning the wardship of Robert, son and heir of Thomas Harcourt, knt.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death his widow, Elizabeth, brought a case concerning the late William Caddysby.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Manor of North Leigh, Oxfordshire:[7] "Sir William's [Wilcote] widow Elizabeth married Sir John Blacket (d. 1430) of Icomb (Glos.), but retained it at her death in 1445. Elizabeth's heirs were the descendants of her first marriage
2 CONC , viz. her surviving daughter Isabel, wife of John Barton, and her grandchildren William Wykeham, Elizabeth and Philippa Bishopsden, Richard Beaufeu, and Thomas Conyers."
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth, widow of John Blaket, granted lands to William Mortymer and William Brayles, chaplains of a chantry in the parish church of North Leigh, Oxfordshire.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms
2 CONT Blacket of Icomb: 'Azure a bend between six cross crosslet fitchy Or'.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT He died on the day of St John the Baptist (24 Jun) 1430.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Will 1430
2 CONT His will dated Wednesday in the week of Easter 1430 requested his burial at "the Chapel of the Blessed Virgin in the church at Icomb". His Executors were his wife Elizabeth, his son Edmund, and John Barton Junior Esq.[1][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Inquisition Post Mortems
2 CONT Inquisition Post Mortem of Margaret, wife of John Blaket, Knight, 1420: She died on 8 Aug. last. Margaret married John Blaket , who survives, and they had a son Edmund. John Worston, son of Margaret and William Worston, formerly h
2 CONC er husband, is her son and next heir, aged 19 years and more.[9][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Inquisition Post Mortem of John Blaket, Chevalier, 1430: Writ 18 Oct 1430. Inquisition 09 Nov 1430. He held no lands or tenements of the king in chief or any other in demesne or service. He died on 24 June last. Edmund Blaket, his
2 CONC  son and next heir, is aged 18 and more.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Inquisition Post Mortem of Elizabeth Blaket, 1445: She died on 20 October last. The next heirs of William Willecotez and Elizabeth are [1.] William Wykeham , son and heir of Elizabeth, daughter and one of the heirs of William and 
2 CONC Elizabeth; [2.] Elizabeth, wife of Thomas Palmer , one of the daughters and heirs of Philippa, daughter and one of the heirs of William and Elizabeth; [3.] Philippa, wife of William Catesby , another daughter and heir of the above
2 CONC  Philippa; [4.] Richard Beaufo , son and heir of Margaret, daughter and one of the heirs of William and Elizabeth; [5.] Isabel Barton , daughter and one of the heirs of William and Elizabeth; and [6.] Thomas Conyers, son and heir 
2 CONC of Anne, daughter and one of the heirs of William and Elizabeth. He is aged 17 and more. The others were aged 24 and more on 29 September last.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT Royce D. Icomb Place. Transactions of the Bristol and Gloucestershire Archaeological Society. Volume 7, 1882-1883, pp172-190 see pp 178-180 pdf.
2 CONT ? 2.0 2.1 2.2 2.3 2.4 2.5 2.6 2.7 Blaket, John (d.1430), of Icomb, Glos. and Noseley, Leics. Published in The History of Parliament: the House of Commons 1386-1421, ed. J.S. Roskell, L. Clark, C. Rawcliffe., 1993 HOP.
2 CONT ? 3.0 3.1 3.2 3.3 Melville, Henry, The Ancestry of John Whitney. New York: De Vinne Press, 1896. Accessed online at Ancestry.com, p.118.
2 CONT ? J. L. Kirby, 'Inquisitions Post Mortem, Henry IV, Entries 861-899', in Calendar of Inquisitions Post Mortem: Volume 19, Henry IV (London, 1992), pp. 306-322. BHO IPM Elizabeth, widow of John, Earl of Kent. Item 861.
2 CONT ? Blaket v Straneley. 1426-1432. C 1/7/113. Plaintiffs: John Blaket, knt. and Elizabeth his wife, John Barton, the younger, William Andrewe, clerk, and Elizabeth, late the wife of John Wilcotes, knt., executors of the said John Wi
2 CONC lcotes. Defendants: Robert Straneley, knt. Joan his wife, and John Potter. Subject: Wardship of Robert, son and heir of Thomas Harcourt, knt. National Archives.
2 CONT ? Blaket v Aunger. Plaintiffs: Elizabeth, late the wife of John Blaket, knt. Defendants: William Aunger and Joan, wife of Henry Renney, executors of William Caddysby, clerk. Subject: Bond. 1432-1443. C 1/9/459. National Archives.
2 CONT ? A P Baggs, W J Blair, Eleanor Chance, Christina Colvin, Janet Cooper, C J Day, Nesta Selwyn and S C Townley, 'North Leigh: Manor and other estates', in A History of the County of Oxford: Volume 12, Wootton Hundred (South) Includ
2 CONC ing Woodstock, ed. Alan Crossley and C R Elrington (London, 1990), pp. 219-224. BHO.
2 CONT ? Elizabeth late the wife of John Blaket, knight, to grant messuages and land in North Leigh, Wilcote, Tilgarsle, Eynsham, and Wood-perry [in Stanton St. John] to William Mortymer and William Brayles, chaplains of a chantry in the
2 CONC  parish church of North Leigh, and their successors. Oxford. 23 HENRY VI. [1444-1445]. C 143/450/3. National Archives.
2 CONT ? Inquisition Post Mortem of Margaret, wife of John Blaket, Knight, 1420. E-CIPM 21-438. Mapping the Medieval Countryside [1].
2 CONT ? Inquisition Post Mortem of Margaret, wife of John Blaket, Knight, 1420. E-CIPM 21-436. Mapping the Medieval Countryside [2].
2 CONT ? Inquisition Post Mortem of John Blaket, Chevalier, 1430. E-CIPM 23-429. Mapping the Medieval Countryside [3].
2 CONT ? Inquisition Post Mortem of Elizabeth Blaket, 1445. E-CIPM 26-403. Mapping the Medieval Countryside [4].
2 CONT See also:
2 CONT
2 CONT Release by Roger Poure, esquire, to Robert Conyers, knight, and Elizabeth, late the wife of John Blaket, knight, of all his right in the manors and advowsons of Takle and Seukeworth, and in all the lands and tenements they had in 
2 CONC Takle, Seukeworth, and Wyghthull, and in 5 acres land and 5 acres meadow called 'le Wylde' or 'Wytheley,' in Seukeworth aforesaid. Endorsed: Memorandum of enrolment before the king at Westminster, in Trinity Term, 19 Henry VI. Ber
2 CONC kshire and Oxfordshire. 7 July, 16 Henry VI. E 40/7544. The National Archives, Kew Discovery.
2 CONT Final Concord. Description: (ii) John Blaket and Margaret his wife, querents. (i) Thomas Weston, priest, deforciant. The manors of Noseley, Gilmorton, Newton Harcourt and Humberstone, and lands in Saddington, Laughton, Scraptoft a
2 CONC nd Ilston, to (ii) and their heirs, then to Isabella daughter of Margaret and her heirs then to Margaret's other daughters. Date: Morrow of All Souls, 4 Henry IV [3rd November 1403]. DG21/24. Held by: Leicestershire, Leicester and
2 CONC  Rutland, Record Office Discovery.
2 CONT https://www.theblacketts.com/node/82
2 CONT https://www.medievalsoldier.org/about/agincourt-600/the-english-army-in-1415/english-army-table/
2 CONT band of brothers pdf
2 CONT https://groups.google.com/g/soc.genealogy.medieval/c/JJXR8_DtLow
2 CONT Find a Grave, database and images (https://www.findagrave.com : accessed 30 September 2020), memorial page for Sir John Blaket (unknown–24 Jun 1430), Find a Grave Memorial no. 117405364, citing St. Mary the Virgin Churchyard, Icom
2 CONC b, Cotswold District, Gloucestershire, England.
2 CONT John Blackett in "Our Royal, Titled, Noble, and Commoner Ancestors & Cousins," (website, compiled by Marlyn Lewis, Portland, OR), accessed 17 Nov 2018: Norr p.85; Rixford 11; Richardson, PA p. 500; MCA 2d ed. Vol. III p. 146; RA V
2 CONC ol. IV p. 87. Born c.1381.
