2 CONT Sir Richard Berkeley (1531 – 1604) of Stoke Gifford, Gloucestershire was MP for Gloucestershire in 1604. He had previously served as Sheriff of Gloucestershire in 1564, and as Deputy Lieutenant of Gloucestershire. He was knighted 
2 CONC by Queen Elizabeth I in 1568.[1] In 1595 he was appointed Lieutenant of the Tower of London. In 1599 he was appointed custodian of Robert Devereux, 2nd Earl of Essex (d. 1601), who was kept under house arrest at Essex House[2] in 
2 CONC London. He died in 1604, whilst serving as MP, and was buried in The Gaunts Chapel, Bristol, where exists an effigy of him, which chapel had been founded in 1220 by Maurice de Gaunt (d. 1230), a member of the Berkeley family, and 
2 CONC which stands opposite St Augustine's Abbey (after 1542 Bristol Cathedral), founded by a member of the Berkeley family of nearby Berkeley Castle.
2 CONT Background
2 CONT He was born in 1531, the eldest son of Sir John Berkeley (d. 1546) of Stoke Gifford, Gloucestershire by Isabel Denys, a daughter of Sir William Denys (d. 1535) of Dyrham, Gloucestershire, by Anne Berkeley, daughter of Maurice Berk
2 CONC eley, de jure 3rd Baron Berkeley (1436–1506). As well as his mother's descent from the Barons Berkeley he was on the paternal side 7th in descent from Maurice de Berkeley (d. 1347, killed at the Siege of Calais), who had acquired 
2 CONC the manor of Stoke Gifford in 1337, the second son of Maurice de Berkeley, 2nd Baron Berkeley (1271–1326).
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT Marriage and progeny
2 CONT Berkeley married twice:
2 CONT Firstly to Elizabeth Read, '''daughter of William Read of Milton(or Mitton[8]), Esq.''', by whom he had issue:
2 CONT *Henry Berkeley (died 1606), of Stoke Gifford, who married Mary (or Mirryell) Throckmorton, daughter of Thomas Throckmorton of Coughton Court, Warwickshire. He was father of Richard Berkeley (1579–1661), MP.
2 CONT *Elizabeth, (or Ellen[9]) who married, as his first wife, Sir Thomas Throckmorton of Tortworth, Gloucestershire. She was mother of:
2 CONT **John Throckmorton(died young, c.1591,[10] not to be confused with John Throckmorton (1572–1623) MP for Gloucestershire 1601 and 1604, on the latter occasion after winning a by-election for the seat vacated by the death of Richar
2 CONC d Berkeley in the same year.)
2 CONT **Sir William Throckmorton, 1st Baronet (c.1579–1628), of Tortworth.
2 CONT *Mary
2 CONT *Anne who married Hugh Lygon (d. 1599 at Hanley Castle, Worcestershire)
2 CONT *Dorothy
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Berkeley_(died_1604)
2 CONT _______
2 CONT BERKELEY, Sir Richard (1531-1604), of Stoke Gifford, Glos.
2 CONT Family and Education
2 CONT b. 18 Apr. 1531, o.s. of Sir John Berkeley of Stoke Gifford and Isabel, da. of Sir William Denys of Dyrham, Glos. m. (1) by 1559, Elizabeth, '''da. of William Rede† of Mitton, Worcs. and Tewkesbury, Glos.''', 2s. (1 d.v.p.) 5da. (
2 CONC 1 d.v.p.); (2) by 1593, Eleanor (d. 17 Mar. 1629), da. of Robert Jermy of Antingham, Norf. and wid. of Robert Rowe (d.1587), Haberdasher, of London, s.p. suc. fa. 1545;1 kntd. 21 Aug. 1574.2 d. 26 Apr. 1604.3
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1604-1629/member/berkeley-sir-richard-1531-1604 
2 CONT ________
2 CONT
2 CONT His (Sylvester Danvers) second wife was Elizabeth, daughter of William Rede, of Milton, by whom he had a son, Giles. 
