2 DATE 1550
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173426885976 (October 18, 2022; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Although it is claimed that a now missing memorial inscription claims that John Pye was truly 106, it is still unlikely to be true. His birthdate is earlier than the birthdate of the FATHER of his first wife Anne de la Bere. She i
2 CONC s most unlikely to have been born before 1467-9, and therefore unlikely to have married until 1481 at the very earliest & more likely the late 1480's or later when John Pye would be in his 40's, which is very late to marry in this
2 CONC  era. More than likely a decade or two had been added on..It is possible that his exploits in the holy lands delayed his marriages. Some of his illegitimate children may have been fathered while he was on crusade. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The inscription that is still in the church gives a date of death as 1547 [rather than the 1550 claimed by Burkes]
2 CONT
2 CONT A welsh book of genealogy compiled only 50 years after his death refers to the inscription but not the venerable age [which seems to be claimed firstly by Burkes Landed Gentry which is often incorrect and was published many centur
2 CONC ies after his death]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the “bible” of Welsh genealogy, the “Llyfr Baglan”, or “Book of Baglan”, compiled between the years 1600 and 1607 by an historian called John Williams and which lists the pedigrees of the most famous Welsh families, there are m
2 CONC any references to the Pyes.  On the subject of this John Pye Llyfr Baglan gives the following quotation:
2 CONT “MYNDE John Pye of the Mynde, sone of John Pye, seconde sone of John Pye of Saddlebow, esq., maried 3 wiefes; his first wief was Anne, da.to Sir Richard Delabyre, knight; his seconde wief was Anne Brigees; and his third wief was a
2 CONC lrothes, da.to Sir Sir Robert Whitnney, Lord of Whitney.  Hee had bye theme 42 children; And hee had bye Concupines 22 Childrene; Soe that in all hee had 64 Childrene, as hitt appeareth upon his Towmbe in the Churche of muchdewchu
2 CONC rche. The said John Pye was ate Rome, at Jerusalem, and at the sepulcher of Christ. Hee did the 24 actes of Chilvalrye w’ch fewe men could do them all.” On the margin of an ancient monument in the church at Much Dewchurch is the f
2 CONC ollowing legend is cut in clear Roman characters’: “Johannes Pye de Mynde, Arm. obiit 1547. Walterus Pye de Mynde, Arm.  obiit2 Nov 1575.”
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://wiki.whitneygen.org/wrg/index.php/Archive:Welsh_Genealogies Whitney Research Group Archive:Welsh Genealogies]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John II (O the Mende) Pye
2 CONT
2 CONT 1460–1486
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth 1460 • England
2 CONT
2 CONT Death 1486 • England
2 CONT
2 CONT John II (O the Mende) Pye was born in 1460 in England. He had one daughter with Lady Elizabeth Whitney in 1487. He died as a young father in 1486 in his hometown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth
2 CONT
2 CONT John II (O the Mende) Pye was born in 1460 in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT England
2 CONT
2 CONT 1460 • England
2 CONT
2 CONT 1486
2 CONT
2 CONT Age 26
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT John II (O the Mende) Pye died in 1486 in England when he was 26 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT England
2 CONT
2 CONT 1486 • England
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT  John Pye of The Mynde 1444-1550  http://thepyeplate.blogspot.com/2013/08/john-pye-of-mynde-1444-1550.html I have had great hesitation in writing about this John (there were many by this name over the centuries), simply because he
2 CONC  presented such a monumental task of sifting through documented facts and speculation. It is documented that he did live from 1444-1550, to the tune of 106 years. This would be a remarkable feat today, but in the 14th and 15th cen
2 CONC turies, it would have been almost incomprehensible. He is buried in Much Dewchurch where an epitaph stated his age, that he had three wives, 42 children and another 26 by ‘other’ women. During the Victorian era, this openly visibl
2 CONC e account of a less than virtuous man was considered offensive to many so it was removed from sight. It would also seem as though many other pieces of information about him were also removed. I can find no indication of what he di
2 CONC d for a living, how he supported all those children and how he traveled to distant places and managed to procreate in a manner more associated with rabbits.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are papers concerning him but they are in the National Archives in London or in the towns where he and his lands were associated. The papers are too delicate for copying or microfilming so it means a hands on approach. Unfor
2 CONC tunately, a trip to London is not in my budget.
2 CONT
2 CONT I’ll proceed with what information there is and state – up front – the caveat is that not much that is known is documented.
2 CONT
2 CONT This John Pye was the son of John Tregos Pye (Tregos is the name of a place) and his wife Agnes Andrews. Agnes was the daughter of Roger Andrews ap Ithell, AKA, Roger ap Ithell ap William ap Andrews of Brown's Place, which became 
2 CONC the Mynde. It is believed this is how The Mynde came into the possession of the Pye family. Roger’s wife is unknown. John Tregos  and Agnes had at least 7 children, but so far I’ve only discovered one son, John, the subject of thi
2 CONC s blog.
2 CONT
2 CONT John married three times. According to birth and death dates of the three women, it would seem likely that Ann de la Bere was his first wife. Ann was the daughter of Sir Richard de la Bere. This family was from Cotentin, in Norman
2 CONC dy, and were followers of William the Conqueror. One branch of the family settled in Gloucestershire, while Sir Richard’s branch were associated with Kinnersley  Castle in Herefordshire. Many of this family served as Sheriff’s of 
2 CONC the county from Edward III on. Richard was married twice. His first wife Anne (Touchet) Audley, daughter of James Touchet, Lord Audley is the one concerned with here. Richard and Anne had a daughter, Ann, who became the first wife
2 CONC  of John Pye of The Mynde.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Touchet, Lord Audley was a distinguished veteran of the One Hundred Year’s Wars and the opening phases of the War of the Roses. He was also quite well connected. His first wife Margaret Roos was a descendant of the FitzAlan/
2 CONC Arundel family. His 2nd wife was Eleanor de Holland, an illegitimate daughter of Edmund de Holland, 4th Earl of Kent by Constance (Plantagenet) of York, daughter of Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York and Infanta Isabella of Casti
2 CONC le. James and Eleanor were the parents of Anne (Touchet) Audley, who married Sir Richard de la Bere. Edmund de Holland maternal and paternal grandmothers were Plantagenets. His mother was of the FitzAlan/Arundell family. Eleanor h
2 CONC erself was a gt. granddaughter of King Edward III of England. Anne de la Bere was a 3x gt. granddaughter of King Edward III of England, in an illegitimate line of descent..
2 CONT
2 CONT So John Pye of The Mynde married well when Ann de la Bere became his wife.  
2 CONT
2 CONT His 2nd wife was Anne Brydges.  The Brydges family was quite prominent in Herefordshire and Gloucestershire in the 1400-1500’s.  I’ve searched about everything I  can find and have only found one Anne Brydges who fits the time lin
2 CONC e of John Pye. This Anne’s father was Rowland Brugges (one of the ways this name was spelled) and her mother was Margaret Kelom. However, no marriage information is given for her so it cannot be said she is the 2nd wife of John Py
2 CONC e. Every other Anne Brydges (and variant spellings) I could find either did not fit the time line or was married to someone else. So the 2nd wife is inconclusive.
2 CONT                 The Church of St. David, Much Dewchurch, Herefordhire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT His third wife was Elizabeth Whitney, the daughter of Sir Robert Whitney and his wife Alice Vaughn. Alice was the daughter of Thomas Vaughn and Ellen Gethyn. The Vaughns were a notable family in Wales.  Earlier ancestors of the Wh
2 CONC itneys had been followers of William the Conqueror. They received lands in the Marches of Wales and took the name De Whitney of the Wye. The estate included about 2,000 acres and remained in the family until 1893. Sadly, for Eliza
2 CONC beth, her father, Robert, was attainted in 1459 as a Yorkist, for this was the time of The War of the Roses.  The Vaughn family has roots in Wales going back to the 10th century. They were mentioned in the Domesday Book and severa
2 CONC l other early records of note. They remained in the Marches of Wales, marrying into many of the families  considered to be Marcher Lords.
2 CONT
2 CONT The engraving on John Pye’s tomb that was removed from public view I given here.  http://www.theroyfamily.com/p44969.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT 1550 Here lyeth the Bodyof John Pye of Minde a travayler in far countryes, his life ended He left behind him Walter, his son, heire of Minde a hundred and six yeares he was truly and had sons and daughters two and fourty!
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pyeof mynde sone of Jon. pye, seconde sone of Jon. pye of Sadlebowe, esq married 3 wiefes his first wief was Anne, da. to Sr. Richard Delabyre, knight his second wief was Anne Brigees and his third wief was alrothes, da. to S
2 CONC r. Robert Whitney, lord of Whitney Hee had bye theme 42 children And hee had by Concubines 22 Children1
2 CONT
2 CONT  It is sad to say that with all these children, there are only names for about a half dozen of them. My belief is that most of them moved to other places in England. If you are searching for your Pye ancestors and have hit a brick
2 CONC  wall, give a thought to the possibility that your ancestor was a child of John Pye of The Mynde.
2 CONT
2 CONT Herefordshire biographies
2 CONT  By John Hutchinson
2 CONT
