1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sir John Pakington (c.1477 – 21 August 1551), was Chirographer of the Court of Common Pleas, a Member of Parliament for Gloucester, and Sheriff of Herefordshire and Worcestershire. In 1529 he received an extraor
2 CONC dinary grant from Henry VIII permitting him to wear his hat in the King's presence.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Although the Pakington family is of great antiquity, being recorded at the time of the foundation of St Mary's Abbey, Kenilworth, in the reign of Henry I, according to Burke the 'founder of the fortunes of the house of Pakingto
2 CONC n' was the lawyer Sir John Pakington in the reign of Henry VIII.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Born about 1477,[2] he was the eldest son of John Pakington of Stanford-on-Teme, Worcestershire, and Elizabeth Washbourne, the daughter and heiress of Thomas Washbourne.[3][4][5] He had three brothers, Humphrey, Robert and Augu
2 CONC stine, and three sisters: Joyce married firstly a husband surnamed Blount, and secondly John Corbet of Leigh, Shropshire;[6] Eleanor married a husband surnamed Gravener, of Shropshire; and Margery married a husband surnamed Neve.[
2 CONC 7]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Pakington began his legal career in about 1498 as an attorney in the Court of Common Pleas.[2] He entered the Inner Temple shortly before 1505, and in 1507 was acting as counsel in the Court of Requests.[2] He may have secured 
2 CONC patronage at court, which could account for his appointment in 1508 as Chirographer of the Court of Common Pleas,[a] an office in the gift of the Crown.[2] In 1512 he was appointed solicitor for the Mercers' Company.[2] In the fol
2 CONC lowing year he was a Justice of the Peace in Gloucestershire, and in 1515 was elected to represent Gloucester in Parliament.[8] He became a Bencher of the Inner Temple in 1517,[2] was Lent Reader in 1520 and again in 1528.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Pakington's place at the Common Pleas was re-granted to himself and his brother Austin (or Augustine) on 12 October 1525.[8] On 5 April 1529 he had an extraordinary grant by letters patent from Henry VIII permitting him:
2 CONT
2 CONT *''' to wear his hat in [the King's] presence, and his successors, or of any other persons whatsoever, and not to be uncovered on any occasion or cause whatsoever against his will and good liking; also that he shall not be appoint
2 CONC ed, called or compelled to take the order of knighthood, or degree, state or order of a Baron of the Exchequer, Serjeant-at-law, or any office or encumbrance thereto relating.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1531 he was offered the position of serjeant-at-law, and declined it.[2][11]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Pakington's name appears many times in the records of the Inner Temple, and at a Parliament on 5 February 1534, in recognition of his contributions, the new Inner Temple chambers which had been built between the library and Bar
2 CONC rington's Rents were named Pakington's Rents.[2][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1532 he was made a justice of North Wales, and was recorder of Worcester by 1539, and a judge on the Brecon circuit in Wales in 1541.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1535 he was appointed a commissioner to compound for all forfeitures, fines and other sums due to Henry VIII or his father, Henry VII.[10] In 1538 he served as Sheriff of Herefordshire, and in 1540 as Sheriff of Worcestershi
2 CONC re.[8] In 1539 he was Knight of the Shire (MP) for Worcestershire, and on 31 August 1540 made Custos Rotulorum for Worcestershire. On 29 September 1540 he was commissioner to inquire what jewels had been embezzled from the shrine 
2 CONC of St. David's. For the rest of his life he worked in Wales, but lived chiefly at Hampton Lovett in Worcestershire.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry VIII enriched Pakington with many grants, and knighted him in 1545.[2][13] He was from time to time in the commission of the peace for various counties. Under Edward VI he was nominated a member of the Council for the Wel
2 CONC sh Marches in 1551.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Pakington is said to have owned thirty-one manors at the time of his death.[14] Henry VIII had given him Westwood, Worcestershire, and other estates, and he had trafficked in abbey lands to some extent,[15] but the account must
2 CONC  have been exaggerated. In the subsidy roll, in which the valuations were always unduly low, he was rated at no more than £50 a year.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Pakington made his last will on 16 August 1551, and died five days later.[3] He was buried at Hampton Lovett in the chapel of St Anne in the parish church.[3][8] As he had no male issue, he was succeeded by Sir Thomas Pakington
2 CONC  (died 2 June 1571), the eldest son of his brother, Robert Pakington (died 1536).[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Pakington married, by 1530,[3] Anne (d. 22 August 1563),[16] the daughter of Henry Dacres of Mayfield, Staffordshire,[17] Alderman of London in 1526 and Sheriff in 1528,[18][19] a descendant of 'the ancient family of the Dacres
2 CONC  in Westmorland',[20] by his first wife, Elizabeth (d. 26 April 1530).[17] She had a brother, Robert Dacres (d. 20 November 1543), Privy Councillor and Master of Requests to Henry VIII, who married Elizabeth Monoux (widow of Sir T
2 CONC homas Denny (d.1527), elder brother of Sir Anthony Denny, and daughter of Sir George Monoux (d.1544), Lord Mayor of London),[21][22] and two sisters, Eleanor Dacres, who married George Rolle (d.1552); and Alice Dacres, who married
2 CONC  Robert Cheeseman (d. 24 July 1547).[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''At the time of the marriage, Anne was the widow of Robert Fairthwaite (d.1521),[2] citizen and Merchant Taylor of London, by whom she had a daughter, Elizabeth Fairthwaite, who married Nicholas Tichborne of Roydon, Essex, as we
2 CONC ll as two sons, Martin Fairthwaite and Geoffrey Fairthwaite, who died without issue.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''By his wife Anne, Pakington is said to have had two daughters, Ursula (d.1558),[24] who married William Scudamore (d.1560),[24] by whom she was the mother of Sir John Scudamore (1542–1623),[24][25] and Bridget, who married Sir 
2 CONC John Littleton of Frankley, Worcestershire,[3] and after his death three other husbands.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''There is a memorial to Pakington's widow, Anne, in the church of St Botolph, Aldersgate which depicts Anne, a knight in armour, and a daughter. The inscription reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Here under this tombe lyeth ye bodye of Dame Anne Packington, Widdow, late wife Sr John Packington, Kt: late Chirographer in the court of ye comon Please wch. Dame Anne deceassed the 22nd. day of August in the yeare of our Lor
2 CONC d God 1563.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Pakington_(died_1551) __________________________
2 CONT *'''PAKINGTON, John (by 1488-1551), of Hampton Lovett, Worcs. and London.
2 CONT *'''b. by 1488, 1st s. of John Pakington by Elizabeth, da. of Thomas Washborne of Stanford-le-Teme, Worcs.; bro. of Robert. educ. I. Temple. m. by 1530, Anne, da. of Henry Dacres of London, wid. of Robert Fairethwatt of London, at
2 CONC  least 1s. d.v.p. 2da. Kntd. 23 Nov. 1546/9 Feb. 1547.3
2 CONT **Offices Held
2 CONT *Christmas butler, I. Temple 1512, marshal 1515, auditor 1516-17, Lent reader 1520, 1528, treasurer 1528-33, gov. 1536.
2 CONT *Chirographer, c.p. 17 June 1509-d.; j.p. Glos. 1515-d., Mdx. 1524-d., N. Wales, Worcs. 1532-d., Herefs., Staffs. 1537-d., Cheshire, Salop 1539-d., Mon. 1543; commr. subsidy, Gloucester 1515, Mdx. 1523, 1524, chantries, Brec., Car
2 CONC d., Carm., Glam., Rad. 1546, Herefs., Worcs. 1546, 1548, relief, Salop, Worcs. 1550; serjeant-at-law 1 Nov. 1531; member, council in the marches of Wales in 1534; sheriff, Herefs. 1538-9, Worcs. 1540-1; recorder, Worcester by 1539
2 CONC -d.; custos rot. Worcs. 31 Aug. 1540; justice, Brec., Glam. and Rad. 1541-50.4
2 CONT **Biography
2 CONT *'''Whether the John Pakington who obtained the lease of some property near Gloucester in June 1513 was the young lawyer or his father is not certain, but that it was the younger man who was chosen to serve for the town in the Par
2 CONC liament of 1515 is consonant with Pakington’s appointment to the Gloucestershire bench in the same year and his remaining on it until his death. Between the Parliament’s two sessions he and his fellow-Member Thomas Porter nominate
2 CONC d the town’s collectors for the recently granted subsidy. His Membership in 1515 may have marked the beginning of a parliamentary career of which, owing to the gaps in the evidence, we have only one further glimpse. He could have 
2 CONC sat in 1523, a Parliament for which few names of Members survive, have been by-elected to the Parliament of 1529 and thus also have been returned again in 1536, and even have sat again for Worcestershire in 1545 (when the knights 
2 CONC for that shire are unknown). The fact that, when elected in 1539, he was sheriff of the neighbouring county of Hereford may indicate not only that Cromwell wanted him in the House but also that he too was eager to sit there. One o
2 CONC f the measures debated during the third session and enacted as the Statute of Fines (32 Hen. VIII, c.36) proved to concern him as chirographer of common pleas and on 19 May 1540 Bishop Rowland Lee appealed to Cromwell to protect h
2 CONC is interests in that office.5
2 CONT *'''After 20 or more years of regular attendance at his inn and of practice both in London and the country, Pakington was exempted in 1529 from wearing his hat in the King’s presence, taking the order of knighthood and being made 
2 CONC a baron of the Exchequer or a serjeant-at-law. Two of these were, however, to be disregarded.6
2 CONT *'''In 1531 Cromwell acted as counsel in a case against Pakington and his brother Robert. Later that year Cromwell received a fine of £267 from him for an unspecified misdemeanour, which appears to have been the reason behind a te
2 CONC mporary coolness between the two. Pakington’s ability was such, however, that when Christopher Hales recommended on 9 Nov. 1532 that he should be made justice of North Wales, Cromwell acquiesced. Within two years Pakington was als
2 CONC o appointed to the council in the marches of Wales, of which he became an active member. Thereafter the two corresponded frequently on administrative and personal matters, Cromwell several times interceding with the King to compen
2 CONC sate Pakington for his expenses in Wales.7
2 CONT *'''By marriage Pakington was well connected: his wife’s brothers-in-law included Robert Cheseman, George Rolle and Robert Dacres, the Privy Councillor, while his brother’s father-in-law was (Sir) John Baldwin. From 1521, when he 
2 CONC bought the manor of Extons, Pakington regularly purchased land in Worcestershire, where he had been born, until by the time of his death he owned a not insignificant estate. His most important acquisitions were the manor of Hampto
2 CONC n Lovett in 1528 and the lease of Westwood priory in 1539. Pakington died at Hampton Lovett on 21 Aug. 1551, five days after making his will, and was buried in the chapel of St. Anne in the parish church despite his own wish to be
2 CONC  interred in the chancel. He had made provision for his wife and his daughters, Bridget, wife of John Lyttleton, and Ursula, wife of William Scudamore, and left numerous small bequests to kinsmen, friends and charities. Pakington’
2 CONC s sons had predeceased him without issue so that he was succeeded by his nephew Thomas Pakington.8
2 CONT *Ref Volumes: 1509-1558
2 CONT *Author: A. D.K. Hawkyard
2 CONT *Notes
2 CONT *1. E179/279/3, m. 23.
2 CONT *2. E159/319, brev. ret. Mich. r. [1-2].
2 CONT *3. Date of birth estimated from first office. Vis. Worcs. (Harl. Soc. xxvii), 102; LP Hen. VIII, xxi; CPR, 1547-8, pp. 11-12; C142/140/196; PCC 30 Bucke; DNB.
2 CONT *4. LP Hen. VIII, i-v, viii, xi-xv; Cal. I.T. Recs. i. 98; Statutes, iii. 171; CPR, 1549-51, pp. 211-12; 1553, pp. 358-9, 362; A. D. Dyer, Worcester in 16th Cent. 201.
2 CONT *5. LP Hen. VIII, i. xv; E179/279/3, m. 23.
2 CONT *6. LP Hen. VIII, iv.
2 CONT *7. Ibid. v-vii, x, xii, xiv, xv; Strype, Eccles. Memorials, ii(2), 161.
2 CONT *8. VCH Worcs. ii. 151; iii. 7, 16, 25-26, 39, 82, 128, 154, 156-7, 234, 262; iv. 228, 234; C142/93/102; Pevsner, Worcs. 183; PCC 30 Bucke
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1509-1558/member/pakington-john-1488-1551 _______________________
2 CONT *'''A genealogical and heraldic dictionary of the peerage and ..., Volume 42, Part 1
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=u6IaAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA587&lpg=PA587&dq=Dacres+Tychbourne&source=bl&ots=5n3hw1Gqsu&sig=_hzQWqctNvHc5vzuNLHILOEXMT8&hl=en&ei=T_mzTLrRCoz6sAO53LW7CA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=4&ved=0CB0Q6AEwAw
2 CONC #v=onepage&q=Dacres%20Tychbourne&f=false
2 CONT *Pg.587
2 CONT *   ROBERT PAKINGTON, living in the reign of HENRY IV. was father of
2 CONT *   JOHN PAKINGTON, recited in an office found before Robert Russel, escheator of the county of Warwick, 14, HENRY VI. He left a son of his own name,
2 CONT *   JOHN PAKINGTON, Esq., who m. Elizabeth, dau. and heir of Thomas Washbourne, Esq. of Stanford, in the county of Warcester, and had three sons, namely,
2 CONT **'''	I. JOHN, of the Inner Temple, constituted chirographer of the Common Pleas in the 24th of HENRY VIII., during life, and being learned in the law was elected, 11 HENRY VIII., Lent reader; and in the 20th of the same reign, tr
2 CONC easurer of the society of the Inner Temple, in which year he had a grant from the King, "that he, the said John Pakington, for the time to come, shall have full liberty during his life, to wear his hat in his presence and his succ
2 CONC essors, or any other persons whatsoever, and not be uncovered on any occasion or cause whatsoever, against his will and good liking; also that he shall not be appointed, called, or compelled to take the order of knighthood, or deg
2 CONC ree, state, or order of a baron of the Exchequer, sergeant-at-law, or any office or incumbrance thereto relating." In 1532, he was called to be sergeant-at-law, for which the king gave him a special discharge for taking the said d
2 CONC egree, and having been appointed a justice of North Wales, was, in 1535, commissioned to conclude and compound all forfeitures, offences, fines, and sums of money due to the king or his father HENRY VII.  In 1542, he had a patent 
2 CONC for justice of Brecknock, Glamorgan, and Radnor, in South Wales, during life having a grant of all the manors belonging to the monastery of Westwood, in the county of Worcester, and soon after received the honour of knighthood. Th
2 CONC e many other honours conferred upon him are too numerous to be recorded here.  At the time of his death, 1560, he was seised of thirty-one manors, and of other lands that he had purchased of seventy different persons, as appears b
2 CONC y a large book concerning his estate, which was preserved amongst the family testimonials.  Sir John Pakington's wife was Anne, widow of Tychbourne, and of the family of Rolle.(t) She d. 22 Aug. 1563. His fortune was divided betwe
2 CONC en his two younger brothers, Robert and Humphrey, and his daughters,
2 CONT ***		URSULA, m. to Sir John Scudamore, Knt.
2 CONT ***		BRIDGET, m. to Sir John Lyttleton, Knt. of Frankley.
2 CONT **	II. ROBERT.
2 CONT **	III. HUMPHREY, a merchant of London, who had the manor of Chaddesley Corbet settled upon him by his brother, Sir John, and left an only son. ....
2 CONT *The 2nd son,
2 CONT *   ROBERT PAKINGTON, Esq., was one of the members of parliament for the city of London, temp. HENRY VIII., and was barbarously murdered in the street, in 1537. He m. Catherine, dau. and co-heir of Sir John Baldwin, lord chief jus
2 CONC tice of the common pleas, son of William .... etc.
2 CONT **'''	(t) Another authority states, that Sir John Pakington, Knt., m. Anne, dau. of Henry Dacres, late sheriff of London, and widow of Robert Fairthwayte, of London, Merchant-taylor, and that she was buried at St. Buttolph's, 22nd
2 CONC  August, 1563, but does not menton her as widow of Tychbourne; by her will, however she bequeaths to her son, Nicholas Tychbourne and his children, the greater part of her estate, plate, jewels, &c. _________________________
2 CONT *'''Burke's genealogical and heraldic history of the peerage, baronetage and ...
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=RVggAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA1113&lpg=PA1113&dq=John+Pakington+1506&source=bl&ots=2AfD2wqeyH&sig=6Efpjzw00rvuCuXHgpkgvP_0gS0&hl=en&ei=RI6zTMD5NoegsQOvysmaCA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CBIQ6A
2 CONC EwAA#v=onepage&q=John%20Pakington&f=false
2 CONT *Pg.942
2 CONT *''' .... Sir John Pakington's wife was Anne, widow of Tychbourne, and of the family of Rolle. She d. 22 Aug. 1563. His fortune was divided between his newphew, Sir Thomas Pakington, his brother, Humphrey Pakington, and his daught
2 CONC ers,
2 CONT **URSULA, m. Sir John Scudamore, Knt. (OTHER SOURCE LISTS WILLIAM SCUDAMORE)
2 CONT **BRIDGET, m. Sir John Lyttleton, Knt. of Frankley _________________________
2 CONT *'''The House of Commons: 1660 - 1690 ; 1, Introductory survey ..., Volume 8 By Stanley T. Bindoff, John S. Roskell, Lewis Namier, Romney Sedgwick
