2 CONT http://encyclopedia.jrank.org/PAI_PAS/PAKINGTON.html
2 CONT
2 CONT PAKINGTON, the name of a. famous English Worcestershire family, now represented by the barony of Hampton.  Sir John Pakington (d . 156o) was a successful lawyer and a favourite at court, and Henry VIII. enriched him with estates, 
2 CONC including that of Westwood in Worcestershire . His grandnephew and heir, Sir John Pakington (1549—1625), was another prominent courtier, Queen Elizabeth's " lusty Pakington," famous for his magnificence of living . His son John (1
2 CONC 600-1624) was created a baronet in 1620 . His son, Sir John, the second baronet (162o-168o), played an active part on the royalist side in the troubles of the Great Rebellion and the Commonwealth, and was taken prisoner at Worcest
2 CONC er in 1651; Lady Dorothy, his wife (d . 1679), daughter of the lord keeper Thomas Coventry, was famous for her learning, and was long credited with the authorship of The Whole Duty of Man (1658), which has more recently been attri
2 CONC buted to Richard Allestree (q.v.) . Their grandson, Sir John, the 4th baronet (1671—1727) was a pronounced high Tory and was very prominent in  political  life; for long he was regarded as the original of Addison's Sir Roger de Co
2 CONC verley, but the reasons for this supposition are now regarded as inadequate . The baronetcy became extinct with the death of Sir John Pakington, the 8th baronet, in January 183o, but it was revived in 1846 for his maternal nephew 
2 CONC and heir, John Somerset Pakington (1799—1880), whose name was originally Russell . Born on the 20th of February 1799 and educated at Oriel College, Oxford, Pakington had a long career as an active and industrious Conservative poli
2 CONC tician, being member of parliament for  Droitwich from 1837 to 1874 . He was secretary for war and the colonies in 1852; first lord of the admiralty in 1858—1859 and again in 1866—1867; and secretary of state for war in 1867—1868 
2 CONC . In 1874 he was created Baron Hampton, and he died in London on the 9th of April 1880 . 
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1875 until his death Hampton was chief civil service commissioner. In 1906 his grandson Herbert (b . 1883) became 4th baron Hampton . It is interesting to note that in 1529 Henry VIII. granted Sir John Pakington the right of 
2 CONC wearing his hat in the royal presence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT _________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Pakington (d. 1560), was a serjeant-at-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pakington was eldest son of John Pakington, by Elizabeth, daughter and heiress of Thomas Washbourne of Stanford, Worcestershire. He entered the Inner Temple, and was Lent reader in 1520. He must have had influence at court, as on 
2 CONC 21 June 1509 he was made choreographer of the court of common pleas. On 3 June 1513 he had a grant of land in Gloucestershire, and in 1515 was a collector of aids for that county. His place at the common pleas was re-granted to hi
2 CONC mself and Austin Pakington on 12 Oct. 1525, and in 1529 he became treasurer of the Inner Temple. On 5 April 1529 he had an extraordinary grant from the king ― namely, that he might wear his hat in his presence and in the presence 
2 CONC of his successors, 'or of any other persons whatsoever, and not to be uncovered on any occasion or cause whatsoever against his will and good liking,' and that, if made a baron of the exchequer or serjeant-at-law, he should be exe
2 CONC mpt from knighthood. In 1532 he was made serjeant-at-law, and was not knighted. He was heavily fined in 1531 for a misdemeanour in the conduct of his office. In 1535 he was made a justice of North Wales, and a commissioner to conc
2 CONC lude and compound for all fines and debts due to Henry VII. On 31 Aug. 1540 he became custos rotulorum for Worcestershire. On 29 September 1540 he was commissioner to inquire what jewels, &c., had been embezzled from the shrine of
2 CONC  St. David's. For the rest of his life he worked in Wales, where he is spoken of as a judge, but he lived chiefly at Hampton-Lovett in Worcestershire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry VIII enriched Pakington with many grants, and knighted him on his return from Boulogne in 1545. He was from time to time in the commission of the peace for various counties. Under Edward VI he was, in 1551, nominated a membe
2 CONC r of the council for the Welsh marches. He was said to own thirty-one manors at the time of his death. Henry VIII had given him Westwood, Worcestershire, and other estates, and he had trafficked in abbey lands to some extent (see 
2 CONC Dep.-Keeper of Publ. Records, 10th Rep. pt. ii. p.247), but the account must have been exaggerated. In the subsidy roll, in which the valuations were always unduly low, he was rated at no more than 50l. a year. Pakington died in 1
2 CONC 560, and was buried at Hampton-Lovett. He married Anne, seemingly daughter of Henry Dacres, sheriff of London, and widow of Robert Fairthwayte, and perhaps also of one Tychborne. She died in 1563. By her he had two daughters: Ursu
2 CONC la, who married William Scudamore, and Bridget, who married Sir John Lyttleton of Frankley, Worcestershire, and after his death three other husbands.
2 CONT
2 CONT _________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John le Corbet, Sheriff in 1526, m. as his 2nd wife, Jocosa, sister to Sir John Packington, widow of John Lee of Avenbury.
