1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Peter Bartrum, https://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/6380/CROFT_343.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (January 23, 2017; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT *''''''A genealogical and heraldic dictionary of the landed gentry of ..., Volume 3 By John Burke, Sir Bernard Burke
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=9NDTAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA83&lpg=PA83&dq=Hugh+Croft&source=bl&ots=kiIv9n5r9F&sig=NH4j3CCGVOca2jh1qT179fVbxs8&hl=en&ei=-Kx3Te-RHoe-sAOxls3IBA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=2&ved=0CBgQ6AEwATgy#v=onep
2 CONC age&q=Hugh%20Croft&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 83
2 CONT *SIR JOHN DE CROFT,* Knt. of Croft Castle, co. Hereford, Capt. of Merk Castle, near Calais who was frequently employed in negotiations in Flanders, between 1402 and 1404. He m. Janet, dau. and presumed co-heir of Owen ap Griffith 
2 CONC Vychan, Lord of Wlyndowrdwy, in Merionethshire, the renowned OWEN GLENDOWER, representative of the Princess of Powys, (see HUGHES OF GWERCLAS,) and was father of WILLIAM DE CROFT, whose son was
2 CONT ***''''''  SIR RICHARD CROFT, Knt. of Croft Castle, high-sheriff of the co. of Hereford, the captor of Prince Edward, son of Henry VI., at the battle of Tewkesbury, in 1471. This gallant soldier was appointed treasurer of the hous
2 CONC ehold to HENRY VII., and had the high honour of knight-banneret conferred upon him at the battle of Stoke, in 1487. He m. Eleanor, dau. of Sir Edward Cornwall, of Burford, and widow of Sir Hugh Morimer, Knt., and d. 29 July 1 HENR
2 CONC Y VIII., 1609, leaving (with other issue) and elder son and heir,
2 CONT ****  SIR EDWARD CROFT, knt. of Croft Castle, sheriff of Hereford, 20 HENRY VII., and 3, 9, 21, and 25 HENRY VIII., and appointed one of the learned counsel to the Princess Mary, (afterwards Queen.) 17 HENRY VIII. He m. Joyce, dau
2 CONC . and sole heir of Sir Walter Skull, of the Holt, co. Worcester, and was father (with other children) of
2 CONT *****  RICHARD CROFT, Esq., who m. Katherine, dau. of Sir Richard Herbert, Knt. of Montgomery, and was father of 
2 CONT ******  SIR JAMES CROFT, Knt., M.P. for the co. of Herford, lord-deputy of Ireland in 1552, and subsequently privy-councillor and comptroller of the household to Queen ELIZABETH. He m. Alice, dau. of Richard Warncombe, and relict 
2 CONC of William Wygmore, and d. 4 Sept., 32 ELIZ., 1590, and was s. by his eldest son,
2 CONT *******  EDWARD CROFT, Esq. of Croft, M. P. for Leominster in 1571 and 1586, who m. Anne, dau. and heir of Thomas Browne, of Aldborough, co. Norfolk, and d. 29 July, 1601, when he was s. by his elder son.
2 CONT ********  SIR HERBERT CROFT, Knt., M.P. for the co. of Hereford, temp. ELIZABETH and JAMES I.  
2 CONT *___________________
2 CONT *''''''Full text of "The house of Cornewall" 
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/houseofcornewall00live/houseofcornewall00live_djvu.txt
2 CONT *''''''The house of Cornewall (1908)
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/details/houseofcornewall00live
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/houseofcornewall00live#page/n238/mode/1up/search/Edmund
2 CONT *CHART Richard Cornewall, 5th Baron of Burford = Cecilia, d. of Sir John Merbury:
2 CONT *  RICHARD who succeeded Sir Bryan, his elder brother, as fifth Baron of the Cornewall line, was born in 1360, being served forty years of age when his eldest brother died, January 17th, 1400. As has already been stated, in 14 Ric
2 CONC h. II. — the Thursday after S. Martin — Bryan de Cornewall, Dominus de Burford, Knight, granted to hmn and to his sister Elena for their support £10 per annum. 
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/houseofcornewall00live#page/n239/mode/1up/search/Edmund
2 CONT *CHART Pedigree of Walcot:
2 CONT *  He married Cecilia, or according to some, Alice, daughter of Sir John Merbury, of Weobley, Knight, and by her had issue, with Matilda who married John Walcot*, of Walcot, Salop, a son, Edmund Cornewall, and probably a second so
2 CONC n in William Cornewall, who was elected member for the City of Hereford in 1427. Of this latter gentleman nothing is known, and he is not included in any MS. pedigree, but as will appear, the grandson of this Richard, Baron of Bur
2 CONC ford, represented the same constituency and both are given in " Williams' Herefordshire Members "- -a volume which includes the M.P.'s for Leominster, Weobley, Bromyard, as well as for the City and County of Hereford. 
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/houseofcornewall00live#page/n240/mode/1up/search/Edmund
2 CONT *  His marriage must have taken place prior to 1394, as in an escheat dated 3, Hen. V., 20th October, 1415, Thomas Wretton — or as Robinson gives it, Whitton — with other trustees granted to Edmund, son of Richard Cornewayle, and 
2 CONC to Alice his (1st) wife, half the Manor of Norton, Co. Northampton, to hold by said Edmund and Alice in tail ; remainder to said Richard in fee ; while 4, Hen. V., 2nd June, 1416, Ricardus de Cornewalye, Armiger, Dominus de Borefo
2 CONC rd, ratifies the said grant. . . . 
2 CONT *  In 1429 (7 King Hen. VI.) — Thursday next after the Feast of St. Gregory the Pope, Edmund son of Richard Cornewalle, and Elizabeth his (2nd) wife, daughter of Thomas Barre the younger deceased, appointed certain attorneys to re
2 CONC ceive seizin of the Manor of Cornewaile Ever, Bucks. Here in this seal the bordure was not engrailed, being the Cornewall arms with a label of 3 points. Legend S., Edmund Cornewayle, Esquire. 
2 CONT *  Richard Cornewall* died January 10th, 21, Hen. VI. (1443), being as will appear, succeeded by his grandson (vide infra), the elder son of the above Edmund, who had married first Alice, said by some to be daughter of Sir John Me
2 CONC rbury, of Weobley, M.P. for Herefordshire, 1419-1427. This lady, as has been shown, was living in 1417 (Vincents MS. vol. VII., Heralds College) but died in that year without issue. The Merbury family were originally of Lyonshall.
2 CONC  There is a recumbent effigy to John and Agnes Merbury in the north aisle of Weobley Church* — Vide "Weobley and the Timber Houses," by George Marshall in " Memorials of old Herefordshire," and " Robinson's Castles of Herefordshir
2 CONC e." Arms of Merbury : Sa. a cross engrailed between 4 passion-nails arg. 
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/houseofcornewall00live#page/n241/mode/1up/search/Edmund
2 CONT *  The second wife of Edmund Comewall was Elizabeth, daughter of Thomas Barre, or De la Barre, the younger, a family of antiquity whose name survives in Barr's Court, the site of the Hereford Railway Station, and in the recently e
2 CONC xtinct family of De la Barre Bodenham of Rotherwas, one of the great mansions of the shire. The following is an excerpt from the magnificently emblazoned pedigree of De la Barre and Bodenham in the possession of Count Bodenham-Lub
2 CONC ienski, of Bullingham and Rotherwas : — . . . . . 
2 CONT *CHART DE LA BARRE.:
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/houseofcornewall00live#page/n242/mode/1up/search/Edmund
2 CONT *CHART  Sir Richard Pembruge = Petronilla.:
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/houseofcornewall00live#page/n244/mode/1up/search/Edmund
2 CONT *  By Elizabeth De la Barre, his second wife, Edmund had Thomas, Otis, Richard, and ''''''Eleanor''''''. Otis is stated to have been Mayor of Hereford in 1467, but this appears to be uncertain. He was M.P. for that borough in 1459
2 CONC , and is stated to have left by some one unknown a son, Thomas, who married Margaret Clifford, and by her had Lawrence, who by Amy, daughter of Elrond, or Stronder, described as of the North, left William. But this, though given o
2 CONC n the authority of the Visitation, is impossible, inasmuch as Otis, and his brother Richard, both died s.p.l. The actual descent is set forth in the Star Chamber proceedings as follows : —   . . . . . 
2 CONT *  After Edmund's death Otis then became possessed of the one moiety of the said manor, and being thereof seised in fee tail he enfeoffed Sir John Barre, Knight, and others to the performance of his last will, by which he left the
2 CONC  said moiety to his brother Richard Cornewall and his heirs, with remainders to Catherine his wife for term of her life, and to'''''' " Dame Aliano'," late the wife of Sir Hugh Mortymer, Knight, and now wife to Richard Crofte, esq
2 CONC uire, " my sister," and her heirs'''''', and to "Edmonde Comewalle, my neveu, son to Thomas Cornewall myn eldest broder," and his heirs, etc., " so that the soules of the said Edmond fader to us and Elizabeth our moder . . . may b
2 CONC e prayed for  . . .   in witness whereof to theise presente indentures I have put my seale the xv. day of January," 8 Edward IV. [a.d. 1468-9]. 
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/houseofcornewall00live#page/n245/mode/1up/search/Edmund
2 CONT *  Afterwards the said Otis died without issue, when his brother Richard took the profits of the moiety of the manor. And after the said Richard died without heirs of his body lawfully begotten the said Katherine took the profits 
2 CONC for the term of her life. And after the said Katherine died the said moiety came to the aforesaid ''''''Eleanor by virtue of the will aforesaid and she took the profits during her life, and after her death the use of the said moie
2 CONC ty descended to Sir Edward Crofte, knight, now complainant, her son and heir''''''. 
2 CONT *''''''  Eleanor the sister of Thomas, Otis, and Richard, married first, Sir Hugh Mortimer, of Kyre Wyard (a cadet of the house of Richard's Castle, whose monument is in Hartley Church, Worcestershire, and whose brother, Sir John 
2 CONC Mortimer, married the Duchess of Suffolk), and secondly Sir Richard Croft, of Croft Castle, erroneously confused with Sir A. Crofts, of Eldersfield, near Tewkesbury. She was governess to the young Princes in Ludlow Castle, and her
2 CONC  tomb in Croft Church is one of great beauty. The Gentleman's Magazine, Nov., 1808, contains the following paragraph : — "The said Edmund married Elizabeth, the eldest daughter of Thomas Barre, Knight, of Herefordshire, by which E
2 CONC lizabeth the said Edmund had a son named Thomas, and a daughter Elenor. This Thomas married Elizabeth, daughter and heyre to Sir Robert Lintall (sic) Knight, of Hampton Court, in Herefordshire, by his wife Dame Lucy, one of the co
2 CONC usins and heyres unto the last Lord Grey of Codner, and had issue by her Sir Edmund Cornewall. The daughter named Elenor was married unto Sir Hugh Mortimer, of Cwyre Ward (sic), by whom she had one daughter and heyre, who was marr
2 CONC ied unto Sir Thomas Worst {sic), Knight, ancester to the Lord Delaware, now living, who had by her a daughter married to Sir Edward Guildford, Knight, who had issue by her Ann, the most beautiful (sic) lady, wife unto the valiant 
2 CONC Duke of Northumberland, father and mother to the noble Ambrose, Earl of Warwick and the Right excellent Robert, Earl of Leicester, and to the lady Catheryne, wife of Henry, Lord Hastings, Earl of Huntingdon, and to Lady Mary, wife
2 CONC  of Sir Henry Sidney, Knight of the Garter, by whom she had issue. Sir Philip Sidney, Knight, and Sir Robert Sidney, created Earl of Leycester." 
2 CONT *______________________
2 CONT *http://newsgroups.derkeiler.com/Archive/Soc/soc.genealogy.medieval/2005-08/msg00683.html
2 CONT *''''''2B) ELEANOR CORNEWALL, born 1430/5, married 1st, Sir Hugh Mortimer, of Kyre Wyard and Martley, Worcestershire, and Tedstone Wafer, Herefordshire, heir of the Tedstone Wafer Mortimers. He was killed at Wakefield in December 
2 CONC 1460, and was buried in the Church of St. Peter, Martley. By him she had a son and a daughter. She married 2nd, Sir Richard Croft (d. 29 July 1509), of Croft Castle, Herefordshire, sheriff of Herefordshire 1471-72, 1477, 1486, MP 
2 CONC Herefordshire 1477, and had three more sons and five more daughters. The Crofts had occupied Croft Castle since before the Conquest. Eleanor was the governess of Edward IV's sons at Ludlow Castle. She died 23 Dec. 1519, at an adva
2 CONC nced age, and was buried with her second husband in the chapel of Croft Castle (tomb now in St. Michael church, Croft). Issue: 
2 CONT **2B1) Sir JOHN MORTIMER, of Kyre Wyard, etc., born 1450/5, sheriff of Herefordshire 1477-8, 1481-2, steward of Abberley, Worcestershire during Warwick's minority 15 Aug. 1480, Squire of the Body 1481-5, knighted 1485, Knight of t
2 CONC he Body 1485-1504, sheriff of Worcestershire 1485-6, banneret at Stoke 16 June 1487, sheriff of Herefordshire 1493-4, 1501-2, MP Worcestershire 1495. He married, after 1485, Margaret Nevill (c.1466-31 Jan. 1528), third daughter an
2 CONC d co-heiress of John Nevill, Marquess of Montagu (d. 1471) by his wife Isabel Ingaldesthorpe. They had no issue, and Margaret went on to marry Charles Brandon, have it annulled the same year (1507), and then marry in 1522, one Rob
2 CONC ert Downes, in addition to having had an illegitimate daughter. Sir John Mortimer died without issue in October 1504 (writ of d.c.e. 1 Nov. 1504). 
2 CONT **2B2) ELIZABETH MORTIMER, LADY DE LA WARR, born 1455/60, married about 1480, Sir Thomas West, 8th Lord De La Warr (c.1457- 11 Oct. 1525), and died 29 June 1502, having had issue at least one son and four daughters. 
2 CONT **2B3) Sir EDWARD CROFT, of Croft Castle, born about 1465, sheriff of Herefordshire 1505, married Joyce Scull, said to be daughter of Sir Walter Scull, of Holt Castle, Worcestershire (d. about 1472; buried at Holt Castle church), 
2 CONC by his first wife Margaret Beauchamp of Holt (d. 1456; buried at Holt Castle church), but this seems unlikely due to chronology, and she was probably his daughter by his second wife Frances Mulle (d. 1483). Sir Edward died 23 Marc
2 CONC h 1541, having had four sons and seven daughters, and was ancestor of the Crofts of Croft Castle, descendants of whom still occupy the castle today. 
2 CONT **2B4) JOHN CROFT, of Holt, Worcestershire, born 1465/70, married Elizabeth Seymour, daughter of John Seymour of Wolf Hall, Wiltshire (d. 1491), by his wife Elizabeth Darrell, and was ancestor of the Crofts of Holt. 
2 CONT **2B5) ROBERT CROFT, of Kyre Wyard, Worcestershire, married and had a sole daughter and heiress. 
2 CONT **2B6) ANN CROFT, married Sir Thomas Blount (1455- 4 June 1524), of Kinlet, Shropshire, and died 27 Sep. 1549, having had at least five sons and six daughters. 
2 CONT **2B7) ELIZABETH CROFT, married John Whittingham, of Pauntley, Gloucestershire. 
2 CONT **2B8) JOYCE CROFT, married Thomas Mill, of Avenbury, Herefordshire. 
2 CONT **2B9) JANE CROFT, married Sir Edward Darrell (1465/6- 9 Mar. 1530), of Littlecote, Witshire, and died by 1492, having had issue, two sons and two daughters. 
2 CONT **2B10) SYBIL CROFT, married Sir George Herbert (c.1463/5- died after 1504), of St Julians, Monmouthshire, third son of William Herbert, Earl of Pembroke. He was knighted in 1487 at the battle of Stoke. Dame Sybil had two sons and
2 CONC  one daughter. 
2 CONT *____________________
2 CONT *http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p1318.htm#i39603
2 CONT *''''''Eleanor Cornwall1
2 CONT *''''''F, b. circa 1430
2 CONT *Father Sir Edmund (Edward) Cornwall b. c 1391, d. 1436 
2 CONT *Mother Elizabeth Barre b. c 1406 
2 CONT *''''''     Eleanor Cornwall was born circa 1430 at of Burford, Shropshire, England. She married Sir Hugh Mortimer, son of Hugh Mortimer, circa 1458 at of Burford, Shropshire, England.1
2 CONT *''''''Family Sir Hugh Mortimer b. c 1429 
2 CONT *Child 
2 CONT **◦Elizabeth Mortimer+1,2 b. c 1459, d. 29 Jun 1502
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *1.[S11572] The Lineage and Ancestry of H.R.H. Prince Charles, Prince of Wales, by Gerald Paget, Vol. II, p. 460.
2 CONT *2.[S5] Douglas Richardson, Plantagenet Ancestry, p. 403.
2 CONT *_____________________
2 CONT *http://www.thepeerage.com/p331.htm#i3306
2 CONT *''''''Eleanor Cornwall
2 CONT *F, #3306
2 CONT *Last Edited=1 Jun 2008
2 CONT *''''''     Eleanor Cornwall was the daughter of John Cornwall.
2 CONT *''''''Children of Eleanor Cornwall and Hugh Mortimer
2 CONT **1.Elizabeth Mortimer+
2 CONT **2.Sir John Mortimer1
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *1.[S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 1075. Hereinafter cited as Burke's P
2 CONC eerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT *________________
2 CONT *http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/WEST.htm#Thomas WEST (4º B. West of Oakhanger / 9º B. De La Warr)
2 CONT *Thomas WEST (4º B. West of Oakhanger / 9º B. De La Warr)
2 CONT *Born: 1457, Offington, Sussex, England
2 CONT *Died: 10 Oct 1525, Sussex, England
2 CONT *Buried: 11 Oct 1525, Broadwater, Sussex, England
2 CONT *Notes: Knight of the Garter. 18 Jan 1477/78 Knight of the Bath. 1497 one of Chief Commanders at Battle of Blackheath. 1510 Knight of the Garter. 1513 Served in Battle of Spurs. Attended Henry VIII on Field of the Cloth of Gold. T
2 CONC homas seems to have married firstly Eleanor Percy. His subsequent wife Elizabeth Mortimer died 29 Jun 1502, according to his will. As she predeceased her brother Sir John Mortimer (d. 1504), it was actually her son Thomas who was 
2 CONC (presumably) Sir John's heir (and he was later the heir of Elizabeth's mother (Eleanor). Roger Copley, the father of his last wife Eleanor, was probably not a knight. In his will Thomas provides for prayers for the soul of Elizabe
2 CONC th and for the soul of his living wife Eleanor, but makes no mention of any other wife. Favoured Henry VIII's divorce and had large grants of monastic lands.
2 CONT *Father: Richard WEST (3º B. West of Oakhanger / 8º B. De La Warr)
2 CONT *Mother: Catherine HUNGERFORD (B. De La Warr)
2 CONT *Married 2: Elizabeth MORTIMER (B. De La Warr) (d. 29 Jun 1502) (dau. of ''''''Hugh Mortimer of Mortimer's Hall, and Eleanor Cornwall'''''') ABT 1480
2 CONT *_________________
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Known at Ludlow and Wigmore as the Lady Governess of Edward IV's sons, Edward and Richard of York, the so-called 'Princes in the Tower'. 
2 CONT
