1 NOTE {geni:about_me} THOMAS GODDARD of Upham, d., 1597, son of John Goddard (5), 
2 CONT
2 CONT married "Anne, sister of Sir George Gifford, Buckingham; from whom descended 
2 CONT
2 CONT the Goddards of Swindon; and secondly Jane, daughter of John Ernie, of Can- 
2 CONT
2 CONT nings, Wiltz, and Ernie, Sussex; from whom descended the Goddards of Hart- 
2 CONT
2 CONT ham." Apparently, he succeeded his father in the Swindon and Upham estates, 
2 CONT
2 CONT or part of them. "Thomas Goddard bought the manor of Swindon in 1560. He 
2 CONT
2 CONT built the farm-house at Westlecote, Swindon, on a pleasant spot overlooking the 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elcombe valley. The date, 1589, and the initials, T. G. for Thomas Goddard, 
2 CONT
2 CONT still remain on a stone in the building. The walls are very thick; and the 
2 CONT
2 CONT porch and nail-studded door give the structure an air of antiquity. When the 
2 CONT
2 CONT Spanish Armada threatened England, the gentlemen of Wiltshire subscribed 
2 CONT
2 CONT for the defence of the country, and Thomas Goddard, Esq., gave L25 in 1588." 
2 CONT
2 CONT On a tablet in Aldbourne Church it is recorded that. "A. D. 1597, Thomas God- 
2 CONT
2 CONT dard, Esq., of Upham, gave forty shillings for ever to the poor of the parish of 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aldbourne. . . . This forty shillings was a rent-charge on the estate at Upham, 
2 CONT
2 CONT and is still paid by the present occupier, Mr. F. Frampton, and deducted from 
2 CONT
2 CONT the rent of the present owner (H. Stone, Esq.). ... A copy of the will of Thomas 
2 CONT
2 CONT Goddard, Esq., is preserved in the church chest. The will was proved on the 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd June, 1597. He desires his body to be buried in the church at Aldbourne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 'I doe give and bequeathe unto the poore people, now within the parish of 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aldbourne aforesaid, forty shillings a-year for ever' — to be paid half-yearly, the 
2 CONT
2 CONT first instalment on St. Michael's day. 'Item, I doe also give and bequeath unto 
2 CONT
2 CONT the poor people of the parish of Swindon five pounds.' There are bequests 
2 CONT
2 CONT also to Winborow, Liddenton, Ramsbury, Ogbourne, and Marlborough, and to 
2 CONT
2 CONT his men and maid-servants. His lands and tenements he chiefly leaves to 
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Richard Goddard, my son.' " 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Goddard was commemorated by the monument in Aldbourne Church. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "This large monument is placed on the right of the chancel in what was once 
2 CONT
2 CONT the Goddard chapel. It is in the Italian style, and reaches to a great height. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There are two large figures — an esquire and his wife — and four smaller, repre- 
2 CONT
2 CONT senting three sons and one daughter. It is considered to be in memory of 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Goddard, Esq., of Upham, and his wife, Anna sister of Sir George 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gifford. ... At the top of the monument the arms of Goddard, gilt and properly 
2 CONT
2 CONT coloured, remain still; but lower down, where there were apparently several 
2 CONT
2 CONT other shields, the charge is entirely gone, as is also the inscription. On a 
2 CONT
2 CONT bracket over the monument hangs an esquire's helmet in very good condition; 
2 CONT
2 CONT a second bracket is empty at present; but Mr. Witt (church-warden) very well 
2 CONT
2 CONT remembers in his youth a breastplate and a pair of gauntletts suspended there. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the late restoration of the church Mr. Witt caused every search to be made 
2 CONT
2 CONT for these relics, but in vain. Before the restoration this part of the church, 
2 CONT
2 CONT forming the Goddard chapel, was railed off by an ancient wooden carved screen." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Goddard married second, Jane, daughter of John Ernie, of Cannings, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wiltz; and Ernie, Sussex; from whom descended the Goddards of Hartham. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Posted By Walter G. Ashworth
2 CONT
2 CONT This is an abstract of Thomas Goddard's will, which was written 1594 and proven 1598
2 CONT
2 CONT THOMAS GODDARD of Upham, in the county of Wilts. To be buried at Albourne. Poor of
2 CONT Albourne, Wanborough, Swindon, Ramsbury, Ogbourne St George and St Andrew and
2 CONT Marlborough. Son Richard Goddard. Son James Goddard, to whom he leaves a farm called
2 CONT "Blackland." Anne daughter of son James. Thomas eldest son of son Richard. Edward second son of
2 CONT son Richard. Richard youngest son of son Richard. Elizabeth daughter of son Richard. Nephew
2 CONT Thomas Goddard of Standen Hussey. Edmond Goddard. Son Richard Goddard exor. Supervisors:
2 CONT Cousins Thos Hinton and John Goddard of Albourne, son James Goddard, cousin Jno: Gardyner.
2 CONT Witnesses: Thomas Goddard, Thos Hinton, Jno: Goddard, Jno: Wylde, James Goddard. Dated 2
2 CONT June 1594. Proved 12 May 1598 by son Richard.
2 CONT P.CC. 40 Lewyn
2 CONT
2 CONT Submitted by Malcolm L.Goddard, Goddard Association of Europe.
