2 CONT From [http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=g7EXvaDEYioC&pg=PA557&dq=hungerford+family&hl=en&sa=X&ei=Nri3T6r7MIiD8gPltO2dCg&sqi=2&ved=0CFMQ6AEwBg#v=onepage&q=hungerford%20family&f=false Greater Medieval Houses of England and Wales, 1
2 CONC 300-1500: Southern England]  By Anthony Emery
2 CONT
2 CONT "It was not until the death of Sir. Thomas Hungerford's Widow, Joan, in 1412 that their son '''Sir Walter took control of the castle'''. At that time the area immediately in front of the castle was occupied by the parish church an
2 CONC d the hamlet that it served. Lord Milton sweeping away Milton Abbas in the 1780s to improve the landscaping of his mansion had a precedent 2300 years earlier when Lord Hungerford similarly swept away the village houses, gated and 
2 CONC wall-enclosed the area, and took over the parish church as his private chapel and family mausoleum. At the same time he endowed two chantries and built a house for the chaplains immediately to the rear of the church. This work may
2 CONC  be attributed to between 1424 and 1435".
2 CONT
