1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Family and Education b. 1508, 1st s. of John, 1st Baron Mordaunt of Turvey by Elizabeth, da. and coh. of Sir Henry Vere of Great Addington, Northants.; bro. of Edmund. m. (1) settlement 26 Feb. 1526, Ella (d. 2 Jun
2 CONC e 1543), da. and h. of John Fitzlewis ?4s. inc. Lewis† 6da.; (2) lic. 3 Dec. 1545, Joan, da. of Richard Fermor of Easton Neston, Northants., wid. of Robert Wilford (d. Sept. or Oct. 1545) of London. KB ?30 May 1533; suc. fa. as 2n
2 CONC d Baron 18 Aug. 1562.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheriff, Essex and Herts. 1540-1; bailiff, duchy of Cornw., Newport Pound, Essex c.1543; j.p. Essex 1544-47 or later, q. by 1554-64; commr. relief, Essex 1550, musters, Essex, ?Herts. c.1557, 1558, 1563, 1565, heresy 1557; steward
2 CONC , duchy of Lancaster, Olney, Bucks. 1552-8, Hertford and constable, Hertford castle 1554-?58; PC 1553-?58.3
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography The Mordaunts had been prominent in Bedfordshire since the 13th century. Their landed wealth had steadily increased and Sir John Mordaunt’s father, besides acquiring much monastic land, had secured the wardship of the gr
2 CONC anddaughter of Sir Richard Fitzlewis and married her to his son and heir. At least until the 1st Baron Mordaunt died, Sir John Mordaunt seems to have lived most frequently on his first wife’s property at West Horndon, and it was m
2 CONC ainly in Essex that he served as a county official. But his family’s long connexion with Bedfordshire, together with his own Privy Councillorship, enabled him to secure the knighthood of that shire in the first four Parliaments of
2 CONC  Mary’s reign.4
2 CONT
2 CONT As a young man, Mordaunt had been introduced by his father into Henry VIII’s court, created a knight of the Bath at the coronation of Anne Boleyn, and been among the courtiers present at the arrival of Anne of Cleves at Blackheath
2 CONC . During the closing years of Henry VIII’s reign, and increasingly under Edward VI, however, both he and his father forfeited royal favour through their opposition to religious change. Although Mordaunt remained a loyal local offi
2 CONC cial, raising troops for the wars in 1543-4, there is no evidence that he accompanied the King abroad, or attended court functions between 1544 and the death of Edward VI. Privy Council letters to him in 1549, at the time of the P
2 CONC rotector Somerset’s fall, were concerned entirely with local matters, and he managed to avoid entanglement in court faction until the succession crisis of 1553 brought him to the forefront of politics.5
2 CONT
2 CONT Although included in Cecil’s list of gentlemen expected to transact ‘affairs for Queen Jane’, Mordaunt, whose second wife had formerly been in the service of Princess Mary, was one of the first prominent men to join the Marian for
2 CONC ces. This promptitude earned him a place on Queen Mary’s Council, and for some years from 22 Aug. 1553, his first recorded appearance, he attended Council meetings regularly. He was present at Mary’s coronation and at her wedding 
2 CONC to Philip of Spain; at the time of Wyatt’s rising against the marriage he had been one of those appointed to organize the supply of the Tower. He was also active in suppressing opponents of Catholicism—first as a justice of the pe
2 CONC ace, and from 1557 as a heresy commissioner in the diocese of London.6
2 CONT
2 CONT With the increasing age and infirmity of his father, Mordaunt began to take more responsibility in and for Bedfordshire. Besides being repeatedly elected knight of the shire, he performed a variety of local duties, as, for instanc
2 CONC e, when he was one of those appointed in August 1554 to look into an unspecified ‘matter in variance between the inhabitants of the town of Bedford’. After 1555 his name does not appear so often with regard to the central governme
2 CONC nt—he is not known to have attended any Council meeting after September 1556—but he remained a loyal servant of the crown. During the last year of the reign he was prominent in levying Essex men for service at Calais.7
2 CONT
2 CONT The accession of Elizabeth again brought him into disfavour at court. By April 1561 his adherence to Catholicism had caused him to be imprisoned in the Fleet, ‘a prisoner for the mass’, but he probably did not remain there long. A
2 CONC s Baron Mordaunt, he was employed by Elizabeth’s government on a number of local commissions; as late as June 1564 he was still an Essex justice of the peace of the quorum, but by November of that year, when the bishops reported t
2 CONC o the Privy Council on the religious attitudes of justices, his name was omitted. Although this is not conclusive evidence that he had been removed for his Catholicism, since peers were often excluded from the episcopal reports, t
2 CONC here is no known later reference to him as a justice, and the next surviving commission, that of 1569, does not include his name. His religion did not prevent him from attending the Parliament of 1563 as a peer and in 1571 he name
2 CONC d Edward, 3rd Lord Windsor, his proxy.8
2 CONT
2 CONT Mordaunt’s public career had been set against a background of domestic discord. He and his eldest son, Lewis, became estranged by the son’s refusal to marry a daughter of his stepmother. According to the family historian, Lewis ha
2 CONC d at first been willing to marry the girl, whom he may have seduced, but his grandfather, the 1st Baron, forbade the marriage as one of disparagement. This led to a series of complicated manoeuvres. Mordaunt secured a recovery of 
2 CONC the Fitzlewis lands to his own use, and drew up a will depriving Lewis of them if he continued to refuse the marriage, whereupon old Lord Mordaunt made arrangements virtually disinheriting Mordaunt himself in favour of Lewis, whom
2 CONC  he ordered to leave his father’s house and take up residence at Turvey. The Mordaunt estates still lay principally in Bedfordshire but included outlying manors in Buckinghamshire, Dorset, Hertfordshire, Northamptonshire and Somer
2 CONC set. There was also a large London house in St. Sepulchre without Newgate.9
2 CONT
2 CONT After consuming much time and money in these disputes, Mordaunt and his father were officially reconciled, probably as late as 1562. Lewis inherited the Mordaunt estates in 1571, although his father’s final will still appears desi
2 CONC gned to keep his son out of full enjoyment of the Fitzlewis lands for at least ten years after that date. It is probable that Lewis reached an agreement with his stepmother over these, to which, by the will of Sir Richard Fitzlewi
2 CONC s, he was undoubtedly the heir on his father’s death. This took place between 16 Apr., when Mordaunt drew up his last will, and 19 Oct. 1571, when it was proved. He left lands to maintain three scholars at Brasenose College, Oxfor
2 CONC d, and four almsfolk at Turvey. He was buried as he had directed, at Turvey. His widow married Sir Thomas Kempe†.10
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1509-1558 Author: N. M. Fuidge Notes 1. C219/24/IV. 2. Date of birth given in CP, ix. 195. R. Halstead (pseud. of Henry Mordaunt, 2nd Earl of Peterborough), Succinct Gen. Mordaunt, 325-6, 400, 599; PCC 13 Jankyn; Vis.
2 CONC  Beds. (Harl. Soc. xix), 42; LP Hen. VIII, vi; Marr. Lic. Fac. Off. (Harl. Soc. xxiv), 5. 3. Duchy Cornw. RO, ministers’ accts. 125/4; LP Hen. VIII, xx; CPR, 1553, p. 352; 1553-4, pp. 19, 36, 107; 1554-5, p. 94; 1555-7, pp. 125, 2
2 CONC 81-2, 405; 1563-6, p. 122; 1569-72, pp. 222, 224; Cam. Misc. ix(3), 28-29, 62-63; VCH Bucks. iv. 429-39; Somerville, Duchy, i. 593, 604-5; APC, iv. 327; v. passim; vi. 162, 237; vii. 242; CSP Dom. 1547-80, p. 225. 4. Beds. N. and 
2 CONC Q. ii. 156-7; LP Hen. VIII, iv, ix; Halstead, 401 seq. 5. LP Hen. VIII, vi, xiv, xv, xviii, xix; Halstead, 401, 588-9. 6. Halstead, 401, 590-1, 600; Lansd. 103, f. 2; Chron. Q. Jane and Q. Mary (Cam. Soc. xlviii), 3; J. E. Oxley, 
2 CONC Ref. in Essex. 223; CPR, 1555-7, pp. 281-2. 7. APC, v. 66; vi. 237 et passim. 8. CSP Dom. 1601-3, Add. 1547-65, p. 510; LJ, i. 582, 626, 667. 9. Halstead, 328-36, 402-3, 593-6, 600 seq.; C3/123/29; Harl. 6767, f. 21v; Morant, Esse
2 CONC x, i. 105, 213-14; ii. 558; Trans. Essex Arch. Soc. n.s. vi. 52; VCH Beds. iii. 97-98, 110-11, 113, 182-3, 219, 443; VCH Bucks. ii. 329-30, 333-4; iv. 409, 503, 516; LP Hen. VIII, xviii, xix; Beds. N and Q. ii. 157. 10. Wards 7/26
2 CONC /28; PCC 13 Jankyn, 39 Holney
2 CONT
2 CONT The second Lord Mordaunt, was yet another John, and born at Turvey.  His first wife was Lady Eleanor FitzLewis. the daughter of Sir Richard Fitzlewis of West Thorndon, Essex, whom he married sometime before 26 February 1526.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor died on 2 June 1543 and John remarried. His second wife was called Lady Joanna.   When they met, during the rising in behalf of Lady Jane Grey in 1553, Joanna was in attendance upon Queen Mary at Framligham Castle in Suffo
2 CONC lk and a young and very beautiful widow.  Both wives share their husband's tomb and have effigies in the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT John held many important positions.  Here is a list from his entry in 'History of Parliament', a biographical dictionary of members of the House of Commons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheriff of Essex and Herts - 1540-1 Balliff of Duchy of Cornwall, Newport Pound, Essex - about 1543 Justice of the Peace, Essex - 1544-47 or later Commander of Relief, Essex - 1550 Steward of the Duchy of Lancaster, Olney, Bucks -
2 CONC  1552-8 Hertford and Constable, Hertford Castle - 1554- about 1558 Privy Councillor - 1553-about 1558
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord John served in the court of Henry VIII and was created a Knight of the Bath at Queen Anne Boleyn's coronation in 1533.  He was also present at Blackheath  for the arrival of King Henry's later wife, Anne of Cleves.  During th
2 CONC e reign of Edward VI he began to fall out of favour at court due to his opposition to religious changes.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was present at Queen Mary's coronation and her wedding to Philip of Spain.  He later served on her Council meetings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord John did not do well under Queen Elizabeth though.  In April 1561 he was imprisoned in the notorious Fleet, as 'a prisioner for the mass' - in other words he was continuing to practice Catholicism under the new Protestant rei
2 CONC gn.  He did however attend the 1563 Parliament and 1571. Lord John and his eldest son, Lewis, had a long time feud.  Lewis did not want to marry his father choice of bride for him, Lady Fermor. It seems that Lewis may have actuall
2 CONC y seduced the girl already, and been quite happy to marry her, until his grandfather, the 1st Baron of Turvey forbade it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John rewrote his will, ensuring that Lewis would not inherit any of the very valuable lands he had obtained through his marriage to Eleanor FitzLewis. To counteract this move, the old 1st Baron promptly rewrote HIS will too, d
2 CONC isinheriting his son, Lord John, in favour of his grandson Lewis!  Lewis promptly moved to Turvey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, probably just after the old Baron's death, Lord John and Lewis were reconciled.  When Lord John died in 1571, Lewis inherited most of the Mordaunt estates but did not get the FitzLewis lands for at least another ten ye
2 CONC ars.  It may well be that he had to negotiate for them with his stepmother, Joanna.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.turveybeds.com/mordaunts.html "The Turvey Website"]
2 CONT --------------------
