1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Elizabeth Brune==
2 CONT * b c1572
2 CONT * 1/12/1633
2 CONT * Father: Henry Brune 
2 CONT * Mother: Elizabeth Martyn
2 CONT
2 CONT MARRIED: 
2 CONT # c1592 Puddletown, Dorset to John Hall of Bradford-on-Avon, Wilts.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of ELIZABETH BRUNE and JOHN HALL are:
2 CONT #                   ii.    BRUNE HALL, b. 1593.
2 CONT #                  iii.    EDWARD HALL, b. 1598.
2 CONT #                  iv.    THOMAS HALL, b. 1600.
2 CONT #                   v.    JANE HALL, b. 1601.
2 CONT #                  vi.    MARY HALL, b. 1604.
2 CONT #                 i.    JOHN "ELISHA8" HALL, b. 1607, Bradford England; d. 1665, Newport, Rhode Island.
2 CONT #                 vii.    DOROTHY HALL, b. 1608.
2 CONT #                viii.    NICHOLAS HALL, b. 1609.
2 CONT #                   ix.    ANNE HALL, b. 1612.
2 CONT #                    x.    HENRY HALL, b. 1614.
2 CONT #                   xi.    WILLIAM HALL, b. 1613.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT CONDENSED WILL OF JOHN HALL OF BRADFORD-ON-AVON
2 CONT
2 CONT 10 Oct 1630, I John Hall of Bradford in the countie of Wilks and diocese of Sarum, Esquire, do make this my last will and testament.  Item - Elizabeth Hall my wife lands at the Abbey house countie of Somerset, with furniture.  Dau
2 CONC ghter Elizabeth Cottrell the some of one hundred pounds, daughter Mary one hundred pounds paid to Christopher Baylie gentleman her husband, grandson John Baylie tenne pounds, unmarried daughters Jane, Dorothie and Anne nine hunder
2 CONC d pounds.  My two sons John and William the yearly some of fifteen pounds a piece, to double after wife's death. Executor Thomas Hall, my son and heir.  Overseers - John Brewin (Brune), Knight, William Eyre (Ayer), Esquire, my kin
2 CONC sman, and John Andry, Theophilus Clarke.  Witnesses: John Andry, Sr., Christopher Bayly (Bailey) and Charles Taylor.
2 CONT
2 CONT About Bradford ....
2 CONT
2 CONT For more than six centuries Bradford-on-Avon was a center of cloth weaving. During this time, most of the buildings along the hillside to the river were spinners and weavers cottages with the woolen mills lining the bank of the ri
2 CONC ver Avon. By the mid sixteenth century the clothiers of Wiltshire, and the Hall’s in particular, became an important family in the cloth trade. This prosperity continued until a devastating outbreak of plague in 1609.  This outbre
2 CONC ak coupled with Wiltshire’s unwillingness to change from the old white undyed broadcloth to the new lighter colored broadcloths, resulted in a sharp and immediate decline in the manufacturing of broadcloth in Wiltshire.  This decl
2 CONC ine, and the rise of religious unrest led many Wiltshire’s residents down a path leading to America.        
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT # Burke's History of the Commoners of Great Britain & Ireland, Publication: 1836, Vols. I - IV
2 CONT # Visitation of the County of Dorset, 1677
2 CONT
