2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/14/16/fb/46/5344483cd947d5f7/duv43vej_original.jpg?hash=3e89b36993a0abfa1c049756de83c0a5b50bf2782690cd0959b8f050d76bcfd3.1794643199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Hall. Sable. 3 poleaxes argent. (This coat with the crest of Hall)
2 NOTE Over the chimney piece of a panelled room upstairs, (being the third of the plates above referred to) are still to be seen two shields carved in oak, each bearing the following quarterings: 
3 CONT
3 CONT
3 CONT 1. Hall. Sable. 3 poleaxes argent. (This coat with the erest of Hall,
3 CONT "an arm unbowed in armour, proper, garnished or, holding a poleaxe argent," is upon a shield in stone over Hall's aimhouse in the town of Bradford).
3 CONT 2. Attobd. Three cylindrical open-barred spindles or reels, apparently
3 CONT for winding yarn. (Or are they eel-traps, called in heraldy, weels ?) The device is very rare and uncertain: but it is evidently some kind of mill apparatus. At-ford was the name of an heiress who married one of the early Halls of
3 CONC  Bradford: and in an old Herald's note book in the Harleian collection of MSS. (4199. p. 91) the word Atford is, just perceptibly, written against this quartering in a rough sketch of the arms of Gore of Alderton.
3 CONT Giles Gore, Esq., of that place (the purchaser, from the Crown, of the Glastonbury Abbey estate at Grittleton in 1561) married Edith, daughter and heiress of a Julian Hall of Bradford (a younger branch of this family). Edith was b
3 CONC uried in Alderton Church, where a gravestone, in the south aisle, still preserves her initials "E. G. 1560" without further inscription. Thomas Gore, the writer on heraldry, used the quarterings 1 and 2 (Hall and Atford) in his bo
3 CONC okplates: and the same arms were also to be seen in Aubrey's time on stained glass in the windows of old Alderton house now destroyed.
3 CONT 8. ?A bend between S leopards or lions heads erased. [The
3 CONT Wilts Visitation of 1565, gives in the drawing of Hall's coat, 3 etoiles on the bend.]
3 CONT *.———? An eagle sable, preying on a fish azure. [This was also found on a seal attached to one of the old deeds lately discovered in Kingston House].
3 CONT 5. Besill. Argent, 3 torteauz, two and one.
3 CONT 6. Hall. As No. 1.
