2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Holy Trinity Church (1812, by J Smith)
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE “A fine large parish church below the bridge on the banks of the Avon”
3 CONT John Leland 1542
3 CONT
3 CONT The parish of Holy Trinity has its origins in a Saxon Minster which administered an area roughly corresponding to the current hundred of Bradford.  The Minster left a legacy of seven chapelries to the parish; Atworth, Broughton Gi
3 CONC fford, Holt, Limpley Stoke, South Wraxall, Westwood and Winsley, all of which had assumed parish status by the mid 19th century.  The parish and the wider area of North and West Wiltshire have a long history of non-conformity, per
3 CONC haps stemming from the close connections between Lollardy and the cloth trade.  
3 CONT   The present church was built around 1150 and originally consisted of a chancel, nave and maybe a tower.  The chancel was lengthened around the beginning of the 13th century, and a section of the south east wall rebuilt in 1707. 
3 CONC  A Lady Chapel was added to the north-east side of the nave in the early 14th century and later that century a chantry was added behind the Lady Chapel.  The two were made into the current north aisle sometime after the Reformatio
3 CONC n and its roof retains the 16th century roof bosses.  In the late-15th/early-16th century the chancel and tower arches were rebuilt; the Lady Chapel converted to a Chantry; and another chantry (the Kingston Chapel) added on the so
3 CONC uth-east side of the nave.  The town was very wealthy from the cloth trade at this time, and work may also have included raising the tower to accommodate a ring of five bells. The graceful tower arch is the only remnant of this wo
3 CONC rk.                                                                                                                             
