1 NAME John /Hall/ III
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Hall
2 NSFX III
2 _MARNM Hall
2 NICK John Elisha Hall
1 SEX M
1 BAPM
2 DATE 1605
2 PLAC Holy Trinity Church
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bradford-on-Avon
3 STAE England
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1605
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bradford-on-Avon
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1665
2 ADDR
3 CITY Newport
3 STAE Rhode Island
1 OCCU Elizabeth Leorned
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 7. John Hall, Bp 1605 Holy Trinity Ch, Wiltshire, England and died after 1665 in Newport, RI
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT * parents: 7th child of JOHN HALL II (b c 1570-1631) & of  Bradford on Avon, Wiltshire	 & Elizabeth Brune (c1572-1/12/1633)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married
2 CONT # possibly Elizabeth Rogers daughter of James Rogers
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT * 1.   Henry Hall b. c1637, MA Bay Colony
2 CONT * 2.   James Hall b. c1639 MA Bay Colony m. Margaret (divorced early in RI according to court documents)  
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT
2 CONT The manner in which John and William Hall (brothers) arrived in New England is cloudy, but they likely arrived on the Griffin in 1633 with William and Anne Hutchinson, who settled in Boston in a home across from Gov. Winthrop. Gov
2 CONC . Winthrop's journal dated 18 Sep 1634 states, "The Griffin and another ship now arriving with about 200 passengers. Mr. Lathrop and Mr. Sims, two godly ministers coming in the same ship".  The names of the 200 passengers on the G
2 CONC riffin is unknown, but approximately one-quarter of the passenger list has been reconstructed using early documents in which they mention having arrived in Boston with the Hutchinson family.   
2 CONT
2 CONT It's believed John Hall was labeled an "antinomian", and was exiled to Exeter, NH due to his association with the Hutchinson's.  In Rhode Island Anne preached meetings from her home that each person should follow their own inner l
2 CONC ight and not depend on ministers for their salvation.   A large number of the passengers on the Griffin in 1633 settled in Rhode Island after being expelled from Boston with the Hutchinson family.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT CONDENSED WILL OF JOHN HALL OF BRADFORD-ON-AVON
2 CONT
2 CONT 10 Oct 1630, I John Hall of Bradford in the countie of Wilks and diocese of Sarum, Esquire, do make this my last will and testament.  Item - Elizabeth Hall my wife lands at the Abbey house countie of Somerset, with furniture.  Dau
2 CONC ghter Elizabeth Cottrell the some of one hundred pounds, daughter Mary one hundred pounds paid to Christopher Baylie gentleman her husband, grandson John Baylie tenne pounds, unmarried daughters Jane, Dorothie and Anne nine hunder
2 CONC d pounds.  My two sons John and William the yearly some of fifteen pounds a piece, to double after wife's death. Executor Thomas Hall, my son and heir.  Overseers - John Brewin (Brune), Knight, William Eyre (Ayer), Esquire, my kin
2 CONC sman, and John Andry, Theophilus Clarke.  Witnesses: John Andry, Sr., Christopher Bayly (Bailey) and Charles Taylor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Links 
2 CONT * http://www.hallfamilyname.com/
2 CONT * http://home.comcast.net/~wdegidio/Hall/hallfamily4.htm
2 CONT *http://www.myhubbardmtn.com/family%20htm/rogers.htm
2 CONT According to his gavestone he died in 1670 in Munmouth, New Jersey (Edited by L. Morgan)
