2 CONT ----
2 CONT Ben M. Angel notes: This profile was apparently mis-merged into the Thomas Rogers lineage. My attempt to reconstruct the near family after separation is based on the following 1998 email from Con Putnam to the Hodges-L list:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/HODGES/1998-02/0888558977
2 CONT
2 CONT *From: ConPutnam <ConPutnam@aol.com> 
2 CONT *Subject: [HODGES-L] Re: Jennifer's William and Mary line.... 
2 CONT *Date: Fri, 27 Feb 1998 00:56:17 EST
2 CONT
2 CONT Hello,
2 CONT
2 CONT >My HODGE line starts with William HODGES b.1602 in ENG d. 1/2/1654 in Taunton
2 CONT
2 CONT >ma m.Mary ANDREWS b. abt 1628 d aft 1700.I have about 9
2 CONT generations from there. If anything sounds familiar would love to hear from you all....
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a reference to a 'mr. Hodges in the Rebecka' in a letter dated 23 June 1636 written by John Winthrop the Elder (in England at the time) to his son John Winthrop, Governor of Connecticut.  'Mr.' indicates a person of a hig
2 CONC her social status than average.  Hodges was almost certainly John Hodges, captain of the Rebecca, who settled with his wife and children at Charlestown circa 1625.  Was he a member of the same Hodges family? 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT == Biography == 
2 CONT William Hodges was born around 1624.  He was born in Kent, England<refname='Hodges'>#Hodges|Hodges Page 71.</ref>He was baptized on 21 Mar 1624 at East Greenwich, Kent, England He may have been the "William Hedges" appointed on th
2 CONC e jury at the Court held in Salem on March 27, 1638, although this may have been William Hedge of Lynn.<ref name='Hodges'/> William went to Taunton shortly after it was purchased by the first proprietors.  He was in the second lis
2 CONC t of early settlers compiled by the town clerk, John Wilbore.  He was in the list of males in Taunton able to bear arms in 1643.<ref name='Hodges'/> On March 24, 1644, the town voted "that sufficient carte-way be made from the hou
2 CONC ses into the woods behind the ground of William Hodges, William Evans and Aaron Knapp, where it is most convenient."<ref name='Hodges'/> William married Mary Andrews around 1648/1649 in Taunton.<ref> Date based on birth date of fi
2 CONC rst child.</ref> William was one of the original stockholders of the first Taunton IronWorks, subscribing £20 for a whole share.<ref name='Hodges'/> William Hodges died April 2, 1654, in Taunton, Plymouth Colony.  He seems to have
2 CONC  held considerable property and probably was comparatively young when he died.<ref name='Hodges'/>
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT <references />
2 CONT
2 CONT *<span id='Hodges'>Hodges: </span>Hodges, Almon D., Jr. ''Space:Genealogical Record of the Hodges Family of New England|Genealogical Record of the Hodges Family of New England, Third Edition'', Frank H. Hodges, Boston, 1896 [https
2 CONC ://archive.org/stream/genealogicalreco00hodg#page/72/mode/2up On Archive.org]*''Abridgement of the Genealogical Record of The Hodges Family of New England, ending December 21, 1954'',  Compiled by George F Hodges and other members
2 CONC  of the Family, page 17. Appears without publishing information, printed sometime after 20 Jan 1956, the last date in the book.(T. H. Bredehoft, private library collection) No. 43 of 200 printed. https://archive.org/details/geneal
2 CONC ogicalreco00hodg/page/70* http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~dearbornboutwell/fam7456.html* ''New England Marriages Prior to 1700'', by Clarence Almon Torrey (Boston, Massachusetts: New England Historic Genealogical Society,
2 CONC  2011)Vol. II, p. 770. HODGES/HEDGES, William (-1654 & Mary ANDREWS, m/2 Peter PITTS 1654?; ca 1648; Taunton (Pope's Pioneers has Hedges) {Taunton 1:30, 89; Reg. 5:259 has Hedges, 261, 4145, 51:455, 52:16; Hodges 11+; Babbitt 30; 
2 CONC Fulton Anc. 248; Kendall Anc. (ms) 20; Gardner (1915) 15; Shurtleff 145; Williams (#16) 98, 132; Bowen 179; Munsey-Hopkins 179; Sv. 1:53; Bassett-Preston 7; Pope's Pioneers; MD 11:152, 12:243; McCormick-Hamilton 469}
