1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Abstracts of Somersetshire wills, etc: copied from the manuscript ..., Volume 1 By Frederick Arthur Crisp, Frederick Brown
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=PElFAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA55&lpg=PA55&dq=John+Symes+1572&source=bl&ots=b3jTjiqeRr&sig=9ws6f1BrfxWuZTkhKfR79Snq3OI&hl=en&ei=PyaJTf66HZKmsQPAzu2RDA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CBYQ6AEwADge#v
2 CONC =onepage&q=John%20Symes%201572&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 34
2 CONT *   ROGER HILL, of Poundsford, Somerset, gent. Will dated Dec. 22, 1606, proved May 24, 1609, by '''W'm Hill'''. [42 Dorset.] To be buried at Pitminster. My father, William Hill, dec'd. My wife Mary3, an annuity of L30, & to have 
2 CONC rooms in my dwelling house at Poundsford. My son Roger Hill. My son John Hill, under 21. My daughters, Ann Shute, Frances Shaplin [Chaplin], & Elizabeth Sampson, three angells each, for three rings. '''My son William Hill, Exor.''
2 CONC ' My friend John Pyne, esq'r, & my brother Alexander Hill, gent., Overseers.
2 CONT *   JOHN YOUNGE, of Coliton, Devon. Will dated Aug. 13, 1610, proved Oct. 1, 1613. [63 Fenner.] My son Walter. My wife Alice. My brother in law, John Starre, of Beare. My brother in law, Hercules Pyne. My daughter Mary Fry, & her 
2 CONC husband William Fry. My daughters, Joan Mallacke, Alice Cockeram, Ann Hill, & '''Jane Hill'''. My son in law Richard Mallacke. Roger, William, Mary, Ann, & Jane Hill, children of '''my son in law, William Hill, of Poundsford'''. L
2 CONC usse & Mary Hill. My sister Ellen S . . . .  My sons in law, '''Will. Hill''' & Rob't Hill.1
2 CONT *Pg. 36
2 CONT **	1 ? Roger. No son Robert is named in Visitation of Somerset.
2 CONT *_____________________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of national biography (1885)
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/dictionarynatio46stepgoog
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/dictionarynatio46stepgoog#page/n421/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.409
2 CONT *HILL, ROGER (1605-1667), judge of a family long settled at Houndstone, Somerset, son of '''William Hill of Poundsford, near Taunton, and Jane, daughter of John Young of Devonshire''', was born at Collaton, Devonshire, at the hous
2 CONC e of Mrs. Sampson, his father's sister, on 1 Dec. 1605. He was admitted a member of the Inner Temple 22 March 1624, was called to the bar 10 Feb. 1632, and became a bencher of his innn 10 June 1649. In March 1644 he was the junior
2 CONC  of five counsel against Archbishop Laud (WOOD, Athenae Oxon. iii. 130), and was elected in 1645 to parliament for Bridport, the sitting member having adhered to King Charles (Parl. Hist. ii. 608). In 1646 he received a grant of t
2 CONC he chambers in the Temple of Mostyn and Stampe (WHITELOCKE, Memorials, p. 201), and was named in the commission of judges to try the king, but did not act. In May 1649 he was appointed to assist the attorney-general for the Common
2 CONC wealth against Lilburne, Walwin, Prince, and Overton, and was again assistant to the attorney-general in the trials in the west in the spring of 1655. On 29 June of that year he became a serjeant-at-law, was a judge of assize in N
2 CONC orthamptonshire in August 1656, and is named as a baron of the exchequer in Easter term 1657 in Hardres's 'Reports.' He was present at the Protector's investiture in June 1657, and was a judge attendant on the House of Peers in Ja
2 CONC nuary 1658 (BURTON, Diary, ii. 240, 512). In 1658 he went the Oxford circuit (WOOD, Athenae Oxon. iii. 754), and in August 1659, with Chief-baron Wylde, held assized in Gloucestershire, with instructions to proceed to Monmouth 'if
2 CONC  it be safe, but otherwise to forbear.' Lambert being on the march from Chester, and the country becoming pacified, the judges were able to proceed (GREENE, Cal. State Papers, Dom. August 1659). On the restoration of the Long parl
2 CONC iament he resumed his seat, and on 17 Jan. 1660 was
2 CONT *Pg.410 '''UNREADABLE.
2 CONT *____________________
2 CONT *'''The Antiquary, Volume 32 edited by Edward Walford, John Charles Cox, George Latimer Apperson
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/antiquary32slsniala
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/antiquary32slsniala#page/222/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.222
2 CONT *   (4) Roger Hill, of Poundisford. In the Somerset Visitation his mother is said to have been Anne, daughter of John Trowbridge, of Colyton, in Devon,
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/antiquary32slsniala#page/224/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg. 224
2 CONT *but in the Dorset Visitation, Lucy, daughter of John Ryves, of Dorset. Roger Hill married Mary, daughter of John Hassard, of Lyme, and had three sons -- '''William''', Roger (of Taunton), and John (of Dorchester), and six daughte
2 CONC rs. He was followed at Poundisford by
2 CONT *'''   (5) William Hill (living in 1623), who married Jane, daugher of John Yonge, of Cullompton, Devon, and had three sons -- Roger, William, and John -- and five daughters. His heir was
2 CONT *   (6) Roger Hill (b. December 1, 1605; d. 1667, who became a serjeant-at-law in 1655, and Baron of the Exchequer. Le Neve says he was "a great man in the Rump Parliament, and in Oliver's time," and an account of him may be seen 
2 CONC in the Dictionary of National Biography. He married (1) Catherine, daughter of Giles Green, by whom he had a son, William, who died childless; and (2) Abigail, daughter of Brampton Gurdon, of Assington, Suffolk, by whom he had a s
2 CONC on (ultimately his heir),
2 CONT *______________________________
