2 CONT === Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT ''A Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Commoners of Great'' ..., Volume 1 By John Burke. Page 240-241. < [https://books.google.com/books?id=QisAAAAAQAAJ&vq=Bind&pg=PA240#v=onepage&q&f=true GoogleBooks] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bond, of Grange 
2 CONT
2 CONT The second son,
2 CONT
2 CONT ROBERT BOND, removed to Beauchamp's Hache, in Somersetshire, and in the year 1431 (9th HENRY VI.) was seated at Lutton, in the Isle of Purbeck, in the county of Dorset, an estate he acquired in marriage with the daughter and heire
2 CONC ss of a family of that name, and which has ever since continued in the possession of his descendants. He had issue two sons, viz.
2 CONT *ROBERT, his successor.
2 CONT *'''William, of Buckland''', in the county of Somerset, who was father of 
2 CONT **1. William Bond, of Crosby Palace, London, alderman and sheriff of that city, who d. in 1576,:and to whom and to his son, Martin Bond, there are curious   monuments in the church of:Great St. Helens, London. 
2 CONT **2. Sir George Bond, lord mayor of London in 1587, from whom descended …
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Bond_(mayor) Wikipedia - George Bond (mayor)]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir George Bond''' (died 1592) was 
2 CONT * a 16th-century English politician who served as Lord Mayor of London in 1587/8.[1] 
2 CONT * A native of Somerset, 
2 CONT * he was the younger son of William Bond of Buckland and younger brother of William Bond, alderman and Sheriff of London.[2] -
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT From ''A Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Extinct and Dormant Baronetcies'' ... By John Burke, Bernard Burke, (1841) p. 70 < [https://books.google.ca/books?id=zs2YIGYcFYMC&newbks=0&lpg=PA70&dq=%22william%20bond%22%20AND%20
2 CONC buckland&pg=PA70#v=onepage&q=%22william%20bond%22%20AND%20buckland&f=false GoogleBooks] > (names wife as __ Hill)
2 CONT
