1 OCCU custos rotulorum of Warwickshire
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 2. Sir Thomas Leigh, 1st Bt., of Stoneleigh, co. Warwick (cr. 29 Jun 1611), Sheriff of Warwickshire 1581-82 (d. 1 Feb 1625/6), mar. c.1570 Katharine Spencer (d. 1639), 3rd dau. of Sir John Spencer, of Wormleighton,
2 CONC  co. Warwick, and Althorp, co. Northampton, by his wife Katharine Kitson, 1st dau. of Sir Thomas kitson, of Hengrave, co. Suffolk, and had issue:
2 CONT * 1a. Sir John Leigh (dvp.), mar. (1) Ursula Hoddesdon, dau. of Sir Christopher Hoddesdon, of Leighton Buzzard, co. Bedford, and (2) Anna Cope, dau. of Sir Anthony Cope, 1st Bt., of Hanwell, co. Oxford, and had issue by his first 
2 CONC wife:
2 CONT * 1b. Sir Thomas Leigh, 2nd Bt., later 1st Baron Leigh
2 CONT * 2a. Sir Thomas Leigh (dsp.)
2 CONT * 3a. Ferdinando Leigh (dsp.)
2 CONT * 1a. Alice Leigh, suo jure Duchess of Dudley (cr. 23 May 1644 for life) (b. c. 1579; dspm. 22 Jan 1668/9), mar. before 25 Sep 1597 Sir Robert Dudley, titular Duke of Northumberland (d. 6 Sep 1649), illegit. son of Robert [Dudley]
2 CONC , 1st Earl of Leicester, and had issue
2 CONT
2 CONT source: [http://www.cracroftspeerage.co.uk/online/content/leigh1839.htm Cracroft's Peerage] The Complete Guide to the British Peerage & Baronetage: Leigh, Baron (UK, 1839)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1604-1629/member/leigh-sir-thomas-1595-1672 LEIGH, Sir Thomas, 2nd Bt.] (c.1595-1672), of Stoneleigh, Warws. and Leighton Buzzard, Beds.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Leigh’s family sprang to prominence in Warwickshire in the mid-sixteenth century, when his great-grandfather, a successful London Mercer, made extensive land purchases there, including the manor of Stoneleigh, a few miles south o
2 CONC f Coventry. The old merchant spawned two significant gentry lines in the county. The junior, based at King’s Newnham, was represented in the early seventeenth century by Sir Francis Leigh I* and Sir Francis Leigh, 1st bt*. Stonele
2 CONC igh became the principal seat of the senior branch, headed at the start of James’s reign by the prominent figure of Leigh’s grandfather, '''Sir Thomas''', custos rotulorum (1) of Warwickshire (2), and a baronet from 1611. (24)
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnotes
2 CONT * 1. From [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Custos_rotulorum Wikipedia].  The Custos rotulorum is the keeper of an English, Welsh and Northern Irish county's records and, by virtue of that office, the highest civil officer in the coun
2 CONC ty. ... Lambarde described the custos rotulorum as a man chosen either for his wisdom, countenance or credit.
2 CONT * 2. From [http://www.history.ac.uk/publications/office/custodes1544#hh Institute of Historical Research] - Custodes Rotulorum 1544-1646 By 1605 Leigh, Sir Thomas, Kt. (cr. Bt. 29 June 1611) First occ. c. 1605 (C 66/1682). Last oc
2 CONC c. Nov. 1621 (C 193/13/1).
2 CONT * 24. Vis. Warws. 10-11; W. Dugdale, Antiqs. of Warws. (1730), i. 258-9; CB, i. 44
2 CONT
