1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === The English Baronetage - by Arthur Collins ===
2 CONT http://books.google.com/books?id=2GQUAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA3&lpg=PA3&dq=sir+thomas+bond+osbaldeston+england&source=bl&ots=bzFk0khPOQ&sig=_l3HDvKDXiULsfmsEMZwMUA4zTQ&hl=en&sa=X&ei=ewNrU42qFoqdyATqkYLYAw&ved=0CD4Q6AEwBA#v=snippet&q=sir%20th
2 CONC omas%20bond&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === Wikipedia article... ===
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Thomas_Bond,_1st_Baronet
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === The Thomas Bond who would later give his ===
2 CONT The Thomas Bond who would later give his name to Bond Street in London (technically Old Bond Street and New Bond Street). This is because Bond developed this area after he (together with a couple of other financiers) bought the fo
2 CONC rmer Duke of Clarendon’s gardens from the Duke of Albermarle (the new owner) for
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === 
2 CONT Nevertheless, it was in 1686 that Sir  ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, it was in 1686 that Sir Thomas Bond, a wily property speculator, replaced the palace and its gardens with the fashionable streets we know today.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 18th Century the street was filled with the 'Bond Street Loungers' who cultivated a special walk called the Bond Street Roll which was much imitated. Beau Brummel and the Regency Dandies frequented the clubs and high-class 
2 CONC brothels which catered to a predominantly male population. Ladies, it seems, could only decently be seen on the streets before midday!
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1840 there were 22 tailors, 17 milliners and shirt-makers, 12 hatters, 12 wine merchants, 12 booksellers and a variety of grocers' shops. Then as now there were suppliers of porcelain, glass and china, alongside gunsmiths, silv
2 CONC ersmiths, goldsmiths and cabinet-makers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Admiral Lord Nelson lived at no fewer than four addresses in Bond Street at different times. At No. 130 and 147 he lived with his mistress, Lady Hamilton; at No. 141 he lived with his wife after losing his arm at Santa Cruz. Both 
2 CONC ladies apparently went to the chemists Savoury & Moore (still there today) to find ointments and drugs to help the hero.
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === !#218-v3-p16,20 and fn(b); ===
2 CONT !#218-v3-p16,20 and fn(b);
