1 NAME Dionysia /Hale/
2 GIVN Dionysia
2 SURN Hale
2 _MARNM Williamson
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 17 MAR 1611
2 PLAC Kings Walden
2 ADDR
3 CTRY England
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Lady Dionysia Williamson (c. 1611-1684)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Dionysia Williamson was the fourth daughter and tenth child of William Hale (1634) of Kings Walden, Hertfordshire, and London, by his wife Rose Bond. Her father, William Hale, and grandfather, Richard Hale, were prominent mem
2 CONC bers of the Grocers' Company in London. They amassed their wealth through the wholesale trade of spices and other goods, which they imported and sold across Great Britain. The family invested in various properties both in London a
2 CONC nd the Home Counties, particularly in Hertfordshire. Notably, Richard Hale founded Hertford Grammar School in 1617.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1647, Dionysia married Sir Thomas Williamson (1609-1657) of East Markham, becoming his second wife. Sir Thomas was a widower, having previously been married to Jane Hussey, with whom he had two sons and a daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following Sir Thomas's death, Lady Dionysia became an extremely wealthy widow. After the Great Fire of London, she made substantial donations to the city's reconstruction efforts. She contributed £2001 to the rebuilding of St. Pau
2 CONC l's Cathedral, the largest single donation, £2000 to St. Mary le Bow, and £4000 to St. Dunstan's, which was the Hale family church. Around 1666, she purchased Hales Hall from William Humberstone, whose father had acquired the prop
2 CONC erty from James Hobart approximately 30 years earlier, becoming the Lady of the Manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also:
2 CONT *''The New England Historical and Genealogical Register, Volume 35'' (available as Google e-book)
