2 CONT *Henry Percy, 8th Earl of Northumberland, 2nd Baron Percy (1532 – 21 June 1585) was an English aristocrat and conspirator.
2 CONT *He was born at Newburn Manor about 1532, was second of the two sons of Sir Thomas Percy, who was executed in 1537 as a chief actor in the Pilgrimage of Grace, and Eleanor Harbottle. Brought up with his elder brother Thomas Percy,
2 CONC  7th Earl of Northumberland, he took part as a youth in border warfare, and on Queen Mary's accession was appointed governor of Tynemouth Castle. He was returned to the House of Commons in 1554 as Member of Parliament for Morpeth,
2 CONC  Northumberland, was knighted in 1557, and became deputy warden of the east and middle marches. .... etc.
2 CONT *In January 1562, he married Lady Katherine Neville (1546–1596), the daughter of John Nevill, 4th Baron Latimer and Lady Lucy Somerset. They had the following children:
2 CONT ** Henry Percy, 9th Earl of Northumberland (1564–1632), married Lady Dorothy Devereux
2 CONT ** Thomas Percy
2 CONT ** William Percy, poet and playwright
2 CONT ** Charles Percy (Sir)
2 CONT ** Lucy Percy, married Sir John Wotton and then Sir Hugh Owen
2 CONT ** Richard Percy
2 CONT ** Joscelyne Percy (Sir)
2 CONT ** Anne Percy
2 CONT **''' Sir Alan Percy (died 1613), married Mary Fitz
2 CONT ** Eleanor Percy (1583–1650), married William Herbert, 1st Baron Powis
2 CONT ** Sir George Percy (1580–1632), married Anne Floyd
2 CONT *His wife Katherine was buried in St Paul's Chapel within Westminster Abbey.[2]
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Percy,_8th_Earl_of_Northumberland
2 CONT *_____________________________
2 CONT *Admiral Thomas Howard, 1st Earl of Suffolk, KG, PC (24 August 1561 – 28 May 1626) was a son of Thomas Howard, 4th Duke of Norfolk by his second wife Margaret Audley, Duchess of Norfolk, the daughter and heiress of the 1st Baron A
2 CONC udley of Walden. .... etc.
2 CONT *After the death of his mother on 10 January 1564, the infant Thomas inherited Saffron Walden and other Audley properties. While imprisoned in the Tower before his execution in 1572, his father urged him to marry his stepsister Ma
2 CONC ry Dacre, the daughter of Thomas Dacre, 4th Baron Dacre and Elizabeth Leybourne, the Duke's third wife. He did so; but Mary died, childless, on April 1578 at Walden.[1]
2 CONT *In or before 1582, Howard remarried to Katherine Knyvet, widow of Richard, son of Robert Rich, 2nd Baron Rich. A noted beauty, she was also the eldest daughter and heiress of her father, Sir Henry Knyvet of Charlton.[2]
2 CONT *Issue
2 CONT **Theophilus Howard, 2nd Earl of Suffolk (13 August 1582 – 3 June 1640) married: Elizabeth Hume had issue
2 CONT ** Elizabeth Howard (c. 1583 - 17 April 1658) married: (1) William Knollys, 1st Earl of Banbury had issue (2) Edward Vaux, 4th Baron Vaux of Harrowden (some say that Elizabeth's and William's children were illegitimate)
2 CONT ** Sir Robert Howard (1584–1653) married: Catherine Nevill
2 CONT ** Gertrude Howard (born c. 1585)[3]
2 CONT ** Sir William Howard (1586 – bef. 1672)
2 CONT ** Thomas Howard, 1st Earl of Berkshire (8 October 1587 – 16 July 1669) married: Elizabeth Cecil had issue
2 CONT ** Catherine Howard (c.1588–1673) married: William Cecil, 2nd Earl of Salisbury had issue
2 CONT ** Emily Howard (born 1589)[3]
2 CONT ** Frances Howard (31 May 1590 – 1632) married: (1) Robert Devereux, 3rd Earl of Essex (2) Robert Carr, 1st Earl of Somerset had issue
2 CONT **''' Sir Charles Howard (1591 – 21 June 1626), married Mary Fitz(john) and had issue
2 CONT ** Henry Howard (1592–1616), married Elizabeth Bassett and had issue
2 CONT ** John Howard (1593–1595)[3]
2 CONT ** Edward Howard, 1st Baron Howard of Escrick (d. 24 April 1675)
2 CONT ** Margaret Howard (c.1599 - 1608)
2 CONT *In December 1584, he was restored in blood as Lord Thomas Howard.  ..... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Howard,_1st_Earl_of_Suffolk
2 CONT *___________________________
2 CONT *'''Sir Richard Grenville, 1st Baronet (or Granville) (1600–1658) was a Cornish Royalist leader during the English Civil War.
2 CONT *He was the third son of Sir Bernard Grenville (1559–1636), and a great-grandson of the famous seaman, Sir Richard Grenville. Having served in France, Germany and the Netherlands, Grenville gained the favour of George Villiers, 1s
2 CONC t Duke of Buckingham, took part in the expeditions to Cádiz, to the island of Rhé and to La Rochelle, was knighted, and in 1628 became member of parliament for Fowey, Cornwall.
2 CONT *'''In 1630, he married Mary Fitz (1596–1671), the wealthy widow of Sir Charles Howard (died 1626), and was made a baronet, of Kilkhampton in the County of Cornwall; his violent temper destroyed the marriage, and he was imprisoned
2 CONC  as the result of two lawsuits, one with his wife, and the other with her kinsman, the Earl of Suffolk'''. In 1633 he escaped from prison and went to Germany, returning to England six years later to join the army which Charles I w
2 CONC as collecting to march against the Scots. Early in 1641, just after the outbreak of the Irish rebellion, Sir Richard led some troops to Ireland, where he won some fame and became governor of Trim; then returning to England in 1643
2 CONC  he was arrested at Liverpool by Parliament, but was soon released and sent to join the parliamentary army.
2 CONT *Instead, having obtained men and money, he hurried  .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Richard_Grenville,_1st_Baronet
2 CONT *____________________________
