3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT JUN 1636
1 OCCU High Sheriff of Cornwall (1612)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Sir William Wrey, 1st Baronet''' was born circa 1566 in North Russell,, Devon, England. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-76056566 John Wrey] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-76056606 Blanch Wrey]. He was a High S
2 CONC heriff of Cornwall (1612). 
2 CONT
2 CONT William married [https://www.geni.com/profile-76056307 Elizabeth Wrey] in 1600. Together they had the following children: [https://www.geni.com/profile-76056044 Sir William Wrey, 2nd Baronet]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died circa June 1636. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Baronets of Cornwall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wrey of Trebigh, in St. Ive. — '''This ancient Devonshire family, in consequence of a marriage with an heiress of the Killigrew family, in the reign of Queen Elizabeth,''' settled at Trebigh, which appears to have been their chief
2 CONC  residence, when William Wrey, Esq. was created a baronet in 1628. His son, Sir Chichester, having married one of the coheiresses of Edward Bourchier, Earl of Bath, became possessed of Tawstock, the ancient seat of that noble fami
2 CONC ly, which has ever since been the chief residence of his posterity, and is now the seat of his descendant Sir Bourchier Wrey, Bart. Trebigh is still in the family but occupied by the tenant of the demesne farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms of Wrey: — Sable, a fesse between three pole-axes, Argent, helved, Gules; quartering Arg.; a cross engrailed Gules, between four water-bougets, Sable—Bourchier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Crest: — On a wreath, a lion's head erased.
2 CONT
2 CONT The grandfather of William Wrey, Esq., who was created a baronet, as abovementioned, married a coheiress of Killigrew.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT References: '''British History Online- Wrey Baronets Baronets of Cornwall.''' His father's marriage to Blanch Wrey (Killigrew) resulted in the Baronet. 
2 CONT  [https://www.british-history.ac.uk/magna-britannia/vol3/lxxxix-xcv]
