1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Thomas Thynne''' (1647/8–12 February 1682) was an English landowner of the family that is now headed by the Marquess of Bath and politician who sat in the House of Commons from 1670 to 1682. He went by the nickn
2 CONC ame "Tom of Ten Thousand" due to his great wealth. He was a friend of the Duke of Monmouth, a relationship referred to in John Dryden's satirical work Absalom and Achitophel where Thynne is described as "Issachar, his wealthy west
2 CONC ern friend".
2 CONT
2 CONT Thynne was the son of Sir Thomas Thynne, and his wife Stuarta Balquanquill, daughter of Dr. Walter Balquanquill.[1] His father was a younger son of Sir Thomas Thynne of Longleat, Wiltshire. In 1670 Thynne succeeded to the family e
2 CONC states at Longleat on the death of his uncle Sir James Thynne without issue. He also succeeded his uncle as Member of Parliament for Wiltshire, and sat until his death in 1682.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 15 November 1681 Thynne married the wealthy Lady Elizabeth Percy, only child of Joceline Percy, 11th Earl of Northumberland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thynne was murdered on 12 February 1682 after the Swedish Count Karl Johann von Königsmark began to pursue his wife. He was shot while riding in his coach in Pall Mall, London, by Königsmark and his three accomplices Christopher V
2 CONC ratz, John Stern and Charles George Borosky. The four were soon arrested; however Königsmark was acquitted of the murder (due to the corruption of the jury according to diarist John Evelyn) but Vratz, Stern and Borosky were hanged
2 CONC  on 10 March 1682.
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Karl von Königsmark was the brother of Count Philip Christoph von Königsmarck who disappeared under mysterious cirucumstances in the state of Hanover in Germany in 1694, possibly murdered by order of the future British monar
2 CONC ch George I.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Thynne's death his widow, Lady Elizabeth, married Charles Seymour, 6th Duke of Somerset.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thynne's remains were interred in a marble tomb in Westminster Abbey. The tomb is decorated in part with a representation of the murder of Thynne in 1682. A popular ballad summed up the episode in form of a mock epitaph:
2 CONT
2 CONT “	Here lies Tom Thynne of Longleat Hall Who ne'er would have miscarried;
2 CONT
2 CONT Had he married the woman he slept withal
2 CONT
2 CONT Or slept with the woman he married.
2 CONT
2 CONT From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Thynne_(landowner)
2 CONT *______________ Links:
2 CONT * http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Thynne,_Thomas_(1648-1682)_(DNB00)
2 CONT * http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/PERCY.htm#Josceline PERCY (11º E. Northumberland)
2 CONT * http://www.thepeerage.com/p1048.htm#i10472
2 CONT * "<em>[http://www.westminster-abbey.org/our-history/people/thomas-thynne Thomas Thynne]</em>", Westminster Abbey
