2 CONT *'''Robert Needham, 1st Viscount Kilmorey (ca. 1565-1631), was an English politician and a peer in the peerage of Ireland.
2 CONT *He was the eldest son of Robert Needham of Shavington, Shropshire, educated at Shrewsbury School (1577) and St John’s College, Cambridge (1582) and trained in the law at the Inner Temple in 1583. He served in Ireland and was knig
2 CONC hted by the Lord Deputy of Ireland in 1594. He succeeded his father in 1603, inheriting Shavington Hall at Adderley, Shropshire.[1]
2 CONT *He was appointed a Justice of the Peace for Shropshire by 1596, a deputy lieutenant of the county in 1600 and High Sheriff of Shropshire for 1606–07. He was a member of the Council in the Marches of Wales in 1609 and vice-preside
2 CONC nt of the council in 1614. He was a Member (MP) of the Parliament of England for Shropshire in 1593 and 1604. He was created Viscount Kilmorey in 1625.[1]
2 CONT *He died in 1631 and was buried at Adderley. He was succeeded by his only son, Robert.[1]
2 CONT *Viscount Kilmorey was married four times:
2 CONT *He married, firstly, Jane Lacy, daughter of John Lacy, circa 10 August 1586.[2] They had a son Robert.[3]
2 CONT *He married, secondly, Anne Doyley before 14 October 1594.[2]
2 CONT *He married, thirdly, Catherine Robinson, daughter of John Robinson.[2]
2 CONT *'''He married fourthly the twice widowed Dorothy, daughter Ambrose Smith of Cheapside.[4][2]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Needham,_1st_Viscount_Kilmorey _____________
2 CONT *'''NEEDHAM, Robert (c.1565-1631), of Shavington, Salop.
2 CONT *b. c.1565, 1st s. of Robert Needham of Shavington by Frances, da. of Sir Edward Aston of Tixall. educ. Shrewsbury 1577; St. John’s, Camb. 1582; I. Temple 1583. m. (1) Jane (d.1591), da. of John Lacy of Borston, Som., 1s.; (2) Ann
2 CONC e Doyley, wid. of one Wilmott; (3) aft. 1627, Catherine (d.1628), da. of John Robinson of London, wid. of George Huxley; '''(4) Dorothy, da. of Humphrey Smith of London, wid. of Sir John Pakington'''. Kntd. 1594; suc. fa. 1603. cr
2 CONC . Visct. Kilmorey [I] 1625.
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT **J.p. Salop by 1596, dep. Lt. 1600, sheriff 1606-7; member, council in marches of Wales 1609, vice-pres. 1614.
2 CONT *As a young man, Needham held several commands in Ireland, where he was knighted by the lord deputy. If he was back in time for the 1593 Parliament Needham would, as a knight of the shire, have been entitled to attend committees o
2 CONC n the subsidy (26 Feb.) and springing uses and perpetuities (9 Mar.). He was certainly in England in 1596, claiming a reimbursement of £75 from the government: he received £30 two years later. In Shropshire he was employed in such
2 CONC  matters as endeavouring to prevent excessive brewing and ‘tippling and disorderly drinking’ in two alehouses in Market Drayton, and in searching for Jesuits and seminary priests. He remained active in local affairs all his life, 
2 CONC and obtained an Irish peerage from Charles I. He died in 1631, and was buried at Adderley.
2 CONT *Vis. Salop (Harl. Soc. xxix), 372; Trans. Salop Arch. Soc. (ser. 4), xi. 156; CP, vii. 260; APC, xxvi. 335; xxx. 399; H. O. Harrod, Hist. Shavington, 22-49; D’Ewes, 474, 496.
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/needham-robert-1565-1631 _______________
2 CONT *'''NEEDHAM, Sir Robert (1567/8-1631), of Shavington Hall, nr. Whitchurch, Salop
2 CONT *b. 1567/8,1 1st. s. of Robert Needham of Shavington and Frances, da. of Sir Edward Aston of Tixall, Staffs.2 educ. Shrewsbury g.s. 1577; St. John’s, Camb. 1582; I. Temple 1584.3 m. (1) settlement 10 Aug. 1586, Joan (d. 16 July 15
2 CONC 91), da. of John Lacy, alderman of London and Borston, Som. 1s., 2da. d.v.p.;4 (2) settlement Dec. 1594, Anne (d. aft. 1613), da. of Henry Doyley of Shottesham, Norf., wid. of Thomas Townshend (d.1591) of Brakenash, Norf. and Geor
2 CONC ge Willmer of London (d.1593/4), s.p.5 (3) 1627, Catherine (admon. 8 Dec. 1628), da. of John Robinson of London, wid. of George Huxley (d.1627) of Edmonton, Mdx., s.p.;6 '''(4) Dorothy (d. by 1639), da. of Humphrey Smith, Grocer o
2 CONC f London, wid. of Benedict Barnham† (d.1598), Draper and alderman of London and Sir John Pakington (d.1625) of Westwood, Worcs., s.p.''' kntd. 1 Sept. 1594;7 suc. fa. 1603;8 cr. Visct. Kilmorey [I] 8 Apr. 1625.9 d. 24 Nov. 1631.10
2 CONC  sig. Robert Nedham.
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT **Vol. [I] 1594; capt. of horse [I] 1596-8, ft. 1599.11
2 CONT **J.p. Salop 1596-d., Cheshire 1614-d.;12 freeman, Southampton, Hants 1599, Shrewsbury, Salop 1614;13 dep. lt., Salop 1600-d.,14 sheriff 1606-7;15 commr. subsidy, Salop 1608, 1621-2, 1624, aid 1609, swans, Midlands 1627;16 member,
2 CONC  Council in the Marches 1609-d., v.-pres. 1614;17 commr. Forced Loan, Salop 1626-7.18
2 CONT *Originally from Needham Grange, Derbyshire, the Needhams acquired Cranage, Cheshire by marriage at the end of Edward III’s reign. The first member of the family to serve as an MP, the lawyer John Needham (who sat for Newcastle-un
2 CONC der-Lyme and London in the reign of Henry VI) built up a substantial estate in Nantwich hundred, and established his main residence just across the county line at Shavington Hall, Shropshire.19 Sir Robert Needham spent much of his
2 CONC  early adult life in Ireland, and was knighted by lord deputy Sir William Russell† at the relief of Eniskillen in 1594. He brought over fresh recruits in 1596 and, despite some misgivings about his youth, was given command of a ho
2 CONC rse troop, which was ambushed south of Dublin a few months later. He missed this action, and may have returned to England before his unit was disbanded in 1598, but in the following year he was listed on Essex’s Irish establishmen
2 CONC t as a garrison captain.20
2 CONT *While Needham’s Irish career ended at this point, his military experience brought him an early appointment as deputy lieutenant in 1600. His main estates were situated in Cheshire, but his administrative experience lay in Shropsh
2 CONC ire, and it was for the latter county that he was returned to Parliament as junior knight of the shire for a second time in 1604. The county seats usually rotated among the Shropshire gentry, and Needham, having just inherited his
2 CONC  estates, had a particularly good claim to election. Moreover, the junior knight for the previous Parliament, (Sir) Roger Owen*, a personal friend, was ruled out of contention because he was then serving as sheriff.21
2 CONT *Needham was not prominent within the Commons, and his appointment as sheriff of Shropshire caused him to miss the 1606-7 session entirely. However, his status as a shire knight brought some wider responsibilities, such as attenda
2 CONC nce at the conference with the Lords of 14 Apr. 1604, at which the king’s initial proposals for the Union were unveiled, and membership of bill committees for important measures such as the continuance and revival of statutes (24 
2 CONC Mar. 1604) and Anne of Denmark’s jointure estates (24 May 1604). The Shrewsbury drapers arranged for both shire knights to be named to their Welsh cloth bill (10 Mar. 1606), while Needham was one of a large group who volunteered t
2 CONC o testify about the abuses of purveyors (7 May 1604). Another local concern, the navigation of the river Severn, was reflected in his nomination to committees for the weirs’ bill (23 June 1604, 7 Feb. 1606), and he was included on
2 CONC  committees for two private bills sponsored by neighbours, Robert Devereux, 3rd earl of Essex and Sir George Booth.22 He is known to have made two speeches, one an appeal for privilege to suspend a Chancery case (15 May 1604), the
2 CONC  other an unsuccessful defence of the bill to restrict hunting rights to the gentry, which was apparently a particular interest, as he was named to committees for four other game bills.23 Finally, when wild rumours of the king’s a
2 CONC ssassination panicked the House on 22 Mar. 1606, Needham, largely because he had a saddled horse ready to hand, was one of three MPs chosen to ride post haste to Woking, Surrey to confirm that James was safe.24
2 CONT *Needham does not appear to have stood for election again, although he secured his son a seat in the Addled Parliament for Newcastle-under-Lyme. He remained active in local affairs for the rest of his life, particularly in the lie
2 CONC utenancy, and at his death the armoury at Shavington contained weapons for several dozen men.25 His purchase of an Irish viscountcy in the final weeks of James’s reign was something of a novelty, but Charles, with a war to fund, o
2 CONC penly sold such honours for cash, and several members of the Cheshire faction to which Needham was linked quickly followed his example.26 In 1629 the House of Lords questioned the Irish and Scottish viscounts’ claims to precedence
2 CONC  before English barons, and George, Lord Chaworth* mounted a spirited defence of their interests before the Privy Council. However, Needham, when approached for a donation towards this cause, rather feebly protested that his creat
2 CONC ion was justified because he had had no knowledge of it ‘till my patent was brought to me to my house in the country, which I conceive was [granted] of mere grace from His Majesty in recompense for the service I had done in Irelan
2 CONC d, for so it is expressed in my patent’.27
2 CONT *Needham was well able to afford his peerage, and his fortunes were further augmented by those of the two widows he married in quick succession at the end of his life, who had generous jointure estates and were respectively worth 
2 CONC £20,000 and £5,000 in goods.28 His will of 22 Dec. 1630 insisted that his funeral be conducted ‘without giving blacks or mourning attire (for that I hold it to be a very idle ceremony)’, and offered small legacies to his immediate
2 CONC  family, but his estates were already entailed upon his eldest son, to whom they passed at his death on 24 Nov. 1631. His widow married the 1st earl of Kellie, but was dead by the time of the latter’s demise in 1639.29
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1604-1629/member/needham-sir-robert-15678-1631 _________________
2 CONT *'''Thomas Erskine, 1st Earl of Kellie KG (1566 – 12 June 1639) was a Scottish peer.
2 CONT *'''Thomas Erskine''' was the eldest surviving son of Sir Alexander Erskine of Gogar and Margaret Home, a daughter of George Home, 4th Lord Home and Mariotta Haliburton.
2 CONT *Thomas was a school classmate and lifelong personal friend of James VI of Scotland (later James I of England) and in 1585 was made one of the Gentlemen of His Majesty's Bedchamber. He was with the king on the occasion of the Gowr
2 CONC ie Conspiracy in 1600, when James was supposedly kidnapped by the Ruthven brothers at their house in Perth. He was afterwards awarded a third of the confiscated land of the Ruthvens and given the title Lord Erskine of Dirletoun in
2 CONC  1604.[1] He was made a Privy Councilor in 1601 and accompanied the Duke of Lennox on a diplomatic visit to France.
2 CONT *Thomas travelled with James to England when James ascended the English throne in 1603 and was made Captain of the Guard (1603–1617) and Groom of the Stool in 1604 and created Viscount Fenton (or Fentoun)in 1606. He acquired Kelli
2 CONC e Castle from the 5th Lord Oliphant in 1613 and was given the barony of Kellie.[2] In 1615 he was made a Knight of the Garter and Earl of Kellie in March 1619.
2 CONT *When King James died in March 1625 the Privy Council drafted a proclamation. Kellie reminded them that James had preferred the title "King of Great Britain", and he assured the council that using "King of England and Scotland" wo
2 CONC uld not please the people of Scotland.[3]
2 CONT *He died intestate[4] in London in 1639 and was buried at Pittenweem, Fife. Thomas's son Alexander predeceased his father in 1633. He was succeeded by his grandson, Thomas Erskine, 2nd Earl of Kellie
2 CONT *The Earl of Kellie Married three times. His first marriage, on 30 November 1587 was to Ann Ogilvie, daughter of Sir Gilbert Ogilvie, of Powrie.[5] A younger brother, James Erskine, married Marie, a daughter of Adam Erskine, Comme
2 CONC ndator of Cambuskenneth on 17 May 1594.[6]
2 CONT *He married secondly in 1604 to Elizabeth Pierpoint, daughter of Sir Henry Pierpoint,[7] and '''thirdly the thrice widowed Dorothy, daughter Ambrose Smith of Cheapside.[8][4]
2 CONT *He married 2nd, Elizabeth Norreys daughter of Sir John Norreys of Heywood, Bray, Berkshire. They then forged her fathers will. http://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C5571072
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Erskine,_1st_Earl_of_Kellie ______________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 17
2 CONT *'''Erskine, Thomas (1566-1639)'''     by Alsager Richard Vian
2 CONT *'''ERSKINE, THOMAS, first Earl of Kellie (1566–1639)''', second son of Sir Alexander Erskine of Gogar, by Margaret, only daughter of George, fourth lord Home, was born in 1566. He was educated and to a great extent brought up wit
2 CONC h James I, whose marked favour he enjoyed till the king's death. In 1585 he became a gentleman of the bedchamber, and between 1594 and 1599 various charters were granted him of Mitchellis, Eastertoun, and Westertoun in Kincardines
2 CONC hire, Windingtoun and Windingtounhall, and Easterrow. He was with the king at Perth in August 1600, when the Gowrie conspiracy was foiled, and in the general scuffle received a wound in the hand, or his services on this occasion a
2 CONC  third part of Gowrie's lordship of Dirleton was granted him, and in warrandice thereof the king's barony of Comtoun, Stirlingshire. He accompanied the Duke of Lennox on his embassy to France in 1601, and on his return was admitte
2 CONC d a member of the privy council, at the meetings of which he became one of the most regular attendants. He accompanied James into England in 1603, and was appointed captain of the yeomen of the guard in succession to Sir Walter Ra
2 CONC leigh, continuing to hold the post till 1632. He was created Baron Dirleton in April 1604, was a groom of the stole in 1605, and in 1606 was raised to the dignity of Viscount Fen ton, being the first to attain that degree in Scotl
2 CONC and. Several further grants of land and a life interest in certain estates were obtained by Erskine, but he remained unsatisfied, and in October 1607 he is found writing to Salisbury proposing various schemes for his own advanceme
2 CONC nt and requesting the minister's influence with the king (Cat. State Papers, Dom. 1603-10, p. 875). The petition appears to have been disregarded, as was also another which Erskine made in the following year for a command in the L
2 CONC ow Countries. In May 1615 he was invested with the order of the Garter at the same time as Lord Knollys, and much popular interest was excited by the rivalry between the two new knights in the splendour of their procession to Wind
2 CONC sor. In 1618 Erskine projected a scheme of respite of homage, the object of which was to raise money for the king, and was rewarded in the following year by his advancement to the earldom of Kellie. A grant of 10,000l. was made to
2 CONC  Erskine in December 1625 for services to the late and present king. From 1630 to 1635 he sat on various commissions, but he did not succeed in gaining the prominence he desired in the direction of state affairs. He died 12 June 1
2 CONC 639 in London, and was buried at Pittenweem, Fifeshire. He married first, Anne, daughter of Sir Gilbert Ogilvy, by whom he had a son, Alexander, and a daughter, Anne; secondly, in 1604, the widow of Sir Edward Norreys; and on her 
2 CONC death he became the '''fourth husband of a daughter of Humphrey Smith of Cheapside, and widow of Benedict Barnham, Sir John Packington, and Robert, viscount Kilmorey, His differences with this last lady were such as to require the
2 CONC  intervention of the king.''' He was succeeded in his honours by his grandson, Thomas, the eldest son of his son Alexander (d. 1033), by Lady Anne Seton, daughter of Alexander, earl of Dunfermline.
2 CONT *[Douglas and Wood's Peerage of Scotland, ii; 17; Anderson's Scottish Nation, ii. 694; Cal. State Papers. Dom. Ser. 1603-10, pp. 100, 135; 196, 343, 470, 1611-18, pp. 286, 374, 1625-6, p. 356, 1637, p. 184; Reg. Privy Council of S
2 CONC cotland (Rolls Ser.), vii. 267.]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Erskine,_Thomas_(1566-1639)_(DNB00)
