2 CONT "The Life of Alice Barnham Wife of Sir Francis Bacon" By Alice Chambers Buntin
2 CONT
2 CONT 1928. A sketch of the life of Alice Barnham who lived from 1592 to 1650. Barnham married at the age of fourteen and escaped from the schoolroom, which may be attributed to the faults shown in her later life. The story is partly fr
2 CONC om various documents found in the London Record Office on old vellum rolls. Every attempt has been made to make certain the dates within are correct.
2 CONT
2 CONT Added by Janet Milburn 8/27/20
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 CONT
2 CONT FYI: Regarding the painting (see Media) of...........
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice, Lady Barnham and her children, 1557, tentatively which is attributed to Hans Eworth.
2 CONT
2 CONT The picture is now in the Denver Art Museum, Colorado (USA).
2 CONT
2 CONT About...........
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife, Alice, who was herself a businesswoman in the silk trade, probably played an important role in his business as well as his household, since Francis himself was increasingly occupied with civic affairs. He built a career 
2 CONC with the Draper's Company and the City of London that culminated in his terms as Sheriff of London in 1570-71 and Master of the Drapers in 1572. Had he lived longer, seniority would no doubt have brought him his turn as Lord Mayor
2 CONC , but he died in 1576, leaving three sons, the youngest of whom was only seventeen. Although Francis had kept most of his wealth in liquid form through his activities as a money-lender, he invested in 1568 in the purchase of two m
2 CONC anors in Kent, which he left to his widow for life and then to his eldest son, Sir Martin Barnham (1548-1610) much more total will be added to Sir Francis Bacon's profile, and perhaps the profiles of his sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Landed families of Britain and Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT Added by Janet Milburn/. 8/27/20
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice Bacon, Viscountess of St Albans is third cousin 12 times removed.
