2 CONC , books.google.com, (: accessed .n Illustrated Quarterly. Vol 3. Northampton, England: Dryden Press, 1886. Google Books. books.google.com. . : . Note: Northamptonshire notes & queries. Publisher: Northampton, [Eng.] : Dryden Press
2 CONC , 1886 - Edition/Format: Journal, magazine : English Editors: Walter Debenham Sweeting; Christopher Alexander Markham; John Taylor Document Type: Journal / Magazine / Newspaper OCLC Number: 1772261 Notes: Subtitle varies slightly.
2 CONC  Imprint varies. Editors: v.1-2, W.D. Sweeting; v.3,6, John Taylor; v.4-5, Christopher A. Markham. Publication suspended between 1896 and 1905 ; Oct. 1914 and Aug. 1920. "A quarterly journal devoted to the antiquities, family hist
2 CONC ory, traditions, parochial records, folk-lore, quaint customs, etc., of the country." Description: v. : ill. ; 22 cm. Other Titles: Northamptonshire notes and queries Text: Pg 173
2 CONT
2 CONT 494. ? Lord Mayors Of London Who Were Natives Of Northamptonshire (358, 409). III. Sir Thomas Pilk.ington. ? His grandfather was John Pilkington, of Oakham, in Rutland.* His father, Thomas Pilkington, settled at Northampton, and m
2 CONC arried as his second wife Anne, daughter of Edward Mercer, of that town. Two sous were born of this marriage, Richard and Thomas, the latter of whom eventually became one of the most wealthy aud popular citizens of London.
2 CONT
2 CONT The date of Thomas Pilkington's removal from Northampton does not transpire, but it is on record that he took as his wife one Hannah Bromwich, a native of London.1" He appears to have quickly obtained a position of very great infl
2 CONC uence in the affairs of the city. He was a member of the Skinners' Company, and held the office of master on three successive occasions, viz., in 1677, 1681, and 1682.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was on the 24th of June, 1681, that he was elected sheriff of London, and Narcissus Luttrell, in his Brief Historical Relation of Slate Affairs, thus refers to the event -.?
2 CONT
2 CONT (i9th May, 1681.) " Tis thought Mr. Pilkington and Mr. Dubois will be chosen sherifs for the citty of London for the ensuing year."
2 CONT
2 CONT "The 24th June was the day for the election of sherifsand officers for the citty of London . . . the persons in nomination for sherifs were Mr. alderman Pilkington, and Mr. Samuell Shute, Mr. Ralph Bos, and Mr. Humphry Nicolson. M
2 CONC r. Pilkington was first put up, who haveing apparently the majority of voices, was declared duely elected." Mr. Shute, with some contest, was put up next, and then Mr. Box. . . . The latter demanded a poll, which being granted, en
2 CONC ded that day with the result that " Pilkington and Shute carried it by hundreds of voices."
2 CONT
2 CONT "The aStb, alderman Pilkington and Samuell Shute, esqs. were sworn sherifs of the citty of London on the hustings in Guildhall, and entred on their office."
2 CONT
2 CONT During his term of office there is no doubt that Mr. Sheriff Pilkington's time was fully occupied, and some of the events in which he played a leading part must have been far from pleasant for him. Luttrell alludes to him several 
2 CONC times in his record of the events of the following year, but only once, when some eminent men of the day, "the duko of Monmouth, earls of Shaftesbury and Essex, lord Howard, lord Grey, and others," dined at his residence, Oq the i
2 CONC ~tb March, 1681-2, is the allusion at all a pleasant one.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the I3th of January, 1681-2, he was summoned before the council because he had failed to carry out an order to transport several Popish priests from Newgate to the Scilly Islands. The order, it appears, had been addressed to th
2 CONC e keeper of Newgate prison, which Pilkingtou did not consider enough to indemnify him. On this explanation being given, a fresh warrant was made out, which be promised the council should be obeyed.
2 CONT
2 CONT (March, 1682.) "At the assizes held at Southwark for the county of Surrey was tryed an action brought by Mr. Bolsworth against Mr. sheriff Pilkington, for words spoken to this effect: You are a broken fellow, goe home and pay your
2 CONC  debts. The plaintiff could not prove he had any damage thereby ; and the defendant prov'd the plaiutiff first very rudely provok'd him ; yet the jury, to the astonishment of most, brought in 8oo/. damages for the plaintiff." ?
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 3rd of May, Pilkington appealed against this " harsh judgment," but the "court would not grant a new tryall, unlesse Pilkington could procure a certificate from the judge of assize that he thought the damages were excessive
2 CONC , and the case fitt for a new tryall."d Under date lath May, Luttrell states that as Pilkington could not get the certificate he was obliged to pay the ,5^800.
2 CONT
2 CONT We now come to the time (Midsummer Day, 1682) when the citizens of London assembled to elect sheriffs in place of Pilkington and Shute. There were at this period two factions in the city....
2 CONT
