1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Kingsmill-101
2 CONT
2 CONT This profile is part of the Kingsmill Name Study.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bridgett is Kingsmill E12 in the extensively researched Kingsmill Records[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptised 2 Sep 1572/3 at Church Oakley, co. Hants.[2]. Kingsmill Records Book says 2 Sep 1573 based on an 18th century noting of the register.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Bridget Kingsmill, was the step-daughter of Lady Elizabeth Hastings, widow of son of 4th Earl of Huntingdon.[3] (?)''
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband Thomas Norris died in Ireland, she wrote letters in c. 1600 that documented her financial difficulties as a widow.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT ''In 1601, she remarried, taking as her second husband Humphrey Pakington of Harvington Hall (c.1555-1631)[4] (Not in the reference, but in other references.)
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a  wealthy man and famous (Catholic) recusant, who built a magnificent manor full of “priest holes.”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Norris_(died_1599)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas Norris (1556–1599) was an English soldier. He sat in the Irish House of Commons, and was made Lord President of Munster in Ireland. His last name is sometimes spelt Norreys. He was the fifth of the six sons of Henry Nor
2 CONC ris, 1st Baron Norreys, of Rycote House and Wytham Abbey in Oxfordshire (the latter previously in Berkshire), and his wife, Margery, the youngest daughter of John Williams, 1st Baron Williams of Thame.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Bridget, daughter of Sir William Kingsmill of Sydmonton Court in Hampshire and Bridget Raleigh, by whom he had one daughter, Elizabeth, his sole heiress, who married Sir John Jephson of Froyle in Hampshire. She died in 
2 CONC 1624, to her husband's intense grief. Their son William Jephson sat in the Long Parliament. Norreys built Mallow Castle, which remained in his daughter's family until the 1980s. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900/Norris, Thomas (1556-1599) < [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1885-1900/Norris,_Thomas_(1556-1599) link] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT Norris was apparently a man of literary tastes, and is mentioned by Lodowick Bryskett [q. v.] as one of the company to whom Spenser on a well-known occasion unfolded his project of the 'Faerie Queen.' According to Edmund Yorke—and
2 CONC  he seems to have expressed the general opinion—Norris was 'a gentleman of very great worth, modesty, and discretion.'
2 CONT
2 CONT In the skirmish 'he received a violent and venomous thrust of a pike where the jaw-bone joins the upper part of the neck.’  Norris's wound was not at first thought likely to prove fatal … but with the exertion his wound became rap
2 CONC idly worse. He was taken to his house at Mallow, and, after lingering for some time in great pain, he died there on 20 Aug. 1599. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ruins of Mallow Castle (1830)
2 CONT '''
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000197832569824&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Source:  "Picturesque views of the Antiquities of Ireland. Drawn on stone by J. D. Harding, from the sketches of R. O'C. Newenham [With text by the latter.]". Page 91.  < [https://www.flickr.com/photos/britishlibrary/11007544145
2 CONC  link] > (public domain)'' 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Harvington Hall, Worcestershire, England'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Built by the patron saint of illusionists [Jesuit builder Nicholas Owens], this Elizabethan manor house is riddled with secret cubbies for hiding priests. 
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000197833666821&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Source: Atlas Oscura < [https://www.atlasobscura.com/places/harvington-hall link] > cites Harvington Hall OZZ13X ON FLICKR (CREATIVE COMMONS)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources
2 CONT # The Kingsmill Records compiled by Major W. K. Cook Kingsmill between 1931 and 1935
2 CONT # Hampshire FHS transcript of Church Oakley Registers
2 CONT # Frye, S. & Robinson, K. (1999). Maids and Mistresses, Cousins and Queens: Women's Alliances in Early Modern England, p. 159. < [https://books.google.com/books?id=4LuYNtek7CYC&lpg=PA159&ots=wdHvQYTZnL&dq=anthony%20paulet%20and%20
2 CONC catherine%20norreys&pg=PA159#v=onepage&q=anthony%20paulet%20and%20catherine%20norreys&f=false GoogleBooks] > .
2 CONT # Emmerson, K.L. (2008). "A who's who of Tudor women." Wayback Machine. Web. < [https://web.archive.org/web/20120124125125/http://www.kateemersonhistoricals.com/TudorWomenK.htm link] > 
2 CONT # Wikipedia: Sir Thomas Norris http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Thomas_Norris
2 CONT # "Kingsmill," in Visitation of Hampshire, page 3. < [https://archive.org/stream/PedigreesFromTheVisitationOfHampshireMadeByThomasBenolt/PedigreesFromTheVisitstionOfHampshire_v64_286pgs#page/n13/mode/2up Archive.org] > . eBook.
2 CONT # https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Pakington-16 https://www.geni.com/people/Humphrey-Pakington/6000000025902802273 Humphrey Pakington of Harvington, Worcestershire, (1555-1631) married 1stly Bridget Kingsmill, and he married 2ndly Ab
2 CONC igail Sacheverell.
2 CONT # http://www.oxford-shakespeare.com/Probate/PROB_11-98-116.pdf By John Pakington, Elizabeth Newport had two sons and three daughters: -Humphrey Pakington (1555-1631), who married firstly Bridget Kingsmill (1572- 1606), and secondl
2 CONC y Abigail Sacheverell (d.1657).
