2 TITL West Woodhay House (The Earliest 'Modern' Country House)
2 NOTE West Woodhay House
3 CONT The Earliest 'Modern' Country House
3 CONT
3 CONT There is no sign of the original medieval manor house that replaced the old castle on this new site in West Woodhay. Perhaps there was also a later Tudor mansion built here for Lord Cheney's family.
3 CONT
3 CONT The present West Woodhay House was built in 1635 for Sir Benjamin Rudyard. The architect was probably Edward Carter, Inigo Jones' deputy during his extensive repairs of St. Paul's Cathedral. Rudyerd may have given considerable inp
3 CONC ut himself however, having been inspired by the work of his friend and patron, the Earl of Pembroke, at Wilton House (Wiltshire) and also by his conversations by Jones himself, with whom he sat on the Privy Council's Commission fo
3 CONC r New Building in London. The date and Latin inscriptions appear in stone panels on the north and south fronts.
