1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/William-Rives/6000000004679912914?through=6000000201446034835
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===William Ryves
2 CONT https://thereevesproject.org/data/tiki-index.php?page=Rives_William_RR73ID1
2 CONT Summary
2 CONT Father: Timothy Rives
2 CONT Mother: Elizabeth
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: About 1636, Woodstock, England
2 CONT Birth Source: Reliques of the Rives
2 CONT
2 CONT Death:
2 CONT Death Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse1:
2 CONT
2 CONT Narrative
2 CONT James Rives Childs' "Reliques of the Rives" showed William to be the first Rives in Virginia and the father of George, Robert, John, and Timothy. However, Childs later came to believe that William's half-brother Timothy could have
2 CONC  been the first Rives in America and the father of these four sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Childs had two records of a William Rives in 1684 and 1695. There is no evidence that this William was the son of Timothy b 1588 or that he was the father of John, George, Robert, and Timothy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''There was a William Reeves who died in 1698/9 in Northumberland County, VA. No evidence has been found to link him to Timothy Rives. Margery Lunseford filed to become administrator of the will of William Reeves, deceased, North
2 CONC umberland County, January 19, 1698/9.
2 CONT '''
2 CONT Here is a transcription of page 1 of Amendments to Reliques of the Rives:
2 CONT
2 CONT Twenty-eight years ago I published Reliques of the Rives (Lynchburg, 1929) and since that time certain corrections and additions have been made available by correspondents or as the results of subsequent research. As in all probab
2 CONC ility the original work will never be reprinted and as it had a fairly wide distribution, the Virginia Magazine of History and Biography has offered me the hospitality of its pages to note the emendations which I would now make in
2 CONC  my work if a new edition were ever issued.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''When the book was written I offered the conjecture that William Rives was the emigrant ancestor. I have recently had occasion to question this assumption'''. The basis for the birth of William to Timothy Ryves and his wife of O
2 CONC xford (pp. 74-75) was the slightest, and his presence in Surry County, Virginia, was made evident only as a tithable in 1684 and 1695. Mr. Laurence B. Gardiner, of Memphis, has recently put me on what I believe is a surer track. H
2 CONC e has brought my attention to the discovery of a Charles City Cocunty Order Book for 1687-1695 where, on page 421, there is found: "Capt. Henry Batte ordered to assign and swear appraisers of Estate of Timothy Rieve" (the French p
2 CONC ronunciation of the name apparently still persisting). I suggest now the likelihood that this Timothy "Rieve" or Rives was the emigrant ancestor and that he was identical with 206. Timothy Ryves, born 1625, son of Timothy and Mary
2 CONC  Ryves of Oxford (p. 51), and that he was the father of George, Robert, John, and Timothy Rives, of Virginia. The William Rives living in Surry I 1684 and 1695 may well have been another son of this Timothy. At any rate I advance 
2 CONC the hypothesis as one for future investigators to bear carefully in mind.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT Childs, James Rives. Amendments to Reliques of the Rives, The Virginia Magazine of History and Biography, 1957, page 350.
