1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography: Elizabeth Hawley (1412-1457)
2 CONT Elizabeth was named heir of her brother Nicholas Hawley in 1442, she was then aged 30.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Hawley was a 15th-century English heiress whose marriage consolidated the mercantile wealth of Dartmouth with the landed prestige of the Coplestone family of Devon. She was the daughter and eventual sole heiress of John 
2 CONC Hawley "the Younger" (d. 1436) of Dartmouth, a twelve-time Member of Parliament, and the granddaughter of the famous merchant-privateer John Hawley "the Elder." [[^1]]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lineage and Inherited Wealth
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth’s mother was possibly “Emma" Tresilian, the daughter and heiress of Sir Robert Tresilian, Chief Justice of the King's Bench. [[^2]] Sir Robert was executed for treason in 1388 following the proceedings of the Merciless P
2 CONC arliament. His widow, Emmeline Hiwishe, later married Sir John Colshull, but the Tresilian lands at Tresilian and in Cornwall were eventually secured by the Hawley family through purchase and strategic wardship. [[^3]]
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of her father in 1436 and the subsequent childless death of her brother, Nicholas Hawley, Elizabeth became the sole representative of the Hawley line. Her inheritance included a formidable fortune, encompassing the 
2 CONC Hawley merchant assets in Dartmouth and extensive properties in Cornwall. [[^4]]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth married John Coplestone (d. 1458) of Coplestone, a prominent lawyer and three-time Member of Parliament for Devon. [[^5]] The union produced several children who established significant branches of the family:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sheriff Philip Coplestone (d 1472): Son and heir, who married the Bonville heiress and served as Sheriff of Devon in 1471. [[^6]]
2 CONT * John (Copplestone) Coplestone III: Noted in family records as the second son.
2 CONT * Walter Coplestone: Established the branch of the family at Yealmpton, Devon. [[^7]]
2 CONT
2 CONT Residence and Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT The Coplestones resided in the Hawleys' prominent residence in Dartmouth, which remained the family's urban seat until 1494. At that time, it was sold to the burgesses for use as the Dartmouth Guildhall. [[^8]] Elizabeth died in 1
2 CONC 457, leaving a legacy that permanently elevated the Coplestone family’s status through the infusion of Hawley and Tresilian estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===NGS Style Register Report
2 CONT
2 CONT First Generation
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Hawley was born 1412, the daughter of John Hawley II and [possibly] Emma Tresilian. She died in 1457. She married John Coplestone, son of John Coplestone and Katherine Graas, circa 1425. He was born circa 1390 and died i
2 CONC n 1458. [[^9]]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John and Elizabeth (Hawley) Coplestone:
2 CONT
2 CONT # i. Philip Coplestone died 1472.
2 CONT # ii. John Coplestone III.
2 CONT # iii. Walter Coplestone, of Yealmpton.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
