2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/2c/37/cf/69/5344486a26415acd/2bfeb6b5-b303-4ace-9cb4-d33f2b9caf01_original.jpg?hash=0f076d421e0679d0acbe0bd6ee247c4076092a834357050c0be74daff5ec0bd0.1794643199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL The Lozenge of Elizabeth Hawley Coplestone
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE Here is the standalone Lozenge (diamond-shaped shield) of Elizabeth Hawley Coplestone, isolated on a plain background for use in your report. This is the most utility-focused version I have generated.
3 CONT
3 CONT It serves as the formal architectural blueprint of her inheritance strategy, illustrating how her family used maritime wealth to climb the social ladder.
3 CONT
3 CONT TECHNICAL HERALDIC RECORD & ANALYSIS
3 CONT
3 CONT The Lozenge of Elizabeth Hawley Coplestone
3 CONT
3 CONT By 15th-century custom, as a sole heiress, Elizabeth’s arms are displayed on a Lozenge. This shape acts as a visual deed, proving she is the "bridge" for the permanent transfer of property.
3 CONT
3 CONT TECHNICAL BLAZON (Formal Description)
3 CONT
3 CONT A Lozenge, Quartered:
3 CONT
3 CONT Quarters 1 & 4 (Hawley): Gules, an arrow in pale argent, point downwards, feathered or, between two mullets (stars) of the last.
3 CONT
3 CONT Quarters 2 & 3 (Tresilian): Gules, a pelican in her piety or.
3 CONT
3 CONT Visual Symbolism of Property Descent:
3 CONT
3 CONT 1st & 4th Quarter (Hawley): The prominent placement of the arrow and gold navigation stars (belonging to John Hawley the Elder) identifies the maritime merchant fortune as the main economic "engine." This capital was required to s
3 CONC ecure the other quarterings.
3 CONT
3 CONT 2nd & 3rd Quarter (Tresilian): The inclusion of the "Pelican in her Piety" (wounding her breast to feed her young) is essential to proving control over the restoring Tresilian bloodline. This quartering validates the Hawley/Coples
3 CONC ton legal claim to the vast Tresilian manors, including Colquite and Tremoderet.
3 CONT
3 CONT The Motto: 'VIRTUTE ET MARI' (By Virtue and the Sea). The Acceptance by heralds of this specific motto validates the consolidation: linking high-status legal "virtue" (Tresilian) with seafaring "maritime" wealth (Hawley), channele
3 CONC d into the ancient Copleston line.
