1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography: Philip Coplestone (c. 1427–1472)
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip Coplestone was a prominent member of the Devonshire landed gentry and a public official during the reigns of Henry VI and Edward IV. A descendant of an ancient West Country family, he is best known for his tenure as Sheriff
2 CONC  of Devon and for his marriage to the Bonville heiress, which brought the manor of Tamerton Foliot into the Coplestone family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lineage and Land
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip was the son and heir of John Coplestone (d. 1458), a lawyer and influential administrator in Devon, and Elizabeth Hawley, daughter and heiress of John Hawley of Dartmouth.[^1] Upon his father's death, Philip inherited the a
2 CONC ncestral seat at Coplestone along with significant holdings in Dartmouth and the South Hams.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and the Bonville Inheritance
2 CONT
2 CONT Circa 1450, Philip married Anne Bonville, the daughter and sole heiress of John Bonville and Joan de Wibbery.[^2] Anne was the granddaughter and co-heiress of John Bonville of Shute and Elizabeth FitzRoger.
2 CONT
2 CONT This marriage was of significant strategic importance, as it eventually brought the manor of Tamerton Foliot and the Ferrers estates to the Coplestones after the failure of the male Bonville line.[^3] Through this union, the Cople
2 CONC stones transitioned from being primarily associated with their namesake manor in Colebrooke to becoming one of the most powerful landowning families in South Devon, assuming the mantle of the Ferrers and Bonville lineages.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple had the following children:[^3]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Ralph Coplestone (c. 1455–1491): Son and heir, known as "The Great Coplestone." He married Ellen Arundell.
2 CONT * Edward Coplestone: Of Instow. He married Alice, daughter of John Reigny, establishing the Instow branch of the family.
2 CONT * Radigunda Coplestone: Married Walter Pollard of Horwood.
2 CONT * Sabine Coplestone: Married Albert de Wyke.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Public Service
2 CONT
2 CONT Coplestone was an active participant in regional governance. He was appointed Sheriff of Devon in 1471, serving during a period of intense political transition during the Wars of the Roses.[^4] His service as Sheriff underscored h
2 CONC is family’s position as one of the "chiefest families" in the county.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Succession
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip Coplestone died in 1472.[^5] His Inquisitions Post Mortem confirmed his extensive holdings across Devon and identified his son, Ralph Coplestone, as his heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===NGS Style Register Report
2 CONT
2 CONT First Generation
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip Coplestone was born circa 1420 in Devon, England. He died in 1472. He married Anne Bonville, daughter of John Bonville and Joan de Wibbery, circa 1450. She was born circa 1430 and died after 1472.[^6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Philip and Anne (Bonville) Coplestone:
2 CONT
2 CONT # i. Ralph Coplestone, born c. 1455; died 3 September 1491.
2 CONT # ii. Edward Coplestone, of Instow.
2 CONT # iii. Radigunda Coplestone.
2 CONT # iv. Sabine Coplestone.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
