1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography: Rafe Tooke (c. 1475 – c. 1540)
2 CONT
2 CONT Rafe Tooke (also recorded as Ralph) was an English landowner and the foundational patriarch of the Tooke family’s administrative dynasty. Seated at the Manor of Goldingston (Goldston) in the parish of Ash-next-Sandwich, Kent, he b
2 CONC ridged the gap between the medieval "atte Toke" regional gentry and the high-ranking Tudor bureaucracy of the 16th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins and Parentage
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tooke lineage is of toponymic origin, likely referring to a specific land feature in East Kent. Rafe was the son of John Tooke (c. 1450–c. 1500) of Westbury and Ash, and Mary Malmains, daughter and co-heir of the Malmains fami
2 CONC ly of Kent. Through this maternal line, Rafe inherited significant status and ancient Kentish lands, establishing the family’s right to bear arms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and the Canterbury Alliance
2 CONT
2 CONT Circa 1500, Rafe married Alice Meggs, the daughter of William Meggs of Canterbury. This union was a strategic alliance between the landed gentry (Tooke) and the rising merchant class (Meggs). Alice’s dowry and connections in Cante
2 CONC rbury provided the liquid capital that allowed the next generation of Tookes to move into the service of the Crown in London.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Estate and Property
2 CONT
2 CONT * Birth: c. 1475, Westbury or Ash-next-Sandwich, Kent.
2 CONT * Primary Seat: Manor of Goldingston, Ash, Kent. Held by knight-service.
2 CONT * Death: c. 1540, Ash-next-Sandwich, Kent.
2 CONT * Burial: Believed to be in St. Nicholas Church, Ash-next-Sandwich, Kent.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Coat of Arms & Heraldry
2 CONT The Tooke family of Popes bore a distinct coat of arms, often quartered with the families they married into (such as the Malmains and Barleys).
2 CONT *Blazon: ''Per chevron sable and argent, three griffins' heads erased counterchanged.''
2 CONT *Crest: ''A griffin’s head erased per chevron sable and argent, holding in the beak a sword point downwards.
2 CONT ''
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Researcher’s Note: The Meggs-Tooke Successions
2 CONT
2 CONT * Objective: To distinguish between the Kentish and London (Whitechapel) Meggs branches to prevent common pedigree collapses.
2 CONT * The Kentish Alliance: Rafe Tooke married Alice Meggs of Canterbury. This marriage is the foundational origin of the lineage but did not involve the Whitechapel land interests.
2 CONT * The Whitechapel Alliance: Thomas Tooke (grandson of Rafe) married Anne Meggs of Whitechapel. Anne was the daughter of the wealthy London Draper, William Meggs the Elder (d. 1598).
2 CONT * Conflict Resolution: Pedigrees frequently collapse these two women into a single "Alice Meggs." However, the 1678 inheritance (including the Manor of Wallington) flowed strictly through Anne Meggs's line (the London branch) foll
2 CONC owing the extinction of her male relatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Appendix: Manorial Descriptions from the 1588 IPM
2 CONT
2 CONT Subject: William Tooke I (died 4 Dec 1588)
2 CONT Source: Chancery Inquisitions Post Mortem, Series II; Vol. 222, No. 46.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of William Tooke I, the following manorial interests were documented as his primary holdings in Hertfordshire:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Manor of Popes (Hatfield): Described as the principal capital messuage with various lands, meadows, and pastures. Held of the Queen as of her Manor of Hatfield in socage by fealty and a yearly rent.
2 CONT * Manor of Essendon: Held by knight-service. The IPM identifies specific acreage including "woodland and turbary rights" associated with the Essendon parish.
2 CONT * Manor of Wormley: Documented as a late acquisition, held of the Queen in chief by the service of the 100th part of a knight's fee.
2 CONT * Messuages in Holborn: Significant urban property in London (St. Andrew’s Parish) providing income via rental tenements.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Register Report: Generation One & Two
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation One
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Rafe Tooke was born circa 1475 in Ash-next-Sandwich, Kent, and died circa 1540 in Ash. He married Alice Meggs, daughter of William Meggs of Canterbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Rafe and Alice (Meggs) Tooke:
2 CONT # 2. i. William Tooke I, born circa 1508.
2 CONT # 3. ii. Mary Tooke, married John Newce of Much Hadham.
2 CONT # 4. iii. Anne Tooke, married John Gyll of Wyddial.
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation Two
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. William Tooke I (Rafe) was born circa 1508 and died 4 December 1588 in Hatfield, Hertfordshire. He married Alice Barley, daughter of Robert Barley and Catherine North. William was the Auditor of the Court of Wards and Liveries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of William and Alice (Barley) Tooke:
2 CONT # 5. i. Walter Tooke, born circa 1540.
2 CONT # 6. ii. Thomas Tooke, born circa 1542; married Anne Meggs of Whitechapel.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Church Memorials & FindAGrave Links
2 CONT * Memorial for William Tooke I (Hatfield): St. Etheldreda's Church.  https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/145450410/william-tooke
2 CONT * St. Nicholas Church (Ash, Kent): (perhaps) Burial site of Rafe Tooke. https://www.findagrave.com/cemetery/2335543/st.-nicholas-churchyard
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
