1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography: Mary Newce (née Tooke) (c. 1510 – c. 1560)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Tooke was a member of the English landed gentry during the Tudor period. As the daughter of Rafe Tooke of Kent and the sister of the Auditor of the Court of Wards, her marriage served as a vital link in the network of adminis
2 CONC trative families that managed the county of Hertfordshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins and Parentage
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary was born circa 1510, likely at the Manor of Goldingston in Ash-next-Sandwich, Kent. She was the daughter of Rafe Tooke (c. 1475–c. 1540) and Alice Meggs of Canterbury. Her paternal lineage traced back to the medieval "atte To
2 CONC ke" family of East Kent, while her maternal lineage connected her to the influential Meggs merchant family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Property
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary married John Newce (also recorded as Newys or Nuce) of Much Hadham, Hertfordshire. John Newce was a gentleman of significant standing, and through this marriage, Mary transitioned from the Kentish sphere of her father to the 
2 CONC Hertfordshire administrative circle of her husband and brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Property: Her primary residence was the Newce family estate in Much Hadham. While she did not inherit the Tooke manors (which followed the male line), her marriage portion helped solidify the Newce family's local influence.
2 CONT *IPM (Inquisition Post Mortem): Mary does not have a standalone IPM, as she was a married woman (feme covert) under English common law. However, she is cited in the records of her father, Rafe Tooke, and her husband, John Newce, c
2 CONC onfirming her identity and dowry rights.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Burial
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary died circa 1560. She is buried in the St. Andrew’s Church, Much Hadham, Hertfordshire. This church served as the primary burial site for the Newce family, and while individual brasses for this period are often worn, the paris
2 CONC h registers confirm the family's presence in the "Newce Chapel" or specific manorial pews.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Researcher’s Notes & Cautions
2 CONT
2 CONT Objective: To prevent genealogical "merging" and clarify the Meggs-Tooke connection.
2 CONT
2 CONT * The "Two Marys" Caution: Do not confuse this Mary Tooke with her niece, Mary Tooke (daughter of William I), who married into the Gyll family. This Mary (Generation 2) is the aunt of the younger Mary (Generation 3).
2 CONT * Meggs Lineage Clarification: As the daughter of Alice Meggs of Canterbury, Mary Newce is not a descendant of the Whitechapel Meggs line. She died over a century before the 1678 "Great Partition" and her descendants had no claim 
2 CONC to those specific London properties.
2 CONT * Name Variations: In some visitation records, the surname is spelled Nuce. Researchers should check Much Hadham parish records under both "Newce" and "Nuce."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Register Report: The Tooke-Newce Descent
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation One
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Rafe Tooke was born circa 1475 in Ash-next-Sandwich, Kent, and died circa 1540. He married Alice Meggs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Rafe and Alice (Meggs) Tooke:
2 CONT
2 CONT # i. William Tooke I, born c. 1508; died 1588.
2 CONT # 3. ii. Mary Tooke, born c. 1510.
2 CONT # 4. iii. Anne Tooke, born c. 1512
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation Two
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Mary Tooke (Rafe) was born circa 1510 and died circa 1560. She married John Newce of Much Hadham.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John and Mary (Tooke) Newce:
2 CONT
2 CONT # i. Clement Newce, born c. 1535; of Much Hadham.
2 CONT # ii. William Newce, identified in contemporary pedigrees.
2 CONT # iii. Mary Newce, married William Gyll (uniting two Tooke-descended lines).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Bibliography
2 CONT # England. Prerogative Court of Canterbury. "Will of William Meggs, Draper of Whitechapel." Proved 22 May 1598. PROB 11/91/429. https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/D932228
2 CONT # "The Manor of Wallington: Records of the Tooke Inheritance, 1678." Hertfordshire Archives and Local Studies. https://www.hertfordshire.gov.uk/services/libraries-and-archives/hertfordshire-archives-and-local-studies/
2 CONT # Metcalfe, Walter C., ed. The Visitations of Hertfordshire, 1572. London: Harleian Society, 1886. https://archive.org/details/visitationofhert00phil
2 CONT # Page, William, ed. "Parishes: Hatfield." In A History of the County of Hertford: Volume 3, 91-111. London: Victoria County History, 1912. British History Online. https://www.british-history.ac.uk/vch/herts/vol3/pp91-111
2 CONT # "Mary Newce (Tooke) - Genealogy." Geni.com. https://www.geni.com/people/Mary-Tooke/6000000224295413904
2 CONT # St. Andrew's Church, Much Hadham (Newce Burials):
2 CONT # https://www.findagrave.com/church/2330754/st-andrews-churchyard
2 CONT # St. Nicholas Churchyard (Ash, Kent - Tooke Origins):
2 CONT # https://www.findagrave.com/cemetery/2335543/st.-nicholas-churchyard
