1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1560
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography: Anne Gyll (née Tooke) (c. 1512 – c. 1560)
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Tooke was a member of the English landed gentry whose marriage served as a primary link in the network of administrative families in Tudor-era Hertfordshire. As the daughter of Rafe Tooke and the sister of the Auditor of the 
2 CONC Court of Wards, her placement at Wyddial Hall solidified the family's regional influence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins and Parentage
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was born circa 1512, likely at the Manor of Goldingston in Ash-next-Sandwich, Kent. She was the daughter of Rafe Tooke (c. 1475–c. 1540) and Alice Meggs of Canterbury. Her paternal lineage is derived from the "atte Toke" regi
2 CONC onal gentry of East Kent, while her maternal line connected her to the wealthy merchant class of Canterbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Property
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne married John Gyll (also recorded as Gill) of Wyddial, Hertfordshire. John Gyll was a significant figure in the county’s governance and a prominent landowner.
2 CONT
2 CONT Property: Her life was centered at Wyddial Hall, the seat of the Gyll family. This estate was a focal point for the landed interests of North Hertfordshire during the mid-16th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT IPM & Legal Status: As a married woman (feme covert), Anne does not possess a standalone Inquisition Post Mortem. Her legal identity and the transfer of her marriage portion are established through the IPMs of her husband, John Gy
2 CONC ll, and her father, Rafe Tooke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Burial
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne died circa 1560. She is buried in St. Giles Church, Wyddial, Hertfordshire. The Gyll family maintained a private chapel and several elaborate memorial brasses within the church, documenting their marital alliances and status 
2 CONC as lords of the manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Researcher’s Notes & Cautions
2 CONT
2 CONT Objective: To distinguish between multiple generations of Tooke-Gyll marriages.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Generational Conflation: Researchers must distinguish this Anne (Generation 2) from later Tooke descendants who married into the Gyll line. This Anne is the aunt of the Mary Tooke who married William Gyll.
2 CONT * Meggs Lineage Clarification: As the daughter of Alice Meggs of Canterbury, Anne is not a descendant of the Whitechapel Meggs line and had no connection to the 1678 "Great Partition" of the Whitechapel fortune.
2 CONT * Geni Linkage: While digital profiles may occasionally show her as a "standalone" figure, the Visitations of Hertfordshire (1572) explicitly list her as the daughter of Rafe Tooke and Alice Meggs.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Register Report: The Tooke-Gyll Descent
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation One
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Rafe Tooke was born circa 1475 and died circa 1540. He married Alice Meggs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Rafe and Alice (Meggs) Tooke:
2 CONT
2 CONT # i. William Tooke I, born c. 1508; died 1588.
2 CONT # ii. Mary Tooke, born c. 1510; married John Newce.
2 CONT # 4. iii. Anne Tooke, born c. 1512.
2 CONT
2 CONT Generation Two
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Anne Tooke (Rafe) was born circa 1512 and died circa 1560. She married John Gyll of Wyddial.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John and Anne (Tooke) Gyll:
2 CONT
2 CONT # i. George Gyll, born c. 1535; succeeded to Wyddial Hall.
2 CONT # ii. John Gyll, of Buntingford.
2 CONT # iii. Margaret Gyll, married into the local gentry.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Bibliography
2 CONT
