1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Henry Churchill = Phyllis Oldnell     (Henry: Approx. 1594 –1652, born in Ambrosden (or close by), died in Steeple Claydon, of Steeple Claydon and moved to Deddington, a carpenter, farmed land in Duchy Manor for 2 
2 CONC years with his brother and cousin Justinian then moved to Steeple Claydon and was a client of the manor there for 10 years when civil war broke out) (Phyllis:  - 1652, a sister or sister in law of Joseph Townsend of Princes Risbor
2 CONC ough, had 10 children including 3 sets of twins with 6 boys and 4 girls) (source: http://www.deddingtonhistory.uk/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/12912/ChurchillChroniclescilckablecontentsjan15.pdf and https://www.geni.com/people/Pene
2 CONC lope-Churchill/6000000025795068892)
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes on Henry Churchill, yeoman, carpenter and farmer: (from: The ‘Churchill Cronicels)  Unlike his brother, Thomas of Deddington, Henry of Steeple Claydon describes himself in his will as a 'gent', and he refers to the house in 
2 CONC which he lived as 'My Mansion - House'. Fortunately, he mentions that it adjoins his close, called 'Sande Furlong', and this enables one to identify it in Steeple Claydon, where it still stands. It is of timber, brick and wattle c
2 CONC onstruction, with a thatched roof, dating from the early sixteenth century. It had a long, attic storey, under the roof, stretching the whole length of the house, and, below that, two storeys, which must each have had at least thr
2 CONC ee large rooms. There is some fine linenfold panelling in the front downstairs room. Henry had the whole house to himself and his large family, but, in 1952, when the present author visited it, it was divided and occupied by two f
2 CONC amilies. Henry evidently owned the freehold, but later the house must have passed into Claydon Manor, owned by the Verneys. When photographed thirty-two years ago the features of the house remained much as they must have been in t
2 CONC he early seventeenth century, but a visit made twenty years later showed that contemporary tenants have made a number of alterations, removing a triple chimney, building a porch over the main entrance door, increasing the size of 
2 CONC some of the windows, and exposing the brickwork at the northern end of the house.  Henry possessed quite a lot of land. He owned three closes in Steeple Claydon, the one adjoining his house called Sande Furlong, and two others cal
2 CONC led, respectively, Peartree Hill and Gabriel’s Ground. He also possessed a stretch of pasture in Steeple Claydon, called Bushey Close, and three meadows, two of them called Cowmeade and Bourne Heads. In addition, he possessed some
2 CONC  freehold land in Leighton Buzzard in Bedfordshire, called Midsomer Plot, and eleven acres of copyhold arable land  in the common field there. As is usual in England, many of these field names are (at any rate) known today, even i
2 CONC f not often used. He left £955 10s. to various members of his family in his will, which was substantially more than his elder brother, Thomas of Deddington, left, and all the land mentioned above to his five elder sons. (See Henry
2 CONC 's will PCC. Brent 367.) His descendants spread to Northamptonshire, Nottinghamshire, and, finally, to South Africa. He married Phyllis Adnell (or Oldnell), a sister or sister-in-law of Joseph Townsend, of Princes Risborough. They
2 CONC  had ten children, six boys and four girls; they will be described at pp. 76 and 77 et seq. And: Origins of the surname Oldnell: (from: various sources) Recorded in a number of spellings including Oldale, Oldall, Oldenall, Oldnall
2 CONC , Oldhall, Holdall, Oldnell, Auldel and Ouldale, this is an English surname. It is clearly locational, and probably originates from a place called Old Hall of which there are a number of examples in the counties of Essex, Westmore
2 CONC land, Hertfordshire, Suffolk and Oxfordshire. These all refer to original manor houses that in the period between the 15th and 18th centuries were largely replaced by more imposing buildings, in some cases the "Old Hall" was divid
2 CONC ed up and used as a number of dwellings, thereby creating a "new" village. 
