1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''The Olds (Old, Ould) family in England and America : American genealogy (1915)
2 CONT *http://archive.org/details/oldsoldouldfamil00olds
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/oldsoldouldfamil00olds#page/21/mode/1up
2 CONT *1. ROGER WOLD, of Yolthorpe, Co. York, Thane, living on his estate between 1189 and 1199 in the reign of Richard I. 
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT **	2. Agnes, married Godfrey, son of William Erneburg, of Flixtune, Co. York. 
2 CONT **	*3. William. 
2 CONT **	4. Roger, a chaunting monk of Whitby. 
2 CONT **	5. John, a minstrel to the King. Composed a miracle play called "St. Cuthbert," performed at York on March 20th, about 1223. 
2 CONT *3. WILLIAM WOLD (2d in descent), son of Roger (1), married Alice, daughter of Wm. Erneburg, of Flixtune, his sister's sister-in-law. He lived in the reign of King John and Henry III, and his seal is 
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/oldsoldouldfamil00olds#page/22/mode/1up
2 CONT *appended to a charter in the British Museum (Campbell I, 25, Hy. Ill, 1239). 
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT **	*6. John Le Olde. 
2 CONT **	7. Roger Wold, of whom nearly nothing is known. 
2 CONT **	*8. Thomas, married Christiana. He went south with Robert (9). 
2 CONT **	9, Robert Old went south with Thomas (8) ; for want of means stopped at Cambridge, where he entered the service of the Lord of Granteste "For velleinage Socage" or "as a free man." (List Vavasouri for Lordship of Granteste. Rob
2 CONC erti Hundridorum Temp. Edw. I.) 
2 CONT *6. JOHN LE OLDE (3d in descent), son of William (3), lived at Liddington, near Cirencester, and was manucaptor for Sir John de Langleye, knight of the shire for Gloucester. (Parliamentary wills, Edw. I, 1297, and Edw. II, 1311.) 
2 CONC The office of manucaptor was always held by a person of position and consideration. He lived to a great age. 
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT **	*10. Richarde, born 1250. 
2 CONT **	11. Christian. 
2 CONT **	12. Agnes, a nun. 
2 CONT *8. THOMAS, son of William (3), married Christiana. He went south with Robert (9) to Colchester, Co. 
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/oldsoldouldfamil00olds#page/23/mode/1up
2 CONT *Essex, 1296-1301. His name appears on a taxation list for that town from 24 to 29 years of Edward I. (Parliamentary Rolls, Vol. I, British Museum.) 
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT **	*13. William. 
2 CONT **	14. Richarde de la Wolde, a priest; through the influence of John of Bonnecomb he was instituted rector of Winterborn St. Nicholas, Co. Dorset, 5 Cal. Mch. 9, Edw. III. (Chandler.) On April 4, 1324, he was presented to a medist
2 CONC y, Winterbourne Clenstone, (List of rectors, Hutchings' Dorset.) 
2 CONT **	15. Thomas. Vide proffers of service raised at the muster of Carlisle, St. John's Eve, 28 Edw. I. 
2 CONT **	16. Henry, married Alice, daughter of John de Bourle (Burley in the New Forest), (Preceptor Warrant Rotulorum originalium in Curia Seaceaurii abbreviation, Vol. II, p. 269.) 
2 CONT *10. RICHARDE (4th in descent), son of John (6), born in 1250. His name was the first on the pedigree of Old of Rowton (first of the Shropshire Olds), and he is stated to be "fourth in descent." 
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/oldsoldouldfamil00olds#page/24/mode/1up
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT **	17. William, of Momersfield, Co. Salop; living in 1331. 
2 CONT **	18. Agnes Wold, married Henry de Rowton. 
2 CONT **	Children. 
2 CONT ***		Agnes, married Richard Hawliss. 
2 CONT ***		William de Rowton, of Rowton Castle, Co. Salop; died without issue. 
2 CONT ***		Alice de Rowton, married Galfridus Wold (21). 
2 CONT **	*19. Roger Wold. 
2 CONT *13. WILLIAM, son of Thomas (8), succeeded to the estate of his kinsman, John le Olde, of Averfel, Isle of Wight. In Doomsday Book he is mentioned as one of the three Thanes who held their lands of the King. 
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT **	20. John Le Olde, born 1304. (Calendarium Inquis., Post-mortem, 1345, No. 47 "Terra Regis in Insula Victes.") 
2 CONT *19. ROGER WOLD (5th in descent), son of Richarde (10), purchased the estate of Sheriff Hales, Co. Salop, the 25th of Edw. Ill, of "ye lord of ye manor" (1350). 
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT **	*21. Galfridus. 
2 CONT **	22. Walter Holde, of Stokestown, Co. Salop. (Exchequer Subsidies, 1325.) 
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/oldsoldouldfamil00olds#page/25/mode/1up
2 CONT *21. GALFRIDUS WOLD (6th in descent), son of Roger (19), married his cousin, Alice de Rowton, and thus came into possession of Sheriff Hales and Rowton Hall. 
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT **	*23. William. 
2 CONT **	24. John (Campbell Charters, British Museum, 8 Hy. Ill, 1406). (First of OLD of YEAVELY, CO. DERBY.) 
2 CONT **	25. Robert, sub-prior of Cern Abbey at the election of Abbot Godmanston. 
2 CONT *23. WILLIAM WOLD (7th in descent), son of Galfridus (21), living at Rowton in 1406. 
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT **	*26. Roger. 
2 CONT **	27. Agnes, married a Benthall. 
2 CONT *26. ROGER WOLD (8th in descent), son of William (23). married Mary, a relation of the celebrated Sir John Talbot; in 1436 he went to France on some military service. In 1451 he was bailiff and receiver of rents for the manors of 
2 CONC Cowley, Colon and Burghall. 
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT **	*28. John. 
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/oldsoldouldfamil00olds#page/26/mode/1up
2 CONT *28. JOHN (9th in descent), son of Roger (26), married Jane Eyton. 
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT **	29. John, a priest; became a reformer and was disinherited ; was a friend of Latimer and Cranmer. Full particulars of him will be found in the Royal Historical Societies' Transactions, Vol. II, p. 198 (1572). He was ordained pr
2 CONC iest in 1540, was Prebend of Hereford and was presented to the vicarage of Cubbington, Co. Warwick, by the Duchess of Somerset. He wrote as "John Bayle" and published many books, among them "Acquittal of King Edward VI" (Nov. 7, 1
2 CONC 555). (Strype's "Cranmer," Vol. I, p. 397. Memorials Ecclesiastical, Vol. I, pt. II, p. 47, and Vol. II, pt. II, p. 273; Haigh's list of books published in England prior to 1600 in the library of King's Inn, Dublin, 1858, p. 14.) 
2 CONC He was exiled in the reign of Queen Mary. 
2 CONT **	30. Agnes, became a reformer; disinherited. 
2 CONT **	*31. William. 
2 CONT *31. WILLIAM OLDE (10th in descent), son of John (28), lived at Staunton ; married his cousin, Elizabeth, daughter of Hez. Eyton, Esq. His name appears in the Salopian Exchequer Subsidies, 1522-1545. He succeeded to the estates on
2 CONC  the death of his father, because John, the eldest son, became a reformer. (Wm. Watkins Old, of Hereford, uncle of Miss Susie Gascoyne Old, was at Broseley in 
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/oldsoldouldfamil00olds#page/27/mode/1up
2 CONT *1861, and in Quaritch's shop found a deed relating to some land at Staunton, dated 1584, and signed by Wm. Olde.) 
2 CONT *Children. 
2 CONT ** 	*32. Thomas, died 1599. 
2 CONT **'''	*33. Richard, died 1599. From him are descended the OLDS OF DORSET, OULDS OF IRELAND, OLDS OF SHERBORNE, and OLDS OF AMERICA. For continuation of these lines see OLD OF DORSET. 
2 CONT **	34. John, married Margaret; afterwards Elizabeth. Founder of the line of OLD OF RAMSEY, Co. Huntingdon. 
2 CONT *32. THOMAS OLDE, son of William (31), died 1599; married Agnes, daughter of James Mason, of Gt. Chotwell; she was granted administration of her husband's will, June 29, 1599, at H. M. C. of Probate, London, and was buried at Bros
2 CONC eley, December 19, 1599. (Parish Register.) 
2 CONT *Children.
2 CONT ** .... etc.
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/oldsoldouldfamil00olds#page/30/mode/1up
2 CONT *'''1. RICHARD OLDE (11th in descent)''', son of William. '''died at Broseley, 1599; married Agnes Courtenay. He is mentioned in the list of bailiffs of the Wenlock Corporation, 26 Eliz., also 1610 and 1617. 
2 CONT *http://archive.org/stream/oldsoldouldfamil00olds#page/31/mode/1up
2 CONT *'''He died upwards of 90 years old. From him are descended the Olds of Dorset, Devon and Cornwall. Administration granted to his widow, May 9, 1599, at H. M. C. of Probate, London. Agnes Courtenay, his wife''', was kinswoman of L
2 CONC ord Courtenay; '''through this marriage the estate of Oldsoke, Co. Somerset, came into the Old family about half a century later. 
2 CONT *'''Children. 
2 CONT **     *2. William, died December 18, 1566.
2 CONT **     3. Richard, St. Veryan, Co. Cornwall, near Tregory; married Charity Penrose. (OLDE OF CORNWALL.)
2 CONT **     4. Edward Ould, living in 1544. (Decree Books in the Record Office, Vol. II, p. 338, Hilary I, Phil. and Mary, 1554)
2 CONT *2. WILLIAM OLDE (12th in descent), '''son of Richard (1)''', married Anne (Eleanor Courtenay) .... He died December 18, 1566, before his father, and was buried in Sherborne Churchyard. (Parish Register.)
2 CONT *Children.
2 CONT **     *Thomas, died 1593.
2 CONT **     *6. Bartholomew.
2 CONT **     7. John, went to Lackingdon, Co. Essex; married Anne Osbourne.
2 CONT **     Children.
2 CONT ***          Anne.
2 CONT * .... etc. ___________
