2 CONT * (i)	Sir Owen Hopton of Cockfield Hall (b 1519, d 09.1591, Lieutenant of the Tower of London, MP) 	m. Anne Itchingham (d before 25.01.1600, dau of Sir Edward Echingham or Itchingham)
2 CONT **(a)	Sir Arthur Hopton of Cockfield Hall & Witham Priory (d 20.11.1607, MP) 	m. Rachel Hall (d 1629, dau of Edmund Ball of Gratford)  … 
2 CONT *** 	((5))	'''Selina Hopton''' m1. Henry Bodenham of Fugglestone (d 1595)  	m2. Robert Baskett of Dewlish (d 1613)''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/zwrk/temp66.php#bask1
2 CONT
2 CONT * 	i.	Thomas Baskett of Dulish 	m1/2. Bridget Morton (dau (sb sister?) of Thomas Morton of Clenston)
2 CONT **a.	Robert Baskett of Dulish 	m. Selena Hopton (dau of Sir Arthur Hopton of Witham)
2 CONT *** 	(1)	Thomas Baskett of Dulish (a 1623) the last mentioned by Visitation
2 CONT **** 	(A)	Elizabeth Baskett probably of this generation 	m. Thomas Turberville of Bere Regis (b c1622, after 18.05.1702)
2 CONT *** 	(2)	Dorothy Baskett 	m. Thomas Turbervile of Bear
2 CONT *** 	(3)+	other issue - Robert, Rachell, Selina, Honnor, Bridget
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000189799891833&size=large}}
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ===Sources:
2 CONT * https://heraldryofthewestcountry.wordpress.com/2022/09/03/baskett-at-charlton-adam/ According to the visitations, Thomas’ son Robert married Selena (Salnicia,) daughter of Sir Arthur Hopton. The wife’s arms here, however, are no
2 CONC t those of Hopton. Selena, moreover was not the heiress of Sir Arthur who had a dozen or so surviving children including several sons. The visitation shows that Robert’s son quartered this coat, which would only have been the case
2 CONC  if his mother (or some earlier female ancestress) had been an heiress.  There is a mystery here. The wife’s coat does not seem to relate to Selena Hopton. Possibly Robert Baskett had another wife who was the mother of young Thoma
2 CONC s of the visitation. It is scarcely probable that young Thomas would not have known who his mother was. The other possibility is that the shield represents a marriage earlier in the Baskett pedigree.
2 CONT
