1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===EDWARD PYE (?-1696)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://msa.maryland.gov/megafile/msa/speccol/sc2900/sc2908/000001/000426/html/am426--669.html “Biographical Dictionary of the Maryland Legislature 1635-1789” by Edward C. Papenfuse, et. al.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT *IMMIGRATED: by 1682, as a free adult. 
2 CONT *RESIDED: in Charles County.
2 CONT * FAMILY BACKGROUND. FATHER: probably John Pye (1620-1701) of Thruckston, Hereford, England. MOTHER: probably Blanche (?-1708), daughter of Edward Lingen, Esq. 
2 CONT *BROTHER: Walter (?-by 1699), of Jamaica, West Indies, a merchant. 
2 CONT *MARRIED by October 1682 Anne (?-1693), widow of Benjamin Rozer (?-1681). Anne was the daughter of Henry Sewall (?-1665). She was the stepdaughter of Charles Calvert, 3rd Lord Baltimore (1637- 1714/15).
2 CONT *CHILDREN. SONS: Charles, who married Mary Booth at St. Gervais, a refuge for Jacobites who fled England with the king; Walter, Gent., who married Henrietta, daughter of John Tant of St. Mary's County; and Henry, who probably died
2 CONC  young. STEPSON: Notley Rozer (1673-1727). DAUGHTER: Ann.  STEPDAUGHTERS: Mary Rozer (1675-?);  Jane Rozer. 
2 CONT * RELIGIOUS AFFILIATION: Catholic. 
2 CONT *SOCIAL STATUS AND ACTIVITIES: perhaps related to George Pye (?-?), who disappeared from Maryland records in 1642/43; arrived in Maryland with high standing, and almost immediately married the proprietor's stepdaughter and receive
2 CONC d appointment to the Council. 
2 CONT *OCCUPATIONAL PROFILE, planter.  PUBLIC CAREER:
2 CONT *LEGISLATIVE SERVICE: Upper House, 1684 (appointed by the 3rd session of the 1682-1684 Assembly), 1686-1688. 
2 CONT *OTHER PROVINCIAL OFFICES: Council, 1683-1689; justice, Provincial Court, 1683-1689; Board of Deputy Governors, 1688-1689. 
2 CONT *LOCAL OFFICE: supervisor of town officers, Charles County, 1686. 
2 CONT *MILITARY SERVICE: colonel, 1685-1689. 
2 CONT *STANDS ON PUBLIC/PRIVATE ISSUES: lost all offices after Revolution of Protestant Associators in 1689; Catholicism excluded him from public office thereafter. 
2 CONT *WEALTH DURING LIFETIME. LAND AT FIRST ELECTION: at least 3,656 acres of the Rozer estate acquired through his marriage; acquired 5,000 additional acres by 1688.
2 CONT *WEALTH AT DEATH. DIED between May and September 1696 in Charles County. 
2 CONT *PERSONAL PROPERTY: TEV, at least £1,199.6.0 (including 2 servants, 83 slaves, 372 oz. plate, and a large map "of the whole world"); FB as recorded, £3.7.9.
2 CONT *ADDITIONAL COMMENT: This final balance is inaccurate, because the administrator of Pye's estate was lax in taking into account unappraised inventories and numerous debts due the estate; thus, though recorded, they never appeared 
2 CONC in his final accounting. LAND: over 5,000 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.robertsewell.ca/othersewell.html#maryland
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann Sewell [F25], married 1) Benjamin Rozier 2) Edward Pye.  She was the daughter of  Henry Sewall, of Warwick & St. Mary's and Jane Lowe, Lady Baltimore.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepyeplate.blogspot.com/2015/01/
2 CONT
2 CONT Various records show that Edward Pye had a tobacco plantation in the area that was known as Port Tobacco and was a slave owner. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepyeplate.blogspot.com/2019/09/col-edward-pye-1640-1697-charles-co.html
2 CONT
2 CONT It didn’t appear that Edward’s will provided for his wife Ann, suggesting that she predeceased him. No record for her death was found.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT *           1. Charles (c1682-1758) m. Mary Booth c 1720 in St. Gervais,                       France – 8 children
2 CONT *           2. Henry (c1683-1716)
2 CONT *           3. Walter (1685-1749) m. Mary Taunt (aka Tant) 1703                                      Prince George’s Co. – 9 children
2 CONT *           4. Ann (c1689-c1720) m. Robert Needham in 1714, England –                       3 children
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Ann_Sewell_%282%29
2 CONT
2 CONT *Tracts Layed Out In Calvert County Prior To April 23, 1696, Before The Formation Of Prince George's County.
2 CONT *Pye, Madam Anne/ Pye Anna's Grove V-8/ Oct 2, 1686/ L 22 f 258
2 CONT *COURT OF 9 Aug 1692, Liber Q, Page 57 - Regarding Notley Rozer, orphan son of Col. Benjamin Rozer; Anne, relict of Benjamin, m. Edward Pye (Charles County Land Records, Volume III, p 8)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I19297&tree=Tree1
2 CONT
2 CONT * Col. Benjamin Rozier 19.164 A £867.3.6 £1983.16.10 Jul 17 1699
2 CONT * Received from: William Deal.
2 CONT * Distribution to: Madam Anne Rozier, Notley Rosier (son).
2 CONT * Administrator: Mr. Notley Rosier (administrator of Col, Edward Pye (administrator of deceased)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.colonial-settlers-md-va.us/getperson.php?personID=I14599&tree=Tree1
2 CONT
2 CONT * Col. Edward Pye 19,163 A CH £1150.13.6 £1199.6.0 Jul 17 1699
2 CONT * Payments to: Notley Rozier his share of his father's (unnamed) estate.
2 CONT * Administrator Notley Rosier.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Anne_Pye_%281%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Pye in Barnes, Robert. MORE ON EDWARD PYE, in The Archivist's Bulldog Vol. 11 No. 17, Newsletter of the Maryland State Archives, September 22, 1997. [https://msa.maryland.gov/msa/refserv/bulldog/bull97/html/bul11-17.html link] 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Edward Pye, son of John, came to Maryland in 1682 and died in 1696. He married Anne, widow of Benjamin Rozer, daughter of Henry and Jane (Lowe) Sewall, sister of Nicholas Sewell. Pye was the step-son-in-law of Charles Calvert, 3r
2 CONC d Lord Baltimore, and Anne was the sister-in-law of Philip Calvert. Edward Pye was a member of the Upper House and Governor's Council, a member of the Board of Deputy Governors, and a colonel in the army. At his death, his persona
2 CONC l property was valued at £1,200.0.0.
2 CONT *Edward and his wife Anne were the parents of the following children: Henry, living 1716; Walter, died s.p. [sine prole, this is, without issue] in MD, having married a daughter of John Faunt of St. Mary's Co.; Charles; and Anne, 
2 CONC married on 23 September 1714 to Robert Needham of Upper Hillston, County Monmouth.
2 CONT *Walter Pye, son of Edward and Anne, was described as "late of MD in the West Indies," when on 4 August 1699 administration was granted to Robert Chaplin, guardian of Charles, Walter, and Anne Pye, minors, nephews and niece of the
2 CONC  deceased. (Withington, "Maryland Gleanings in England," Maryland Historical Magazine 2:282).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://thepyeplate.blogspot.com/2015/01/ “ Col. Edward Pye 1640 – 1697”]
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a report in the Maryland Bulldog that Edward Pye was from Dymock, in Gloucestershire. However, going through various Visitation and genealogical accounts for several counties, there seems to be no other reference to Dymoc
2 CONC k or that any member of the Pye family resided there or leased it out to anyone.                                                   
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Pye was b. c 1647. He was the son of John and Blanche Lingen Pye.  John was the 7th son of Sir Walter and Joane Rudhall Pye of the Mynde, Much Dewchurch, Herefordshire, England. Sir Walter was the Knight Attorney General Co
2 CONC urt of Wards and Liveries and was considered to be the richest man in Herefordshire, at one time. He invested  with the West Country Adventurers and helped to finance Lord Baltimore’s (George Calvert) settlement of Ferryland in Ne
2 CONC wfoundland.  
2 CONT
2 CONT John graduated from Exeter in 1637, possibly as an attorney. He married Blanche Lingen soon after and they had a very large family of 23 children, Edward being one of the older ones.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Col. Edward came from such a large family, to date I’ve only been able to discover the names of six of them. Nothing is known about Col. Edward’s childhood, whether it was spent in England, Barbados, Jamaica (where some P
2 CONC ye’s also had sugar plantations), Newfoundland, or the Colonies, before he settled in Maryland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward was well established in Maryland by c. 1682 when he married Anne Sewell (Sewall) Rozer, a widow with one son, Notley Rozer.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
