0 @N6000000003463826827@ NOTE William Herbert, 1st Earl of Pembroke (c. 1423-1469), known as "Black William", was the grandson of Dafydd Gam, an adherent of King Henry V of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Herbert supported the Yorkist faction during the Wars of the Roses, even though his father, William ap Thomas, had been an adherent of Henry VI. Herbert was rewarded by King Edward IV with the title Lord Herbert of Raglan in 1461,
1 CONC  having assumed an English-style surname in place of the Welsh patronymic. In 1468 he was promoted to Earl of Pembroke. He obtained custody of the young Henry, Earl of Richmond, whom he planned to marry to his own daughter. Howeve
1 CONC r, he soon fell out with his great rival, Warwick "the Kingmaker", who turned against the king. Herbert was executed by the Lancastrians, now led by Warwick, after the Battle of Edgecote Moor, near Banbury.
1 CONT
1 CONT Herbert was succeeded by his legitimate son, William, but the earldom was surrendered in 1479. It was later revived for a grandson, another William Herbert, the son of Black William's illegitimate son Richard.
0 @N6000000008176810485@ NOTE [st1.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Name Prefix:<NPFX> Earl
0 @N6000000001877504930@ NOTE Sir
1 CONT Knighted KCB April 1475 at Westminster when Prince Edward created Prince of Wales
1 CONT Kt Banneret 24.7.1482 in Scotland by Richard Duke of Gloucester (Richard iii)
1 CONT of Caldicot Castle
1 CONT owned manors of Llanbadock and Henrhiw
1 CONT Most of the administration of the Herbert estates fell to him
1 CONT By 1476 Steward of Raglan
1 CONT Easter 1476 farmed Lordship of Cilgerran
1 CONT 1477 - 78 & 1480 - 81 Steward of Crickhowell
1 CONT 1478 - 81 Constable of Caio and Mallean
1 CONT Steward of Gower & Swansea
1 CONT 1480s Steward of Herbert properties Caldicot & Magor
1 CONT His public career was almost non existent before H vii
1 CONT Probably joined H vii and Rhys ap Thomas as they neared Shrewsbury. Even his neutrality was important. Henry toyed with the idea of marrying his sister to win him over. At the same time Richard iii thought he was safe as he was gu
1 CONC arded by Sir Walter and Sir Rhys ap Thomas.
1 CONT False rumour at Cardigan told H vii that Sir Walter had raised a force against him.
1 CONT Sir Walt had shared in the Herbert's good fortune inder Richard iii, granted properties in Beds & Surrey
1 CONT H vii's favour shown when Sir Walt allowed to retain part of Elizabeth Herbert's property (NB he was chief overseer of 2nd Earl's will and therefore well placed to manipulate)
1 CONT The Herberts were not treated well by E iv after the death of William 1st E 1469
1 CONT 1485 Steward of Abergavenny, from Jasper Tudor
1 CONT Aug 1485 - re Henry vii's advance to Bosworth. "Richard (iii) had heard of Henry's arrival but was so certain that Sir Rhys (ap Thomas) and Sir Walter H would easily defeat him that ....." Richard had not heard of Sir Rhys' defect
1 CONC ion.
1 CONT 28.5.1486 Grant during pleasure to Walter Herbert Kt of office of Steward and Constable of Contreselly & Steward of Lordship of Talgarth
1 CONT 1487 Banneret at coronation of Hvii's queen
1 CONT 1487 Steward of Usk - til death
1 CONT 1489 Steward of the Royal Lordship of Trelleck
1 CONT Feb 1496 Steward of Caldicot & Magor (for Duchy of Lancs)
1 CONT 1502 entertained Queen Elizabeth, wife of H vii, at Raglan
1 CONT Alledged extortion by him as Marcher Lord of Chepstow. The President of the Council of Marches reported to the king 1534 that Sir Walter was giving his protection within his lordship to 5 men who had committed wilful murder, 18 wh
1 CONC o had committed other killings, 25 thieves and outlaws who had committed every variety of crime including torture and robbing the cathedral at Llanduff.
1 CONT Case re land at Llanbadock. Def Henry Earl of Worcester. He argues that Sir Walter H Kt was seised of it for 38 years when he died it descended to Earl of Worcester as cousin and next heir, ie son and heir of Elizabeth, dau and he
1 CONC ir of William Earl of Huntingdon, brother and heir of the said Sir Walter
1 CONT By deed he settled estates on feoffees ? including William Herbert (probably of Troy, later Sir William), Sir George of St Julians, and many others named
1 CONT remained a Yorkist after accession of Henry vii
1 CONT Sir John Trevelyan wrote to H vii in 1488? H had been despoiled of his lands by "Sir Walter now deceased" in the time of Jasper Tudor. Sir Walter left them to "his widow Lady Ann now living". Sir John got his lands back.
1 CONT 7.7.1491 Commission to raise assistance for the king's war in France in lordships of Overwent, Netherwent & co. Meryonnothin
1 CONT July 1497 Bond - Sir Walter H to make no unlawful gathering before Xmas & not to assault Sir Morgan ap John
1 CONT "Sir Walter was a person of honour in his time and commanded a Brigade of 5000 Welsh against the Scots"
1 CONT H vii, before his landing, thought that Sir Walter's sister might be a suitable wife for himself "mature and ripe to be coupled in matrimony". Sir Walter "a man of ancient stock & great power amongst the Welsh"
1 CONT Jan 1508/9 Re castle & lordship of Raglan - "Sir Walter entered these from Wm E of P's death 16 Jul 6 H vii til the day of his death 16 Sept 23 H vii by what right was not known". Castle and lordship allowed to remain for life to 
1 CONC Anne, his widow together with a long list of goods that "which belonged to sd Walter which by reason of a certain outlawry promulgated against him were forfeited"
1 CONT Buried Tintern
1 CONT Source GWH 1 / 1B
1 CONT www.stirnet.com
1 CONT rootsweb turner-thomas
