0 @N6000000001744948509@ NOTE from Mike Faris:
1 CONT                Some confusion as to the extension of the line at this point.  John
1 CONT                Hussey had two wives and two sons named Giles.  The Giles from the first
1 CONT                wife died as an infant and the Giles from the second wife is the one
1 CONT                whose mother was Anne Grey.   Burkes "Extinct Baronages" lists Anne Grey
1 CONT                as John's first wife and mother of the Giles that died young - this is
1 CONT                wrong.   Anne Grey was John Hussey's second wife.  John was beheaded at
1 CONT                Lincoln on June 29, 1537, and Anne Grey had her will proved on February
1 CONT                11,1545/6 and mentions son Giles.
1 CONT
1 CONT                She was sent to the Tower for calling the "illigitimized" Mary (daughter
1 CONT                of Henry VIII and Catherine of Arragon) "Princess". Henry's reason was
1 CONT                he did not want a female heir to the throne.  Queen Elizabeth restored
1 CONT                the children in blood only in 1562.
1 CONT
1 CONT                Anne's older half brother married Sir John Hussey's sister.
0 @N6000000003949813063@ NOTE Almost everyone, including several published sources, have Joan as daughter of Thomas Percy, son of Henry Algernon Percy. However, they do not explain how Joan Percy, b. in or after 1521, had a son William, b. 1505 and a son John,
1 CONC  b. 1513. Henry Algernon Percy didn't marry Catherine Spencer until "bef. 1502" [CP IX:720]. His son Thomas Percy was a younger son & born after his elder brother Henry was born "circa 1502" [CP IX:720 and Burke's Peerage, p. 2120
1 CONC ]. Therefore Thomas was born at the earliest 1503/4. Therefore it is impossible to think that Thomas was grandfather by Eleanor Harbottle of William, b. 1505 (only a year or two after).
1 CONT This line comes from Eileen Suggs, [suggs16@msn.com], who found it in the Library of Congress (Washington, DC) several years ago, while documenting one of her Mayflower descents. Unfortunately she did not write down the sources, b
1 CONC ecause this line was not what she was concentrating on; she just happened on it while researching the other. It has the Harbottle connection, just as the Thomas Percy ancestry does, but it is John Percy & Margaret Harbottle.
