0 @N6000000003658827144@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT Knt., K.G., of Grafton Regis, Pattishall, and Wicken, Northamptonshire, and London, king's knight, Privy Councillor, Lieutenant of Calais, Constable and Treasurer of England, son and heir of Richard Wydeville, Knt., of Grafton Reg
1 CONC is, Northamptonshire and the Mote, near Maidstone, Kent, King's esquire, Seneschal of Normandy, treasurer-general of Normandy, Captain of Guines and Caen, Lieutenant of Calais, Constable of the Tower of London and Rochester Castle
1 CONC , by Joan, daughter of Thomas Bittlesgate.
1 CONT
1 CONT He fought in the French wars under King Henry V and John, Duke of Bedford.  He was knighted in 1426 at Leicester by King Henry VI.  In 1435 he was a knight bachelor in the Regent's court and is said to have been captured by the Fr
1 CONC ench at Gerberoy.  He was in the Earl of Suffolk's retinue in 1436.  He took part in the attempt to relieve Meaux in 1439.  In 1440 he and his wife were part of the escort of Margaret of Anjou coming to England for her marriage.  
1 CONC he was created Lord Rivers 9 May 1448 in tail male.  In 1461 he was taken prisoner at the Battle of Towton, but soon obtained a pardon from King Edward IV.  On 24 May 1466 he was created Earl of Rivers.  SIR RICHARD WYDEVILLE, 1st
1 CONC  Earl Rivers, was executed after the Battle of Edgecote without trial outside Coventry, Warwickshire 12 August 1469.  he was buried at Pontefract, Yorkshire.  His widow, Jacquette, Duchess of Bedford, died 30 May 1472.
0 @N6000000004533169667@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers (d. 1469) was an English nobleman,best remembered as the father of Elizabeth Woodville, wife of EdwardIV.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of another Sir Richard Woodville, chamberlain to theDuke of Bedford. After the duke died the younger Richard married thewidowed duchess, Jacquetta of Luxembourg.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was created Baron Rivers by Henry VI in 1448. Initially he was onthe Lancastrian side in the Wars of the Roses, but he later switchedand became a Yorkist. After the marriage of his daughter Elizabeth toEdward IV, he was created
1 CONC  Earl Rivers (1466) and appointed LordTreasurer by his new son-in-law.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lord Rivers was executed by the Lancastrians after the Battle ofEdgecote. His eldest son, Anthony succeeded him as earl.
