0 @N6000000002188342137@ NOTE Elizabeth Woodville
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth Woodville
1 CONT Queen consort of England
1 CONT Coronation 26 May 1465
1 CONT Born 3 February 1437
1 CONT Grafton Regis
1 CONT Died 8 June 1492
1 CONT Bermondsey
1 CONT Buried St. George's Chapel, Windsor
1 CONT Consort 1 May 1464 - 9 April 1483
1 CONT Consort to Sir John Grey
1 CONT Edward IV
1 CONT Issue Thomas Grey, Richard Grey, Elizabeth, Mary, Cecily, Edward V, Margaret Plantagenet, Richard, Anne, George, Catherine, Bridget
1 CONT Royal House York
1 CONT Father Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers
1 CONT Mother Jacquetta of Luxembourg
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth Woodville or Wydeville (3 February 1437 – 7 June/8 June 1492) was the Queen consort of King Edward IV of England from 1464 until his death in 1483.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life and first marriage
1 CONT     * 2 Queen consort
1 CONT     * 3 Queen Mother
1 CONT     * 4 Elizabeth and Richard III
1 CONT     * 5 Later life
1 CONT     * 6 Legacy
1 CONT     * 7 Children of Elizabeth Wydeville
1 CONT           o 7.1 By Sir John Grey
1 CONT           o 7.2 By King Edward IV
1 CONT     * 8 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life and first marriage
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth was born circa 1437 at Grafton Regis, Northamptonshire, the daughter of Sir Richard Wydeville (later made first Earl Rivers) and Jacquetta of Luxembourg, who had previously been married to John of Lancaster, Duke of Bedf
1 CONC ord. Although spelling of the family name has sometimes been modernized to "Woodville", it was spelled "Wydeville" in contemporary publications by Caxton and "Widvile" on Queen Elizabeth's tomb at St. George's Chapel, Windsor Cast
1 CONC le. Through her mother, Elizabeth was a distant descendant of King John of England. She was a maid of honour to Margaret of Anjou, Queen of Henry VI in 1445 when she was only about 10. In about 1452, she married Sir John Grey of G
1 CONC roby, who was killed at the Second Battle of St Albans in 1461, fighting for the Lancastrian cause, which would become a source of irony as Edward IV was the Yorkist claimant to the throne. Elizabeth had two sons from the marriage
1 CONC , Thomas (later Marquess of Dorset) and Richard.
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth was called "the most beautiful woman in the Island of Britain" with "heavy-lidded eyes like those of a dragon", suggesting a different standard of beauty from the one we have today.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Queen consort
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward IV had many mistresses, the most notorious being Jane Shore, and did not have a reputation for fidelity. His marriage to Elizabeth Wydeville took place secretly (with only the bride's mother and two ladies in attendance) on
1 CONC  1 May 1464, at her family home in Northamptonshire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's preference for high living meant that he relied heavily on his mentor, Richard Neville, 16th Earl of Warwick (also known as Warwick the Kingmaker), who was responsible for having placed him on the throne and the effective
1 CONC  ruler of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT At around the time of Edward's secret marriage, Warwick was negotiating an alliance with France in an effort to thwart a similar arrangement being made by his sworn enemy Margaret of Anjou, wife of the deposed Henry VI. The plan w
1 CONC as that Edward should marry a French princess. When the marriage to Elizabeth became public, its concealment was the cause of considerable rancour on Warwick's part. Later, when Elizabeth's relatives, especially her brother, Antho
1 CONC ny Woodville, 2nd Earl Rivers, began to be favoured over him, he turned against Edward and eventually changed sides. In fact, he and Margaret of Anjou formed an alliance of their own to restore Henry to the throne and Warwick's da
1 CONC ughter Anne married Margaret's son Edward.
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth was crowned Queen on Ascension Day, 26 May 1465. There was an infamous incident at her coronation which was not attended by Edward IV (kings traditionally did not attend their consorts' coronations) in which her mother's
1 CONC  Luxembourg kinsmen landed in a ship at Ship's Green and arrived at Westminster Abbey carrying shields painted with the figure of Melusine, a "water-witch" (actually a medieval version of the old pagan goddess) described variously
1 CONC  as a mermaid or possibly as a female figure depicted as a snake from the waist down, but with the face clearly that of the young queen. This immediately caused whispers of witchcraft to circulate throughout the Abbey, as it was i
1 CONC ndeed the intention of the Luxembourgers to suggest an accusation of witchcraft thereby. Elizabeth's brother Anthony came to her rescue, driving the Luxembourg kinsmen forth from the Abbey all the way to Ship's Green where he woul
1 CONC d not allow them to embark and depart until he had answered this charge of witchcraft in single combat with every one of them and scratched every Melusine shield. (If this incident is true, it is not recorded in any of Elizabeth W
1 CONC oodville's biography. The charge of witchcraft was later laid against the Duchess of Bedford in February 1467, some considerable time after the Coronation.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Nor was Warwick the only one who resented the way the queen's relatives scooped up favours and lucrative opportunities; in 1480, for example, when Elizabeth's obscure brother-in-law Sir Anthony Grey died (the second child of Lady 
1 CONC Katherine Percy and Edmund Grey, Earl of Kent), he was interred in St Albans Abbey with a brass marker to rival the one for the abbey's greatest abbot.
1 CONT
1 CONT That was nothing compared to the marriages the queen arranged for her family, the most outrageous being when her 20-year-old brother John Wydeville married Lady Katherine Neville, daughter of Ralph Neville, 1st Earl of Westmorland
1 CONC  by Joan Beaufort, widow of John Mowbray, 2nd Duke of Norfolk and dowager Duchess of Norfolk. The wealthy Katherine had been widowed three times and was probably in her sixties.
1 CONT
1 CONT The queen also married her sister, Catherine Wydeville, to her 11-year-old ward Henry Stafford, 2nd Duke of Buckingham. Another sister, Mary Wydeville, married William Herbert, 2nd Earl of Pembroke.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Queen Mother
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth and Edward's marriage had produced ten children, including two sons who were still living at the time of the king's sudden death in 1483. The elder, Edward, had been born in sanctuary at Westminster Abbey in 1470, during
1 CONC  the period when his father was out of power and in exile following his overthrow by Warwick in favour of Henry VI — Edward later returned to England and Warwick was killed at the Battle of Barnet in 1471. Henry VI was later murde
1 CONC red.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following Edward's death, Elizabeth now, briefly, became Queen Mother, but on 25 June 1483, her marriage was declared null and void by Parliament in the act Titulus Regius on the grounds that Edward had previously promised to marr
1 CONC y Lady Eleanor Butler, which was considered a legally binding contract that rendered any other marriage contract invalid as bigamous. (It was said that Eleanor Butler had done the same thing Elizabeth Wydeville did later: a widow 
1 CONC who caught Edward's eye, she refused to give in to him until he promised to marry her.) This information came to the fore when a priest (believed to be Robert Stillington, Bishop of Bath and Wells), testified that he had carried o
1 CONC ut the ceremony.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the basis of his evidence, all Elizabeth's children by Edward, including King Edward V, were declared illegitimate, and her brother-in-law, Richard III, was given the crown. Young Edward and his brother Richard, Duke of York, w
1 CONC ere kept in the Tower of London, where they had already been lodged to await the coronation. The exact fate of the so-called Princes in the Tower is unknown but both were dead in this or the next reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth now lost the title of Queen Mother and was referred to as Dame Elizabeth Grey. She and her other children were in sanctuary again, fearing for their safety. This may have been to protect themselves against jealous courti
1 CONC ers who wanted revenge against the entire Wydeville clan.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Elizabeth and Richard III
1 CONT
1 CONT On 1 March 1484 Elizabeth and her daughters came out of sanctuary and returned to court. Rumours even spread that the now-widowed King Richard was going to marry his niece Elizabeth of York. Richard issued a denial, but according 
1 CONC to the Crowland Chronicle he was pressured to do so by the Wydevilles' enemies who feared, among other things, that they would have to return the lands they had confiscated from the Wydevilles.
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth's behaviour has been a source of frustration to historians. They reason that she would never have recognised Richard as King unless she knew for sure that both her sons were dead and that she would have to resort to othe
1 CONC r means to keep her family in power. There was also the fact that Richard had had her brother Earl Rivers executed.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Wars of the Roses are notorious for the number of times that leading figures changed sides whenever it suited them (examples include the Earl of Warwick and the Duke of Clarence), and it is possible that Elizabeth was no excep
1 CONC tion. But would she have been heartless or thoughtless enough to side with a man who had quite likely killed her own sons and could thus arrange the deaths of herself and her daughters?
1 CONT
1 CONT There are several possible explanations for Elizabeth's willingness to reconcile with Richard:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. The Princes had died of natural causes for which Richard could not be held responsible (but then why did he not make this public, especially since rumours about their fate were already circulating?)
1 CONT    2. The Princes had been killed by a third party, and Richard had convinced Elizabeth that he was not involved. (In his biography of Richard III, Paul Murray Kendall suggests that the Duke of Buckingham may have been responsible
1 CONC . Margaret Beaufort or her third husband, Lord Stanley, are other possibilities.)
1 CONT    3. It is also known that by this time Elizabeth had been plotting with agents of Henry Tudor, another claimant to the throne, and it is possible that she was getting closer to Richard in case Henry's attempt failed.
1 CONT    4. Elizabeth may have planned to coerce Richard into marrying her daughter, thereby regaining her power, wealth, and prestige.
1 CONT    5. Elizabeth realised that by Richard marrying her daughter he would be acknowledging her daughter's legitimacy and thus her marriage to Edward IV. This would imply that her son Edward V was also legitimate and so the rightful 
1 CONC King, this would at least discredit Richard.
1 CONT    6. Elizabeth viewed people in light of what they could do for her. She may simply have been more concerned with herself than with the fate of her sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the end, Richard was defeated and killed at the Battle of Bosworth. Henry Tudor became King Henry VII and married Elizabeth of York. Elizabeth Wydeville's marriage to Edward IV was declared to have been valid, and thus their ch
1 CONC ildren were once again legitimised (because Henry wanted his wife to be the Yorkist heir to the throne, to cement his hold on it). Elizabeth was accorded the title of Queen Dowager.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Later life
1 CONT
1 CONT Scholars differ about why Queen Dowager Elizabeth spent her last five years living at Bermondsey Abbey. Among her modern biographers, David Baldwin believes that Henry VII forced her retreat from the Court, while Arlene Okerlund p
1 CONC resents evidence that indicates she was planning a religious, contemplative life as early as July 1486. At the Abbey, Elizabeth was treated with all the respect due to a Queen Mother, lived a regal life, and received a pension of 
1 CONC £400 and small gifts from the King. She did not attend her daughter's coronation, but was present at the birth of her second grandchild, Margaret, at Westminster Palace in November 1489. The Queen rarely visited her, although Eliz
1 CONC abeth's younger daughter, Cecilia Welles, who had secretly married Viscount Welles, came to see her as often as she could.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry VII briefly contemplated marrying her off to King James III of Scotland, when James' wife, Queen Margaret, died in 1488. James was killed in battle later that year, rendering the plans of Henry VII moot.
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen Dowager Elizabeth, nèe Wydeville, died on 8 June 1492. With the exception of the Queen, who was awaiting the birth of her fourth child and Cecilia (Viscountess Welles), her daughters attended the funeral at Windsor Castle: A
1 CONC nne (the future Countess of Surrey), Catherine (the future Countess of Devon) and Bridget (a sister at Dartford Priory). Her will specified a simple funeral. Many ardent Yorkists, who considered themselves slighted by the ordinary
1 CONC  and very simple burial of Edward IV's Queen on 12 June 1492, were not pleased. Elizabeth was laid to rest in the same chantry as her husband King Edward IV in St George's Chapel in Windsor Castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT During her later years, Elizabeth Wydeville had the satisfaction of knowing that her daughter was securely on the consort's throne. She lived to see the birth of two grandsons, Princes Arthur and Henry, the latter of whom would la
1 CONC ter become Henry VIII. Through her granddaughter, Queen Margaret of Scotland, Elizabeth became an ancestress of the Stuart, Hanover, and Windsor dynasties, whose descendants reign in England, Scotland and Wales today. Her great gr
1 CONC anddaughter and namesake Elizabeth I (the Virgin Queen) became arguably the greatest female monarch to sit on the English throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children of Elizabeth Wydeville
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] By Sir John Grey
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Thomas Grey, Earl of Huntingdon, Marquess of Dorset and Lord Ferrers de Groby
1 CONT     * Richard Grey
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] By King Edward IV
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Elizabeth of York (1466-1503), Queen Consort of England
1 CONT     * Mary of York (1467-1482), buried in St George's Chapel, Windsor Castle
1 CONT     * Cecily of York (1469-1507), Viscountess Welles
1 CONT     * Edward V of England (1470-1483/5), one of the Princes in the Tower
1 CONT     * Margaret Plantagenet (Princess of York) (Apr. 1472-Dec. 1472), buried in Westminster Abbey
1 CONT     * Richard, Duke of York (1473-1483/5), one of the Princes in the Tower
1 CONT     * Anne of York (1475-1511), Countess of Surrey
1 CONT     * George Plantagenet (1477-1479), Duke of Bedford; buried in St George's Chapel, Windsor Castle
1 CONT     * Catherine of York (1479-1527), Countess of Devon
1 CONT     * Bridget of York (1480-1517), nun at Dartford Priory, Kent
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
