0 @N6000000001744948495@ NOTE from Mike Faris:
1 CONT
1 CONT               John Hussey was appointed in 1533 chamberlain to the illegitimized 'Princess'
1 CONT                Mary, and his allegiance to her father seems about the same time tb have
1 CONT                begun to waver.
1 CONT
1 CONT                On the outbreak of the Lincolnshire rebellion, known is the Pilgrimage
1 CONT                of Grace, in the autumn of 1536,  Hussey seemed to remain firm in his
1 CONT                allegiance to the king.  Lord Hussey had been unable to bring in his
1 CONT                tenants to fight for the king when he had been ordered to do so.  Ha was
1 CONT                accused of making no effort to raise men to put down the rebellion, and
1 CONT                the king accused him of being a traitor when he refused to tell the
1 CONT                names of the men behind the rebellion.  He was tried with Lord Darcy at
1 CONT                Westminster on May 15, 1537.  Cromwell offered him a pardon if he would
1 CONT                furnish particulars concerning the rebellion and he said he could not do
1 CONT                so.  There is a difference of opinion as to where he was executed --
1 CONT                Sleaford or Tyburn, and as to whether he was hung or beheaded.
1 CONT
1 CONT                After John Hussey was executed, his manor of Sleford, with other lands
1 CONT                to the value of five thousands pounds per annum were confiscated, and
1 CONT                barony forfeited.  His children were, however, afterwards restored in
1 CONT                Parliament and Queen Elizabeth; but neither his estates nor titles were
1 CONT                granted to his heirs.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT source: WFT 2 #216
1 CONT
1 CONT There is some dispute as to which Giles, son of Baron John Hussey of Sleaford, was Lord of Caythorpe.
1 CONT
1 CONT From: Theodore Sarphie <tsarphie@earthlink.net>
1 CONT
1 CONT Dear Pam,
1 CONT
1 CONT The Babb family report sent to me on June 16, 1998, has an error that breaks the line of descent as it was presented.  In Generation No. 9, the Mother of Sir Giles Hussey of Caythorpe was not Lady Anne Grey.   Sir Giles¹ mother wa
1 CONC s the second wife of Baron Hussey of Sleford--Margaret Blount.  I consulted the following distinguished references:
1 CONT
1 CONT  1)  ³Genealogical History of the Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited,
1 CONT       and Extinct Peerages of the British Empire.²
1 CONT       by Sir Bernard Burke, C.B., LL.D., Ulster King of Arms
1 CONT       New Edition
1 CONT       London: Harrison, 59, Pall Mall, 1883.
1 CONT       Genealogical Publishing Co.
1 CONT       Baltimore, Maryland
1 CONT       Page 294.
1 CONT
1 CONT  2)  ³General and Heraldic Dictionary of the Peerages of
1 CONT       England, Ireland, and Scotland, Extinct, Dormant, and in
1 CONT       Abeyance.²
1 CONT       by John Burke, Esq.
1 CONT       England
1 CONT       London: Henry Colburn and Richard Bentley
1 CONT       New Burlington Street, 1831
1 CONT       Page 288.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Baron Hussey of Sieford, Lord John Hussey,
1 CONT  m. 1st Anne Grey, dau. of George Earl of Kent and had issue;
1 CONT       Giles--died without issue
1 CONT       Thomas--died without issue
1 CONT       Bridget, M. 1st Sir Richard Morrison, Knt.
1 CONT       Elizabeth, M. to ______ Hungerford, Esq.
1 CONT       Ann, M. 1st Sir Humphrey Browne, Knt.,
1 CONT               2nd to _____ Dimock, Esq.
1 CONT       Dorothy, M. to _____ Dockway, Esq.
1 CONT
1 CONT  m. 2nd Margaret, dau and heir of Sir Simon Blount, of
1 CONT         County Gloucester, and had issue;
1 CONT       Sir William
1 CONT       Sir Giles of Caythorpe, County Lincoln
1 CONT       Sir Gilbert
1 CONT       Elizabeth
1 CONT
1 CONT With time and some luck, I may be able to substantiate another connection of Mary Babb¹s back to Charlemagne.  I will keep you informed. Personally, I was really disappointed to find the error.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Best regards,
1 CONT
1 CONT  Sue Reid Sarphie
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Mike Faris <faris@ipa.net> wrote:
1 CONT
1 CONT There is a more recent series "The Complete Peerage" that clears up the matter placing Anne Grey as Hussey's second wife rather than the first wife.  The Giles that died as an infant or young child was of the first wife.  Wills ha
1 CONC ve been found of John Hussey and Anne Grey.  John's dated October 22, 1535,
1 CONT has provisions for wife, Lady Anne, sons William, Thomas, Gilbert and Sir Giles and others.   Lady Anne's will was dated March 1, 1544/5 and probated February 11, 1545/6, mentions two daughters and sons Sir William and Sir Giles. 
1 CONC  If you have access to "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists", Fifth Edition by Frederick Lewis Weis with additions and revisions by Walter Lee Shepard, Jr. you will find links between John and Anne (Grey) Hussey back to the Planteg
1 CONC anets and beyond.
1 CONT
1 CONT Regards,   Mike
0 @N6000000002116061121@ NOTE After reaching great prominence at Court, he refused to support Henry VIII's divorce from Catherine of Aragon.  He remained a supporter of Princess Mary after being the Comptroller of her Household. His second wife, Anne Grey, was
1 CONC  arrested for addressing Mary as "Princess" rather than "Lady", and was placed in the Tower from June to August 1536.   He sympathised with several Catholic insurrections in the North, and failed to take steps as Lord Lieutenant t
1 CONC o put them down.  He was arrested for High Treason and he was imprisoned in the Tower of London and then taken to Lincoln, where he was executed aged 71 despite having paid a fine of £3,000 to the king to win his clemency.
